window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"p":"Home","h":"","u":"index.html","t":"A hands-on tutorial course for high school FRC students. It starts with installing the tools and goes through PID, feedforward, SysId, and motion profiles, then odometry, vision, PathPlanner, logging, and swerve: the same techniques top teams use. You don't need any programming experience to start. You don't need a robot for most of it, either. Nearly every exercise runs in simulation on your laptop. 🔧 Go to the Test Bed Labs Two tabs at the top of every page switch between the Lessons and the Labs ."},{"p":"Home","h":"Before you start: learn Java","u":"index.html#before-you-start-learn-java","t":"This course doesn't teach Java from scratch. Before the labs, work through an interactive Java course: Coddy: Learn Java (free) Codecademy: Learn Java Finish at least through classes and objects . Lesson 3: Java Reference has the exact checklist, and it's the page to come back to whenever robot code uses Java you've forgotten."},{"p":"Home","h":"How this course works","u":"index.html#how-this-course-works","t":"The WPILib documentation is the official reference, and it's excellent. This course does not replace it. Think of WPILib as the textbook and this site as the lab manual: we explain things in order, at a beginner's pace, with exercises, and send you to WPILib for the full details. Every lesson follows the same shape: Section What it's for Why it matters The robot problem we're solving Read this WPILib pages to skim before or alongside the lesson Walkthrough Step-by-step explanation, with code you type yourself Try it Exercises in simulation or on the robot Check yourself Questions to answer before moving on. Click each one to check your answer. Type the code, don't paste it You learn more from fixing your own typos than from running someone else's working code."},{"p":"Home","h":"The path","u":"index.html#the-path","t":"flowchart LR A[Foundations<br/>0–3] --> B[Hardware<br/>4–5] B --> C[Robot Code<br/>6–9] C --> D[Drivetrain Project<br/>10–11] D --> E[Controls<br/>12–14] D --> F[Team Workflow<br/>15–16] E --> G[Going Further<br/>17–21] Part Lessons You'll be able to... Foundations 0–3 Set up WPILib, use Git and the terminal, and look up the Java robot code uses Hardware 4–5 Trace power and data through the robot; know our motors (NEO, CIM, 775pro), the SPARK MAX, and our sensors; control pneumatics Robot Code 6–9 Explain the 20 ms loop and the command scheduler, write subsystems and commands, read sensors in real units, debug in simulation Drivetrain Project 10–11 Build a driveable robot with good controls and distance-based autonomous routines Controls 12–14 Tune PID, add feedforward, characterize mechanisms with SysId, use motion profiles Team Workflow 15–16 Work on a shared codebase with branches, reviews, unit tests, and CI Going Further 17–21 Track the robot's pose with odometry and AprilTag vision, follow PathPlanner paths, debug matches from logs, and understand swerve"},{"p":"Home","h":"Test bed labs","u":"index.html#test-bed-labs","t":"Labs are hands-on exercises on the team's test bed , a board with a roboRIO, SPARK MAX controllers, motors, and sensors. They have their own Labs tab at the top of every page. Each one goes with a lesson: Labs What you'll do 0–2 Set up, spin your first motor, and drive the West Coast Drive 3–6 Buttons, limit switches, a knob, and an encoder: one new sensor or idea per lab 7–8 Autonomous on the WCD, then turning with the NavX2 gyro 9–10 PID: turn a wheel exactly 10 times, then hold an elevator against gravity 11 Odometry: watch the WCD move on a field map See the Labs overview for the full list and when to do each one."},{"p":"Home","h":"Where to start","u":"index.html#where-to-start","t":"If you... Start at Have never programmed An interactive Java course first ( Coddy or Codecademy ), then Lesson 0 Know Java from a class Skim the Java Reference , then Lesson 6 Already write robot code Lesson 7.1 , then Controls and Going Further"},{"p":"Home","h":"Time","u":"index.html#time","t":"Plan on about one lesson per meeting for most people, after the Java course. The Controls lessons usually take two or three meetings each. Don't rush the exercises. They're where the learning happens."},{"p":"Home","h":"Getting help","u":"index.html#getting-help","t":"Ask a returning programmer or mentor. Everyone was a beginner once. Search Chief Delphi , the FRC forum, before posting. Most questions have been answered. Found a mistake in this course? See How to Contribute ."},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"Why it matters","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#why-it-matters","t":"Learn Java first Before the robot lessons, complete an interactive Java course. See Lesson 3: Java Reference for which course and which topics. You can't learn to drive without a car. Before writing robot code, you need a working development environment, and the good news is it can simulate a robot on your laptop . You don't need a robot, a roboRIO, or even a controller to do most of this course. By the end of this lesson you'll have: WPILib's tools installed a robot project created from the official template that project running in the simulator"},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"Read this","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#read-this","t":"Zero to Robot: Introduction WPILib Installation Guide Creating a Robot Program"},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"Walkthrough","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"What you're installing, and why","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#what-youre-installing-and-why","t":"The WPILib installer bundles everything an FRC programmer needs. It's worth knowing what each piece is, because you'll hear the names constantly: Piece What it is When you use it WPILib VS Code A copy of the VS Code editor with the WPILib extension built in Writing, building, and deploying code Java JDK The Java compiler and runtime, the exact version WPILib expects Every build. You never open it directly. GradleRIO The build tool that compiles your code and sends it to the robot Every build and deploy Simulation GUI A window that fakes the Driver Station and robot hardware Testing code without a robot Glass, AdvantageScope, SysId, DataLogTool, and more Dashboards and analysis tools Debugging, tuning, and later lessons Why a separate VS Code? WPILib's copy is pinned to versions that are tested together. If you already use VS Code for other things, keep both. Open the one with the WPILib logo for robot code."},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"Step 1: Install WPILib","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#step-1-install-wpilib","t":"Go to the WPILib releases page and download the installer for this season for your operating system. The installation guide above has direct links. Run it. On Windows, the file is an .iso : double-click to mount it, then run WPILibInstaller.exe . Choose Everything and Install for this user . When it asks about VS Code, choose Download for this computer only . Wait. It's a large download (a few gigabytes). Use the same season version as the team WPILib releases a new version every January. A project made with one year's tools won't build with another year's without upgrading. Ask a returning programmer which version the team's robot code uses."},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"Step 2: Install Git","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#step-2-install-git","t":"Download and install Git from git-scm.com . The default options are fine. You'll use it in Lesson 1 ."},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"Step 3: Create your first project","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#step-3-create-your-first-project","t":"Open WPILib VS Code (look for the WPILib logo). Press Ctrl + Shift + P to open the command palette . You'll use this constantly. It's how you run every WPILib action. Type WPILib: Create a new project and press Enter . Fill in the form: Project type: Template Language: Java Project base: Command Robot Base folder: somewhere you'll find again, like a robotics folder in Documents Project name: my-first-robot Team number: 4674 Click Generate Project , then choose to open it in the current window. The New Project Creator, filled in. From the WPILib docs, Creating a Robot Program (CC BY 4.0). Mac and Linux Use Cmd + Shift + P on Mac. Everything else is the same."},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"Step 4: Look around the project","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#step-4-look-around-the-project","t":"Expand the folders on the left. You don't need to understand all of this yet, but know where things are: my-first-robot/ ├── build.gradle ← build settings (rarely edited) ├── gradlew, gradlew.bat ← the build tool launcher (Lesson 2) ├── vendordeps/ ← vendor libraries like CTRE and REV └── src/main/java/frc/robot/ ├── Main.java ← starts the program. Never edit. ├── Robot.java ← the robot's main loop (Lesson 6) ├── RobotContainer.java ← creates subsystems, binds buttons (Lesson 7) ├── Constants.java ← numbers like ports and gear ratios (Lesson 8) ├── commands/ ← command classes └── subsystems/ ← subsystem classes"},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"Step 5: Run it in simulation","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#step-5-run-it-in-simulation","t":"Press Ctrl + Shift + P and run WPILib: Simulate Robot Code . The first build takes a minute or two while Gradle downloads what it needs. Later builds are much faster. When asked, check Sim GUI and click OK. A window opens. In the Robot State box, click Teleoperated . That's it. Your robot code is running, just on fake hardware."},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"A tour of the Sim GUI","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#a-tour-of-the-sim-gui","t":"The Sim GUI, with each panel numbered. From the WPILib docs, Simulation Specific User Interface Elements (CC BY 4.0). Robot State: the robot's current mode. Click a label to change modes, just like the real Driver Station. Timing: the robot's timers. You can pause and step time here. System Joysticks: the controllers and keyboards your computer can see. FMS: pretends to be the competition field system. NetworkTables: the values your code publishes to the dashboard. Joysticks: the joysticks your robot code actually reads. Drag a device here from System Joysticks. Other Devices: devices that don't fit the other panels, like some gyros. More panels, like PWM outputs and DIO, can be opened from the Hardware menu."},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"Using a controller or the keyboard","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#using-a-controller-or-the-keyboard","t":"Drag a controller (or Keyboard 0 ) from System Joysticks into slot 0 of Joysticks . Your code's CommandXboxController(0) reads whatever is in slot 0. Drag a device from System Joysticks into a Joysticks slot. From the WPILib docs, Simulation Specific User Interface Elements (CC BY 4.0). No controller? Use the keyboard Drag Keyboard 0 into slot 0. To choose which keys control which axis, open DS → Keyboard 0 Settings in the Sim GUI's menu bar. The WPILib docs show a setup that makes the keyboard act like an Xbox controller's two sticks (axes 1 and 4): Keyboard settings that act like an Xbox controller. From the WPILib docs, Simulation Specific User Interface Elements (CC BY 4.0)."},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"When it doesn't work","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#when-it-doesnt-work","t":"Problem Fix Command palette doesn't show WPILib commands You opened regular VS Code, or the folder isn't a WPILib project. Open the WPILib VS Code and File → Open Folder the project. Build fails on the first run with a download error You're offline or behind a school firewall. Try another network. \"Unsupported class file version\" or a JDK error The project and the installed WPILib are different seasons. Reinstall the matching year. Sim GUI never appears Check the terminal panel at the bottom of VS Code for the first red error line."},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"Try it","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#try-it","t":"Install WPILib and Git Create my-first-robot from the Command Robot template Run it in simulation and switch to Teleoperated Drag a keyboard or controller into Joystick 0 Take a screenshot and post it in the team channel Next: real hardware Once simulation works, do Lab 0 to install the rest of the tools and deploy a project to the test bed."},{"p":"0. Setup","h":"Check yourself","u":"foundations/00-setup.html#check-yourself","t":"Where is Robot.java in your project? src/main/java/frc/robot/Robot.java What's the difference between Simulate Robot Code and Deploy Robot Code ? Simulate builds your code and runs it on your laptop with fake hardware. Deploy builds it and copies it to the roboRIO on a real robot, where it controls real motors. What's the command palette, and how do you open it? It's VS Code's search box for actions. Open it with Ctrl + Shift + P ( Cmd + Shift + P on Mac). Every WPILib action starts there. Why does everyone on the team need the same WPILib version? Each season's tools only build projects made for that season. Different versions mean the code won't build on someone's laptop."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Why it matters","u":"foundations/01-git.html#why-it-matters","t":"Picture build season: five programmers, one robot project, a competition in two weeks. Without a system, someone copies an old version over a new one, a working autonomous gets lost, and nobody knows what changed when the robot suddenly stops driving straight. Git fixes all of that. It: saves a snapshot every time you commit , so you can always go back shows exactly what changed between any two versions, and who changed it lets several people work at the same time without overwriting each other Every FRC team that's serious about software uses Git, usually with GitHub to share the code. This course website is stored in Git too."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Read this","u":"foundations/01-git.html#read-this","t":"Git Version Control Introduction (WPILib) GitHub Skills : short, free, interactive courses. Do \"Introduction to GitHub.\""},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Walkthrough","u":"foundations/01-git.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Git vs. GitHub","u":"foundations/01-git.html#git-vs-github","t":"These get mixed up constantly: Git GitHub What it is A program on your computer A website What it does Tracks changes to files in a folder Stores a shared copy of the project online Works offline? Yes No Analogy Your notebook, with every draft saved The team's shared binder You can use Git without GitHub, but teams use both: Git on each laptop, GitHub as the copy everyone syncs with."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"The words you need","u":"foundations/01-git.html#the-words-you-need","t":"Term Meaning Repository (repo) A project folder that Git tracks. It has a hidden .git folder holding all the history. Commit A saved snapshot of the project, with a message saying what changed Staging Choosing which changes go into the next commit ( git add ) Branch A separate line of work, so experiments don't break the working code Remote A copy of the repo somewhere else, usually GitHub. The default one is named origin . Push Upload your new commits to the remote Pull Download other people's new commits and merge them into yours Clone Download a full copy of a repo from GitHub for the first time"},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"The three places your changes live","u":"foundations/01-git.html#the-three-places-your-changes-live","t":"This is the idea that makes Git click: flowchart LR W[\"Working folder<br/>(files you edit)\"] -- \"git add\" --> S[\"Staging area<br/>(changes picked for<br/>the next commit)\"] S -- \"git commit\" --> L[\"Local history<br/>(on your laptop)\"] L -- \"git push\" --> R[\"GitHub<br/>(shared with team)\"] R -- \"git pull\" --> W Editing a file changes only your working folder. Nothing is saved in Git until you add and commit , and nobody else sees it until you push ."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"One-time setup","u":"foundations/01-git.html#one-time-setup","t":"Tell Git who you are. Your name goes on every commit you make: git config --global user.name \"Your Name\" git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\" Use the same email as your GitHub account so GitHub links commits to you."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Your first commit","u":"foundations/01-git.html#your-first-commit","t":"Open a terminal in your my-first-robot folder. In VS Code: Terminal → New Terminal . git init # start tracking this folder git status # see what Git notices git add . # stage every changed file git commit -m \"Initial robot project\" git log --oneline # see your history WPILib projects already have a .gitignore The build/ folder and other generated files are listed in .gitignore , so Git skips them. Never commit build output. It's huge and it changes on every build."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Reading git status","u":"foundations/01-git.html#reading-git-status","t":"You'll run this more than any other Git command. It tells you exactly where your changes are: On branch main Changes to be committed: ← staged; will go in the next commit modified: src/main/java/frc/robot/RobotContainer.java Changes not staged for commit: ← edited but not staged yet modified: src/main/java/frc/robot/Constants.java Untracked files: ← new files Git isn't tracking yet src/main/java/frc/robot/subsystems/Intake.java"},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Writing good commit messages","u":"foundations/01-git.html#writing-good-commit-messages","t":"Future you, at 11 PM before a competition, will read these. Make them useful: ❌ Bad ✅ Good stuff Add intake subsystem with run and stop commands fixed it Fix right drive motors spinning backward changes Lower arm kP to stop oscillation at 90 degrees A good message says what changed and, if it isn't obvious, why . Keep each commit to one idea. Small commits are easy to understand and easy to undo."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Push to GitHub","u":"foundations/01-git.html#push-to-github","t":"Make a free account at github.com if you don't have one. Click New repository , name it my-first-robot , and leave every box unchecked (no README, no .gitignore ). You already have files. GitHub shows commands under \"…or push an existing repository from the command line.\" They look like this: git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-NAME/my-first-robot.git git branch -M main git push -u origin main Refresh the GitHub page. Your code is there. The first push may open a browser window to sign in. That's normal."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Getting the team's code: clone","u":"foundations/01-git.html#getting-the-teams-code-clone","t":"To work on a repo that already exists, like the team's robot code, you clone it instead of using git init : git clone https://github.com/Robojacks/REPO-NAME.git That creates a folder with the full project and all its history, already connected to GitHub."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"The daily loop","u":"foundations/01-git.html#the-daily-loop","t":"git pull # 1. get everyone's latest work # ...edit code, build, test... git status # 2. see what you changed git diff # 3. read your own changes before saving them git add . # 4. stage git commit -m \"Describe the change\" git push # 5. share Pull before you start, every time If you start working on old code, your changes and a teammate's may collide. Pulling first avoids most conflicts."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Undoing things","u":"foundations/01-git.html#undoing-things","t":"Situation Command Undo edits to a file you haven't staged git restore FILE Unstage a file (keep the edits) git restore --staged FILE Fix the message of the commit you just made (before pushing) git commit --amend -m \"Better message\" Undo a commit that's already pushed git revert COMMIT_ID (makes a new commit that reverses it) See an old version of a file git log --oneline FILE , then git show COMMIT_ID:path/to/FILE Don't rewrite shared history Commands like git reset --hard and git push --force can permanently delete teammates' work. Don't use them on the team repo unless a lead programmer is helping you."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Merge conflicts","u":"foundations/01-git.html#merge-conflicts","t":"When two people change the same lines of the same file, Git can't pick a winner, so it asks you. After a git pull , the file will contain markers like this: <<<<<<< HEAD private final double kP = 0.05 ; // your version ======= private final double kP = 0.08 ; // the version you pulled >>>>>>> origin / main To fix it: Decide what the line should be. Sometimes it's one version, sometimes a mix. Ask whoever wrote the other one. Delete the marker lines ( <<<<<<< , ======= , >>>>>>> ). Build to make sure it compiles. git add the file and git commit . VS Code highlights conflicts and gives you Accept Current / Accept Incoming / Accept Both buttons above each one."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Using Git inside VS Code","u":"foundations/01-git.html#using-git-inside-vs-code","t":"You don't have to type everything. The Source Control panel (the branch icon on the left side) lets you stage files with + , type a message, commit, and sync. It's the same Git underneath. Learn the commands first so you know what the buttons do."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Try it","u":"foundations/01-git.html#try-it","t":"Set your user.name and user.email Make three commits to your project, each with a clear message Push them to GitHub and find them on the website Change a line, run git diff , then git restore the file to throw the change away With a partner: both clone the same repo, both change the same line , both push. Whoever pushes second resolves the conflict."},{"p":"1. Git & Version Control","h":"Check yourself","u":"foundations/01-git.html#check-yourself","t":"What's the difference between a commit and a push? A commit saves a snapshot on your computer . A push uploads your commits to GitHub so others can see them. You edited a file but git commit says nothing changed. Why? You didn't stage it. Run git add first. Commit only saves staged changes. Why should you git pull before starting work? So you build on top of everyone else's latest changes instead of an old version. That avoids merge conflicts. What causes a merge conflict, and how do you fix one? Two people changed the same lines. Edit the file to the correct final version, delete the conflict markers, build, then add and commit. Why isn't the build/ folder in Git? It's generated every build, it's large, and it's different on every computer. .gitignore tells Git to skip it. We'll cover branches and pull requests in Lesson 15 ."},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Why it matters","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#why-it-matters","t":"The terminal (also called the command line, shell, or console) is a text window where you type commands instead of clicking buttons. It can look old-fashioned, but FRC programmers use it constantly: Git is a terminal program. The VS Code buttons just run terminal commands for you. Building and deploying with Gradle works from the terminal, and the terminal shows errors more clearly than the buttons do. At competition, when the VS Code buttons misbehave and the queue is calling your team, typing ./gradlew deploy is the fastest way to get code on the robot."},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Read this","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#read-this","t":"Introduction to GradleRIO Building and Deploying Robot Code"},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Walkthrough","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Opening a terminal","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#opening-a-terminal","t":"The easiest way is inside VS Code: Terminal → New Terminal , or Ctrl + ` (the key left of 1). It opens at the bottom of the window, already inside your project folder. Which terminal you get depends on your computer: Computer Default terminal Notes Windows PowerShell Most commands below work. Use gradlew.bat instead of ./gradlew if ./gradlew fails. Mac zsh Everything below works as written Linux bash Everything below works as written"},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Reading the prompt","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#reading-the-prompt","t":"Before the blinking cursor, the terminal shows a prompt . It tells you where you are: PS C:\\Users\\kedrick\\robotics\\my-first-robot> ← Windows PowerShell kedrick@laptop my-first-robot % ← Mac The folder you're \"in\" is the current directory . Commands act on files there unless you say otherwise."},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Moving around","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#moving-around","t":"Command What it does Example pwd Print the folder you're in pwd ls List files and folders here ls cd FOLDER Move into a folder cd src cd .. Go up one level cd .. cd ~ Go to your home folder cd ~ mkdir NAME Make a new folder mkdir robotics code . Open the current folder in VS Code code . Two habits that save you time Tab completion: type the first few letters of a file or folder, then press Tab , and the terminal finishes the name. Press it twice to see options. History: press Up to bring back the last command. Keep pressing to go further back."},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Paths","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#paths","t":"A path is the address of a file. A relative path starts from where you are: src/main/java An absolute path starts from the top of the drive: C:\\Users\\kedrick\\robotics or /Users/kedrick/robotics . means \"this folder\" and .. means \"the folder above\" Spaces in names A folder called My Robot must be typed in quotes: cd \"My Robot\" . Name your project folders without spaces to avoid this."},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Gradle: building from the terminal","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#gradle-building-from-the-terminal","t":"Every WPILib project includes a Gradle wrapper , gradlew , in the project's top folder. Run these from that folder: Command What it does ./gradlew build Compile the code and run the unit tests (Lesson 16) ./gradlew deploy Build, then copy the code to the roboRIO and restart it ./gradlew simulateJava Build and start the simulator ./gradlew test Run only the unit tests ./gradlew clean Delete old build output. Try this when a build acts strangely. On Windows, if ./gradlew gives an error, use .\\gradlew.bat build or gradlew build . Deploying needs a connection to the robot Your laptop must be connected to the robot's network (Wi-Fi or USB/Ethernet) for deploy to find the roboRIO. With team number 4674 set, Gradle finds it automatically."},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Reading a build error","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#reading-a-build-error","t":"A failed build prints a lot. Here's what to look for: > Task :compileJava FAILED /home/kedrick/my-first-robot/src/main/java/frc/robot/subsystems/Intake.java:14: error: ';' expected motor.set(0.75) ^ 1 error FAILURE: Build failed with an exception. ... BUILD FAILED in 3s Scroll up to the first line that says error: . The bottom is just a summary. It names the file ( Intake.java ) and line ( 14 ). It says what's wrong ( ';' expected ). The ^ points at the exact spot. Fix the first error and build again. One mistake often causes a pile of later errors that disappear once the first is fixed."},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Common errors and what they mean","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#common-errors-and-what-they-mean","t":"Error message Usual meaning ';' expected Missing semicolon at the end of a line cannot find symbol A typo in a name, or a missing import incompatible types Passing the wrong type, like a double where a Command is expected class X is public, should be declared in a file named X.java The class name and file name don't match Could not find roboRIO The laptop isn't connected to the robot, or the team number is wrong Let VS Code help Red squiggly lines show many errors before you build. Hover over one to see the message, and use the lightbulb icon to add a missing import ."},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Try it","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#try-it","t":"Open a terminal in VS Code and run pwd and ls Use cd to walk into src/main/java/frc/robot , then cd .. back out to the project folder Run ./gradlew build and get BUILD SUCCESSFUL Delete a ; in RobotContainer.java , build, and find the file and line number in the error. Put the ; back. Run ./gradlew simulateJava to start the simulator from the terminal"},{"p":"2. The Terminal","h":"Check yourself","u":"foundations/02-terminal.html#check-yourself","t":"How do you get to the folder above the one you're in? cd .. What's the difference between a relative and an absolute path? A relative path starts from your current folder ( src/main ). An absolute path starts from the top of the drive ( C:\\Users\\... or /Users/... ) and works from anywhere. Where in the output is the real cause of a build failure? At the first error: line, which names the file and line number. The lines at the bottom are just a summary. What does ./gradlew deploy do? It builds the code, copies it to the roboRIO over the robot's network, and restarts the robot program."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"How to use this page","u":"foundations/03-java.html#how-to-use-this-page","t":"You learn Java before this course, from an interactive Java course ( see below ). This page doesn't replace that. It's a reference : the Java that robot code actually uses, in one place, with notes on how each idea shows up on the robot. Come back here whenever robot code uses something you've forgotten, or something your Java course didn't cover. Three topics matter a lot in FRC and are often skipped or rushed in beginner courses, so read them even if you finished a course: static and final , used for Constants Inheritance: extends and @Override , used by every subsystem Lambdas and method references , used by every command and button binding Robot connection boxes Look for the Robot connection boxes. They show where each Java idea appears in robot code."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Learn Java first","u":"foundations/03-java.html#learn-java-first","t":"Before Lab 1 , work through one of these interactive Java courses. Both run in your browser, so there's nothing to install. Course Notes Coddy: Learn Java Short interactive lessons, free Codecademy: Learn Java Interactive lessons with projects Finish at least these topics. Robot code uses every one of them: Variables and types ( int , double , boolean , String ) Math and comparison operators if / else Loops ( for , while ) Methods: parameters and return values Classes and objects: fields, methods, new Constructors public and private Inheritance, interfaces, and lambdas are a bonus if your course covers them. If it doesn't, this page does. Already know Java from a class? Skip the outside course. Skim this page, read the three FRC topics above, and do the check-your-Java exercises at the bottom."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Read this","u":"foundations/03-java.html#read-this","t":"dev.java Learn : the official Java tutorials, if you want to go deeper on any topic Java Units Library (WPILib) : skim now, come back in Lesson 8"},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Java for robot code","u":"foundations/03-java.html#java-for-robot-code","t":""},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Where to practice","u":"foundations/03-java.html#where-to-practice","t":"You can run plain Java without a robot. For the small examples, use the online Java Playground so you don't have to set anything up. For the classes later in the lesson, create them in your robot project. Every Java program starts in a main method: public class Practice { public static void main ( String [] args ) { System . out . println ( \"Hello, RoboJacks!\" ); } } System.out.println(...) prints a line of text. You'll use it to check your work throughout this lesson."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Statements, semicolons, and comments","u":"foundations/03-java.html#statements-semicolons-and-comments","t":"Java runs statements in order, top to bottom. Most statements end with ; . Curly braces { } group statements into a block . Comments are notes for humans. Java ignores them. // A one-line comment /* A comment that can span several lines */ /** A Javadoc comment: describes the method or class below it. */"},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Variables and types","u":"foundations/03-java.html#variables-and-types","t":"A variable is a named box that holds a value. Every variable has a type that says what kind of value fits in the box. int ballsScored = 0 ; // whole numbers double motorSpeed = 0.75 ; // decimals: most robot math uses double boolean hasGamePiece = false ; // true or false String robotName = \"Jack\" ; // text, in double quotes Type Holds Robot example int Whole numbers CAN ID, PWM port, game pieces scored double Decimal numbers Motor output, encoder position, voltage boolean true / false Limit switch pressed, has game piece String Text Dashboard labels, command names Once declared, you can change a variable's value but not its type: motorSpeed = 0.5 ; // ✅ fine motorSpeed = \"fast\" ; // ❌ error: a String doesn't fit in a double Robot connection Motor outputs are double values from -1.0 (full reverse) to 1.0 (full forward). 0.0 is stopped."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Constants with final","u":"foundations/03-java.html#constants-with-final","t":"final means the value can never change after it's set. Use it for things like ports and gear ratios: final int INTAKE_MOTOR_PORT = 3 ; INTAKE_MOTOR_PORT = 4 ; // ❌ error: it's final By convention, constants are named in ALL_CAPS . They live in Constants.java in robot projects."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Math","u":"foundations/03-java.html#math","t":"double a = 10 + 3 ; // 13 double b = 10 - 3 ; // 7 double c = 10 * 3 ; // 30 double d = 10.0 / 4 ; // 2.5 int e = 10 / 4 ; // 2 ← integer division throws away the decimal! int f = 10 % 4 ; // 2 ← remainder (\"mod\") Integer division 1 / 2 is 0 in Java, because both numbers are int s. Write 1.0 / 2 to get 0.5 . This bug shows up in gear-ratio math all the time. The Math class has the functions you'll need: Math . abs ( - 0.4 ); // 0.4 absolute value Math . max ( 0.2 , 0.9 ); // 0.9 Math . min ( 0.2 , 0.9 ); // 0.2 Math . sqrt ( 16 ); // 4.0 Math . PI ; // 3.14159... Math . toRadians ( 90 ); // 1.5707... Math . cos ( Math . PI ); // -1.0 (trig functions use radians)"},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Comparisons and logic","u":"foundations/03-java.html#comparisons-and-logic","t":"These produce a boolean : Operator Meaning Example == equal count == 3 != not equal count != 0 < , > , <= , >= comparisons speed > 0.5 && and hasPiece && armIsUp || or leftPressed || rightPressed ! not !hasPiece = vs == = sets a value. == compares . if (x = 3) is an error in Java; you meant if (x == 3) ."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"If statements","u":"foundations/03-java.html#if-statements","t":"if ( hasGamePiece ) { motorSpeed = 0.0 ; // stop, we're full } else if ( sensorSeesPiece ) { motorSpeed = 0.3 ; // slow down, almost there } else { motorSpeed = 0.75 ; // full speed } Java checks each condition in order and runs only the first block whose condition is true."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Loops","u":"foundations/03-java.html#loops","t":"Loops repeat code. // for: when you know how many times for ( int i = 0 ; i < 4 ; i ++ ) { System . out . println ( \"Swerve module \" + i ); } // while: repeat while something is true int count = 0 ; while ( count < 3 ) { count ++ ; } Loops are different on a robot You'll rarely write a while loop that waits for something on a robot. The robot already runs your code in a loop every 20 ms. A loop that waits, like while (!limitSwitch.get()) {} , freezes the whole robot: no driving, no other mechanisms, and it may never stop. Lesson 6 explains the right way. for loops over a fixed list, like the four swerve modules, are fine."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Methods","u":"foundations/03-java.html#methods","t":"A method is a named, reusable chunk of code. It can take parameters (inputs) and return a value (output). // return type name parameters double clamp ( double value , double min , double max ) { return Math . max ( min , Math . min ( max , value )); } clamp(1.5, -1, 1) returns 1.0 . clamp(-0.3, -1, 1) returns -0.3 . A method that returns nothing uses void : void printSpeed ( double speed ) { System . out . println ( \"Speed: \" + speed ); } Name methods with verbs getAngle() , setTarget() , isAtSpeed() , runIntake() . Methods that return a boolean usually start with is or has ."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Classes and objects (the big one)","u":"foundations/03-java.html#classes-and-objects-the-big-one","t":"A class is a blueprint. An object is a thing built from the blueprint. A class bundles data (fields) with the actions that use that data (methods). public class Intake { // Fields: data each Intake object remembers private double speed = 0.0 ; private int piecesCollected = 0 ; // Methods: things an Intake can do public void run () { speed = 0.75 ; } public void stop () { speed = 0.0 ; } public void collectPiece () { piecesCollected ++ ; } public double getSpeed () { return speed ; } } Use it by building an object with new : Intake intake = new Intake (); // build one from the blueprint intake . run (); System . out . println ( intake . getSpeed ()); // 0.75 intake . stop (); System . out . println ( intake . getSpeed ()); // 0.0 You can build many objects from one class, and each keeps its own fields: Intake front = new Intake (); Intake back = new Intake (); front . run (); // front.getSpeed() is 0.75, back.getSpeed() is still 0.0 Robot connection Every mechanism on the robot (drivetrain, intake, arm) becomes a class called a subsystem . The motor objects you create, like new PWMSparkMax(0) , are objects built from WPILib's classes."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Constructors","u":"foundations/03-java.html#constructors","t":"A constructor runs once, when the object is built with new . It sets up the starting state. It has the same name as the class and no return type: public class Shooter { private final double maxRpm ; public Shooter ( double maxRpm ) { // constructor this . maxRpm = maxRpm ; } } Shooter shooter = new Shooter ( 5000 ); this.maxRpm means \"this object's field,\" which tells it apart from the parameter maxRpm with the same name."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"public and private","u":"foundations/03-java.html#public-and-private","t":"private fields and methods can only be used inside the class. public ones can be used from anywhere . Make fields private and give other code methods to use instead. That way, the class controls its own data. For example, a setSpeed() method can refuse values above 1.0, but a public field can't protect itself."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"static","u":"foundations/03-java.html#static","t":"Most fields belong to each object . A static field or method belongs to the class itself , shared by everything: public final class Constants { public static final int INTAKE_MOTOR_PORT = 3 ; } int port = Constants . INTAKE_MOTOR_PORT ; // no object needed Math.max(...) is a static method, which is why you never write new Math() ."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Inheritance: extends","u":"foundations/03-java.html#inheritance-extends","t":"A class can extend another class to get all its fields and methods, then add its own: public class Intake extends SubsystemBase { // Intake gets everything SubsystemBase has, plus whatever we add here } WPILib uses this everywhere: your subsystems extend SubsystemBase , and your robot extends TimedRobot . @Override marks a method that replaces one from the parent class: @Override public void periodic () { // replaces SubsystemBase's empty periodic() with ours } If you misspell the name, @Override makes the compiler warn you instead of silently creating a new method that never runs."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Interfaces","u":"foundations/03-java.html#interfaces","t":"An interface is a promise: \"any class that implements me has these methods.\" You'll mostly use interfaces rather than write them. For example, many WPILib methods take a BooleanSupplier , which is any code that can answer true or false."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Imports and packages","u":"foundations/03-java.html#imports-and-packages","t":"Classes live in packages , which are like folders. To use a class from another package, import it at the top of the file: package frc.robot.subsystems ; // the package this file is in import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.motorcontrol.PWMSparkMax ; VS Code adds most imports for you. Click a red-underlined class name and use the lightbulb."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Null","u":"foundations/03-java.html#null","t":"A variable for an object can hold null , meaning \"no object.\" Calling a method on null crashes with a NullPointerException : Intake intake = null ; intake . run (); // 💥 NullPointerException On a robot this usually means you forgot to create something with new , or used it before its constructor ran. The crash message names the file and line."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Lambdas and method references","u":"foundations/03-java.html#lambdas-and-method-references","t":"WPILib uses these everywhere , so learn the syntax now. A lambda is a small piece of code you can pass around and run later : Runnable sayHi = () -> System . out . println ( \"Hi!\" ); sayHi . run (); // prints \"Hi!\" now The shape is (parameters) -> what to do : () -> intake . run () // no inputs () -> controller . getLeftY () // returns a value x -> x * 2 // one input A method reference is a shortcut for a lambda that just calls one method: intake :: run // same as () -> intake.run() intake :: hasGamePiece // same as () -> intake.hasGamePiece() Robot connection: why lambdas matter In driver.a().whileTrue(intake.runIntake()) , the scheduler needs to run your code every loop while the button is held. You can't hand it the result of running the code once; you hand it the code itself . That's what lambdas are. Lesson 7 builds on this."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Reading a stack trace","u":"foundations/03-java.html#reading-a-stack-trace","t":"When the robot code crashes, it prints a stack trace : java.lang.NullPointerException: Cannot invoke \"Intake.run()\" because \"this.intake\" is null at frc.robot.RobotContainer.configureBindings(RobotContainer.java:42) at frc.robot.RobotContainer.<init>(RobotContainer.java:25) at frc.robot.Robot.robotInit(Robot.java:18) Read the first line for what went wrong, then find the first at frc.robot... line for where : RobotContainer.java , line 42."},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Check your Java","u":"foundations/03-java.html#check-your-java","t":"If you can do all of these without looking anything up, you're ready for the labs. Part 1: Warm-up. In a main method: Compute how many meters a 4-inch wheel travels in one rotation (circumference = π × diameter; 1 inch = 0.0254 m) and print it Print 1 / 2 and 1.0 / 2 . Explain the difference. Write clamp(value, min, max) and test it with three values Part 2: The Shooter class. Write a Shooter class with: a private double targetRpm field a constructor that takes a double maxRpm and stores it in a private final field setTarget(double rpm) , which refuses (ignores) any value above maxRpm getTarget() isAtSpeed(double currentRpm) , which returns true if within 50 RPM of the target Test it from main() , or come back after Lesson 16 and write a unit test. Part 3: Lambdas. Make a Runnable that prints your name, and run it three times with a for loop Make a BooleanSupplier lambda that calls your shooter's isAtSpeed(...) and print the result"},{"p":"3. Java Reference","h":"Quick quiz","u":"foundations/03-java.html#quick-quiz","t":"What's the difference between a class and an object? A class is the blueprint ( Intake ). An object is one actual thing built from it ( new Intake() ). You can build many objects from one class, and each keeps its own data. What does 10 / 4 equal in Java, and why? 2 . Both are int s, so Java does integer division and drops the decimal. Use 10.0 / 4 to get 2.5 . Why mark fields private ? So other code can't change them directly. It has to go through your methods, which can check that values are valid. What does a constructor do, and when does it run? It sets up a new object's starting state. It runs once, when the object is created with new . What does extends SubsystemBase give your class? Everything SubsystemBase already has: registration with the scheduler, a periodic() method you can override, and command factory methods like run() and runOnce() . What does () -> intake.run() mean? A chunk of code that will call intake.run() when something runs it later. It doesn't run yet. Your robot crashes with a NullPointerException. Where do you look first? The first line of the stack trace says what was null. The first at frc.robot... line gives the file and line number in your code."},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"Why it matters","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#why-it-matters","t":"Half of all \"code bugs\" at competition are actually electrical problems: a loose wire, a tripped breaker, a dying battery, a duplicated CAN ID. A programmer who can tell the difference saves the team hours of chasing a bug that isn't in the code. Programmers who understand the wiring debug faster and get trusted more. You won't be wiring the robot yourself in this lesson. The goal is to understand what each part does, how power and data flow, and what the electrical system means for your code."},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"Read this","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#read-this","t":"Intro to FRC Robot Wiring Wiring Best Practices CAN Wiring Basics roboRIO Brownout and Understanding Current Draw"},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"Walkthrough","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"The three ideas you need","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#the-three-ideas-you-need","t":"Concept Symbol Unit Water-pipe analogy Voltage V Volts (V) Water pressure : how hard it pushes Current I Amps (A) How much water flows per second Resistance R Ohms (Ω) How narrow the pipe is Ohm's Law connects them: V = I × R With the same pressure (voltage), a narrower pipe (more resistance) lets less water (current) through. Power is how fast energy is used: P = V × I A motor pulling 40 A at 12 V uses about 480 W, about the same as a small microwave. Why thin wires get hot A wire has a little resistance. Push a lot of current through a thin wire and some power turns into heat (P = I² × R). That's why big motors use thick wire, and why the rules specify minimum wire sizes for each breaker."},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"What's on an FRC robot","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#whats-on-an-frc-robot","t":"Part What it does What programmers should know Battery 12 V lead-acid battery, the only power source Its voltage drops under load and as it drains. Check it before every match. Main breaker 120 A switch and circuit breaker Turns the whole robot on and off. Also the emergency off switch. Power Distribution (PDH or PDP) Splits power into many channels, each with its own breaker Can report current per channel to your code Breakers / fuses Cut power to one channel if it draws too much A tripped breaker looks like \"the motor stopped working\" roboRIO The robot's computer. Runs your code. Has PWM, DIO, analog, and CAN ports Radio Wi-Fi link to the Driver Station No radio = no robot control Motor controllers Turn small signals from the roboRIO into big motor power TalonFX, SPARK MAX/Flex, and others. Each has a CAN ID. Motors Turn electrical power into motion Kraken, Falcon, NEO, and others. Many have built-in encoders. Sensors Measure the world Encoders, gyros, limit switches, cameras Robot Signal Light (RSL) Orange light. Solid = disabled, blinking = enabled Check it before walking near the robot"},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"Follow the power","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#follow-the-power","t":"flowchart LR B[Battery 12 V] --> M[Main Breaker 120 A] M --> P[Power Distribution<br/>PDH / PDP] P -->|40 A breakers| MC[Motor Controllers] --> Mo[Motors] P -->|10–20 A| R[roboRIO, Radio] P -->|low current| S[Sensors, LEDs] Power always flows from the battery, through the main breaker, into the power distribution, and then out to each device through its own breaker."},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"Follow the data","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#follow-the-data","t":"Power and data travel on different wires: flowchart LR DS[Driver Station laptop] <-- Wi-Fi --> Radio Radio <-- Ethernet --> RIO[roboRIO<br/>runs your code] RIO <-- CAN bus --> MC[Motor controllers,<br/>PDH, Pneumatic Hub, gyro] RIO -- PWM --> PWMDev[PWM devices] RIO -- DIO / Analog --> Sens[Switches, sensors] Your code runs on the roboRIO. Joystick data arrives from the Driver Station over Wi-Fi, and your code sends commands out to motor controllers, mostly over CAN ."},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"The roboRIO's ports","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#the-roborios-ports","t":"Port Signal Used for CAN Digital messages on a shared bus Most motor controllers, PDH, Pneumatic Hub, many sensors PWM (0–9) A pulse that sets a speed Older or simpler motor controllers, servos DIO (0–9) On/off digital in or out Limit switches, beam breaks, some encoders Analog In (0–3) A voltage from 0 to 5 V Potentiometers, analog distance sensors I2C, SPI, USB Data buses Some gyros, color sensors, cameras Port numbers in code must match the wiring new PWMSparkMax(2) means \"the controller plugged into PWM port 2.\" new DigitalInput(0) means DIO port 0. If the wire is in port 3 and the code says 2, nothing happens and no error appears."},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"Batteries and brownouts","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#batteries-and-brownouts","t":"A fresh battery reads about 12.5–13 V with nothing running. The voltage sags while motors pull current, and drops further as the battery drains through a match. If the voltage drops too low, the roboRIO protects itself by disabling outputs to motors. That's a brownout . It happens in stages: From the WPILib docs: roboRIO brownout stages Battery voltage Stage What happens Below 6.8 V 1: 6 V output drop The 6 V power on the PWM pins starts to drop Below 6.3 V 2: Output disable Motors are disabled : PWM outputs turn off, CAN motor controllers get a disable command, pneumatics turn off, and the 5 V and 3.3 V sensor power rails turn off. The roboRIO's power light turns amber and the Driver Station shows \"Voltage Brownout.\" This lasts until the voltage climbs back above 7.5 V . Below about 4.5 V 3: Blackout The roboRIO may shut off completely, then reboot when the voltage recovers Stage 2 also cuts power to sensors plugged into the DIO and analog ports, so sensors can glitch during a brownout too. Adapted from roboRIO Brownout and Understanding Current Draw , CC BY 4.0. A full reboot in stage 3 costs you a big chunk of the match while the roboRIO and radio start back up. What causes brownouts: many motors starting at full power at the same time motors that are stalled (pushing against something but not moving), which pull huge current an old or partly charged battery loose battery connections Programmer takeaway: current limits Motor controllers let you set a current limit in code, a cap on how many amps the motor can pull. A current limit trades a little peak performance for a robot that doesn't brown out or trip breakers mid-match. Your team's code almost certainly sets these. Find them. You can see brownouts after a match in the Driver Station log viewer, along with battery voltage over time."},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"CAN bus","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#can-bus","t":"Most motor controllers talk to the roboRIO over CAN , a two-wire daisy chain (yellow and green). Every device is wired one after the other in a chain that starts at the roboRIO and ends at a terminator . CAN wiring runs from device to device in one chain. From the WPILib docs, CAN Wiring Basics (CC BY 4.0). From the WPILib docs: CAN wiring and termination CAN is generally wired with yellow and green wire, with yellow as CAN-High and green as CAN-Low. Many devices use the same colors to show how the wires plug in. A CAN network must be terminated with a 120 Ω resistor at each end. The roboRIO and the power distribution panel (PDP) have these built in, so the recommended layout starts the chain at the roboRIO , ends it at the PDP , and puts every other CAN device in between. The PDP ships with its termination jumper on ; leave it that way. A REV Power Distribution Hub (PDH) has a termination switch instead, labeled TERM , next to its CAN terminals: make sure it's ON when the PDH is at the end of the chain. Adapted from CAN Wiring Basics , CC BY 4.0. Every device needs a unique CAN ID for its device type. The ID you set in the vendor tool (Phoenix Tuner for CTRE, REV Hardware Client for REV) must match the ID in your code. If the chain breaks in the middle, every device after the break goes silent. Common CAN mistakes Symptom Likely cause Motors twitch, or the wrong one moves Two devices with the same CAN ID A whole group of devices stops responding Broken connection in the chain before them One device missing in Phoenix Tuner or REV Hardware Client Its CAN wires are loose or its ID was never set \"CAN frame not received\" errors in the console The device the code expects isn't on the bus at that ID Every device times out (for REV: timed out while waiting for Period Status 0: CAN Receive has Timed Out ) The yellow and green wires are swapped somewhere, often right at the roboRIO. Yellow goes to CAN-H and green to CAN-L at every connection. Check the colors at the roboRIO first The roboRIO's CAN port is labeled H and L . Yellow goes to H , green goes to L . One swapped connection anywhere in the chain can silence the whole bus, so when nothing on CAN responds, check the colors at each connector, starting at the roboRIO. Keep a CAN ID map , a list of every device, its ID, and what it does, somewhere the whole team can see it."},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"Safety basics","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#safety-basics","t":"The robot is always potentially live once the battery is plugged in. Turn off the main breaker before touching wiring. Never connect a battery backward. It can destroy electronics instantly. Say \"Enabling!\" out loud before enabling the robot, and wait until people are clear. Know where the main breaker is, so you can reach it in an emergency."},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"Try it","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#try-it","t":"Next: the parts on our robots Lessons 4.1 (motors), 4.2 (the SPARK MAX), and 4.3 (sensors) cover the exact hardware on the test bed and West Coast Drive. With a mentor, on a real robot: Find each part in the table above: battery, main breaker, power distribution, roboRIO, radio, motor controllers, RSL Trace one motor from the battery to the motor, naming every part the power passes through Find that motor's breaker and CAN ID Open Phoenix Tuner or REV Hardware Client and list every device on the CAN bus Find where the team's code sets a current limit"},{"p":"4. Electronics","h":"Check yourself","u":"hardware/04-electronics.html#check-yourself","t":"A motor pulls 60 A at 12 V. How much power is that? P = 12 × 60 = 720 W What is a brownout, and how can code help prevent one? The battery voltage drops so low that the roboRIO disables motor outputs. Current limits in code reduce how much current motors can pull, which keeps the voltage up. Why does every CAN device need a unique ID? The roboRIO addresses devices by ID. Duplicates mean commands go to the wrong device or conflict. Half the motors stop responding at once, all after a certain point in the CAN chain. What's the likely cause? A broken or loose CAN connection just before the first device that stopped. Everything after a break loses communication. Your code says new PWMSparkMax(2) , but the motor doesn't move. Name two non-code things to check. That the PWM cable is actually in port 2 (and plugged in the right way around), and that the motor controller has power: its breaker isn't tripped and its status light is on."},{"p":"4.1 Motors: NEO, CIM, and 775pro","h":"Why it matters","u":"hardware/04b-motors.html#why-it-matters","t":"The labs use three different motors, and they are not interchangeable. Each one has to be set up differently in code, protected differently, and used for different jobs. Telling a SPARK MAX the wrong motor type, or stalling the wrong motor, can destroy hardware in seconds. This lesson covers the motors on the team's test bed and West Coast Drive: Motor Where it's used Labs NEO Test bed motor, WCD elevator 1, 3, 4, 5, 10 CIM WCD drivetrain (4 of them) 2, 7, 8 775pro Test bed wheel 6, 9"},{"p":"4.1 Motors: NEO, CIM, and 775pro","h":"Read this","u":"hardware/04b-motors.html#read-this","t":"NEO V1.1 (REV) Using Motor Controllers in Code (WPILib)"},{"p":"4.1 Motors: NEO, CIM, and 775pro","h":"Walkthrough","u":"hardware/04b-motors.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"4.1 Motors: NEO, CIM, and 775pro","h":"Brushed vs. brushless","u":"hardware/04b-motors.html#brushed-vs-brushless","t":"Every electric motor spins by switching which coils get current as the shaft turns. The difference is what does the switching . Brushed Brushless Who switches the coils Little carbon brushes rubbing on the spinning shaft The motor controller , using a sensor that tells it where the shaft is Wires 2 power wires 3 power wires + a sensor cable Wear Brushes wear out over time Nothing rubs, so very little wear Built-in encoder No Yes (the controller needs it to switch the coils) Our motors CIM , 775pro NEO Brushed or brushless must match in code A SPARK MAX drives the two kinds completely differently. In code, CIMs and 775pros are MotorType.kBrushed , and NEOs are MotorType.kBrushless . Getting it wrong can damage the motor and the controller. Lab 1 (NEO) and Lab 2 (CIMs) are opposites on purpose."},{"p":"4.1 Motors: NEO, CIM, and 775pro","h":"Reading a motor's specs","u":"hardware/04b-motors.html#reading-a-motors-specs","t":"Four numbers tell you most of what you need to know about a motor: Spec What it means Why it matters Free speed How fast it spins with nothing attached, at 12 V The top speed you could ever get (before gearing) Free current Current it draws spinning with no load Small. A motor doing no work barely draws anything. Stall torque Twisting force when held completely still How hard it can push Stall current Current it draws when held completely still The worst case for heat, breakers, and brownouts A motor's current goes up as it's loaded down. Spinning freely, it draws almost nothing. Held still ( stalled ), it draws the most it ever will, turning all that power into heat."},{"p":"4.1 Motors: NEO, CIM, and 775pro","h":"Our three motors","u":"hardware/04b-motors.html#our-three-motors","t":"Spec NEO CIM 775pro Type Brushless Brushed Brushed Free speed 5,676 RPM 5,310 RPM 18,730 RPM Free current 1.8 A 2.7 A 0.7 A Stall torque 2.6 N·m 2.42 N·m 0.71 N·m Stall current 105 A 133 A 134 A Built-in encoder Yes, 42 counts per revolution No No Size Compact Large and heavy Small and light Compare them: The NEO and CIM have similar speed and torque. The NEO is lighter, has a built-in encoder, and doesn't wear out brushes. That's why many teams replaced CIMs with NEOs. The 775pro spins more than 3 times faster but has less than a third of the torque. It almost always needs a big gear reduction, like the gearbox on the test bed's wheel. All three pull over 100 A when stalled. That's why every lab sets a current limit. Why gearboxes? A 775pro at 18,730 RPM is spinning over 300 times a second, far too fast to drive a wheel or lift anything directly. A gearbox trades speed for torque. A 100:1 gearbox gives about 187 RPM at the output, with up to 100 times the torque (minus losses to friction). Gear ratios multiply: a 10:1 stage followed by a 5:1 stage is 50:1 total."},{"p":"4.1 Motors: NEO, CIM, and 775pro","h":"Stalls and heat","u":"hardware/04b-motors.html#stalls-and-heat","t":"A stalled motor turns almost all of its power into heat. How fast that heat does damage depends on the motor: Motor Stall behavior 775pro Small, light, and spins fast, so it heats up very quickly when stalled. It can be damaged in seconds. Never hold the test bed wheel still while the motor is pushing. CIM Big and heavy, so it takes longer to heat up. But four CIMs pushing against a wall can still trip breakers and brown out the robot. NEO REV says to read their stall-testing documentation and always set a Smart Current Limit before using one. Current limits ( smartCurrentLimit in every lab) are the main protection. They cap how much current the SPARK MAX lets the motor draw, so a stall heats the motor more slowly and doesn't trip the 40 A breakers."},{"p":"4.1 Motors: NEO, CIM, and 775pro","h":"Wiring","u":"hardware/04b-motors.html#wiring","t":"Never wire a motor straight to the battery Every motor connects to a motor controller , never directly to the battery or power distribution. REV specifically warns against connecting a NEO to the battery. Motor Connects to the SPARK MAX with CIM, 775pro 2 power wires (red and black) NEO 3 power wires (red, black, and white) plus the sensor cable into the encoder port. A NEO won't spin without its sensor cable."},{"p":"4.1 Motors: NEO, CIM, and 775pro","h":"Inversion","u":"hardware/04b-motors.html#inversion","t":"Every motor spins one way for positive voltage. Which way is \"forward\" for the mechanism depends on how it's mounted. If a mechanism moves backward, you fix it in code with config.inverted(true) , not by swapping wires. That keeps the wiring the same on every motor, so nobody gets confused later."},{"p":"4.1 Motors: NEO, CIM, and 775pro","h":"Try it","u":"hardware/04b-motors.html#try-it","t":"On the test bed and WCD, find every motor. Say which type each is, and which SPARK MAX controls it. Find the NEO's sensor cable and the CIMs' two power wires Using the table, figure out roughly how fast the test bed wheel would spin with a 100:1 gearbox on the 775pro Find the current limit in each lab's code. Why are the drivetrain and the NEO set to 40 A? In Lab 1 , watch the NEO's current on the dashboard when it starts. Compare it to the free current in the table."},{"p":"4.1 Motors: NEO, CIM, and 775pro","h":"Check yourself","u":"hardware/04b-motors.html#check-yourself","t":"What's the difference between a brushed and a brushless motor? In a brushed motor, carbon brushes inside the motor switch the coils. In a brushless motor, the motor controller does the switching, using a sensor in the motor to know where the shaft is. Which motors in our labs are brushed, and which are brushless? CIMs and the 775pro are brushed. NEOs are brushless. Why does every lab set a current limit? A stalled motor draws over 100 A. The limit keeps motors from overheating, keeps breakers from tripping, and prevents brownouts. Why does the 775pro need a big gearbox? It spins over 18,000 RPM with little torque. A gearbox trades that speed for the torque needed to actually move something. Why is a stalled 775pro more dangerous than a stalled CIM? Both draw about the same stall current, but the 775pro is much smaller and lighter, so it heats up much faster. A NEO won't spin, but the SPARK MAX shows it's being commanded. What's one likely cause? The NEO's sensor cable isn't plugged in. A brushless motor can't run without it."},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"Why it matters","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#why-it-matters","t":"Every motor in the labs runs through a SPARK MAX : 7 of them across the test bed and West Coast Drive. When a motor doesn't move, or moves the wrong way, or two motors fight each other, the answer is almost always in how its SPARK MAX is wired or set up. This lesson covers what a SPARK MAX does, how it connects to the roboRIO, and how to check and set it up with the REV Hardware Client before you ever write code."},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"Read this","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#read-this","t":"SPARK MAX Basic Configuration (REV) Using Encoders with the SPARK MAX (REV) REVLib documentation"},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"Walkthrough","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"What a motor controller does","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#what-a-motor-controller-does","t":"The roboRIO can't power a motor directly. Its outputs are tiny signals. A motor controller sits between the battery power and the motor, and turns a small signal (\"run at 50% forward\") into the big, fast-switching current the motor actually needs. flowchart LR PDP[Power distribution<br/>40 A breaker] -- 12 V power --> SM[SPARK MAX] RIO[roboRIO] -->|\"CAN: run at 50%\"| SM SM -- motor power --> M[Motor] M -. NEO sensor cable .-> SM A SPARK MAX can drive brushed motors (CIM, 775pro) and brushless NEOs ( Lesson 4.1 )."},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"The connections on a SPARK MAX","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#the-connections-on-a-spark-max","t":"Connection What plugs in Power input (red and black) 12 V from the power distribution, through a breaker Motor output The motor's power wires: 2 for brushed, 3 for a NEO Encoder port A NEO's sensor cable, or an encoder for a brushed motor CAN (yellow and green) The CAN bus to the roboRIO and other devices PWM An alternative to CAN for simple speed control (we use CAN) USB-C Your laptop, for setup with the REV Hardware Client Data port (on top) Limit switches and extra encoders (advanced)"},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"CAN IDs","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#can-ids","t":"All 7 SPARK MAXes share the same CAN bus ( Lesson 4 ), so each one needs a unique CAN ID . That number is how your code picks which controller to talk to: new SparkMax ( 8 , MotorType . kBrushless ); // \"the SPARK MAX with CAN ID 8\" Robot Controller CAN ID Test bed NEO 8 Test bed 775pro wheel (check yours) WCD Left leader, left follower, right leader, right follower 1, 2, 3, 4 (check yours) WCD Elevator NEO (check yours) Duplicate IDs If two SPARK MAXes on the same robot share an ID, motors twitch, don't respond, or the wrong one moves. Fill in the real IDs above for your robots and keep the list posted."},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"Setting up with the REV Hardware Client","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#setting-up-with-the-rev-hardware-client","t":"Before writing code, connect each SPARK MAX to your laptop with USB-C and open the REV Hardware Client (installed in Lab 0 ). With it you can: Task Why See the CAN ID , and change it Make sure it matches your code Update firmware Firmware should match your REVLib version. Ask a mentor before updating. Check the motor type (brushed or brushless) Must match the motor actually attached Spin the motor right from the laptop Proves the controller and motor work, with no robot code involved See faults and live values Current, temperature, and any errors Debug in layers If a motor won't move from your code, try spinning it from the REV Hardware Client. If it spins there, the hardware is fine and the problem is in your code (CAN ID, motor type, or command). If it doesn't spin there either, it's wiring or hardware."},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"The status light","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#the-status-light","t":"The SPARK MAX's LED changes color and blink pattern to show its state: whether it's getting a signal, whether it's in brake or coast mode, which direction it's driving, and any faults. The full color table is in REV's SPARK MAX documentation. Learn the few you see most on your robots, especially the ones for \"no signal\" and \"fault.\""},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"Settings your code sends","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#settings-your-code-sends","t":"Every lab configures its SPARK MAXes the same way, with a SparkMaxConfig : Setting Code What it does Motor type new SparkMax(id, MotorType.kBrushed) or kBrushless Brushed or brushless driving. Must match the motor. Current limit config.smartCurrentLimit(40) Caps current to protect the motor, breaker, and battery Idle mode config.idleMode(IdleMode.kBrake) Brake: resists turning when stopped. Coast: spins freely. Inversion config.inverted(true) Flips which way is \"forward\" Follower config.follow(leaderCanId) Copies another SPARK MAX's output ( Lab 2 ) The settings are sent once, when the robot code starts: m_motor . configure ( config , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); Option Meaning kResetSafeParameters Start from known defaults, then apply your settings, so leftovers from someone else's code can't sneak in kPersistParameters Save the settings in the SPARK MAX, so they survive if it loses power for a moment, like during a brownout"},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"Brake vs. coast","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#brake-vs-coast","t":"Brake Coast When output is 0 Shorts the motor's coils, so it resists turning Lets the motor spin freely Feels like Stops quickly Rolls to a stop Good for Elevators (slows falling), precise stops, drivetrains for most drivers Shooter flywheels (no sudden stop), pushing a robot by hand"},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"Leaders and followers","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#leaders-and-followers","t":"When two motors drive the same gearbox, like the WCD's CIMs, one SPARK MAX is the leader and the other is its follower . Your code commands only the leader, and the follower copies it automatically over CAN. The two motors in one gearbox must always push the same way, or they fight each other, overheat, and trip breakers ( Lab 2 )."},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"Try it","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#try-it","t":"Connect each test bed SPARK MAX to the REV Hardware Client. Write down its CAN ID and motor type. Do the same for all 5 SPARK MAXes on the WCD, and fill in the CAN ID table above Spin the test bed NEO from the REV Hardware Client, with no robot code Trace one SPARK MAX's power wires back to its breaker, and its CAN wires to the next device on the bus Look up the status light table in REV's docs. What does the light show when a SPARK MAX has no signal? Spin the NEO in Lab 1 with brake mode, then coast mode. Describe the difference when it stops."},{"p":"4.2 The SPARK MAX","h":"Check yourself","u":"hardware/04c-spark-max.html#check-yourself","t":"Why can't the roboRIO power a motor directly? Its outputs are small signals. A motor controller turns that signal into the large current the motor needs. What does a CAN ID do? It identifies one device on the shared CAN bus, so your code can talk to the right SPARK MAX. A motor won't move from your code, but spins fine from the REV Hardware Client. Where's the problem? In the code: most likely a wrong CAN ID, wrong motor type, or a command that isn't running. Why use kPersistParameters ? So the SPARK MAX keeps its settings, like the current limit, if it loses power for a moment and restarts during a match. Why does the elevator use brake mode? In brake mode the motor resists turning when it isn't powered, which slows the carriage if it starts to fall. What happens if a follower in the same gearbox pushes the opposite way from its leader? The two motors fight each other. They heat up, draw a lot of current, and can trip breakers or damage the gearbox."},{"p":"4.3 Sensors","h":"Why it matters","u":"hardware/04d-sensors.html#why-it-matters","t":"Motors let the robot act. Sensors let it know what's happening. The labs use four kinds of sensor, each wired a different way and answering a different question: Sensor Answers Wired to Labs Limit switch \"Am I at the end?\" roboRIO DIO 4 10-turn potentiometer \"Where is the knob?\" roboRIO Analog In 5 REV Through Bore Encoder \"How far has this shaft turned, and how fast?\" roboRIO DIO (two ports) 6, 9, 10 NavX2 \"Which way is the robot facing?\" roboRIO MXP port 8 This lesson covers the hardware side: what each sensor is, how it's wired, and what can go wrong. The labs cover the code. Lesson 8 covers sensors and units in general."},{"p":"4.3 Sensors","h":"Read this","u":"hardware/04d-sensors.html#read-this","t":"Digital Inputs – Hardware (WPILib) Analog Inputs – Hardware (WPILib) Encoders – Hardware (WPILib) Gyroscopes – Hardware (WPILib)"},{"p":"4.3 Sensors","h":"Walkthrough","u":"hardware/04d-sensors.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"4.3 Sensors","h":"The roboRIO's sensor ports","u":"hardware/04d-sensors.html#the-roborios-sensor-ports","t":"Port How many Signal Our sensors DIO (Digital I/O) 10 (0–9) On or off, read very fast Limit switches, encoder channels A and B Analog In 4 (0–3) A voltage from 0 to 5 V Potentiometer MXP 1 wide connector Many signals, including SPI NavX2 Every DIO and Analog In port has three pins: ground , 5 V power , and signal . Sensors that need power use all three. A plain switch uses just signal and ground. Plug the right way around Most sensor plugs fit either way. Check the pin labels on the roboRIO (they're printed next to each row) and match ground, power, and signal. A backward plug usually just doesn't work, but it can damage some sensors."},{"p":"4.3 Sensors","h":"Limit switches","u":"hardware/04d-sensors.html#limit-switches","t":"The test bed's limit switches are industrial plunger switches. Pushing the plunger flips a set of contacts inside. Contact When not pressed When pressed NO (normally open) Open: no connection Closed: connected NC (normally closed) Closed: connected Open: no connection The switch connects between a DIO signal pin and ground : A limit switch wired to a DIO port: signal and ground only. From the WPILib docs, Digital Inputs - Hardware (CC BY 4.0). From the WPILib docs: Why a switch reads high when open All DIO ports have built-in pull-up resistors between the power pins and the signal pins. These make sure that when the signal pin is \"floating\" (not connected to anything), it reads high . A simple switch doesn't need power, so it has just two wires, between signal and ground . When the switch is open, the signal pin floats and the pull-up makes it read high. When the switch closes, it connects the signal straight to ground, so it reads low. Adapted from Digital Inputs - Hardware , CC BY 4.0. So with NO contacts: Switch Signal get() returns Not pressed Pulled up true Pressed Connected to ground false That's why Lab 4 uses !m_limitSwitch.get() to mean \"pressed.\" NC is safer for real mechanisms With NC wiring, a broken or unplugged wire reads the same as \"pressed,\" so the robot stops instead of driving past the end. With NO wiring, a broken wire looks like \"never pressed\" and the motor keeps going. Many teams wire safety limits NC for this reason."},{"p":"4.3 Sensors","h":"Potentiometers","u":"hardware/04d-sensors.html#potentiometers","t":"A potentiometer (\"pot\") is a variable resistor with three pins: two ends of a resistive strip, and a wiper that slides along it as the shaft turns. 5 V ──┬─────────────────┐ │ resistive strip │ GND ──┴────────▲────────┘ │ wiper ──→ Analog In signal The wiper's voltage goes from about 0 V at one end to about 5 V at the other. The test bed's is a 10-turn pot: ten full turns from end to end, which gives very fine resolution. A potentiometer on an Analog In port (left) is a voltage divider (right). From the WPILib docs, Analog Potentiometers - Hardware (CC BY 4.0). From the WPILib docs: Which end reads 0 V? The middle pin of a potentiometer is the signal . The two outer pins can each be either power or ground. As the shaft turns, the two resistances R1 and R2 change, but their sum stays the same, so the signal voltage changes evenly as you turn. Because the circuit is symmetric, you choose which end of the travel reads 0 V. To reverse the direction, wire it backwards : swap the two outer pins. Adapted from Analog Potentiometers - Hardware , CC BY 4.0. Potentiometer Measures Absolute position, within its range After power-on Knows its real position right away Range Limited: has hard stops at each end Weaknesses A little noisy; can wear out with lots of use Potentiometers are cheap and simple, so teams use them to measure arm angles and as driver knobs, like in Lab 5 ."},{"p":"4.3 Sensors","h":"The REV Through Bore Encoder","u":"hardware/04d-sensors.html#the-rev-through-bore-encoder","t":"The Through Bore Encoder mounts around a 1/2\" hex shaft (the shaft passes through its center), and measures the shaft's rotation with a magnet and sensor. It has two outputs: Output Signal What it gives you We use it? Quadrature (A and B) Two square waves How far the shaft has turned, any number of turns, plus speed. 2048 cycles (8192 counts) per revolution. Yes , on two DIO ports Absolute One pulse whose width shows the angle The shaft's angle within one turn, even right after power-on Not yet Quadrature, explained. Channels A and B each switch on and off as the shaft turns, a quarter-cycle apart. One full cycle steps through four states, and the order of the states tells the roboRIO the direction: Step Turning forward (A, B) Turning backward (A, B) 1 off, off off, off 2 on , off off, on 3 on, on on , on 4 off, on on, off Turning forward, A switches first. Turning backward, B switches first. Every one of those switches is one count , so one cycle is 4 counts. The direction comes from which channel (A or B) changes first. From the WPILib docs, Encoders - Software (CC BY 4.0). The roboRIO counts these in hardware (its FPGA chip), so it never misses a count, even though your code only runs every 20 ms. Quadrature encoder Measures How far, and how fast, from where it started After power-on Reads 0 , wherever the shaft is Range Unlimited turns So you must... Start the mechanism in a known spot, like the elevator at the bottom ( Lab 10 ) Wiring the Through Bore Encoder to the roboRIO The encoder has a small white 6-pin JST socket. Its cable splits into four 3-pin Dupont plugs , one per signal, each with its own red (5 V) and black (ground) wire. The color of the third wire tells you which signal it is: The encoder end of the cable is the white JST plug (right). The four 3-pin Dupont plugs go to the roboRIO. Each has a red wire (5 V), a black wire (ground), and one colored signal wire. Plug (signal wire color) Signal Where it goes Blue Quadrature channel A A DIO port, like 0 Yellow Quadrature channel B The next DIO port, like 1 Green Index: one pulse per turn Not used in this course. Tape it out of the way. White Absolute position (a duty-cycle signal) Not used in this course. It's for reading the shaft's angle right after power-on, with WPILib's DutyCycleEncoder . Which way around? Each roboRIO DIO port is three pins in a row, and the roboRIO has a label printed next to each row of pins: the ground symbol (⏚), V (5 V), and S (signal). Turn each plug so the black wire is on ground (⏚), the red wire is on V , and the blue or yellow wire is on S . Every plug on this cable has its own red and black, so each one powers itself; that's normal. The two DIO port numbers become EncoderChannelA and EncoderChannelB in Constants . Which robot uses which ports: Encoder Robot Blue (A) Yellow (B) Lab Test bed wheel Test bed DIO 0 DIO 1 Lab 6 Left drive gearbox WCD DIO 2 DIO 3 Lab 8 Right drive gearbox WCD DIO 4 DIO 5 Lab 8 Elevator WCD DIO 0 DIO 1 Lab 10 Counting the wrong way? If the encoder counts down when the mechanism moves the way you call forward, you can either set its ...Inverted constant to true in code, or swap the blue and yellow plugs. Pick one. Doing both puts it back where it started. Cycles, not counts WPILib's Encoder class calls one full quadrature cycle a \"pulse.\" The Through Bore is 2048 cycles per revolution. Using 8192 (the count with 4x decoding) makes every distance 4 times too small."},{"p":"4.3 Sensors","h":"The NavX2-MXP gyro","u":"hardware/04d-sensors.html#the-navx2-mxp-gyro","t":"The NavX2 is a small board with its own processor and three kinds of sensors: gyros (rotation), accelerometers (acceleration), and a magnetometer (compass). It combines them to report the robot's orientation. NavX2-MXP Mounts Plugs straight into the roboRIO's MXP port, on top of the roboRIO Talks over SPI, through the MXP port (USB also works) Most useful value Yaw : which way the robot is facing ( Lab 8 ) At power-on Calibrates for a few seconds. The robot must sit still . Weakness Slowly drifts : the heading creeps a little over time Yaw is turning left and right; pitch and roll are tipping. From the WPILib docs, Gyroscopes - Hardware (CC BY 4.0). Mounting matters The NavX measures rotation around the axis pointing up through the board. If the roboRIO is mounted flat, that's the robot's yaw. If it's mounted on its side, the NavX has to be told which way is up, or the headings will be wrong. Keep it flat, and firmly attached, so it moves exactly with the robot."},{"p":"4.3 Sensors","h":"Which sensor for which job?","u":"hardware/04d-sensors.html#which-sensor-for-which-job","t":"Job Best sensor Why Stop an elevator at the bottom Limit switch Simple, reliable, and can re-zero an encoder Know how high the elevator is Encoder Precise over the whole range of travel Measure an arm angle Potentiometer or absolute encoder Knows the angle right after power-on Drive a set distance Encoder on the drivetrain Counts wheel rotations Turn to an angle Gyro Measures the robot's rotation directly Driver adjusts a value Potentiometer A knob is easy for people to use"},{"p":"4.3 Sensors","h":"Try it","u":"hardware/04d-sensors.html#try-it","t":"Find each sensor on the test bed and WCD, and follow its wire to the roboRIO. Write down the port number for each. On a limit switch, find the NO and NC terminals. Which one is wired on the test bed? Find the Through Bore Encoder's two quadrature wires and which DIO ports they go to Find the NavX2 on the WCD's roboRIO. Is it mounted flat? Fill in a wiring table for your team: every sensor, its port, and which lab uses it. Post it near the test bed."},{"p":"4.3 Sensors","h":"Check yourself","u":"hardware/04d-sensors.html#check-yourself","t":"What's the difference between DIO and Analog In ports? DIO reads on or off. Analog In reads a voltage from 0 to 5 V, so it can measure a range of values. With NO wiring, what does a limit switch read when pressed? Why? false . Pressing connects the signal pin to ground, which pulls it low. Why might a team wire a safety limit switch NC instead of NO? With NC, a broken wire looks the same as \"pressed,\" so the mechanism stops. With NO, a broken wire looks like \"never pressed,\" and the motor keeps going. Why does a quadrature encoder need two channels? The order the two channels change tells the roboRIO which direction the shaft is turning. What's the difference between a potentiometer and a quadrature encoder after the robot turns on? The potentiometer knows its real position immediately. The quadrature encoder reads 0 wherever the shaft is, so the mechanism has to start in a known position. Why must the robot sit still when the NavX2 powers on? It calibrates itself during the first few seconds. Moving during calibration makes the heading drift for the rest of the session."},{"p":"5. Pneumatics","h":"Why it matters","u":"hardware/05-pneumatics.html#why-it-matters","t":"Pneumatics use compressed air to push and pull pistons. They're great for mechanisms that only need two positions, moved fast and with force: a claw: open or closed an intake: deployed or stowed a gearbox: high gear or low gear a climber hook: extended or retracted Pneumatics are simple to control in code (usually just \"extend\" or \"retract\"), but they come with their own hardware, safety rules, and failure modes. Many teams have moved away from pneumatics in recent years, but they still show up often, so you should know how they work."},{"p":"5. Pneumatics","h":"Read this","u":"hardware/05-pneumatics.html#read-this","t":"Wiring Pneumatics - REV Pneumatic Hub Pneumatics APIs"},{"p":"5. Pneumatics","h":"Walkthrough","u":"hardware/05-pneumatics.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"5. Pneumatics","h":"The air path","u":"hardware/05-pneumatics.html#the-air-path","t":"flowchart LR C[Compressor] --> T[Storage tanks<br/>up to ~120 psi] T --> R[Regulator<br/>~60 psi working] R --> S[Solenoid valve] S --> Cy[Cylinder] PH[Pneumatic Hub / PCM] -. controls .-> C PH -. controls .-> S PS[Pressure switch /<br/>sensor] -. reports to .-> PH Part Job Compressor An electric pump that fills the tanks with air Storage tanks Hold a supply of air so mechanisms can move many times between refills Pressure switch or analog pressure sensor Tells the control module when the tanks are full so the compressor stops Relief valve Vents air automatically if the pressure gets dangerously high. A required safety part. Regulator Lowers tank pressure to a steady, lower working pressure for the mechanisms Gauges Show the stored and working pressure so you can see them at a glance Solenoid valve An electrically controlled valve. This is what your code switches. Cylinder (piston) Moves in or out when air is sent to one side or the other Pneumatic Hub (REV) or PCM (CTRE) The control module: runs the compressor and powers the solenoids, connected to the roboRIO over CAN Dump (vent) valve A manual valve that releases all the stored air"},{"p":"5. Pneumatics","h":"Why two pressures?","u":"hardware/05-pneumatics.html#why-two-pressures","t":"The tanks store air at high pressure (up to about 120 psi) so they hold a lot of it. The mechanisms run at a lower working pressure (about 60 psi) from the regulator, which keeps the force steady and stays within the rules. As the tanks drain during a match, the working pressure stays the same until the tanks drop below it. Safety first Stored air is stored energy, and a cylinder can move with real force without warning. Always open the dump valve and check that the gauges read zero before touching fittings, tubing, or cylinders. Wear safety glasses around pressurized systems. Keep hands out of a pneumatic mechanism's path, even when the robot is disabled. A valve can still be holding air in the cylinder."},{"p":"5. Pneumatics","h":"How a cylinder moves","u":"hardware/05-pneumatics.html#how-a-cylinder-moves","t":"A double-acting cylinder has two ports. Air into one side pushes the piston out ; air into the other side pulls it in . The solenoid valve decides which side gets air. Single vs. double solenoids: Single solenoid Double solenoid Control wires One channel Two channels (forward and reverse) Powered Moves to one position Forward channel: one way. Reverse channel: the other way. Unpowered A spring returns the valve to its rest position Usually stays wherever it was last set When the robot disables Returns to rest position Usually holds its last position Good for Mechanisms with a clear safe position Mechanisms that should hold position when disabled What happens when the robot disables matters When the robot is disabled at the end of a match, all solenoid outputs turn off. A single-solenoid intake springs back to its rest position, which might drop a game piece. A double-solenoid claw usually stays where it was. Your mechanism team should choose the valve with this in mind."},{"p":"5. Pneumatics","h":"Code it","u":"hardware/05-pneumatics.html#code-it","t":"WPILib needs to know which control module you have. Use PneumaticsModuleType.REVPH for a REV Pneumatic Hub or PneumaticsModuleType.CTREPCM for a CTRE PCM. A double solenoid: import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.DoubleSolenoid ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.PneumaticsModuleType ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.Command ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; public class Claw extends SubsystemBase { // Forward channel 0, reverse channel 1 on the Pneumatic Hub private final DoubleSolenoid piston = new DoubleSolenoid ( PneumaticsModuleType . REVPH , 0 , 1 ); public Command open () { return runOnce (() -> piston . set ( DoubleSolenoid . Value . kForward )); } public Command close () { return runOnce (() -> piston . set ( DoubleSolenoid . Value . kReverse )); } public Command toggle () { return runOnce ( piston :: toggle ); } } DoubleSolenoid.Value Meaning kForward Power the forward channel kReverse Power the reverse channel kOff Power neither. The valve usually stays where it was. A single solenoid: private final Solenoid intakeDeploy = new Solenoid ( PneumaticsModuleType . REVPH , 2 ); public Command deploy () { return runOnce (() -> intakeDeploy . set ( true )); } public Command stow () { return runOnce (() -> intakeDeploy . set ( false )); } Bind them to buttons in RobotContainer : driver . leftBumper (). onTrue ( claw . open ()); driver . rightBumper (). onTrue ( claw . close ()); Each command is runOnce because setting a solenoid is instant: it sends the signal and is done. The air does the rest."},{"p":"5. Pneumatics","h":"The compressor","u":"hardware/05-pneumatics.html#the-compressor","t":"The compressor runs automatically by default whenever the robot is enabled and the tanks aren't full. You can create a Compressor object to check it or change its behavior: private final Compressor compressor = new Compressor ( PneumaticsModuleType . REVPH ); private final TelemetryTable telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Pneumatics\" ); // Lesson 6.1 @Override public void periodic () { telemetry . log ( \"Compressor Running\" , compressor . isEnabled ()); telemetry . log ( \"Pressure (psi)\" , compressor . getPressure ()); } getPressure() only works if the robot has an analog pressure sensor wired to the Pneumatic Hub. Turn the compressor off when you need power The compressor pulls a lot of current. Some teams disable it during climbing or other high-power moments to avoid brownouts: compressor.disable() , then compressor.enableDigital() (or enableAnalog(min, max) with a pressure sensor) afterward."},{"p":"5. Pneumatics","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"hardware/05-pneumatics.html#troubleshooting","t":"Symptom Likely cause Nothing moves, compressor never runs Pneumatic Hub not powered, wrong module type in code, or its CAN ID doesn't match Compressor runs but pressure never builds A leak. Listen for hissing, check fittings, or a tank or the dump valve is open. Mechanism moves the wrong direction Tubes swapped on the cylinder, or forward and reverse channels swapped in code Mechanism moves slowly or weakly Working pressure too low, or the tanks are nearly empty Solenoid clicks but the cylinder doesn't move No air reaching the valve, or a kinked or disconnected tube"},{"p":"5. Pneumatics","h":"Try it","u":"hardware/05-pneumatics.html#try-it","t":"With a mentor, on a robot with pneumatics: Find each part in the table: compressor, tanks, relief valve, regulator, gauges, solenoids, cylinders, dump valve Trace the air from the compressor to one cylinder Find which solenoid channels your team's mechanism uses Write open() and close() commands and bind them to two buttons Put the compressor status on the dashboard and watch it turn off when the tanks are full Vent the system with the dump valve and confirm both gauges read zero"},{"p":"5. Pneumatics","h":"Check yourself","u":"hardware/05-pneumatics.html#check-yourself","t":"Why is there a regulator between the tanks and the solenoids? The tanks store air at high pressure for capacity, while mechanisms run at a lower, steady working pressure. The regulator steps it down. Why do solenoid commands use runOnce instead of run ? Setting a solenoid is a single, instant action. Once the valve is switched, there's nothing more for the command to do every loop. A single-solenoid intake is deployed when the match ends. What happens when the robot disables? The solenoid loses power and its spring returns it to the rest position, so the intake moves to wherever that is, probably stowed. The compressor runs constantly and pressure never reaches the target. What's the most likely problem? A leak somewhere in the system, or the dump valve left open. What must you do before touching a cylinder or fitting? Open the dump valve and confirm both gauges read zero."},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"Why it matters","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#why-it-matters","t":"Robot code is not like a normal program that starts, runs top to bottom, and exits. A robot program starts once and then repeats the same code about 50 times a second until the robot is turned off. Every sensor read, motor command, and button check happens inside that repeating loop. Once you understand the loop , everything else in this course makes sense: why you never wait inside your code, why commands are built the way they are, and why \"loop overrun\" warnings matter."},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"Read this","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#read-this","t":"What is WPILib? Creating a Robot Program , especially the TimedRobot section"},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"Walkthrough","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"What happens when the robot turns on","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#what-happens-when-the-robot-turns-on","t":"The roboRIO boots and starts your program by calling Main.main() . main() creates your Robot object. Its constructor (or robotInit() ) runs once , and this is where RobotContainer creates all your subsystems and binds your buttons. The program enters the loop and stays there until power is cut."},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"Robot modes","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#robot-modes","t":"The Driver Station tells the robot which mode it's in. Each mode has its own pair of methods in Robot.java : Mode When Methods Disabled Before and after a match, and any time the robot isn't enabled disabledInit() , disabledPeriodic() Autonomous The first 15 seconds of a match, no driver input autonomousInit() , autonomousPeriodic() Teleop The rest of the match, drivers in control teleopInit() , teleopPeriodic() Test Practice and tuning only, never in a match testInit() , testPeriodic() (every mode) Always robotPeriodic() An Init() method runs once , when the robot enters that mode. A Periodic() method runs every loop while the robot is in that mode. robotPeriodic() runs every loop in every mode , including disabled."},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"The 20 ms loop","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#the-20-ms-loop","t":"TimedRobot calls your periodic methods every 20 milliseconds , which is 50 times per second : flowchart TD I[\"Robot constructor / robotInit() — once at power-on\"] --> L subgraph L [\"Every 20 ms, forever\"] RP[\"robotPeriodic()\"] --> MP[\"the current mode's Periodic()<br/>teleopPeriodic(), autonomousPeriodic(), ...\"] end One loop, start to finish, typically looks like this: Read the joysticks and sensors. Decide what each mechanism should do. Set motor outputs. Return, and wait for the next 20 ms tick. Each loop is a snapshot : \"Given what I see right now, what should every motor do right now?\" Then 20 ms later, you answer the question again with fresh data."},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"A full match, step by step","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#a-full-match-step-by-step","t":"Time What the Driver Station does What runs Before the match Robot is disabled disabledInit() once, then disabledPeriodic() every 20 ms 0:00 Field starts autonomous autonomousInit() once, then autonomousPeriodic() every 20 ms 0:15 Autonomous ends, brief disable disabledInit() , disabledPeriodic() 0:18 Teleop starts teleopInit() once, then teleopPeriodic() every 20 ms 2:33 Match ends disabledInit() , disabledPeriodic() robotPeriodic() runs in every row."},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"The golden rule","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#the-golden-rule","t":"Never block the loop Your code has 20 ms to finish each loop. Do a little work, then return . Next loop, do a little more. No Thread.sleep() . No while loops that wait for something. No slow work like reading a big file. Here's why. Suppose you want to run a motor for 2 seconds: // ❌ BAD: freezes the robot for 2 seconds motor . set ( 0.5 ); Thread . sleep ( 2000 ); motor . set ( 0 ); During those 2 seconds, the loop is stuck. Nothing else runs: the drivetrain ignores the joysticks, other mechanisms freeze, and the robot can't even respond to being disabled. The roboRIO's motor safety feature may also shut off motors because they stopped getting updates. The right way spreads the work across many loops. Each loop checks how much time has passed: // ✅ GOOD: checked once per loop, finishes in microseconds private final Timer timer = new Timer (); @Override public void autonomousInit () { timer . restart (); // start timing when auto begins } @Override public void autonomousPeriodic () { if ( timer . get () < 2.0 ) { motor . set ( 0.5 ); } else { motor . set ( 0 ); } } Over 2 seconds, autonomousPeriodic() runs 100 times. Each time it asks \"has it been 2 seconds yet?\" and sets the motor accordingly. Every other part of the robot keeps running the whole time. The same goes for waiting on a sensor: // ❌ BAD: waits forever if the switch never closes while ( ! limitSwitch . get ()) { motor . set ( 0.3 ); } // ✅ GOOD: one check per loop if ( ! limitSwitch . get ()) { motor . set ( 0.3 ); } else { motor . set ( 0 ); } This is why command-based programming exists Writing every mechanism as timers and if statements inside teleopPeriodic() gets messy fast. Commands ( Lesson 7 ) are a cleaner way to write \"do this a little each loop until it's done.\" Under the hood, they follow exactly this rule."},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"Loop overruns","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#loop-overruns","t":"If your code takes longer than 20 ms, the console prints a warning: Warning: Loop time of 0.02s overrun teleopPeriodic(): 0.045s robotPeriodic(): 0.001s That means the next loop started late. An occasional overrun isn't a disaster, but frequent ones make the robot sluggish and unpredictable. The warning shows which method took the time, so you know where to look. Common causes: Thread.sleep() , waiting loops, printing too much with System.out.println every loop, or slow work like file reads."},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"Where your code goes in the Command Robot template","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#where-your-code-goes-in-the-command-robot-template","t":"In the template you created in Lesson 0, Robot.java is short, and you'll rarely edit it: public class Robot extends TimedRobot { private Command autonomousCommand ; private final RobotContainer robotContainer ; public Robot () { robotContainer = new RobotContainer (); // builds subsystems, binds buttons } @Override public void robotPeriodic () { CommandScheduler . getInstance (). run (); // runs all commands, every loop } @Override public void autonomousInit () { autonomousCommand = robotContainer . getAutonomousCommand (); if ( autonomousCommand != null ) { autonomousCommand . schedule (); } } @Override public void teleopInit () { if ( autonomousCommand != null ) { autonomousCommand . cancel (); // stop auto when teleop starts } } } The important line is CommandScheduler.getInstance().run() in robotPeriodic() . Every loop, the command scheduler runs your commands, checks your buttons, and calls your subsystems' periodic() methods. You'll write subsystems and commands; the scheduler decides when they run. Lesson 7.1 covers it in detail."},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"Try it","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#try-it","t":"Open your project's Robot.java and find each mode's Init() and Periodic() methods Add a counter field. Increase it in robotPeriodic() and print it every 50th loop ( if (count % 50 == 0) ). Run in simulation. Does it print about once per second? Add a print to teleopInit() and disabledInit() . Switch modes in the Sim GUI and watch when each prints. Put Thread.sleep(100); in teleopPeriodic() (you'll need to wrap it in try { } catch (InterruptedException e) { } ). Enable teleop and read the loop overrun warnings. Then remove it. Use a Timer to print \"Two seconds!\" exactly once, two seconds after teleop starts"},{"p":"6. How a Robot Program Runs","h":"Check yourself","u":"robot-code/06-robot-program.html#check-yourself","t":"How many times per second does teleopPeriodic() run? About 50, once every 20 ms. What's the difference between teleopInit() and teleopPeriodic() ? teleopInit() runs once when teleop starts. teleopPeriodic() runs every loop for the rest of teleop. Which method runs in every mode, including disabled? robotPeriodic() Why does Thread.sleep() cause a loop overrun warning? The loop has 20 ms to finish. Sleeping blocks it, so the next loop starts late and the whole robot stops responding until it's done. How do you run a motor for 2 seconds without blocking? Start a Timer when the action begins, then check timer.get() every loop and stop the motor once it passes 2 seconds."},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"Why it matters","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#why-it-matters","t":"Your robot code runs 50 times a second on a computer with no screen. To see what it's doing (sensor readings, targets, motor outputs, which command is running), you send values to a dashboard on the laptop. To tune it, you send numbers back the other way. Both directions go through NetworkTables , WPILib's system for sharing data between the robot and the laptop. This course uses: NetworkTables 4 publishers in robot code, to send values Elastic , a drag-and-drop dashboard that comes with WPILib, to see and change them Why this course doesn't use SmartDashboard.putNumber Older code (and a lot of examples online) sends values with SmartDashboard.putNumber(...) . That still works in 2026, but it's on its way out: The SmartDashboard and Shuffleboard apps are removed in WPILib 2027. WPILib's advice is to use Elastic as the driver dashboard and AdvantageScope for debugging. In the 2027 previews, the SmartDashboard class itself is replaced by new Telemetry and Tunables APIs, and SendableChooser is replaced by Selectable . SendableChooser only reaches the dashboard through SmartDashboard.putData , so this course uses Selectable instead. NetworkTables 4 stays. The new 2027 APIs are built on top of it. So this course uses a small 2026 copy of the 2027 Telemetry and Tunables APIs . The code you write now already looks like 2027 code."},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"Read this","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#read-this","t":"What is NetworkTables Publishing and Subscribing to a Topic Elastic (WPILib docs) and the Elastic documentation Removed features in 2027"},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"Walkthrough","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"How NetworkTables works","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#how-networktables-works","t":"The roboRIO runs a NetworkTables server . Dashboards and tools on the laptop connect to it as clients . Any of them can send values, and all of them see the same values. flowchart LR R[\"roboRIO<br/>NetworkTables server<br/>(your robot code)\"] <--> E[Elastic<br/>driver dashboard] R <--> A[AdvantageScope<br/>graphs and logs] R <--> G[Glass / Sim GUI] Each value lives on a topic . A topic has a name that looks like a file path, and a fixed type: Topic Type Example value /Wheel/Position (rot) double 3.42 /Wheel/At Target boolean true /Elevator/kP double 10.0 /Auto/selected string \"Square\" The part before the last / is the table , like a folder. Group each subsystem's values in its own table, and put the units in the name so nobody has to guess. Code doesn't write to a topic directly. It creates a publisher to send values, or a subscriber to read them: Object Direction Used for Publisher ( DoublePublisher , BooleanPublisher , ...) Robot → dashboard Showing sensor readings, targets, outputs Subscriber ( DoubleSubscriber , ...) Dashboard → robot Reading a value someone else sets Entry ( DoubleEntry , ...) Both A tunable number: the robot sets a starting value, and you change it from the dashboard"},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"Publishing a value","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#publishing-a-value","t":"import edu.wpi.first.networktables.DoublePublisher ; import edu.wpi.first.networktables.NetworkTable ; import edu.wpi.first.networktables.NetworkTableInstance ; public class Arm extends SubsystemBase { // The \"Arm\" table, like a folder on the dashboard private final NetworkTable table = NetworkTableInstance . getDefault (). getTable ( \"Arm\" ); // Create each publisher ONCE, and keep it in a field private final DoublePublisher anglePub = table . getDoubleTopic ( \"Angle (deg)\" ). publish (); @Override public void periodic () { anglePub . set ( getAngleDegrees ()); // send the newest value, every loop } } Line What it does NetworkTableInstance.getDefault() The robot's NetworkTables. You'll almost never need any other. .getTable(\"Arm\") The Arm table. Every topic in it starts with /Arm/ . .getDoubleTopic(\"Angle (deg)\") The topic /Arm/Angle (deg) , which holds a double .publish() Starts publishing. Keep the publisher in a field: publishing stops if it's closed. anglePub.set(...) Sends a new value. Call it every loop in periodic() . Make publishers once, set them every loop Create publishers as fields (or in the constructor), not inside periodic() . Creating a new one 50 times a second wastes memory and time."},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"Reading a value: tunable numbers","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#reading-a-value-tunable-numbers","t":"To change a number from the dashboard while the robot runs, use an entry : it publishes a starting value and also reads back whatever the dashboard writes. import edu.wpi.first.networktables.DoubleEntry ; private final DoubleEntry kP = table . getDoubleTopic ( \"kP\" ). getEntry ( ArmConstants . kP ); public Arm () { kP . set ( ArmConstants . kP ); // put the starting value on the dashboard } @Override public void periodic () { pid . setP ( kP . get ()); // use whatever is on the dashboard right now } When the robot restarts, the value goes back to ArmConstants.kP . Dashboard edits are not saved. When you find a good number, copy it into Constants.java ."},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"The course's telemetry library: 2027's API, today","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#the-courses-telemetry-library-2027s-api-today","t":"Writing a separate publisher field for every value gets long. WPILib 2027 fixes that with two new APIs, Telemetry (for sending values) and Tunables (for numbers and menus you change from the dashboard). They're built on NetworkTables 4. This course uses a small 2026 copy of those APIs, in a folder called telemetry . It has the same class names, the same method names, and publishes to the same places in NetworkTables as WPILib 2027. So the code you write now is already 2027 code: when the team moves to 2027, you delete the folder and change the imports. You don't type it in. In Lab 1.1 you'll copy the folder into your project. Class What it's for Example Telemetry Where you start: get a table, or log a one-off value Telemetry.getTable(\"Arm\") TelemetryTable One folder of values, usually one per subsystem telemetry.log(\"Angle (deg)\", angle) Tunables Make a number you can change from the dashboard, or publish a menu Tunables.addDouble(\"Arm/kP\", 0.05) TunableDouble A tunable number kP.get() Selectable A dropdown menu, like the auto chooser autoChooser.getSelected() Using it in a subsystem: import frc.robot.telemetry.Telemetry ; import frc.robot.telemetry.TelemetryTable ; import frc.robot.telemetry.TunableDouble ; import frc.robot.telemetry.Tunables ; public class Arm extends SubsystemBase { // Everything this subsystem sends goes in /Telemetry/Arm private final TelemetryTable m_telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Arm\" ); // A gain you can change in Elastic, at /Tunables/Arm/kP. Starts at the value in Constants. private final TunableDouble m_kP = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Arm/kP\" , ArmConstants . kP ); @Override public void periodic () { m_telemetry . log ( \"Angle (deg)\" , getAngleDegrees ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Target (deg)\" , m_targetDegrees ); m_telemetry . log ( \"Output (V)\" , m_lastVolts ); m_telemetry . log ( \"At Target\" , atTarget ()); m_pid . setP ( m_kP . get ()); // use whatever kP is on the dashboard right now } } Line What it does Telemetry.getTable(\"Arm\") The Arm folder, inside /Telemetry . Keep it in a field. m_telemetry.log(name, value) Sends a value: a number, true / false , or text. Call it every loop. Tunables.addDouble(\"Arm/kP\", ...) Makes a tunable number at /Tunables/Arm/kP . Call it once, in a field. m_kP.get() The number on the dashboard right now What's inside log ? The same publishers you just learned, made once and kept in a Map (a lookup table from each name to its publisher): public void log ( String name , double value ) { // The first call for a name creates its publisher; every later call reuses it m_numbers . computeIfAbsent ( name , n -> kNT . getDoubleTopic ( topic ( n )). publish ()). set ( value ); } computeIfAbsent means \"find the publisher for this name; if there isn't one yet, make it with this code and remember it.\" Read the full source : every line is commented. Structured values. log also takes WPILib types like Pose2d , and sends them as structs , which AdvantageScope can draw on a field: m_telemetry . log ( \"Pose\" , m_odometry . getPoseMeters ()); Tunable numbers reset. A tunable goes back to its starting value every time the robot code starts. Dashboard edits are not saved. When you find a good number, copy it into Constants.java ."},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"A dropdown menu: Selectable","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#a-dropdown-menu-selectable","t":"The drive team picks an autonomous routine from a dropdown on the dashboard before each match. Older code does this with SendableChooser and SmartDashboard.putData . In 2027 that becomes Selectable , and the course library has it now: private final Selectable < Command > m_autoChooser = new Selectable <> (); public RobotContainer () { m_autoChooser . addDefault ( \"Do nothing\" , Commands . none ()); m_autoChooser . add ( \"Drive forward\" , driveForwardAuto ()); m_autoChooser . add ( \"Square\" , squareAuto ()); Tunables . publish ( \"Auto\" , m_autoChooser ); // shows up as /Tunables/Auto } public Command getAutonomousCommand () { return m_autoChooser . getSelected (); } A menu is just a few topics in one table. Elastic reads them and shows a ComboBox Chooser : Topic Who writes it What it holds .type Robot \"String Chooser\" , which tells Elastic to show a dropdown options Robot Every option's name, in order default Robot The option used if nobody picks selected Dashboard The option the drive team picked active Robot The option the robot will really use, so the dashboard can confirm it Selectable<Command> is a generic class: the part in < > is the type of thing in the menu. Add every option in the constructor Add all the options in RobotContainer 's constructor, before the robot is enabled, then publish the menu."},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"What to send","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#what-to-send","t":"For any mechanism, send these four kinds of values. With them, you can usually tell in seconds whether a problem is the sensor, the code, or the hardware ( Lesson 9 ): Value Why The sensor reading (position, velocity) Is the sensor working and pointing the right way? The target Is the code asking for what you think? The output (percent or volts) Is the code actually commanding the motor? State (at target, has piece, at limit) Do your conditions flip when they should?"},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"Elastic","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#elastic","t":"Elastic is a dashboard made by Team 353, installed with WPILib. It's built for the driver station at competition, but it's just as good for testing. Open it: in VS Code, press Ctrl + Shift + P , run WPILib: Start Tool , and choose Elastic . Connect it: click the gear (settings) and pick the IP address mode: Mode Use it when Driver Station Connected to a real robot. Elastic gets the address from the Driver Station, so open the Driver Station first. localhost Running simulation on your own laptop Team Number or roboRIO mDNS Connecting without the Driver Station (these don't work in simulation) The bar at the bottom of Elastic shows Network Tables: Disconnected in red until it connects, then turns green and shows the robot's address. While it's disconnected, every widget is grayed out. Add widgets: open Elastic's list of topics. Values you log are under Telemetry , in a folder for each subsystem (like Telemetry → Wheel ); tunable numbers and menus are under Tunables . Drag a value onto the grid. Drag widgets to move them and drag their corners to resize. You can make several tabs , for example one for driving and one for each mechanism you're testing. Widget Shows Can change the value? Text Display A number, boolean, or text Yes. Edit it to send a new value. In its settings, turn on the submit button if you want one. Graph A number over time No Number Bar A number as a bar No Number Slider A number on a slider Yes, by dragging Boolean Box A color for true or false No Toggle Switch / Toggle Button A boolean Yes, by clicking ComboBox Chooser An autonomous chooser ( Selectable ) Yes, by picking an option Save your layout. Elastic remembers the layout you build. To keep a copy for the team, export it from Elastic's file menu into your robot project and commit the file to Git. Graph your target and your measurement together Put the target and the sensor reading on one Graph widget. When you tune a controller in Lesson 12 , the gap between the two lines is the error, and you can see overshoot and oscillation at a glance."},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"What still uses SmartDashboard.putData in 2026","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#what-still-uses-smartdashboardputdata-in-2026","t":"Some WPILib objects aren't single numbers. They're Sendables : the command scheduler, a Field2d , a Mechanism2d . In 2026, the way to put these on the dashboard is still SmartDashboard.putData . It sends them through NetworkTables 4 (under /SmartDashboard/ ), and Elastic shows them with special widgets. SmartDashboard . putData ( CommandScheduler . getInstance ()); // the running commands SmartDashboard . putData ( \"Arm/Mechanism\" , mech ); // a Mechanism2d drawing This course uses SmartDashboard only for these. Plain values always go through Telemetry , tunable numbers through Tunables , and the auto chooser uses Selectable ."},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"Logging comes free","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#logging-comes-free","t":"Once you call DataLogManager.start() in Robot 's constructor, everything you publish to NetworkTables is also recorded to a log file on the roboRIO. After a match, open the log in AdvantageScope and replay every value ( Lesson 9 )."},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"Moving to 2027","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#moving-to-2027","t":"Because the course library copies WPILib 2027's API, moving to 2027 is short: Delete the telemetry folder from the project. Change each import frc.robot.telemetry... line to WPILib's import for the same class. VS Code's lightbulb can do it for you. Build. The class names and method calls stay the same. Telemetry and tunable numbers are published to the same paths in 2027 ( /Telemetry/... and /Tunables/... ), so most Elastic widgets keep working. In 2027 the auto chooser gets a new type of table, so you may need to drag it onto your layout again. 2026 (this course) 2027 Telemetry.getTable(\"Wheel\") , table.log(...) Same Tunables.addDouble(\"Wheel/kP\", 1.0) , kP.get() Same new Selectable<>() , Tunables.publish(\"Auto\", chooser) Same SmartDashboard.putData(\"Field\", field) once Telemetry.log(\"Field\", field) every loop The 2027 APIs were still in preview when this was written, so check WPILib's documentation for the final details: the Telemetry design document and the Tunables design document ."},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"Try it","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#try-it","t":"In simulation (no robot needed): Copy the telemetry folder into a project. In robotPeriodic() , log Timer.getFPGATimestamp() with Telemetry.log(\"Robot/Time (s)\", ...) . Run WPILib: Simulate Robot Code , open Elastic, set it to localhost , and find Telemetry → Robot → Time (s) . Show it as a Text Display, then as a Graph. Add a tunable with Tunables.addDouble(\"Robot/Multiplier\", 1.0) , and log the time times the multiplier. Change the multiplier in Elastic and watch the logged value change. Restart the simulation. What happens to the multiplier you set? Build a tab with your values, save the layout, and export it."},{"p":"6.1 Dashboards: NetworkTables and Elastic","h":"Check yourself","u":"robot-code/06b-dashboards.html#check-yourself","t":"What's the difference between a publisher and a subscriber? A publisher sends values from your code to NetworkTables. A subscriber reads values that something else (like the dashboard) put there. Why create publishers once, in a field, instead of inside periodic() ? periodic() runs 50 times a second. Making a new publisher every time wastes memory and time; making it once and calling set() every loop is cheap. You changed kP in Elastic and found a great value. Then the robot rebooted and it's gone. Why, and what should you do? Tunables start at their default every time the code starts. Copy the value into Constants.java and deploy. Elastic says \\\"Network Tables: Disconnected\\\" while you're running simulation. What should you check? That the IP address mode is set to localhost , and that the simulation is actually running. Why does this course use Selectable instead of SendableChooser ? SendableChooser only reaches the dashboard through SmartDashboard.putData , which is going away. Selectable is the 2027 replacement; the course library has a 2026 copy that sends the same menu over NetworkTables 4. Where does Telemetry.getTable(\\\"Wheel\\\").log(\\\"Position (rot)\\\", x) show up in NetworkTables? At /Telemetry/Wheel/Position (rot) . In Elastic, open Telemetry , then Wheel . What do you have to change in your code when the team moves to WPILib 2027? Delete the course's telemetry folder and change the imports to WPILib's. The class and method names stay the same. In a Selectable menu, who writes selected , and who writes active ? The dashboard writes selected when the drive team picks. The robot writes active : the option it will really use. What happens to the SmartDashboard and Shuffleboard apps in 2027? They're removed. WPILib recommends Elastic for the driver dashboard and AdvantageScope for debugging."},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Why it matters","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#why-it-matters","t":"Imagine writing a whole robot inside teleopPeriodic() : the drivetrain, the intake, the arm, the shooter, every button, every timer, every \"only do this if that.\" It works for a week. Then it's 600 lines of tangled if statements where changing the intake breaks the arm. Command-based programming organizes the robot into small, reusable pieces: Subsystems are the robot's parts (nouns). Commands are the things those parts do (verbs). Triggers decide when commands run. Almost every competitive FRC team uses it, and it's what the WPILib template is built for."},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Read this","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#read-this","t":"What Is Command-Based Programming? Subsystems Commands Binding Commands to Triggers Structuring a Command-Based Robot Project"},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Walkthrough","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"The mental model","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#the-mental-model","t":"Piece Grammar Example Lives in Subsystem Noun Intake , Arm , Drivetrain subsystems/ Command Verb \"run intake,\" \"move arm to 90°\" factory methods on the subsystem, or commands/ Trigger When \"while the A button is held\" RobotContainer.configureBindings() Only one command can use a subsystem at a time . The scheduler enforces this, so two commands can't fight over the same motor. Subsystems are the robot's parts; commands are things the robot does with them. From the WPILib docs, What Is \"Command-Based\" Programming? (CC BY 4.0). From the WPILib docs: Why command-based? Command-based programming is declarative : you describe what should happen, and WPILib handles checking for it every loop. For example, \"fire the piston when a condition becomes true\" is one line: new Trigger ( condition :: get ). onTrue ( Commands . runOnce (() -> piston . set ( DoubleSolenoid . Value . kForward ))); Without command-based, you'd have to check the button every loop yourself, and remember whether it was already pressed so the piston only fires once: if ( condition . get ()) { if ( ! pressed ) { piston . set ( DoubleSolenoid . Value . kForward ); pressed = true ; } } else { pressed = false ; } Command-based code tends to be clean, extensible, and easy to reuse from year to year. Adapted from What Is \"Command-Based\" Programming? , CC BY 4.0. What's the scheduler? The command scheduler is the part of WPILib that runs your commands every 20 ms, checks your buttons, and enforces that one-command rule. This lesson uses it; Lesson 7.1 explains exactly how it works, step by step."},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"What goes in a subsystem","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#what-goes-in-a-subsystem","t":"A subsystem owns hardware and offers actions . A good subsystem: creates its own motors and sensors as private fields, so nothing outside can touch them directly offers methods that read state, like hasGamePiece() or getAngle() offers command factories : methods that return commands, like runIntake() puts telemetry in its periodic() method, which runs every loop A subsystem should not know about buttons or controllers. \"Run the intake\" belongs in the subsystem; \"when A is pressed\" belongs in RobotContainer . Keeping them apart means you can reuse the same intake command in teleop, in autonomous, and in a test."},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Build an Intake subsystem","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#build-an-intake-subsystem","t":"Create src/main/java/frc/robot/subsystems/Intake.java : package frc.robot.subsystems ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.DigitalInput ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.motorcontrol.PWMSparkMax ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.Command ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; import frc.robot.telemetry.Telemetry ; import frc.robot.telemetry.TelemetryTable ; public class Intake extends SubsystemBase { private final PWMSparkMax motor = new PWMSparkMax ( 0 ); private final DigitalInput beamBreak = new DigitalInput ( 0 ); private final TelemetryTable telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Intake\" ); // Lesson 6.1 /** True when a game piece is blocking the beam break sensor. */ public boolean hasGamePiece () { return ! beamBreak . get (); // many beam breaks read false when blocked } /** Runs the rollers in. Stops them when the command ends. */ public Command runIntake () { return startEnd ( () -> motor . set ( 0.75 ), // when it starts () -> motor . set ( 0.0 ) // when it ends ). withName ( \"Intake\" ); } /** Spits a game piece out. */ public Command outtake () { return startEnd (() -> motor . set ( - 0.5 ), () -> motor . set ( 0.0 )). withName ( \"Outtake\" ); } /** Intakes until a game piece is detected, then stops on its own. */ public Command intakeUntilFull () { return runIntake (). until ( this :: hasGamePiece ). withName ( \"Intake Until Full\" ); } @Override public void periodic () { telemetry . log ( \"Has Piece\" , hasGamePiece ()); telemetry . log ( \"Output\" , motor . get ()); } } Why PWMSparkMax ? It works in simulation with no vendor libraries. On the real robot you'll use your vendor's class (TalonFX, SparkMax, etc.). The command-based structure is identical."},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Command factories","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#command-factories","t":"The methods startEnd , run , and others come from SubsystemBase . Each one builds a command that automatically requires this subsystem : Factory What the command does Ends by itself? runOnce(action) Runs action one time Yes, immediately run(action) Runs action every loop No startEnd(start, end) Runs start once, then end once when it stops No runEnd(action, end) Runs action every loop, then end when it stops No Commands that don't end by themselves keep going until a button is released, another command takes over the subsystem, or you add an end condition."},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Changing how a command ends","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#changing-how-a-command-ends","t":"Chain these onto any command: Decorator Effect Example .until(condition) Ends when condition becomes true runIntake().until(this::hasGamePiece) .withTimeout(seconds) Ends after that many seconds outtake().withTimeout(0.5) .unless(condition) Doesn't start at all if condition is true runIntake().unless(this::hasGamePiece) .withName(\"...\") Gives it a readable name on the dashboard .withName(\"Intake\")"},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"The Commands helpers","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#the-commands-helpers","t":"The Commands class has factories for commands that don't belong to one subsystem: import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.Commands ; Commands . none () // does nothing (useful as a placeholder) Commands . waitSeconds ( 1.0 ) // waits one second Commands . print ( \"Got here\" ) // prints a message Commands . runOnce (() -> System . out . println ( \"hi\" ))"},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Binding commands to buttons","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#binding-commands-to-buttons","t":"All bindings go in RobotContainer.java , in configureBindings() : public class RobotContainer { private final Intake intake = new Intake (); private final CommandXboxController driver = new CommandXboxController ( 0 ); public RobotContainer () { configureBindings (); } private void configureBindings () { driver . a (). whileTrue ( intake . runIntake ()); driver . b (). whileTrue ( intake . outtake ()); driver . x (). onTrue ( intake . intakeUntilFull ()); } } Read them out loud: \"While driver A is held, run the intake.\" \"When X is pressed, intake until full.\" Good command-based code reads like English. Binding What it does whileTrue(cmd) Starts on press, cancels on release onTrue(cmd) Starts on press, runs until it finishes on its own onFalse(cmd) Starts on release toggleOnTrue(cmd) First press starts it, second press cancels it Use whileTrue with commands that never end on their own, like runIntake() , so the driver controls how long they run. Use onTrue with commands that do end, like intakeUntilFull() . onTrue with a command that never ends driver.a().onTrue(intake.runIntake()) starts the intake on the first press and never stops it, because runIntake() doesn't end by itself and onTrue doesn't cancel on release. This is one of the most common beginner bugs."},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Controller buttons","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#controller-buttons","t":"CommandXboxController gives you a trigger for every button: Method Button a() , b() , x() , y() Face buttons leftBumper() , rightBumper() Bumpers leftTrigger() , rightTrigger() Analog triggers, pressed past halfway povUp() , povDown() , povLeft() , povRight() D-pad start() , back() Menu buttons getLeftX() , getLeftY() , getRightX() , getRightY() Stick positions, -1 to 1 (not triggers)"},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Combining triggers","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#combining-triggers","t":"Triggers can be combined, and any true / false check can be a trigger: // Only while both are held driver . a (). and ( driver . rightBumper ()). whileTrue ( intake . runIntake ()); // Automatically, whenever a game piece arrives new Trigger ( intake :: hasGamePiece ). onTrue ( Commands . print ( \"Got one!\" ));"},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Longhand command classes","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#longhand-command-classes","t":"Factories cover most commands. For a command with more logic, you can write a full class in the commands/ folder. It has four methods the scheduler calls for you: public class RunIntake extends Command { private final Intake intake ; public RunIntake ( Intake intake ) { this . intake = intake ; addRequirements ( intake ); // \"this command uses the intake\" } @Override public void initialize () { /* once, at the start */ } @Override public void execute () { /* every loop */ } @Override public boolean isFinished () { return false ; } // checked every loop @Override public void end ( boolean interrupted ) { /* once, at the end */ } } Lesson 7.1 explains exactly when each of these runs."},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Try it","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#try-it","t":"Have the test bed? Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor builds this same pattern on real hardware, and Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers tries every kind of binding. Build the Intake subsystem above in your project Bind A to runIntake() and B to outtake() with whileTrue Run in Sim GUI. Drag your keyboard or a controller into Joystick 0 and watch PWM[0] change as you press buttons. Bind X to intakeUntilFull() with onTrue . In the Sim GUI, toggle DIO[0] to fake a game piece arriving and watch the command end. Change A's binding to onTrue(intake.runIntake()) . What happens when you release A? Change it back. Add an outtake().withTimeout(0.5) binding on Y, so one tap spits a piece out for half a second"},{"p":"7. Command-Based Programming","h":"Check yourself","u":"robot-code/07-command-based.html#check-yourself","t":"What's the difference between a subsystem and a command? A subsystem is a part of the robot that owns hardware (the intake). A command is an action that uses one or more subsystems (run the intake). Why shouldn't a subsystem know about controller buttons? So its commands can be reused anywhere: teleop bindings, autonomous routines, and tests. Button choices belong in RobotContainer . What's the difference between whileTrue and onTrue ? whileTrue runs the command while the button is held and cancels it on release. onTrue starts it once on press and lets it finish on its own. How do you make runIntake() stop automatically once a game piece is detected? Add .until(intake::hasGamePiece) . Two commands that both require Intake are scheduled at the same time. What happens? By default the newer command interrupts the older one, and the older one's end action runs. Lesson 7.1 covers the details."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Why it matters","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#why-it-matters","t":"In Lesson 7 you bound a button to a command and it \"just worked.\" That's the command scheduler doing its job. Most confusing command-based bugs come from not knowing what the scheduler does: a command that never stops a command that stops the moment it starts a button that does nothing two mechanisms fighting each other Once you can picture what the scheduler does every 20 ms, you can predict exactly what your robot will do, and debugging gets much easier."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Read this","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#read-this","t":"The Command Scheduler Commands , especially the command lifecycle Binding Commands to Triggers Command Compositions"},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Walkthrough","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"The scheduler is one object, run once per loop","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#the-scheduler-is-one-object-run-once-per-loop","t":"There is exactly one scheduler in your robot program: CommandScheduler.getInstance() . It doesn't run on its own. The Command Robot template calls it from Robot.java : @Override public void robotPeriodic () { CommandScheduler . getInstance (). run (); } Remember the 20 ms loop from Lesson 6 . Every time robotPeriodic() runs, the scheduler gets one turn . Everything it does happens inside that turn, on one thread, one step at a time. Nothing happens \"in the background.\" If you delete that line, nothing works No commands run, no buttons respond, and no subsystem periodic() methods are called. The robot code still builds and deploys without errors, which makes this hard to spot."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"What happens in one run()","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#what-happens-in-one-run","t":"Every 20 ms, the scheduler does these steps, in this order: flowchart TD A[\"1. Run every subsystem's periodic()\"] --> B[\"2. Check every trigger<br/>(buttons, sensors, conditions)\"] B --> C[\"3. Schedule or cancel commands<br/>based on those triggers\"] C --> D[\"4. For each running command:<br/>execute(), then isFinished()\"] D --> E[\"5. End finished commands:<br/>end(false)\"] E --> F[\"6. Start default commands for<br/>subsystems with nothing running\"] Here's the same sequence as drawn in the WPILib docs: One run of the scheduler. From the WPILib docs source, scheduler-run-sequence.drawio.svg (CC BY 4.0). Subsystem periodic() methods run. Every registered subsystem gets its periodic() called, whether or not a command is using it. This is the place for telemetry and reading sensors. Triggers are checked. Every button binding and other trigger you set up in configureBindings() is checked: is the A button pressed right now? Was it pressed last loop? Commands are scheduled or canceled. If a trigger changed (button just pressed, just released), the scheduler starts or cancels the bound command. Running commands run. For each scheduled command, it calls execute() , then asks isFinished() . Finished commands end. Any command that returned true from isFinished() gets end(false) called and is removed. Default commands start. Any subsystem that now has no command using it gets its default command scheduled. Then run() returns, the loop finishes, and 20 ms later it all happens again."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"The life of a command","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#the-life-of-a-command","t":"Every command has four methods. The scheduler, not you, decides when each one is called. Method When the scheduler calls it Typical use initialize() Once , when the command is scheduled Reset a timer, reset a PID controller, set a target execute() Every loop while it's running Set motor outputs, read sensors isFinished() Every loop , right after execute() Return true when the job is done end(boolean interrupted) Once , when it stops Stop motors. interrupted is true if it was canceled instead of finishing stateDiagram-v2 [*] --> Scheduled: schedule() Scheduled --> Running: initialize() Running --> Running: execute() then isFinished() == false Running --> Ended: isFinished() == true → end(false) Running --> Ended: canceled or interrupted → end(true) Ended --> [*] Here's a command written out longhand so you can see all four: public class SpinUpShooter extends Command { private final Shooter shooter ; private final double targetRpm ; public SpinUpShooter ( Shooter shooter , double targetRpm ) { this . shooter = shooter ; this . targetRpm = targetRpm ; addRequirements ( shooter ); // \"I need the shooter\" (see below) } @Override public void initialize () { shooter . setTarget ( targetRpm ); // runs once } @Override public void execute () { shooter . runControlLoop (); // runs every 20 ms } @Override public boolean isFinished () { return shooter . isAtSpeed (); // checked every 20 ms } @Override public void end ( boolean interrupted ) { if ( interrupted ) { shooter . stop (); // only stop if we were canceled } } } The shortcut factories from Lesson 7 build these same four methods for you: Factory initialize() execute() isFinished() end() runOnce(a) a nothing true right away nothing run(a) nothing a never finishes nothing startEnd(a, b) a nothing never finishes b runEnd(a, b) nothing a never finishes b Most of these never finish on their own. They run until a button release, a timeout, or another command interrupts them. Add .until(condition) or .withTimeout(seconds) when you want one to stop by itself."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Requirements: one command per subsystem","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#requirements-one-command-per-subsystem","t":"A requirement is a command saying \"I'm using this subsystem.\" The scheduler enforces a simple rule: Only one running command may require a given subsystem at a time. This is what stops two commands from setting the same motor to different speeds in the same loop. Factories made from a subsystem ( intake.run(...) ) require that subsystem automatically. A longhand command calls addRequirements(...) in its constructor. When you schedule a command whose requirements are already in use, the scheduler has to pick one: By default, the new command wins. The old command is interrupted: its end(true) runs, then the new one starts. A command can refuse to be interrupted. If the running command was built with .withInterruptBehavior(InterruptionBehavior.kCancelIncoming) , the new command is ignored instead. Here's how the scheduler decides, as a flowchart: What happens when a command is scheduled. From the WPILib docs source, scheduler.drawio.svg (CC BY 4.0). From the WPILib docs: What schedule() checks When a command is scheduled, the scheduler: Checks that the command isn't part of a composition. Does nothing if the scheduler is disabled, the command is already running, or the robot is disabled and the command isn't allowed to run while disabled. If the command's requirements are in use: If all the conflicting commands can be interrupted, cancels them. If not, doesn't schedule the new command. Calls the command's initialize() . Users must call CommandScheduler.getInstance().run() from robotPeriodic() . If this isn't done, the scheduler never runs, and the command framework won't work. The template already includes this call. Adapted from The Command Scheduler , CC BY 4.0. Forgetting a requirement If a command uses a motor but doesn't require its subsystem, the scheduler can't protect it. Two commands will both run and fight over the motor every loop, and the mechanism twitches or does whichever one ran last. Using the subsystem's own factories avoids this."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Default commands","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#default-commands","t":"A default command runs whenever nothing else requires its subsystem. The drivetrain's joystick command from Lesson 10 is the classic example. When another command takes the subsystem, the default command is interrupted . When that command ends, the scheduler restarts the default command in step 6 of the same loop. A default command must require its own subsystem, and it shouldn't finish on its own. If it finishes, the scheduler just starts it again the next loop."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"How triggers turn button presses into commands","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#how-triggers-turn-button-presses-into-commands","t":"Triggers are checked every loop (step 2). The scheduler remembers whether each one was true last loop, so it can tell a press from a hold: Binding Starts the command Cancels the command onTrue(cmd) When the button goes from released to pressed Never. It runs until it finishes. whileTrue(cmd) When the button is pressed When the button is released onFalse(cmd) When the button is released Never toggleOnTrue(cmd) First press Second press Triggers aren't only buttons. Any BooleanSupplier can be a trigger: new Trigger ( intake :: hasGamePiece ) . onTrue ( leds . flashGreen (). withTimeout ( 1.0 ));"},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Walk through it loop by loop","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#walk-through-it-loop-by-loop","t":"Here's exactly what happens when a driver holds A, which is bound with driver.a().whileTrue(intake.runIntake()) , while the intake has a default command that holds the rollers still. Loop Driver What the scheduler does 1 Not pressing A Intake default command runs its execute() 2 Presses A Trigger sees a new press. runIntake() requires the intake, so the default command is interrupted: end(true) . runIntake() is scheduled: initialize() sets rollers to 0.75 3 to 40 Holding A runIntake() keeps running. Its isFinished() is always false 41 Releases A Trigger sees the release and cancels runIntake() : end(true) sets rollers to 0. The intake is free, so the default command starts again 42 Not pressing A Default command runs The driver held the button for about 40 loops × 20 ms = 0.8 seconds."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Compositions and the scheduler","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#compositions-and-the-scheduler","t":"When you combine commands, such as a.andThen(b) , the scheduler sees one command: the composition. It requires every subsystem that a or b needs, for its whole run. That has two consequences that surprise people: A sequence holds every subsystem, the whole time. In drive.andThen(shoot) , the shooter is reserved even while the robot is still driving, so the shooter's default command won't run. A command inside a composition belongs to it. You can't schedule it separately or put the same command object in two compositions. Build a fresh one each time, which is one reason factory methods that return a new command are the standard pattern."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Disabled mode","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#disabled-mode","t":"When the robot is disabled, the scheduler still runs every loop, but it cancels running commands, and it won't start new ones unless they're marked .ignoringDisable(true) . Use that for things that are safe when disabled, like LEDs or resetting a sensor."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Seeing the scheduler work","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#seeing-the-scheduler-work","t":"On the dashboard. Add this to RobotContainer , then open Elastic and find Scheduler in the SmartDashboard table: SmartDashboard . putData ( CommandScheduler . getInstance ()); It lists every running command, live. The scheduler is a Sendable , so in 2026 it still goes on the dashboard with SmartDashboard.putData ( Lesson 6.1 ). You can also put a subsystem on the dashboard ( SmartDashboard.putData(intake) ) to see which command currently owns it. In the console. The scheduler can call your code whenever a command starts, gets interrupted, or finishes: CommandScheduler . getInstance (). onCommandInitialize ( cmd -> System . out . println ( \"START \" + cmd . getName ())); CommandScheduler . getInstance (). onCommandInterrupt ( cmd -> System . out . println ( \"CANCEL \" + cmd . getName ())); CommandScheduler . getInstance (). onCommandFinish ( cmd -> System . out . println ( \"DONE \" + cmd . getName ())); Give commands readable names with .withName(\"Intake\") . Loop overruns. If the scheduler's turn takes longer than 20 ms, you'll see a loop overrun warning that lists how long each step took: each subsystem's periodic() , and each command's execute() . The slow one is your culprit, usually blocking code (Lesson 6) or slow logging."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Common scheduler bugs","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#common-scheduler-bugs","t":"Symptom Likely cause Fix Nothing responds at all CommandScheduler.getInstance().run() missing from robotPeriodic() Put it back Command ends the instant it starts isFinished() is already true (sensor already \"at target,\" or runOnce used by mistake) Check the condition. Use run() for something that should keep going. Command never ends run() / startEnd() with no until , timeout, or whileTrue binding Add an end condition Mechanism twitches between two speeds Two commands use the motor and one doesn't require the subsystem Add the requirement, or use the subsystem's factories Default command doesn't come back Another command (often a sequence) still requires the subsystem Check what's running on the dashboard Error about a command already in a composition Same command object used twice Call the factory again to make a new command Command restarts constantly Scheduling it every loop from periodic() Bind it to a trigger instead"},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Try it","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#try-it","t":"Add the three onCommand... print hooks and SmartDashboard.putData(CommandScheduler.getInstance()) In simulation, press and hold your intake button. Match what prints to the loop-by-loop table above. Give the intake a default command that sets the motor to 0. Watch it get interrupted and come back. Change the intake binding from whileTrue to onTrue . What happens now when you release the button, and why? Write the SpinUpShooter command longhand, then rewrite it with runOnce , run , and .until() . Both should behave the same. Break it on purpose: make a command that sets the intake motor without requiring the intake, bind it to B, and press A and B together. Explain what you see."},{"p":"7.1 How the Command Scheduler Works","h":"Check yourself","u":"robot-code/07b-scheduler.html#check-yourself","t":"In what order does the scheduler do its work each loop? Subsystem periodic() methods, then triggers, then scheduling or canceling commands, then execute() and isFinished() for each running command, then ending finished commands, then starting default commands. What's the difference between end(true) and end(false) ? end(false) means the command finished on its own because isFinished() returned true. end(true) means it was interrupted: canceled, its button released, or another command took its subsystem. A command is running on the arm. You schedule a different command that also requires the arm. What happens? By default the running command is interrupted ( end(true) ) and the new one starts. If the running command was set to kCancelIncoming , the new command is ignored instead. Why does drive.andThen(shoot) stop the shooter's default command, even while driving? A composition requires every subsystem any of its parts needs, for its whole run. The shooter is reserved from the first loop of the sequence. Your intake binding uses onTrue(intake.runIntake()) . The driver taps A. When does the intake stop? Never on its own. runIntake() doesn't finish and onTrue doesn't cancel on release, so it runs until something else requires the intake or the robot is disabled."},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Why it matters","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#why-it-matters","t":"To control something, you have to measure it. You can't hold an arm at 90° if you don't know where the arm is, drive 2 meters if you don't know how far you've gone, or stop the intake when it's full if nothing can see the game piece. Sensors turn the physical world into numbers your code can use. Getting those numbers into the right units is half the job, and getting it wrong is one of the most common sources of robot bugs."},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Read this","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#read-this","t":"Encoders – Software Gyroscopes – Software Digital Inputs (limit switches) Java Units Library"},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Walkthrough","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Common FRC sensors","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#common-frc-sensors","t":"Sensor Measures Output Typical use Encoder Rotation of a shaft Position and velocity Drive distance, arm angle, flywheel speed Absolute encoder The shaft's angle, even right after power-on Angle Arm angle, swerve module direction Gyro / IMU Robot rotation Heading (yaw), sometimes pitch and roll Driving straight, field-relative swerve Limit switch Pressed or not true / false Hard stops, homing an elevator Beam break Something blocks a light beam true / false \"Do we have a game piece?\" Distance sensor (time-of-flight, ultrasonic) Distance to an object Distance Detecting game pieces, measuring to a wall Camera Images, AprilTags, game pieces Targets and poses Aiming, pose estimation (later lessons)"},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Digital sensors: limit switches and beam breaks","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#digital-sensors-limit-switches-and-beam-breaks","t":"These are the simplest: true or false , read from a DIO port. private final DigitalInput armDownSwitch = new DigitalInput ( 1 ); public boolean isArmDown () { return ! armDownSwitch . get (); } Which way is pressed? Many switches are wired so that get() returns true when not pressed and false when pressed. That's why the example uses ! . Don't guess: press the switch and watch the value on the dashboard, then write the method so the name tells the truth. Name methods for what they mean ( isArmDown() , hasGamePiece() ), not for the hardware ( getSwitch1() ). Everywhere else in the code reads more clearly."},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Encoders: relative vs. absolute","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#encoders-relative-vs-absolute","t":"Relative (incremental) encoder Absolute encoder What it reports How far it has turned since power-on or the last reset The actual angle of the shaft, always After power-on Reads 0, wherever the mechanism is Reads the real angle Good for Wheels, flywheels, elevators with a homing switch Arms, wrists, swerve module steering Examples Motor built-in encoders, quadrature encoders Through Bore Encoder (absolute mode), CANcoder Relative encoders and arms If an arm uses a relative encoder, it reads 0 at power-on wherever the arm happens to be . Turn the robot on with the arm halfway up, and your code thinks halfway up is 0°. Either start the robot with the mechanism in a known position every time, home it against a limit switch, or use an absolute encoder. Most modern motors (Kraken, Falcon, NEO) have an encoder built in, read through the vendor library. Separate encoders can be wired to DIO ports or the CAN bus."},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Encoders count rotations: conversion factors","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#encoders-count-rotations-conversion-factors","t":"An encoder reports how far a shaft turned, usually in rotations of the motor. To get something useful (meters, degrees), multiply by a conversion factor that accounts for gearing and wheel size. Example 1: drivetrain wheel Gear ratio: 8:1 (the motor turns 8 times for every 1 turn of the wheel) Wheel diameter: 4 in = 0.1016 m Circumference: π × 0.1016 ≈ 0.319 m wheel rotations = motor rotations / 8 meters traveled = wheel rotations × 0.319 meters per motor rotation = 0.319 / 8 ≈ 0.0399 So if the motor encoder reads 50 rotations, the robot has driven about 50 × 0.0399 ≈ 2.0 m . Example 2: arm Gear ratio: 60:1 from motor to arm One arm rotation = 360° degrees per motor rotation = 360 / 60 = 6° If the motor encoder reads 15 rotations, the arm has moved 15 × 6 = 90° . Gear ratios multiply A 5:1 gearbox followed by a 3:1 chain reduction is 5 × 3 = 15:1 total. Ask the mechanical team for every stage. Velocity uses the same factor. Motor encoders often report rotations per minute or per second , so check the vendor docs and convert to per second: wheel speed (m/s) = motor RPM / 60 × meters per motor rotation"},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Write the math once, in Constants","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#write-the-math-once-in-constants","t":"Never scatter magic numbers through your code. Put each one in Constants.java with its unit in the name: public final class Constants { public static final class DriveConstants { public static final double GEAR_RATIO = 8.0 ; public static final double WHEEL_DIAMETER_METERS = 0.1016 ; public static final double METERS_PER_MOTOR_ROTATION = ( Math . PI * WHEEL_DIAMETER_METERS ) / GEAR_RATIO ; } public static final class ArmConstants { public static final double GEAR_RATIO = 60.0 ; public static final double DEGREES_PER_MOTOR_ROTATION = 360.0 / GEAR_RATIO ; } } Then write subsystem methods that return real units, so nothing else in the code ever sees raw encoder values: public double getDistanceMeters () { return motorEncoderRotations () * DriveConstants . METERS_PER_MOTOR_ROTATION ; }"},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"A WPILib encoder example","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#a-wpilib-encoder-example","t":"WPILib's Encoder class reads a quadrature encoder on two DIO ports. You set the conversion once with setDistancePerPulse , and then getDistance() returns real units: private final Encoder armEncoder = new Encoder ( 2 , 3 ); // DIO 2 and 3 public Arm () { // A 2048-pulse encoder on the arm shaft: 360° / 2048 pulses armEncoder . setDistancePerPulse ( 360.0 / 2048.0 ); } public double getAngleDegrees () { return armEncoder . getDistance (); } public double getVelocityDegPerSec () { return armEncoder . getRate (); } Vendor motor encoders work the same way. Look up your controller's method for setting the conversion factor (for example, CTRE's SensorToMechanismRatio or REV's positionConversionFactor )."},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Gyros","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#gyros","t":"A gyro measures how much the robot has rotated . The value you'll use most is yaw , the robot's heading. public Rotation2d getHeading () { return gyro . getRotation2d (); // most WPILib-compatible gyros offer this } Sign conventions WPILib's math assumes counterclockwise is positive when looking down at the robot. Some gyros report clockwise as positive. If turning left makes the heading go down, your code needs to flip the sign, or your odometry will be mirrored. Gyros drift a little over time and are zeroed at power-on. Many teams add a driver button that resets the heading to 0 when the robot is facing away from the driver."},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Units: pick a system and stick to it","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#units-pick-a-system-and-stick-to-it","t":"WPILib's math classes (kinematics, odometry, controllers, feedforward) assume SI units : Quantity Use Not Distance meters inches, feet Angle radians (for math) degrees (fine for display) Time seconds milliseconds Velocity meters per second, radians per second RPM, feet per second Common mistake: mixing units The code runs fine, but every number is off. Inches passed where meters are expected are off by about 39×; degrees passed where radians are expected are off by about 57×. The robot drives or moves to completely wrong places with no error message. Two habits prevent most unit bugs: Put the unit in the name: WHEEL_DIAMETER_METERS , getAngleRadians() , targetRpm . Convert at the edges: convert from vendor units as soon as you read a sensor, and to vendor units only when you set a motor. Everything in between uses SI. WPILib also has helpers for conversions: Units . inchesToMeters ( 4.0 ); // 0.1016 Units . degreesToRadians ( 90 ); // 1.5708 Units . rotationsPerMinuteToRadiansPerSecond ( 6000 ); WPILib's Java Units Library goes further, with types like Distance and Angle that make it a compile error to mix them up. Some vendor libraries use it directly."},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Check sensors before you trust them","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#check-sensors-before-you-trust-them","t":"Before writing any code that depends on a sensor, put it on the dashboard and check it by hand: private final TelemetryTable telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Arm\" ); // Lesson 6.1 @Override public void periodic () { telemetry . log ( \"Angle (deg)\" , getAngleDegrees ()); telemetry . log ( \"Down Switch\" , isArmDown ()); } Move the mechanism by hand (robot disabled). Does the number change in the right direction ? Move it a known amount, like exactly one rotation or 90°. Is the number right ? Press the switch. Does the boolean change, and is it the value the method name promises?"},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Try it","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#try-it","t":"Have the test bed? Labs 4 (limit switch), 5 (potentiometer), 6 (encoder), and 8 (gyro) put each sensor in this lesson on real hardware. For your team's drivetrain, get the gear ratio and wheel diameter, and calculate meters per motor rotation For your team's arm or elevator, calculate degrees (or meters) per motor rotation, including every gear stage Add both to Constants.java with units in the names Add a limit switch to a subsystem with a method named for what it means. In the Sim GUI, toggle its DIO and watch your boolean on the dashboard. On a real robot (with a mentor), put an encoder value on the dashboard, move the mechanism by hand, and check the direction and scale"},{"p":"8. Sensors & Units","h":"Check yourself","u":"robot-code/08-sensors.html#check-yourself","t":"Why use a conversion factor instead of raw encoder counts? Raw counts depend on the encoder and gearing. Converting gives real units (meters, degrees) you can reason about and use with WPILib's math. A drivetrain has a 10:1 gear ratio and 6-inch wheels. How far does the robot move per motor rotation? Circumference = π × 6 in ≈ 18.85 in ≈ 0.479 m. Divide by 10: about 0.0479 m (4.8 cm) per motor rotation. Why is a relative encoder risky on an arm? It reads 0 at power-on wherever the arm is, so if the arm isn't in the expected starting position, every angle is wrong. Why is mixing inches and meters dangerous? The code runs without errors, but every number is off by about 39×, so the robot moves to completely wrong places. Your gyro heading goes down when the robot turns left. What's wrong? The gyro reports clockwise as positive, but WPILib expects counterclockwise positive. Flip the sign."},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"Why it matters","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#why-it-matters","t":"Robot time is scarce. Mechanical always needs the robot \"for just one more hour,\" and at competition you get a few minutes between matches. Good programmers find most of their bugs before the robot is available, and fix the rest quickly when it is. This lesson covers three skills: Seeing what your code is doing with dashboards and logs Finding bugs with a step-by-step process instead of guessing Simulating mechanisms so you can test without a robot"},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"Read this","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#read-this","t":"Robot Simulation Introduction Simulation GUI Physics Simulation with WPILib Glass , Elastic , and AdvantageScope On-Robot Telemetry Recording (DataLog)"},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"Walkthrough","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"Seeing what your code is doing","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#seeing-what-your-code-is-doing","t":"Your robot runs 50 loops a second, so you need a way to watch values change live. Don't use System.out.println for live values. Fifty lines a second floods the console, and you can't read any of them. Send values over NetworkTables. Each value updates in place on the dashboard. With the course's telemetry library from Lesson 6.1 : private final TelemetryTable telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Arm\" ); @Override public void periodic () { telemetry . log ( \"Angle (deg)\" , getAngleDegrees ()); telemetry . log ( \"Target (deg)\" , targetDegrees ); telemetry . log ( \"Output (V)\" , lastVoltage ); telemetry . log ( \"At Target\" , atTarget ()); } A subsystem's periodic() runs every loop, which makes it the right place for telemetry. Name values like folders Telemetry.getTable(\"Arm\") groups every arm value in an Arm folder on the dashboard, and the unit in \"Angle (deg)\" means nobody has to guess. What to publish for any mechanism: Value Why The sensor reading (position, velocity) Is the sensor working and pointing the right way? The target (setpoint) Is the code asking for what you think? The output (percent or volts) Is the code actually commanding the motor? Any state booleans (at target, has piece, at limit) Do your conditions flip when they should? With those four, you can usually tell in seconds whether a problem is the sensor, the logic, or the hardware."},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"Dashboards","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#dashboards","t":"All of these read the same NetworkTables data, so you can switch freely: Tool Best for Sim GUI Quick checks during simulation Glass Plotting values live, viewing field and mechanism widgets Elastic The driver dashboard, and this course's everyday dashboard ( Lesson 6.1 ) AdvantageScope Graphs, 3D robot views, and replaying log files after a match SmartDashboard and Shuffleboard are removed in WPILib 2027, so this course doesn't use them. Seeing commands on the dashboard (Sendables still use SmartDashboard.putData in 2026): SmartDashboard . putData ( CommandScheduler . getInstance ()); // what's running right now SmartDashboard . putData ( intake ); // which command owns the intake"},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"Logging: what happened during the match?","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#logging-what-happened-during-the-match","t":"NetworkTables shows what's happening now . Logs record everything , so you can look back after a match: // In Robot's constructor: DataLogManager . start (); // record NetworkTables and console output DriverStation . startDataLog ( DataLogManager . getLog ()); // also record joystick and mode data Logs are saved on the roboRIO (or a USB stick plugged into it). Download them with DataLogTool and open them in AdvantageScope to scrub through a match like a video, with every value graphed. When the robot did something weird in a match Don't guess. Pull the log, find the moment, and look at the target, the sensor, and the output side by side. The log also shows battery voltage and brownouts."},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"A debugging process","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#a-debugging-process","t":"When something doesn't work, resist changing code at random. Work through the layers in order, from the most basic to the most specific: Is the code running? Check the Driver Station: does it show robot code (green \"Robot Code\" light)? Any errors in the console? Is the robot enabled, in the mode you think? Is the command scheduled? Check the scheduler on the dashboard. Did the button binding fire? Is the code commanding output? Is the \"Output\" value on the dashboard what you expect? Is the output reaching the motor? Check the motor controller's status light and its vendor tool. Is it electrical? CAN IDs, breakers, unplugged wires ( Lesson 4 ). Is it mechanical? Jammed, inverted, belt slipping, a set screw loose. The first layer where something looks wrong is where the bug is. Change one thing at a time If you change three things and it starts working, you don't know which one fixed it, and the other two might have caused a new bug you won't see until competition. Other habits that help: Reproduce it first. If you can't make the bug happen on purpose, you can't confirm you fixed it. Read the whole error. The first line of a stack trace says what happened; the first line in frc.robot says where ( Lesson 3 ). Explain it out loud. Walk a teammate (or a rubber duck) through what the code does, line by line. You'll often spot the bug mid-sentence. Check Git. If it worked yesterday, git diff or git log shows exactly what changed since then."},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"Common bugs by symptom","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#common-bugs-by-symptom","t":"Symptom Check first Nothing works at all Robot code light on the Driver Station, console errors One mechanism doesn't move Is its command running? Is the output nonzero? CAN ID and breaker. Mechanism moves the wrong way Motor inversion, or sensor direction Mechanism moves the right way, then runs away Sensor direction doesn't match motor direction, so feedback pushes the wrong way Works in sim, not on the robot Wrong ports or CAN IDs, unset vendor config, inversion Works sometimes Loose wire, low battery, or a race between two commands Robot stutters Loop overruns (Lesson 6) or brownouts (Lesson 4)"},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"Simulation: testing without a robot","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#simulation-testing-without-a-robot","t":"WPILib's simulation runs your real robot code on your laptop. By default, motors and sensors are fake but don't move : setting a motor to 0.5 shows 0.5 in the Sim GUI, but the encoder stays at 0. Physics simulation fixes that. WPILib includes models of common mechanisms that calculate how they'd move: Class Simulates DCMotorSim A motor spinning a load: flywheels, rollers FlywheelSim A flywheel ElevatorSim An elevator, with gravity SingleJointedArmSim An arm on one pivot, with gravity DifferentialDrivetrainSim A tank drivetrain You feed the model the motor's voltage, step it forward 20 ms, and write its position back into the simulated encoder: private final SingleJointedArmSim armSim = new SingleJointedArmSim ( DCMotor . getNEO ( 1 ), // one NEO motor 60.0 , // gear ratio SingleJointedArmSim . estimateMOI ( 0.6 , 4.0 ), // arm length (m), mass (kg) 0.6 , // arm length (m) Units . degreesToRadians ( - 90 ), // min angle Units . degreesToRadians ( 90 ), // max angle true , // simulate gravity Units . degreesToRadians ( - 90 ) // starting angle ); private final EncoderSim encoderSim = new EncoderSim ( armEncoder ); @Override public void simulationPeriodic () { armSim . setInputVoltage ( motor . get () * RobotController . getBatteryVoltage ()); armSim . update ( 0.020 ); // step 20 ms encoderSim . setDistance ( Units . radiansToDegrees ( armSim . getAngleRads ())); } simulationPeriodic() runs every loop, but only in simulation. It never runs on the real robot. Start from an example Ctrl + Shift + P → WPILib: Create a new project → Example , then pick ArmSimulation , ElevatorSimulation , or DriveDistanceOffboard . They're complete, working simulations to learn from and copy. Simulation isn't the real robot Sim models are idealized: no friction you didn't model, no loose belts, perfect sensors. Use sim to catch logic bugs and get gains roughly right, then confirm on the real robot."},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"Visualizing mechanisms","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#visualizing-mechanisms","t":"Mechanism2d draws a simple stick-figure of your arm or elevator on the dashboard, so you can see it move in simulation: private final Mechanism2d mech = new Mechanism2d ( 3 , 3 ); private final MechanismLigament2d armLigament = mech . getRoot ( \"pivot\" , 1.5 , 1.5 ). append ( new MechanismLigament2d ( \"arm\" , 1.0 , 0 )); public Arm () { SmartDashboard . putData ( \"Arm/Mechanism\" , mech ); } @Override public void periodic () { armLigament . setAngle ( getAngleDegrees ()); }"},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"Try it","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#try-it","t":"Add the four standard values (sensor, target, output, state) for a subsystem with Telemetry , and watch them in Elastic (IP mode localhost in simulation) Add SmartDashboard.putData(CommandScheduler.getInstance()) and watch commands start and stop as you press buttons Open the ArmSimulation example, run it, and move the arm with the joystick. Find its simulationPeriodic() . Add DataLogManager.start() , run in sim for a minute, then open the log in AdvantageScope Break your intake on purpose (wrong PWM port), then walk the debugging process to find it. Which layer revealed it?"},{"p":"9. Testing, Debugging & Simulation","h":"Check yourself","u":"robot-code/09-debugging.html#check-yourself","t":"Why send values over NetworkTables instead of using println ? It updates one value in place instead of printing 50 lines per second. What four values should you publish for any mechanism? The sensor reading, the target, the motor output, and any state booleans like \"at target.\" The dashboard shows a target of 90°, an output of 0 V, and the arm sitting still. Where's the bug? In the code between the target and the output: the command or controller isn't producing output. The sensor and hardware may be fine. What's the difference between NetworkTables and a log file? NetworkTables shows live values right now. A log records everything over time, so you can review a match afterward. What does simulationPeriodic() do, and when does it run? It updates physics models so simulated sensors respond to simulated motors. It runs every loop, only in simulation."},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Why it matters","u":"project/10-teleop.html#why-it-matters","t":"This is where everything so far comes together. Over the next two lessons you'll build a complete drivetrain in simulation: a Drivetrain subsystem with motors and encoders driver controls with deadband, curved response, and slow mode autonomous routines, first timed and then by distance (Lesson 11) The drivetrain is the most important mechanism on the robot. If the robot can't drive well, nothing else matters."},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Read this","u":"project/10-teleop.html#read-this","t":"Using the WPILib Drive Classes Joysticks Using Motor Controllers in Code"},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Walkthrough","u":"project/10-teleop.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Drivetrain types","u":"project/10-teleop.html#drivetrain-types","t":"Type How it moves Notes Tank / differential Left and right sides, like a tank. Turns by driving the sides at different speeds. Simple, strong, cheap. Can't move sideways. This lesson uses it. Swerve Each wheel steers and drives independently Moves in any direction while turning. Most competitive teams use it now, but it's much more complex. See Where to Go Next . Mecanum Special rollers on each wheel allow sideways motion Easier than swerve, but easy to push around Learn tank drive first. The ideas (subsystem structure, joystick processing, default commands) carry straight over to swerve."},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Arcade vs. tank controls","u":"project/10-teleop.html#arcade-vs-tank-controls","t":"There are two common ways to drive a differential drivetrain with a controller: Style Controls Feels like Arcade One stick forward/back, the other stick (or the same one) turns A video game. Most drivers prefer it. Tank Left stick drives the left side, right stick drives the right side Steering a tank. Precise, but takes practice. This lesson uses arcade. Try both and ask your drivers what they like."},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Step 1: Constants","u":"project/10-teleop.html#step-1-constants","t":"Put every port and number in Constants.java first: public final class Constants { public static final class DriveConstants { public static final int LEFT_MOTOR_PORT = 0 ; public static final int RIGHT_MOTOR_PORT = 1 ; public static final int [] LEFT_ENCODER_PORTS = { 0 , 1 }; public static final int [] RIGHT_ENCODER_PORTS = { 2 , 3 }; public static final double WHEEL_DIAMETER_METERS = 0.1524 ; // 6 in public static final double ENCODER_PULSES_PER_REV = 2048 ; public static final double METERS_PER_PULSE = Math . PI * WHEEL_DIAMETER_METERS / ENCODER_PULSES_PER_REV ; } public static final class OIConstants { public static final int DRIVER_PORT = 0 ; public static final double DEADBAND = 0.08 ; public static final double SLOW_MODE_SCALE = 0.4 ; } } This example puts the encoders on the wheel shafts, so there's no gear ratio in the conversion."},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Step 2: The Drivetrain subsystem","u":"project/10-teleop.html#step-2-the-drivetrain-subsystem","t":"package frc.robot.subsystems ; import java.util.function.DoubleSupplier ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.Encoder ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.drive.DifferentialDrive ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.motorcontrol.PWMSparkMax ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.Command ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; import frc.robot.telemetry.Telemetry ; import frc.robot.telemetry.TelemetryTable ; import frc.robot.Constants.DriveConstants ; public class Drivetrain extends SubsystemBase { private final PWMSparkMax left = new PWMSparkMax ( DriveConstants . LEFT_MOTOR_PORT ); private final PWMSparkMax right = new PWMSparkMax ( DriveConstants . RIGHT_MOTOR_PORT ); private final DifferentialDrive drive = new DifferentialDrive ( left , right ); private final TelemetryTable telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Drive\" ); // Lesson 6.1 private final Encoder leftEncoder = new Encoder ( DriveConstants . LEFT_ENCODER_PORTS [ 0 ] , DriveConstants . LEFT_ENCODER_PORTS [ 1 ] ); private final Encoder rightEncoder = new Encoder ( DriveConstants . RIGHT_ENCODER_PORTS [ 0 ] , DriveConstants . RIGHT_ENCODER_PORTS [ 1 ] ); public Drivetrain () { // The two sides face opposite directions, so one must be inverted // for \"forward\" to mean forward on both. right . setInverted ( true ); leftEncoder . setDistancePerPulse ( DriveConstants . METERS_PER_PULSE ); rightEncoder . setDistancePerPulse ( DriveConstants . METERS_PER_PULSE ); rightEncoder . setReverseDirection ( true ); } /** Average distance driven by both sides, in meters. */ public double getDistanceMeters () { return ( leftEncoder . getDistance () + rightEncoder . getDistance ()) / 2.0 ; } public void resetEncoders () { leftEncoder . reset (); rightEncoder . reset (); } /** Arcade drive: one value for forward/back, one for turning. */ public Command arcadeDrive ( DoubleSupplier forward , DoubleSupplier rotation ) { return run (() -> drive . arcadeDrive ( forward . getAsDouble (), rotation . getAsDouble ())) . withName ( \"Arcade Drive\" ); } @Override public void periodic () { telemetry . log ( \"Left (m)\" , leftEncoder . getDistance ()); telemetry . log ( \"Right (m)\" , rightEncoder . getDistance ()); telemetry . log ( \"Left Output\" , left . get ()); telemetry . log ( \"Right Output\" , right . get ()); } } Why DoubleSupplier instead of double ? A double would be read once , when the command is created at robot startup, and the robot would drive at that one value forever. A DoubleSupplier is a lambda that reads the joystick every loop . This is the lambda idea from Lesson 3 paying off."},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"What DifferentialDrive does for you","u":"project/10-teleop.html#what-differentialdrive-does-for-you","t":"arcadeDrive(forward, rotation) turns two inputs into left and right motor outputs. Roughly: left = forward − rotation right = forward + rotation A positive rotation is counterclockwise (turning left), so it slows the left side and speeds up the right. …then scales them so neither goes past ±1. It also includes motor safety : if your code stops calling it (because something blocked the loop), it stops the motors after a short timeout instead of driving off at the last speed. By default, arcadeDrive also squares the inputs , which gives finer control at low speed. More on that below."},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Step 3: Make it the default command","u":"project/10-teleop.html#step-3-make-it-the-default-command","t":"In RobotContainer.java : private final Drivetrain drivetrain = new Drivetrain (); private final CommandXboxController driver = new CommandXboxController ( OIConstants . DRIVER_PORT ); public RobotContainer () { drivetrain . setDefaultCommand ( drivetrain . arcadeDrive ( () -> - driver . getLeftY (), // forward is negative Y on Xbox sticks () -> - driver . getRightX () // positive rotation = counterclockwise = left ) ); configureBindings (); } A default command runs whenever nothing else is using the subsystem, so the robot always responds to the sticks. When an autonomous command or an \"auto-align\" button takes over the drivetrain, the default command pauses, then comes back when that command ends. ( Lesson 7.1 explains how.) Why the minus signs? Xbox controllers report pushing a stick forward as negative Y, and pushing it right as positive X. WPILib's convention is forward-positive and counterclockwise (left)-positive. Negating both makes forward drive forward and left turn left."},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Step 4: Clean up the joystick input","u":"project/10-teleop.html#step-4-clean-up-the-joystick-input","t":"Raw joystick values have two problems drivers notice right away. Problem 1: stick drift. A stick resting in the middle rarely reads exactly 0. It reads something like 0.03, and the robot creeps. The fix is a deadband : treat anything close to 0 as 0. MathUtil . applyDeadband ( 0.03 , 0.08 ); // 0.0 (inside the deadband) MathUtil . applyDeadband ( 0.50 , 0.08 ); // about 0.46 (rescaled so it still reaches 1.0) Problem 2: too twitchy at low speed. With a straight-line response, small stick movements produce big speed changes, which makes lining up hard. Squaring the input (keeping its sign) gives fine control near the middle and full speed at the edge: Stick Linear output Squared output 0.2 0.20 0.04 0.5 0.50 0.25 0.8 0.80 0.64 1.0 1.00 1.00 DifferentialDrive.arcadeDrive squares inputs by default, so with it you only need the deadband. If you process inputs yourself, square them with Math.copySign(x * x, x) . Put the processing in one helper so both axes get the same treatment: private static double processAxis ( double raw ) { return MathUtil . applyDeadband ( raw , OIConstants . DEADBAND ); } drivetrain . setDefaultCommand ( drivetrain . arcadeDrive ( () -> - processAxis ( driver . getLeftY ()), () -> - processAxis ( driver . getRightX ()) ) );"},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Step 5: Slow mode","u":"project/10-teleop.html#step-5-slow-mode","t":"Drivers want precise control when lining up to score. A common pattern: hold a button to scale everything down. private double speedScale () { return driver . rightBumper (). getAsBoolean () ? OIConstants . SLOW_MODE_SCALE : 1.0 ; } drivetrain . setDefaultCommand ( drivetrain . arcadeDrive ( () -> - processAxis ( driver . getLeftY ()) * speedScale (), () -> - processAxis ( driver . getRightX ()) * speedScale () ) ); Because the suppliers run every loop, slow mode switches on and off the instant the bumper changes. No extra command needed."},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Step 6: Simulate it","u":"project/10-teleop.html#step-6-simulate-it","t":"Out of the box, the encoders won't move in simulation. To see the robot drive on a field, add a DifferentialDrivetrainSim and Field2d . The WPILib SimpleDifferentialDriveSimulation example has a full working version to copy. For now, check in the Sim GUI that: pushing the left stick forward moves both PWM outputs (the right one may show the opposite sign because it's inverted, which is correct: it's the same physical direction) pushing the right stick right makes left go up and right go down releasing the sticks returns both to exactly 0"},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Testing on a real robot","u":"project/10-teleop.html#testing-on-a-real-robot","t":"Put it on blocks first The first time new drive code runs, put the robot on blocks so the wheels are off the ground. Check that: Forward on the stick spins every wheel forward. Turning left spins the left side backward and the right side forward. Letting go stops everything. Only then put it on the floor, in open space, at low speed. If one side spins backward, fix the motor inversion , not the joystick sign."},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Try it","u":"project/10-teleop.html#try-it","t":"Have the West Coast Drive? Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive builds this same drivetrain on a real robot with SPARK MAXes. Build the Drivetrain subsystem and make arcadeDrive its default command Drive in Sim GUI with a controller or keyboard, and check the outputs against the list in Step 6 Add the deadband. Release the sticks and confirm the outputs are exactly 0. Add slow mode on the right bumper Add a tankDrive command and a toggleOnTrue button that switches between arcade and tank. Ask a driver which they prefer. Challenge: open the SimpleDifferentialDriveSimulation example and drive the simulated robot around a field"},{"p":"10. Teleop","h":"Check yourself","u":"project/10-teleop.html#check-yourself","t":"Why is one side's motor inverted? The motors on each side face opposite directions. Without inverting one, \"forward\" on both sides would spin the robot in place. Why negate getLeftY() ? Xbox controllers report pushing the stick forward as negative Y, and WPILib expects forward to be positive. What does a deadband fix? Sticks rarely read exactly 0 at rest. The deadband treats small values as 0, so the robot doesn't creep. Why does squaring the input help drivers? It makes small stick movements produce even smaller outputs, giving fine control at low speed while still reaching full speed at full stick. Why does arcadeDrive take DoubleSupplier s instead of double s? A double is read once when the command is created. A supplier is called every loop, so the robot follows the joystick as it moves. What happens to the default drive command when an autonomous command uses the drivetrain? It's interrupted while the autonomous command runs, then restarted automatically when that command ends."},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"Why it matters","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#why-it-matters","t":"The first 15 seconds of every match have no driver input . The robot runs entirely on code. Autonomous points often decide close matches, and a reliable auto is one of the things alliance captains look for when picking partners. In this lesson you'll build autonomous routines step by step: first a simple timed drive, then driving by measured distance, then multi-step routines, and finally a dashboard menu so the drive team can pick a routine before each match."},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"Read this","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#read-this","t":"Command Compositions Lesson 6.1: A dropdown menu with Selectable"},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"Walkthrough","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"How autonomous starts and stops","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#how-autonomous-starts-and-stops","t":"In the Command Robot template, Robot.java already handles this: When autonomous begins, autonomousInit() calls robotContainer.getAutonomousCommand() and schedules whatever it returns. The scheduler runs that command every loop. When teleop begins, teleopInit() cancels it, in case it's still running. So your whole job is to make getAutonomousCommand() return the right command. That command is usually built from smaller commands you already have."},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"Building routines from small commands","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#building-routines-from-small-commands","t":"Command compositions chain commands together like sentences: Composition Meaning Ends when a.andThen(b) Run a , then b b ends a.alongWith(b) Run both at the same time both end a.raceWith(b) Run both at the same time either ends (the other is canceled) a.deadlineFor(b) Run both at the same time a ends ( b is canceled) a.withTimeout(2.0) Run a , but give up after 2 seconds a ends or time runs out a.until(condition) Run a until condition is true a ends or the condition is true Compositions let several things happen at once, then wait for all of them before moving on: Moving the wrist and elevator in parallel, then scoring once both are done. From the WPILib docs source, concurrent-commands.drawio.svg (CC BY 4.0). The Commands class has matching helpers for longer lists: Commands . sequence ( a , b , c ) // a, then b, then c Commands . parallel ( a , b ) // all at once, until all end Commands . race ( a , b ) // all at once, until any ends Commands . deadline ( a , b , c ) // all at once, until the first one (a) ends Commands . waitSeconds ( 1.0 ) // pause Parallel commands can't share a subsystem drivetrain.arcadeDrive(...).alongWith(drivetrain.driveDistance(...)) is an error: both require the drivetrain. Parallel commands must use different subsystems, like driving while running the intake."},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"Version 1: a timed auto","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#version-1-a-timed-auto","t":"The simplest auto drives at a fixed speed for a fixed time. Add to Drivetrain.java : public Command driveForSeconds ( double speed , double seconds ) { return run (() -> drive . arcadeDrive ( speed , 0 , false )) // false = don't square the input . withTimeout ( seconds ) . finallyDo (() -> drive . stopMotor ()) . withName ( \"Drive \" + seconds + \"s\" ); } .finallyDo(...) runs when the command ends for any reason : finished, timed out, or canceled. Use it to make sure motors always stop. Then in RobotContainer.java : public Command getAutonomousCommand () { return drivetrain . driveForSeconds ( 0.5 , 2.0 ); } Run it in the Sim GUI: select Autonomous , then enable. Timed autos are unreliable The same motor output moves the robot different distances depending on battery charge, wheel wear, carpet, and robot weight. A timed auto that's perfect on a fresh battery comes up short on a tired one. Use it to get started, then switch to distance."},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"Version 2: drive by distance","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#version-2-drive-by-distance","t":"With encoders, the robot can drive until it has actually gone the right distance: public Command driveDistance ( double meters , double speed ) { return runOnce ( this :: resetEncoders ) . andThen ( run (() -> drive . arcadeDrive ( Math . copySign ( speed , meters ), 0 , false ))) . until (() -> Math . abs ( getDistanceMeters ()) >= Math . abs ( meters )) . finallyDo (() -> drive . stopMotor ()) . withName ( \"Drive \" + meters + \" m\" ); } Reading it line by line: Reset the encoders to 0, once. Then drive at speed , in the direction of meters (negative = backward). Stop as soon as the measured distance reaches the target. Always stop the motors at the end. This is much more consistent than a timer, but it still overshoots a little, because the robot is moving at full speed when it reaches the target and takes time to stop. In Lesson 12 you'll replace the fixed speed with a PID controller that slows down smoothly as it arrives. Add a safety timeout If an encoder is unplugged, the distance never increases and the robot drives forever. Add .withTimeout(5.0) to any distance command, so a broken sensor can't send the robot across the field."},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"Version 3: a multi-step routine","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#version-3-a-multi-step-routine","t":"Real autos combine several actions. Here's \"drive out, score, drive back,\" using the intake from Lesson 7 : public Command scoreAndBackUp () { return Commands . sequence ( drivetrain . driveDistance ( 1.5 , 0.5 ). withTimeout ( 4.0 ), // drive out intake . outtake (). withTimeout ( 0.5 ), // score Commands . waitSeconds ( 0.25 ), // let the piece clear drivetrain . driveDistance ( - 1.0 , 0.5 ). withTimeout ( 4.0 ) // back up ). withName ( \"Score and Back Up\" ); } And one that runs mechanisms at the same time, picking up a game piece while driving: public Command driveAndIntake () { return Commands . deadline ( drivetrain . driveDistance ( 2.0 , 0.4 ). withTimeout ( 5.0 ), // the deadline intake . runIntake () // runs until driving ends ). withName ( \"Drive and Intake\" ); } deadline ends when the drive finishes, and cancels the intake at the same moment. Write autos as named methods Build each routine in its own method, like scoreAndBackUp() . Each call makes a fresh command object. Reusing one command object in two routines causes an error, because a command can only belong to one composition."},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"Let the drive team choose: Selectable","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#let-the-drive-team-choose-selectable","t":"Different match strategies need different autos. A Selectable ( Lesson 6.1 ) puts a dropdown menu in Elastic: private final Selectable < Command > autoChooser = new Selectable <> (); public RobotContainer () { // ...subsystems and default commands... autoChooser . addDefault ( \"Do nothing\" , Commands . none ()); autoChooser . add ( \"Drive forward 2 m\" , drivetrain . driveDistance ( 2.0 , 0.5 )); autoChooser . add ( \"Score and back up\" , scoreAndBackUp ()); autoChooser . add ( \"Drive and intake\" , driveAndIntake ()); Tunables . publish ( \"Auto\" , autoChooser ); // shows up as /Tunables/Auto configureBindings (); } public Command getAutonomousCommand () { return autoChooser . getSelected (); } The drive team picks a routine in Elastic ( Tunables → Auto , shown as a ComboBox Chooser ) before the match. Make \"Do nothing\" the default, so a robot whose drive team forgot to choose sits still instead of running a routine meant for a different starting spot."},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"Testing autos","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#testing-autos","t":"In simulation first. Watch the dashboard distance and command names. Does each step start and end when you expect? On blocks. Wheels off the ground, check every step runs in order and the motors stop at the end. On the floor , in open space, with a person ready to disable. Mark the starting position with tape so every test starts the same way. Test with a partly drained battery too. That's what you'll have in some matches."},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"Try it","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#try-it","t":"Have the West Coast Drive? Lab 7: Timed Autonomous builds a timed auto with a chooser on the real robot. Add driveForSeconds and run it in simulation (select Autonomous , then enable) Add driveDistance and confirm on the dashboard that it stops near the target distance Build scoreAndBackUp() and watch each step on the scheduler dashboard Add a Selectable with at least three options, with \"Do nothing\" as the default Build a routine that uses deadline to run the intake while driving Challenge: write turnDegrees(double degrees) using the gyro from Lesson 8 , and use it to drive a square"},{"p":"11. Autonomous","h":"Check yourself","u":"project/11-autonomous.html#check-yourself","t":"What's the difference between andThen and alongWith ? andThen runs commands in sequence . alongWith runs them at the same time , ending when both are done. What's the difference between race and deadline ? A race ends when any of its commands ends. A deadline ends when one specific command (the first one) ends. Why are timed autos unreliable? The same motor output moves the robot different distances depending on battery charge, wheel wear, and carpet. Why add a timeout to a distance-based command? If the encoder fails, the distance never reaches the target, and without a timeout the robot would drive forever. Why should 'Do nothing' be the default auto? If the drive team forgets to pick, the robot stays still instead of running a routine meant for a different starting position, which could hit another robot or a field element."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Why it matters","u":"controls/12-pid.html#why-it-matters","t":"\"Set the motor to 0.3 and hope\" doesn't work. Battery voltage changes during a match, mechanisms wear, game pieces add weight, and carpet adds friction. A fixed motor output gets a different result every time. Feedback control measures where the mechanism actually is, compares it to where you want it, and corrects automatically, 50 times a second. PID is the most common feedback controller in FRC, and in robotics generally. Once you understand it, you can make arms hold their angle, flywheels hold their speed, and drivetrains stop exactly where you tell them."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Read this, then come back","u":"controls/12-pid.html#read-this-then-come-back","t":"Introduction to Control Theory Introduction to PID PID Control in WPILib Tuning a Flywheel Velocity Controller and Tuning a Vertical Arm Position Controller Use the interactive simulators The WPILib tuning pages above include simulators right in the browser, for a flywheel, turret, and arm. Move the sliders and watch what happens. They're the fastest way to build intuition, and you'll use them in the exercises."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Walkthrough","u":"controls/12-pid.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Open loop vs. closed loop","u":"controls/12-pid.html#open-loop-vs-closed-loop","t":"Open loop: \"Run the motor at 30%.\" The code never checks the result. Closed loop: \"Get the arm to 90°.\" Measure, compare, correct, repeat every 20 ms. flowchart LR S[Setpoint<br/>where you want to be] --> E((error)) E --> C[PID controller] --> M[Motor] --> Mech[Mechanism] Mech --> Sen[Sensor] Sen -- measurement<br/>where you are --> E The loop is closed because the sensor's measurement feeds back into the next decision. The WPILib docs draw it like this, using the standard control-theory names: A closed loop. The **plant** is the mechanism being controlled. From the WPILib docs, Control System Basics (CC BY 4.0). Symbol Name in the docs In this course r(t) reference, setpoint setpoint (where you want to be) e(t) error error u(t) control input, control effort output (what you send the motor) y(t) output, process variable measurement (where you are) Two names for the same thing The docs call the motor command the control input and the measured value the output . This course says output for the motor command and measurement for the sensor value. Same ideas, different words."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"The vocabulary","u":"controls/12-pid.html#the-vocabulary","t":"Term Meaning Arm example Setpoint (reference) Where you want to be 90° Measurement (process variable) Where you are, from a sensor 60° Error Setpoint − measurement 30° Output (control effort) What you send to the motor 0.4, or 4.8 V Gain A tuning number that scales one part of the output kP = 0.02"},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Error is everything","u":"controls/12-pid.html#error-is-everything","t":"error = setpoint − measurement Positive error: you're short of the target, so push forward. Negative error: you're past it, so push back. Zero error: you're there. The controller's whole job is to drive the error to zero and keep it there."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Build PID one letter at a time","u":"controls/12-pid.html#build-pid-one-letter-at-a-time","t":"P: Proportional. \"Push harder the farther away you are.\" output = kP × error With kP = 0.02 and the arm 30° short, the output is 0.02 × 30 = 0.6 . At 5° short, it's 0.1. As the arm approaches the target, the push gets gentler. kP What happens Too small Slow and sluggish. May stop short, because the push gets too weak to overcome friction. About right Moves quickly and settles close to the target Too big Overshoots, swings back, overshoots again: oscillation A P controller: the output is K p times the error. From the WPILib docs, Introduction to PID (CC BY 4.0). From the WPILib docs: P is a software spring Proportional gains act like software-defined springs that pull the system toward the setpoint. A spring pushes harder the more you stretch it. P pushes harder the farther the mechanism is from the setpoint, in exactly the same way. Adapted from Introduction to PID , CC BY 4.0. P alone has a classic flaw: steady-state error . Near the target, the error is small, so the output is small, and a small output may not beat friction or gravity. The mechanism stops a little short and stays there. D: Derivative. \"Slow down as you approach.\" output += kD × (how fast the error is changing) The derivative is the rate of change of the error. When the mechanism is rushing toward the target, the error is shrinking fast, and D pushes against that motion. It acts like a shock absorber. A PD controller adds a second term based on how fast the error is changing. From the WPILib docs, Introduction to PID (CC BY 4.0). From the WPILib docs: D is a software damper When the setpoint isn't moving, the D term slows the system down whenever it's moving. It acts like a software-defined damper , like the door closers that stop a door from slamming. Their pushback grows the faster the door moves. Adapted from Introduction to PID , CC BY 4.0. With D, you can use a higher kP for a faster response without as much overshoot. Too much D makes the mechanism sluggish, and because it reacts to change, it amplifies sensor noise into jittery output. I: Integral. \"Fix the leftover error.\" output += kI × (error added up over time) The integral keeps a running total of the error. If the mechanism sits a little short of the target for a while, that total grows until the output is big enough to finish the job. That's how I removes steady-state error. Steady-state error: P control alone leaves a gap below the setpoint. From the WPILib docs, Introduction to PID (CC BY 4.0). Left: adding I removes the gap. Right: too much I causes overshoot. From the WPILib docs, Introduction to PID (CC BY 4.0). From the WPILib docs: What WPILib says about I Integral gain is generally not recommended for FRC use. It's almost always better to use a feedforward controller to eliminate steady-state error. If you do use integral gain, it's crucial to protect against integral windup . Adapted from Introduction to PID , CC BY 4.0. Use I sparingly: integral windup If the mechanism is stuck (against a hard stop, or held by a person, or still far from the target), the integral keeps growing the whole time. When the mechanism is finally free, that huge stored value sends it flying past the target. This is integral windup . Most FRC mechanisms don't need I at all once you add feedforward ( Lesson 13 ), which handles gravity and friction directly. If you do use I, limit it with setIZone() and setIntegratorRange() . All three together output = kP × error + kI × ∫error + kD × d(error)/dt (present) (past) (future trend)"},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Watching the response","u":"controls/12-pid.html#watching-the-response","t":"When you tune, plot the setpoint and measurement together on a graph. The three response types. From the WPILib docs, Introduction to PID (CC BY 4.0). From the WPILib docs: Response types An underdamped response oscillates around the setpoint before settling. An overdamped response is slow to rise and doesn't overshoot. A critically damped response has the fastest rise time without overshooting. Rise time is how long the system takes to first reach the setpoint. Settling time is how long it takes to settle there. Adapted from Introduction to PID , CC BY 4.0. These shapes tell you what to change: What you see Name Usual fix Rises slowly, stops short Sluggish Raise kP Shoots past, swings back once, settles Overshoot Raise kD, or lower kP a little Keeps swinging back and forth Oscillation Lower kP, raise kD Settles a little off the target Steady-state error Add feedforward (Lesson 13), or a small kI Output buzzes or jitters Noise Lower kD"},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"The PIDController class","u":"controls/12-pid.html#the-pidcontroller-class","t":"WPILib does the math for you: import edu.wpi.first.math.controller.PIDController ; PIDController pid = new PIDController ( kP , kI , kD ); // Every loop: double output = pid . calculate ( measurement , setpoint ); Useful extras: Method What it does setTolerance(position) How close counts as \"there\" setTolerance(position, velocity) …and how slow it must be moving atSetpoint() true when within tolerance enableContinuousInput(-180, 180) For things that wrap around, like a turret or swerve module, so it takes the short way around reset() Clears the stored integral and previous error. Call it when starting a new move. setIZone(range) Only build up the integral when the error is within this range The controller assumes a 20 ms loop PIDController assumes calculate() is called every 20 ms. Call it exactly once per loop , from a command's execute() or a run() factory. Calling it twice per loop, or only sometimes, breaks the D and I terms."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Code it: an arm that goes to an angle","u":"controls/12-pid.html#code-it-an-arm-that-goes-to-an-angle","t":"import edu.wpi.first.math.MathUtil ; import edu.wpi.first.math.controller.PIDController ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.Encoder ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.motorcontrol.PWMSparkMax ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.Command ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; import frc.robot.telemetry.Telemetry ; import frc.robot.telemetry.TelemetryTable ; public class Arm extends SubsystemBase { private final PWMSparkMax motor = new PWMSparkMax ( 1 ); private final Encoder encoder = new Encoder ( 0 , 1 ); private final PIDController pid = new PIDController ( 0.05 , 0.0 , 0.002 ); private final TelemetryTable telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Arm\" ); // Lesson 6.1 public Arm () { encoder . setDistancePerPulse ( ArmConstants . DEGREES_PER_PULSE ); pid . setTolerance ( 2.0 ); // within 2° counts as \"there\" } public double getAngleDegrees () { return encoder . getDistance (); } /** Moves to the angle and ends once it's there. */ public Command goTo ( double degrees ) { return runOnce ( pid :: reset ) . andThen ( run (() -> { double output = pid . calculate ( getAngleDegrees (), degrees ); motor . set ( MathUtil . clamp ( output , - 0.5 , 0.5 )); // limit power while tuning })) . until ( pid :: atSetpoint ) . withName ( \"Arm to \" + degrees ); } /** Moves to the angle and keeps holding it until interrupted. */ public Command hold ( double degrees ) { return runOnce ( pid :: reset ) . andThen ( run (() -> motor . set ( pid . calculate ( getAngleDegrees (), degrees )))) . withName ( \"Hold \" + degrees ); } @Override public void periodic () { telemetry . log ( \"Angle\" , getAngleDegrees ()); telemetry . log ( \"Setpoint\" , pid . getSetpoint ()); telemetry . log ( \"Error\" , pid . getError ()); telemetry . log ( \"Output\" , motor . get ()); } } goTo stops pushing when it ends When goTo ends, nothing is holding the arm up, and gravity pulls it down. For arms and elevators, use a hold -style command (or a default command that holds the last position) instead of one that ends."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Units of the gains","u":"controls/12-pid.html#units-of-the-gains","t":"The gains depend on your units. With degrees in and motor percent out, kP = 0.02 means \"2% output per degree of error.\" Measure in radians instead and the same behavior needs kP ≈ 1.15. If you change units, you must retune. That's one more reason to pick units once and keep them ( Lesson 8 )."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"A tuning recipe","u":"controls/12-pid.html#a-tuning-recipe","t":"Check the sensor first. Move the mechanism by hand. Does the measurement go up when the motor would push it positive? If the sensor and motor disagree on direction, PID will push the wrong way and run away. Set kI = 0 and kD = 0. Start with a small kP. Raise it (doubling works well) until the mechanism oscillates around the target. Back kP off by about half. Raise kD in small steps until the overshoot is gone, or it starts to jitter. If it still settles short, add feedforward (Lesson 13) before reaching for kI. Write down the gains that work, and put them in Constants.java . Safety Tune with the robot on blocks or with a low output limit, like the clamp(..., -0.5, 0.5) above, and keep a hand near the disable key. A badly tuned arm can slam into its hard stops hard enough to break something."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Tuning live from the dashboard","u":"controls/12-pid.html#tuning-live-from-the-dashboard","t":"Rebuilding and redeploying for every gain change is slow. Make the gains tunable with Tunables ( Lesson 6.1 ), and copy them into the controller every loop: private final TunableDouble kP = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Arm/kP\" , 0.05 ); private final TunableDouble kI = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Arm/kI\" , 0.0 ); private final TunableDouble kD = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Arm/kD\" , 0.002 ); @Override public void periodic () { pid . setPID ( kP . get (), kI . get (), kD . get ()); // ...the telemetry.log lines from above... } Change kP, kI, and kD in Elastic (as Text Display widgets) and watch the response immediately. When you're happy, copy the numbers into Constants.java . Dashboard edits are lost when the robot restarts."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"On-controller PID","u":"controls/12-pid.html#on-controller-pid","t":"Modern motor controllers (TalonFX, SPARK MAX, SPARK Flex) can run PID on the controller itself , at 1000 Hz instead of 50 Hz, using the motor's built-in encoder. Many teams use this for flywheels and arms. The ideas and tuning process are the same; only where the math runs is different. Learn it with WPILib's PIDController first, then read your vendor's docs."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Try it","u":"controls/12-pid.html#try-it","t":"Have the test bed? Do Lab 9: PID on the Wheel , then Lab 10: Elevator Position Control . They're this whole lesson on real hardware."},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Tune a turret, right here","u":"controls/12-pid.html#tune-a-turret-right-here","t":"This is WPILib's turret simulator, running PID only . Click a spot around the turret to set a new angle, or click and drag the pointer to make the setpoint move smoothly. Interactive simulator from the WPILib docs, Tuning a Turret Position Controller (CC BY 4.0). Set kP, kI, kD, and kV to zero. Raise kP until the turret moves sharply to each new angle. If it oscillates too much, lower kP until it stops. Raise kD to reduce the \"lag\" when you drag the setpoint smoothly. If it starts to oscillate, lower kD. Turn on System Noise . Does your tuning still work? Try the arm tuning simulator with PID only . Notice how hard it is to hold the arm level, and how it sags at some angles. Write down your best kP and kD. You'll compare in the next lesson. In the ArmSimulation example project, replace its controller with your goTo and hold commands, bind them to two buttons, and tune them with the dashboard Graph Arm/Angle and Arm/Setpoint together in one Elastic Graph widget while you tune"},{"p":"12. PID Feedback","h":"Check yourself","u":"controls/12-pid.html#check-yourself","t":"In your own words, what do P, I, and D each do? P pushes in proportion to how far off you are. D resists fast changes to reduce overshoot. I accumulates small leftover error over time and pushes harder the longer it lasts. The arm is 20° short and kP = 0.03. What's the P term's output? 0.03 × 20 = 0.6 (60% output). Why is a very high kP bad? The mechanism overshoots, swings back, and overshoots again: it oscillates and may never settle. Why does P alone often stop a little short of the target? Near the target, the error is tiny, so the output is tiny, and it can't overcome friction or gravity. That leftover is steady-state error. What is integral windup, and how do you avoid it? The integral keeps growing while the mechanism is stuck or far away, then causes a big overshoot once it's free. Avoid it by using feedforward instead of I, or by limiting the integral with setIZone() . PID makes your arm run away faster and faster instead of stopping. What's the most likely cause? The sensor direction doesn't match the motor direction, so every correction pushes the arm further from the target. Fix the inversion."},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Why it matters","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#why-it-matters","t":"PID only reacts after something goes wrong: it needs error before it does anything. That means a PID-only arm always sags a little below its target (the error has to exist to make the output that holds it up), and a PID-only flywheel always runs a little slow. Feedforward predicts what the motor needs before there's any error, using a simple model of the mechanism. Most good FRC control is mostly feedforward, with a little PID for cleanup . Add feedforward and your PID gains get smaller, your mechanisms get faster and smoother, and tuning gets easier. Analogy: driving up a hill Feedforward is pressing the gas because you see the hill coming . PID is pressing harder after you notice you're slowing down . A good driver does both: most of the pedal from looking ahead, a little correction from feeling the speed."},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Read this","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#read-this","t":"Introduction to DC Motor Feedforward Feedforward Control in WPILib Introduction to System Identification"},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Walkthrough","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Feedforward vs. feedback","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#feedforward-vs-feedback","t":"Feedforward Feedback (PID) Uses A model of the mechanism + the target The error from a sensor Acts Before error appears After error appears Good at The predictable part: gravity, friction, the voltage to hold a speed The unpredictable part: bumps, game pieces, model mistakes Needs a sensor? No Yes On its own Close, but drifts Works, but lags and sags Used together, the output is simply added: volts = feedforward(target) + PID(measurement, target) flowchart LR T[Target] --> FF[Feedforward] T --> E((error)) FF --> Sum((+)) E --> PID --> Sum Sum --> M[Motor] --> Mech[Mechanism] --> S[Sensor] S --> E"},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Where the voltage goes: the motor model","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#where-the-voltage-goes-the-motor-model","t":"A DC motor's voltage goes to three jobs, plus a fourth for mechanisms that lift something: Term Name What the voltage is for Unit kS Static Overcoming friction, just to start moving volts kV Velocity Holding a speed (faster needs more voltage) volts per (unit/second) kA Acceleration Speeding up (accelerating takes extra) volts per (unit/second²) kG Gravity Holding the mechanism up against gravity volts Flywheel or drivetrain: volts = kS·sign(v) + kV·v + kA·a sign(v) is +1 when moving forward and −1 when moving backward, because friction always opposes motion. Elevator (gravity is the same at every height): volts = kS·sign(v) + kG + kV·v + kA·a Arm (gravity's pull depends on the angle): volts = kS·sign(v) + kG·cos(θ) + kV·v + kA·a"},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Why cos(θ) for an arm","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#why-cos-for-an-arm","t":"Gravity pulls straight down. How much of that pull tries to rotate the arm depends on its angle: Arm angle (θ) cos(θ) Gravity's effect 0° (horizontal) 1.0 Strongest. The full weight acts as torque. 45° 0.71 About 71% 90° (straight up) 0.0 None. The weight is directly over the pivot. −90° (straight down) 0.0 None. The weight hangs from the pivot. So kG is the voltage needed to hold the arm level , and kG·cos(θ) gives the right amount at every other angle."},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"A worked example","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#a-worked-example","t":"A flywheel has kS = 0.2 V and kV = 0.12 V per (rotation/second). To hold 50 rotations/second (3000 RPM): volts = 0.2 + 0.12 × 50 = 6.2 V Without any feedback at all, sending 6.2 V gets the flywheel close to 50 rot/s. PID then only has to correct small differences, like when a game piece passes through and slows the wheel."},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Why volts instead of percent?","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#why-volts-instead-of-percent","t":"Battery voltage sags during a match. 50% output at 12.5 V is 6.25 V to the motor; 50% at 10.5 V is only 5.25 V. Same command, different result. Commanding volts with setVoltage() compensates for this: the motor controller adjusts the percent to deliver the voltage you asked for. Feedforward gains are measured in volts, so always use setVoltage() with feedforward. motor . setVoltage ( 6.2 ); // ✅ 6.2 V, whatever the battery reads motor . set ( 0.5 ); // ❌ changes as the battery sags"},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"The WPILib classes","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#the-wpilib-classes","t":"Mechanism Class Constructor calculate(...) takes Flywheel, roller, drivetrain side SimpleMotorFeedforward (kS, kV, kA) velocity Elevator ElevatorFeedforward (kS, kG, kV, kA) velocity Arm ArmFeedforward (kS, kG, kV, kA) angle (radians), velocity"},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Code it: a flywheel with feedforward + PID","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#code-it-a-flywheel-with-feedforward-pid","t":"private final SimpleMotorFeedforward ff = new SimpleMotorFeedforward ( kS , kV ); private final PIDController pid = new PIDController ( kP , 0 , 0 ); public Command spinAt ( double targetRotPerSec ) { return run (() -> { double ffVolts = ff . calculate ( targetRotPerSec ); double pidVolts = pid . calculate ( getVelocityRotPerSec (), targetRotPerSec ); motor . setVoltage ( ffVolts + pidVolts ); }). withName ( \"Shooter \" + targetRotPerSec ); } Because feedforward does most of the work, kP here is small and only cleans up."},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Code it: an arm with feedforward + PID","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#code-it-an-arm-with-feedforward-pid","t":"import edu.wpi.first.math.controller.ArmFeedforward ; private final ArmFeedforward ff = new ArmFeedforward ( kS , kG , kV ); private final PIDController pid = new PIDController ( kP , 0 , kD ); public Command holdAt ( double targetRadians ) { return run (() -> { double ffVolts = ff . calculate ( targetRadians , 0.0 ); // position, velocity double pidVolts = pid . calculate ( getAngleRadians (), targetRadians ); motor . setVoltage ( ffVolts + pidVolts ); }). withName ( \"Arm hold\" ); } Angle convention ArmFeedforward expects radians with 0 = horizontal and positive angles going up. If your encoder reads 0 when the arm hangs straight down, subtract π/2 before passing the angle in. Get this wrong and the gravity term pushes hardest at the wrong angle. Here the velocity passed to feedforward is 0, because holdAt jumps straight to the target. In Lesson 14 , a motion profile supplies a real velocity for every step of the move, and kV and kA start pulling their weight."},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Getting the numbers","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#getting-the-numbers","t":"Quick and dirty, by hand: kG (arm or elevator): slowly raise the voltage until the mechanism holds still without drifting. For an arm, do this with it horizontal. That voltage is roughly kG. kS : on a flywheel or roller with no gravity, raise the voltage slowly from 0 until it just starts to turn. That's roughly kS. kV : run at a few steady voltages, measure the speed each time, and calculate (volts − kS) ÷ speed. The right way: use SysId . It runs the mechanism through test motions and calculates kS, kV, kA, and kG from real data. Lesson 13.1 walks through it step by step. For motors you haven't built yet: WPILib's DCMotor models (like DCMotor.getKrakenX60(1) ) and online calculators like ReCalc can estimate kV and kA from the motor, gear ratio, and load."},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Tuning with feedforward","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#tuning-with-feedforward","t":"Set kP, kI, and kD to 0. Set the feedforward gains (from SysId, or by hand). Run the mechanism. It should get close to the target with no PID at all. Add a small kP to remove what's left. It'll be much smaller than your PID-only kP. Add kD only if it overshoots."},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Try it","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#try-it","t":"This is WPILib's vertical arm simulator, with feedforward and feedback together. Click a point around the arm's circle to set a new angle, or click and drag for smooth motion. Interactive simulator from the WPILib docs, Tuning a Vertical Arm Position Controller (CC BY 4.0). Set every PID gain to 0. Set kG until the arm holds still at horizontal. Move the setpoint to other angles. Does the arm stay close with feedforward alone? Add a small kP to remove the remaining error Compare with your PID-only gains from Lesson 12. How much smaller is kP now? How much better does it hold? In code: Calculate by hand the feedforward voltage for kS = 0.25, kV = 0.1, at 40 rot/s Add ArmFeedforward to your arm from Lesson 12, using setVoltage() In the ArmSimulation example, find kG by raising the voltage until the arm holds level"},{"p":"13. Feedforward","h":"Check yourself","u":"controls/13-feedforward.html#check-yourself","t":"What's the main difference between feedforward and feedback? Feedforward predicts the output from a model before error appears. Feedback (PID) reacts to error after it appears. Why does a PID-only arm sag a little below its target? PID only produces output when there's error. Holding the arm up takes output, so some error has to exist to produce it. Why does an arm use cos(θ) for gravity but an elevator doesn't? Gravity's torque on an arm depends on its angle: maximum when horizontal, zero when vertical. An elevator lifts the same weight at every height. kS = 0.3 V and kV = 0.15 V per (m/s). How many volts does feedforward give at 2 m/s? 0.3 + 0.15 × 2 = 0.6 V Why is setVoltage() better than set() with feedforward? Feedforward gains are in volts. setVoltage() delivers that voltage no matter how much the battery has sagged, so the same command produces the same motion all match."},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"Why it matters","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#why-it-matters","t":"In Lesson 13 you guessed kG by slowly raising the voltage. That works for one term, but kS, kV, and kA are almost impossible to guess well. SysId (System Identification) measures them for you. It drives your mechanism through a few controlled tests, records voltage, position, and velocity, and fits the feedforward equation to that real data. Good SysId numbers mean feedforward does most of the work, and PID only has to clean up small errors."},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"Read this","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#read-this","t":"Introduction to System Identification Creating an Identification Routine Running the Identification Routine Loading Data and Viewing Diagnostics"},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"Walkthrough","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"The big picture","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#the-big-picture","t":"flowchart LR A[1. Add a SysIdRoutine<br/>to your subsystem] --> B[2. Run the 4 tests<br/>on the real robot] B --> C[3. Pull the log file<br/>off the roboRIO] C --> D[4. Load it in the<br/>SysId tool] D --> E[5. Copy kS, kV, kA, kG<br/>into Constants] SysId needs a real robot Simulation already knows its own physics, so characterizing it tells you nothing new. Do Steps 1–2 in sim to make sure the code works, then run the real tests on the robot."},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"The four tests","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#the-four-tests","t":"SysId runs two kinds of tests, each in both directions: Test What the motor does What it measures best Quasistatic forward / reverse Voltage ramps up slowly (default 1 V per second) kS and kV : acceleration is near zero, so voltage is mostly fighting friction and speed Dynamic forward / reverse A sudden constant voltage step (default 7 V) kA : the mechanism accelerates hard, so the extra voltage goes into acceleration Running both directions matters because friction and gravity act differently going up vs. down. That's how SysId separates kS from kG."},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"Step 1: Add a SysIdRoutine to your subsystem","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#step-1-add-a-sysidroutine-to-your-subsystem","t":"This example characterizes an arm. The same pattern works for an elevator, flywheel, or drivetrain. import static edu.wpi.first.units.Units.* ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.RobotController ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.Command ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.sysid.SysIdRoutine ; public class Arm extends SubsystemBase { private final PWMSparkMax motor = new PWMSparkMax ( 1 ); private final Encoder encoder = new Encoder ( 0 , 1 ); private final SysIdRoutine sysId = new SysIdRoutine ( // Test settings. Defaults are 1 V/s ramp, 7 V step, 10 s timeout. // An arm needs a gentler step so it doesn't slam into its stops. new SysIdRoutine . Config ( Volts . of ( 1 ). per ( Second ), // quasistatic ramp rate Volts . of ( 4 ), // dynamic step voltage Seconds . of ( 5 ) // safety timeout per test ), new SysIdRoutine . Mechanism ( // How SysId drives the motor: voltage -> motor . setVoltage ( voltage . in ( Volts )), // What SysId records every loop: log -> log . motor ( \"arm\" ) . voltage ( Volts . of ( motor . get () * RobotController . getBatteryVoltage ())) . angularPosition ( Radians . of ( getAngleRadians ())) . angularVelocity ( RadiansPerSecond . of ( getVelocityRadPerSec ())), this ) ); public Command sysIdQuasistatic ( SysIdRoutine . Direction direction ) { return sysId . quasistatic ( direction ); } public Command sysIdDynamic ( SysIdRoutine . Direction direction ) { return sysId . dynamic ( direction ); } // getAngleRadians() and getVelocityRadPerSec() come from your encoder, // with 0 radians = arm horizontal (see Lesson 13). } Linear vs. angular For an elevator or drivetrain , log .linearPosition(Meters.of(...)) and .linearVelocity(MetersPerSecond.of(...)) instead. Use the real units your mechanism moves in. SysId's gains come out in those same units. Get the units right before testing SysId gains are only as good as the position and velocity you log. Double-check your conversion factor from Lesson 8 . If the logged angle is wrong, every gain will be wrong, and you'll have to redo all four tests."},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"Step 2: Bind the tests and run them","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#step-2-bind-the-tests-and-run-them","t":"In RobotContainer.java , put the tests on a separate controller (port 1) so a driver can't trigger them by accident: private final CommandXboxController sysIdController = new CommandXboxController ( 1 ); private void configureSysIdBindings () { sysIdController . a (). whileTrue ( arm . sysIdQuasistatic ( SysIdRoutine . Direction . kForward )); sysIdController . b (). whileTrue ( arm . sysIdQuasistatic ( SysIdRoutine . Direction . kReverse )); sysIdController . x (). whileTrue ( arm . sysIdDynamic ( SysIdRoutine . Direction . kForward )); sysIdController . y (). whileTrue ( arm . sysIdDynamic ( SysIdRoutine . Direction . kReverse )); } whileTrue is a safety feature: let go of the button and the motor stops. Also make sure WPILib's data log is running, so the test data gets saved to a file. In Robot.java 's constructor or robotInit() : DataLogManager . start (); Running the tests on the robot: Use a fully charged battery . A tired battery gives noisy data. Clear space. For an arm, start somewhere in the middle of its travel. For a drivetrain, put it on the floor with lots of room (not on blocks, since the wheels need real load). Enable in Teleop (or Test mode). Hold A (quasistatic forward). Let go before the mechanism hits a hard stop. Move the mechanism back to the start if needed, then hold B , X , and Y the same way. Disable the robot. Be ready to let go Dynamic tests jump straight to the step voltage. Hold the button with your thumb ready to lift, and keep everyone clear of the mechanism. For arms and elevators, consider adding .until(() -> atLimit()) to each test command as a backup. Longer is better, until you hit the stop Each test should run long enough to reach a fairly steady speed. A quasistatic test that lasts half a second doesn't have enough data. If your mechanism's travel is short, lower the ramp rate or step voltage so the test lasts longer before you have to stop."},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"Step 3: Get the log file","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#step-3-get-the-log-file","t":"WPILib logs are .wpilog files saved on the roboRIO (or a USB stick plugged into it). Connect to the robot. Open DataLogTool (installed with WPILib). Download the newest .wpilog file to your laptop. Using vendor logging instead CTRE's Phoenix 6 has its own SignalLogger that records .hoot files, and it can capture data faster and more precisely than WPILib's log. If your team uses it, follow CTRE's SysId guide and convert the .hoot file to .wpilog with Phoenix Tuner before Step 4. The analysis is identical."},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"Step 4: Analyze in the SysId tool","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#step-4-analyze-in-the-sysid-tool","t":"Open the SysId tool (in the WPILib tools folder, or Ctrl + Shift + P → WPILib: Start Tool → SysId ). Open your .wpilog file. In the Data Selector : Pick the test-state entry. It's named something like sysid-test-state-arm . Drag your logged position , velocity , and voltage entries into the matching slots. Set the units to match what you logged (radians for this arm). Click Load . In the Analyzer , set the mechanism type : Mechanism Choose Gains you get Flywheel, roller, turret Simple kS, kV, kA Drivetrain Simple (characterize left and right sides) kS, kV, kA Elevator Elevator kS, kV, kA, kG Arm Arm kS, kV, kA, kG Read off the feedforward gains ."},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"Step 5: Check the data before trusting it","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#step-5-check-the-data-before-trusting-it","t":"SysId always produces numbers, even from bad data. Before using them, look at the Diagnostics plots: The SysId diagnostic plots (highlighted in red). From the WPILib docs, Viewing Diagnostics (CC BY 4.0). Fit quality: each plot should show the fitted line passing through the middle of the data points, not off to one side. Velocity vs. time: the quasistatic plots should rise smoothly. Jagged plots or big gaps mean a noisy sensor or a test that was too short. Sanity check the signs: kS, kV, and kA should all be positive . A negative gain almost always means a sensor is inverted relative to the motor, or the units are wrong. Common problems Symptom Usual cause Negative kV or kA Encoder direction doesn't match motor direction. Invert one of them. Huge kG on an arm Angle zero isn't horizontal Wildly noisy plots Test too short, dying battery, or a loose belt / chain Gains change a lot between runs Mechanism is binding or slipping. Fix the mechanical issue first."},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"Step 6: Use the gains","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#step-6-use-the-gains","t":"Copy the numbers into Constants.java and plug them into the feedforward from Lesson 13: public static final class ArmConstants { // From SysId, <date>, fresh battery public static final double kS = 0.21 ; // volts public static final double kG = 0.48 ; // volts public static final double kV = 1.87 ; // volts per (rad/s) public static final double kA = 0.06 ; // volts per (rad/s²) } private final ArmFeedforward ff = new ArmFeedforward ( ArmConstants . kS , ArmConstants . kG , ArmConstants . kV , ArmConstants . kA ); These example numbers are made up. Yours will be different. SysId's analyzer can also suggest PID gains for a velocity or position loop. Treat them as a starting point, then fine-tune with the recipe in Lesson 12 . Re-run SysId when the mechanism changes New gear ratio, added weight, a different motor? The old gains no longer describe the mechanism. Re-characterize it."},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"Try it","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#try-it","t":"Add a SysIdRoutine to your arm (or drivetrain) and confirm the four commands run in simulation without errors With a mentor, run all four tests on the real robot Load the log in SysId and get kS, kV, kA (and kG if it applies) Check the diagnostics: are all gains positive? Does the fit look good? Put the gains in your feedforward and compare tracking to your hand-tuned version from Lesson 13"},{"p":"13.1 Characterizing with SysId","h":"Check yourself","u":"controls/13b-sysid.html#check-yourself","t":"Why does SysId run both quasistatic and dynamic tests? Quasistatic tests have almost no acceleration, so they isolate kS and kV. Dynamic tests accelerate hard, which reveals kA. Why run each test in both directions? Friction always opposes motion while gravity always pulls down. Comparing forward and reverse lets SysId separate kS from kG. SysId gives you a negative kV. What's probably wrong? The sensor direction doesn't match the motor direction (or the logged units are wrong). Fix the inversion and re-run the tests. Why bind the tests with whileTrue on a separate controller? Releasing the button stops the motor immediately, and a separate controller keeps a driver from triggering a test by accident."},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"Why it matters","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#why-it-matters","t":"When you tell an arm to go from 0° to 90° with plain PID, the error jumps from 0 to 90° instantly. The controller responds with a huge output, the arm launches toward the target as fast as it can, overshoots, and stresses the gearbox, the belts, and anything it's holding. You're effectively asking for infinite acceleration . A motion profile plans a smooth path instead: speed up gently, cruise, slow down gently, and arrive. Every 20 ms the profile hands the controller a small, reachable step instead of the far-away final target. The result is faster, smoother, more repeatable motion, and much easier tuning."},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"Read this","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#read-this","t":"Trapezoidal Motion Profiles Combining Motion Profiling and PID (ProfiledPIDController) Tuning a Vertical Elevator with Motion Profiling , with an interactive simulator"},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"Walkthrough","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"Goal vs. setpoint","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#goal-vs-setpoint","t":"This distinction is the key to the whole lesson: Goal Setpoint What it is Where you ultimately want to end up Where the profile says you should be right now Changes When you choose a new target Every 20 ms Example 90° 37.2°, moving at 85°/s Without a profile, the setpoint is the goal. With a profile, the setpoint moves smoothly toward the goal, and the controller only ever chases the setpoint."},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"The trapezoid","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#the-trapezoid","t":"A trapezoidal profile has three phases. Plotted as velocity over time, it makes a trapezoid: velocity ▲ ________________ │ / \\ │ / cruise \\ │ / \\ │ / accelerate decelerate\\ └───────────────────────────────► time Accelerate at the maximum acceleration until reaching the maximum velocity. Cruise at the maximum velocity. Decelerate at the maximum acceleration so you stop exactly at the goal. You choose two numbers, the constraints : Constraint What it limits Too high Too low Max velocity Top speed The motor can't keep up, so the mechanism falls behind the profile Slow moves Max acceleration How fast speed changes Jerky starts and stops, the motor can't keep up Sluggish starts For short moves, the mechanism never reaches max velocity. It accelerates, then immediately decelerates, and the trapezoid becomes a triangle ."},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"A worked example","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#a-worked-example","t":"An arm moves 90° with max velocity 180°/s and max acceleration 360°/s². Accelerating from 0 to 180°/s at 360°/s² takes 180 ÷ 360 = 0.5 s and covers ½ × 360 × 0.5² = 45° . Decelerating takes the same: 0.5 s and 45° . That's already 90°, so there's no cruise : the profile is a triangle. Total move time: 1.0 s . Halfway through (0.5 s) the arm should be at 45°, moving at 180°/s. Every 20 ms in between, the profile gives an exact position and velocity."},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"Why this works so well with feedforward","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#why-this-works-so-well-with-feedforward","t":"The profile gives you not just a position at every moment, but also a velocity (and acceleration). That's exactly what feedforward from Lesson 13 needs: kV × velocity supplies the voltage to keep up with the planned speed. kA × acceleration supplies the extra voltage to speed up and slow down on schedule. kG·cos(θ) holds the arm up along the way. So feedforward does almost all the work of following the profile, and PID only corrects small differences. This is the same structure top teams use: flowchart LR G[Goal] --> P[Profile] -->|setpoint position + velocity| FF[Feedforward] P -->|setpoint position| PID S[Sensor] --> PID FF --> Sum((+)) PID --> Sum --> V[setVoltage] Profile → feedforward (prediction) → PID (correction)."},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"Option 1: ProfiledPIDController","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#option-1-profiledpidcontroller","t":"ProfiledPIDController combines a trapezoidal profile and a PID controller in one object: import edu.wpi.first.math.controller.ArmFeedforward ; import edu.wpi.first.math.controller.ProfiledPIDController ; import edu.wpi.first.math.trajectory.TrapezoidProfile ; private final ProfiledPIDController controller = new ProfiledPIDController ( kP , 0 , kD , new TrapezoidProfile . Constraints ( MAX_VEL_RAD_PER_S , MAX_ACCEL_RAD_PER_S2 ) ); private final ArmFeedforward ff = new ArmFeedforward ( kS , kG , kV , kA ); public Command goTo ( double targetRadians ) { return run (() -> { // PID on the profile's current setpoint, which moves toward the goal double pidVolts = controller . calculate ( getAngleRadians (), targetRadians ); TrapezoidProfile . State setpoint = controller . getSetpoint (); double ffVolts = ff . calculate ( setpoint . position , setpoint . velocity ); motor . setVoltage ( pidVolts + ffVolts ); }) . beforeStarting (() -> controller . reset ( getAngleRadians (), getVelocityRadPerSec ())) . withName ( \"Arm to \" + targetRadians ); } Line What it does controller.calculate(measurement, goal) Advances the profile 20 ms toward the goal, then runs PID against the new setpoint controller.getSetpoint() Where the profile says to be right now : .position and .velocity ff.calculate(setpoint.position, setpoint.velocity) Feedforward for that exact point on the path controller.reset(position, velocity) Starts the profile from where the arm actually is Always reset before a new move If you don't call reset() with the current position, the profile starts from wherever it last ended, possibly far from where the arm is now. The first loop then sees a huge error and the arm lurches. .beforeStarting(...) makes sure it happens every time the command starts. This command doesn't end goTo keeps running after it arrives, so the arm keeps holding the goal against gravity. To make it end, add .until(controller::atGoal) , but then something else must hold the arm."},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"Option 2: TrapezoidProfile on its own","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#option-2-trapezoidprofile-on-its-own","t":"Sometimes you want the profile without WPILib's PID, for example to feed setpoints to a motor controller's onboard PID. Use TrapezoidProfile directly: private final TrapezoidProfile profile = new TrapezoidProfile ( new TrapezoidProfile . Constraints ( MAX_VEL , MAX_ACCEL )); private TrapezoidProfile . State setpoint = new TrapezoidProfile . State (); public Command goTo ( double goalPosition ) { TrapezoidProfile . State goal = new TrapezoidProfile . State ( goalPosition , 0 ); return runOnce (() -> setpoint = new TrapezoidProfile . State ( getPosition (), getVelocity ())) . andThen ( run (() -> { setpoint = profile . calculate ( 0.020 , setpoint , goal ); // advance 20 ms // send setpoint.position and setpoint.velocity to your controller })); }"},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"Choosing constraints","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#choosing-constraints","t":"Find what the mechanism can actually do. A rough maximum velocity is about 80% of free speed at the mechanism (motor free speed ÷ gear ratio). Leave headroom so the motor can catch up when it falls behind. Start slow. Use about half that velocity and a modest acceleration, so the first tests are gentle. Raise them until moves are as fast as you need, or until the mechanism starts lagging behind the profile. Watch for lag. Graph the setpoint and the measured position together. If the measurement falls further and further behind during a move, your constraints are more than the motor can deliver. Graph three things while tuning The goal , the setpoint , and the measured position . The setpoint should glide toward the goal, and the measurement should follow the setpoint closely. Big gaps between the setpoint and measurement mean feedforward or PID needs work; a setpoint that jumps means the profile isn't being reset."},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"Elevators work the same way","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#elevators-work-the-same-way","t":"Swap ArmFeedforward for ElevatorFeedforward , and use meters and meters per second instead of radians. Everything else is identical."},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"On-controller profiles","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#on-controller-profiles","t":"Modern motor controllers can run profiles on the controller itself at much faster rates: CTRE TalonFX: Motion Magic REV SPARK MAX / Flex: MAXMotion You give them the constraints and feedforward gains once, then just send a goal. They're excellent and widely used. Learn the WPILib version first so you understand what they're doing and can tune them."},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"Try it","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#try-it","t":"By hand: an elevator moves 1.2 m with max velocity 1.5 m/s and max acceleration 3 m/s². Is the profile a trapezoid or a triangle? How long does the move take? Open the ArmSimulation example project Replace its controller with a ProfiledPIDController plus ArmFeedforward Bind two buttons to two angles and tune it to move smoothly between them Graph the goal, setpoint, and measured angle together in Elastic or AdvantageScope Remove the .beforeStarting(...) reset and see what happens on the first move. Then put it back."},{"p":"14. Motion Profiles","h":"Check yourself","u":"controls/14-profiles.html#check-yourself","t":"What's the difference between the goal and the setpoint? The goal is where you finally want to be. The setpoint is where the profile says to be right now, and it moves toward the goal every loop. Why does a motion profile reduce overshoot? The setpoint moves gradually and slows down near the goal, so the controller never sees a huge sudden error. When does a trapezoidal profile become a triangle? When the move is too short to reach max velocity before it has to start slowing down. Why feed the profile's setpoint velocity into feedforward instead of the final goal? Feedforward needs to know how fast the mechanism should be moving right now, and that's the profile's current setpoint. At the goal, the velocity is 0. Why must you call reset() before each move? So the profile starts from the mechanism's actual position. Otherwise the first setpoint can be far from reality and the mechanism lurches. The measured position falls further behind the setpoint during every move. What should you change? Lower the max velocity or acceleration, because the motor can't keep up, or improve the feedforward gains (kV and kA)."},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"Why it matters","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#why-it-matters","t":"During build season, many programmers write code at once on one robot project. Without a process: someone's half-finished arm code breaks the drivetrain the night before competition two people rewrite the same file and one person's work disappears nobody remembers which version of the code was on the robot when auto worked Branches let everyone work separately without breaking the shared code. Pull requests let the team review a change before it joins the main code. Together they keep main always working, which is the one rule that matters most."},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"Read this","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#read-this","t":"About pull requests (GitHub Docs) About branches (GitHub Docs) Git Version Control Introduction (WPILib)"},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"Walkthrough","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"The rule: main always deploys","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#the-rule-main-always-deploys","t":"At any moment, anyone should be able to pull main , deploy it, and get a working robot. So: Nobody commits directly to main . All work happens on a branch . A branch joins main through a pull request (PR) , after someone reviews it. gitGraph commit id: \"working robot\" branch feature/intake commit id: \"add intake subsystem\" commit id: \"bind to A button\" checkout main branch fix/drive-inverted commit id: \"invert right side\" checkout main merge fix/drive-inverted id: \"PR #11 merged\" merge feature/intake id: \"PR #12 merged\" Two people worked at the same time on separate branches, and each change joined main only after review."},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"What a branch is","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#what-a-branch-is","t":"A branch is a separate line of commits. When you create one, it starts as a copy of main . Your commits go on your branch only, so main and everyone else's branches are untouched until you merge. Command What it does git branch List branches. * marks the one you're on. git switch -c feature/arm-pid Create a new branch and switch to it git switch main Switch back to main git push -u origin feature/arm-pid Upload the branch to GitHub (first time) git branch -d feature/arm-pid Delete a branch after it's merged Commit before you switch Switching branches with uncommitted changes either carries them along or refuses to switch. Commit (or git stash ) first."},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"Branch naming","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#branch-naming","t":"Names should say what the branch is for at a glance: Prefix Use for Example feature/ New functionality feature/arm-pid fix/ Bug fixes fix/intake-inverted tune/ Gain and constant changes tune/shooter-ff comp/ Changes made at an event comp/week2-auto-tweak"},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"The full workflow","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#the-full-workflow","t":"1. Start from the latest main : git switch main git pull git switch -c feature/arm-pid 2. Work and commit as you go. Small commits with clear messages ( Lesson 1 ). 3. Keep up with main if your branch lives more than a day or two, so conflicts stay small: git switch main git pull git switch feature/arm-pid git merge main 4. Push your branch: git push -u origin feature/arm-pid 5. Open a pull request. On GitHub, click Compare & pull request . Fill in the description and request a reviewer. 6. Respond to review. Push more commits to the same branch; the PR updates automatically. 7. Merge once it's approved and CI passes ( Lesson 16 ). Then delete the branch."},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"Writing a good PR","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#writing-a-good-pr","t":"A reviewer should understand your PR in a minute. Include: What changed and why , in a few sentences How you tested it : in simulation, on the real robot, or not yet Anything risky : \"this changes every drive constant,\" \"needs retuning on the real robot\" ## What Adds PID position control to the arm, with goTo() and hold() commands. ## Why The arm sagged at the scoring angle with open-loop control. ## Testing - Tuned in ArmSimulation: settles in under 1 s, no overshoot - NOT yet tested on the real robot. Gains will need adjustment. Keep PRs small . One feature per PR is easy to review and easy to undo. A PR that changes 40 files gets a rubber-stamp review, which defeats the purpose."},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"Reviewing someone else's PR","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#reviewing-someone-elses-pr","t":"Code review isn't about catching people. It's how the team learns from each other and catches bugs before they reach the robot. What to look for: Does it build? Does CI pass? Does it do what the description says? Are units clear in names? Are magic numbers in Constants ? Do commands require the subsystems they use? What happens if a sensor disconnects? Is there a timeout on anything that could run forever? Is anything blocking the loop ( Lesson 6 )? How to comment: ❌ Not helpful ✅ Helpful \"This is wrong.\" \"This uses degrees, but ArmFeedforward expects radians. Should this be Math.toRadians(angle) ?\" \"Fix the names.\" \"Could getVal() be getAngleDegrees() , so the unit is clear?\" \"Why?\" \"What happens here if the encoder is unplugged?\" Ask questions, explain your reasoning, and say what's good too. Approve when it's ready, or Request changes with a clear list of what's needed."},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"Tracking work with issues","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#tracking-work-with-issues","t":"Use GitHub Issues as the team's to-do list: One issue per task: \"Add slow mode to drivetrain,\" \"Arm overshoots at 90°\" Assign an owner, so everyone knows who's on it Link the PR to its issue by writing Closes #14 in the PR description. The issue closes automatically when the PR merges. A GitHub Project board with columns like To do / In progress / In review / Done gives the whole team a view of build-season progress."},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"At competition","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#at-competition","t":"Competitions are fast and stressful, but the process still matters, maybe more: Make a comp/ branch for each event. Small fixes go there, reviewed by a second programmer even if the review is 30 seconds long. Tag the exact code that ran in each match day: git tag week2-day1 then git push --tags . If something breaks, you can always get back to what worked. Merge the competition branch back into main after the event, so fixes aren't lost."},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"Try it","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#try-it","t":"Create a branch, make two commits, and push it Open a PR with a description that covers what, why, and how you tested it Review a teammate's PR and leave at least one helpful comment, written as a question Respond to review on your own PR by pushing another commit Merge after approval, then delete the branch Open an issue, then write Closes #N in a PR and watch it close on merge"},{"p":"15. Branches, PRs & Code Review","h":"Check yourself","u":"workflow/15-team-process.html#check-yourself","t":"Why not commit directly to main ? main must always build and deploy. Unreviewed changes there can break the robot for everyone. Your branch has existed for a week and main has moved on. What should you do before opening a PR? Merge the latest main into your branch and fix any conflicts there, so the PR merges cleanly. What makes a PR easy to review? It's small, focused on one change, and explains what changed, why, and how it was tested. What's a helpful way to point out a possible bug in review? Explain what you noticed and ask a specific question, like \"What happens here if the encoder is unplugged?\" Why tag the code at competition? So you can always get back to the exact version that ran in a match, even after more changes."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Why it matters","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#why-it-matters","t":"A unit test is code that checks your code automatically. Instead of deploying to the robot to discover your conversion math is wrong, a test catches it in seconds, on your laptop, without a robot. CI (continuous integration) runs your tests automatically every time someone opens a pull request. If anything is broken, GitHub shows a red ❌ and the PR can't merge. Broken code never reaches main , and nobody has to remember to run the tests. Tests are an investment: a few minutes writing one saves hours of robot time later, and it keeps checking the code every time anyone changes it, all season."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Read this","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#read-this","t":"Unit Testing (WPILib) JUnit 5 User Guide : the \"Writing Tests\" section GitHub Actions quickstart"},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Walkthrough","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"What's worth testing?","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#whats-worth-testing","t":"Start with logic that doesn't need hardware. These are fast, reliable, and catch real bugs: Test this Example Unit conversions Does METERS_PER_MOTOR_ROTATION give the right distance for 10 rotations? Conditions Does isAtSpeed() return true at 3040 RPM with a 3000 target? Limits and safety Does setTarget() refuse a value above the max? Input processing Does the deadband turn 0.03 into 0? State machines Intake has piece → stop → index → ready to shoot Things that are hard to unit test, like \"does the robot drive straight,\" are better checked in simulation ( Lesson 9 ) and on the robot. Design for testing Logic that's pulled out into plain methods, with inputs and outputs, is easy to test. Logic buried inside a command's execute() that reads hardware directly is not. When you write a calculation, put it in a method you can call from a test."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"How a test is structured","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#how-a-test-is-structured","t":"Every test follows the same three steps, often called Arrange, Act, Assert : @Test void isAtSpeedWhenClose () { Shooter shooter = new Shooter ( 5000 ); // Arrange: set up what you need shooter . setTarget ( 3000 ); boolean result = shooter . isAtSpeed ( 3040 ); // Act: do the thing being tested assertTrue ( result ); // Assert: check the result } One test checks one behavior . Name it for what it checks, so a failing test's name tells you what broke."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Your first test","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#your-first-test","t":"WPILib projects already include JUnit 5. Tests go in src/test/java , mirroring the src/main/java folders. Create src/test/java/frc/robot/ShooterTest.java to test the Shooter class from Lesson 3 : package frc.robot ; import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.* ; import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach ; import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test ; class ShooterTest { private Shooter shooter ; @BeforeEach void setUp () { shooter = new Shooter ( 5000 ); // runs before every test, so each starts fresh } @Test void isAtSpeedWhenClose () { shooter . setTarget ( 3000 ); assertTrue ( shooter . isAtSpeed ( 3040 )); } @Test void notAtSpeedWhenFar () { shooter . setTarget ( 3000 ); assertFalse ( shooter . isAtSpeed ( 2800 )); } @Test void refusesTargetAboveMax () { shooter . setTarget ( 6000 ); assertEquals ( 0 , shooter . getTarget (), 1e-9 ); } } Run it: ./gradlew test Or click the ▷ icon next to a test in VS Code to run just that one."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"The assertions you'll use","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#the-assertions-youll-use","t":"Assertion Passes when assertTrue(x) x is true assertFalse(x) x is false assertEquals(expected, actual) They're equal (ints, strings, objects) assertEquals(expected, actual, delta) Two double s are within delta of each other assertThrows(Exception.class, () -> ...) The code throws that exception Always use a delta for doubles Decimal math isn't exact on computers: 0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004 . assertEquals(0.3, 0.1 + 0.2) fails. Write assertEquals(0.3, 0.1 + 0.2, 1e-9) instead."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Testing a conversion","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#testing-a-conversion","t":"@Test void tenRotationsIsAboutFourTenthsOfAMeter () { double meters = 10 * DriveConstants . METERS_PER_MOTOR_ROTATION ; assertEquals ( 0.399 , meters , 0.001 ); // 4-inch wheel, 8:1 gearing } Work out the expected answer by hand first. A test that just copies the code's formula will happily agree with a wrong formula."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Reading a failing test","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#reading-a-failing-test","t":"ShooterTest > refusesTargetAboveMax() FAILED org.opentest4j.AssertionFailedError: expected: <0.0> but was: <6000.0> at frc.robot.ShooterTest.refusesTargetAboveMax(ShooterTest.java:31) The test name says what broke, the message says expected vs. actual , and the line number points to the assertion. Here, setTarget() isn't refusing values above the max."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Testing code that uses WPILib hardware classes","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#testing-code-that-uses-wpilib-hardware-classes","t":"Some WPILib classes need the hardware layer started before they work in a test. Initialize it once at the start of the test class: @BeforeEach void setUp () { assert HAL . initialize ( 500 , 0 ); // start WPILib's hardware layer in test mode intake = new Intake (); } @AfterEach void tearDown () { intake . close (); // free the simulated ports for the next test } Then you can use simulation classes like PWMSim or DIOSim to fake sensor inputs and check motor outputs. The WPILib unit testing page above has a complete example."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Test-driven fixes","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#test-driven-fixes","t":"When you find a bug, try this order: Write a test that fails because of the bug. Fix the code. Watch the test pass . Now that bug can never come back without the test catching it."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Set up CI with GitHub Actions","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#set-up-ci-with-github-actions","t":"GitHub Actions runs commands on GitHub's computers whenever something happens in your repo. Create .github/workflows/build.yml in your robot repo: name : Build on : push : branches : [ main ] pull_request : jobs : build : runs-on : ubuntu-latest steps : - uses : actions/checkout@v4 - uses : actions/setup-java@v4 with : distribution : temurin java-version : 17 - run : chmod +x gradlew - run : ./gradlew build Reading it: on: run on every push to main and every pull request checkout download the code setup-java install the right Java version ./gradlew build compile and run every test, exactly like on your laptop Now every PR shows ✅ or ❌, and you can click through to the full log to see which test failed. Match the Java version Use the JDK version your season's WPILib installer ships with. Check the WPILib release notes each year, and update java-version to match."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Branch protection","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#branch-protection","t":"CI only helps if a red ❌ actually stops a merge. In the repo's Settings → Branches , add a rule for main : Require a pull request before merging Require approvals: 1 Require status checks to pass: select the build job Now nobody, including you, can merge broken code by accident."},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Try it","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#try-it","t":"Write three tests for your Shooter class and run ./gradlew test Break the logic on purpose and read the failing test's message. Then fix it. Write a test for a conversion constant, working out the expected answer by hand Add the CI workflow to your practice repo and open a PR to see it run Turn on branch protection for main , then try to merge a PR with a failing test"},{"p":"16. Unit Testing & CI","h":"Check yourself","u":"workflow/16-unit-testing.html#check-yourself","t":"What are the three steps of a unit test? Arrange (set up), Act (do the thing being tested), Assert (check the result). Why does assertEquals(0.3, 0.1 + 0.2) fail, and how do you fix it? Decimal math on computers isn't exact, so the result is 0.30000000000000004. Pass a small delta: assertEquals(0.3, 0.1 + 0.2, 1e-9) . Why work out a test's expected value by hand? If you copy the code's formula into the test, a wrong formula passes its own test. What does CI add on top of running tests yourself? It runs them automatically on every PR, so nobody can forget, and with branch protection, broken code can't merge. You found a bug. What should you do before fixing it? Write a test that fails because of it. Then fix the code and watch the test pass, so the bug can't come back unnoticed."},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"Why it matters","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#why-it-matters","t":"So far the robot has only known things about itself: how fast a wheel spins, how far it has rolled, which way it's facing. To score in autonomous, line up on a goal, or follow a path, it needs to know where it is on the field . That's called the robot's pose , and keeping track of it is called pose estimation . This lesson covers how WPILib describes a pose and how odometry tracks it from the encoders and gyro you already have. Lesson 18 adds cameras to correct it."},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"Read this","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#read-this","t":"Coordinate System (WPILib) Kinematics and Odometry (WPILib) Differential Drive Odometry (WPILib)"},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"Walkthrough","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"What a pose is","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#what-a-pose-is","t":"A pose is three numbers: Part Meaning Units X How far along the field's long side meters Y How far along the field's short side meters Heading (θ) Which way the robot's front points degrees or radians WPILib stores them in a Pose2d , which is a Translation2d (X and Y) plus a Rotation2d (heading): Pose2d pose = new Pose2d ( 2.0 , 3.0 , Rotation2d . fromDegrees ( 45 )); pose . getX (); // 2.0 meters pose . getY (); // 3.0 meters pose . getRotation (). getDegrees (); // 45.0 pose . getTranslation (); // just the (2.0, 3.0) part A few things you can do with poses: // How far apart are two poses? (straight-line distance in meters) double meters = pose . getTranslation (). getDistance ( target . getTranslation ()); // How far do I still have to turn to face the target's heading? Rotation2d turnLeft = target . getRotation (). minus ( pose . getRotation ()); // Where would I be after driving 1 m forward from here? (forward = the robot's own front) Pose2d ahead = pose . transformBy ( new Transform2d ( 1.0 , 0.0 , Rotation2d . kZero ));"},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"The field's coordinates","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#the-fields-coordinates","t":"WPILib uses one coordinate system for the whole field, called the field frame , and it's the same for both alliances: (0, 0) is the corner on the blue alliance wall, on the right as the blue drivers look at the field. +X points away from the blue wall, toward the red wall. +Y points to the left, as seen from the blue driver station. Heading 0° faces +X (toward red). Turning counterclockwise (left, seen from above) makes it go up . That last rule is the same one the gyro followed in Lab 8 . WPILib uses \"counterclockwise is positive\" everywhere. Red alliance The origin always stays on the blue side. A red robot just starts at a big X, near the red wall. PathPlanner ( Lesson 19 ) can flip blue paths to the red side for you, so you only draw them once. There's also the robot frame : +X straight out the robot's front, +Y out its left side. Camera positions (\"15 cm forward of center, 30 cm up\") are written in the robot frame."},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"Three ways to know the pose","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#three-ways-to-know-the-pose","t":"Method How Good Bad Odometry Add up wheel distances and gyro heading every loop Fast (every 20 ms), always available, smooth Drifts : small errors add up and nothing removes them Vision A camera sees AprilTags on the field and works out where it must be Absolute: no drift, can fix a wrong start Only when tags are in view; noisy; slower Fusion Run odometry all the time and nudge it toward vision when vision is good Smooth and doesn't drift More code and tuning Competition robots use fusion. It starts with good odometry, so that's first."},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"How odometry works","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#how-odometry-works","t":"Every 20 ms, odometry asks the sensors two questions: How far did the robot roll since last loop? The average of the left and right encoder changes. Which way was it facing? The gyro heading. A step of 1 cm while facing 0° adds 1 cm to X. The same step facing 90° adds 1 cm to Y. Add up 50 of those steps a second and you have a running position. This is dead reckoning , the same way sailors navigated before GPS. flowchart LR E[Encoders<br/>left and right meters] --> O[DifferentialDriveOdometry<br/>update every 20 ms] G[NavX2 gyro<br/>heading] --> O O --> P[Pose2d<br/>X, Y, heading] P --> F[Field map in Elastic] P --> A[Autos and path following] WPILib does the math in DifferentialDriveOdometry : // Once, in the constructor: remember the starting readings m_odometry = new DifferentialDriveOdometry ( m_gyro . getRotation2d (), getLeftMeters (), getRightMeters ()); // Every loop, in periodic(): add this loop's step m_odometry . update ( m_gyro . getRotation2d (), getLeftMeters (), getRightMeters ()); // Any time: where are we? Pose2d pose = m_odometry . getPoseMeters (); Lab 11 adds exactly this to the West Coast Drive. Why the heading comes from the gyro You saw in Lab 8 that a skid-steer's encoders overcount turns because the wheels slide sideways. DifferentialDriveOdometry only uses the encoders for how far , and takes which way from the gyro."},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"Resetting the pose","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#resetting-the-pose","t":"Odometry starts wherever you tell it to, (0, 0, 0°) by default. To move it: m_odometry . resetPosition ( m_gyro . getRotation2d (), getLeftMeters (), getRightMeters (), newPose ); You always pass the sensors' current readings, because odometry measures changes from them. You don't reset the encoders or gyro themselves. If a sensor reading suddenly jumps (because you reset it), odometry thinks the robot moved. At the start of autonomous, teams reset the pose to where the robot really starts on the field, such as \"touching the blue wall, 4.0 m from the right side, facing red.\" PathPlanner autos do this for you."},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"Why odometry drifts","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#why-odometry-drifts","t":"Every error gets added to the pose and stays there: Cause What happens Fix Wheel diameter is a little off Every meter is a little too long or short Calibrate with a tape measure (Lab 11, Step 9) Wheels slip (hard acceleration, pushing matches, carpet seams) Encoders count rolling that didn't move the robot Gentle acceleration; vision Robot bumped while sitting still Robot moved; encoders didn't turn Vision Gyro drifts Heading slowly creeps Keep the robot still while the NavX calibrates; vision A well-calibrated drivetrain is usually within a few centimeters after a short auto, and off by much more after a full match of pushing. That's why vision matters."},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"Swerve odometry","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#swerve-odometry","t":"Swerve robots use the same idea with SwerveDriveOdometry . Instead of two encoder distances, each of the four modules reports how far it rolled and which way it was pointing. Lesson 21 covers swerve."},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"Seeing the pose","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#seeing-the-pose","t":"Two ways to look at the pose: Elastic : a Field2d (sent with SmartDashboard.putData ) shows the robot on a field picture. Good for drivers and quick checks. AdvantageScope : log the Pose2d and drag it onto the 2D or 3D field. You can replay a whole match afterward. See Lesson 20 ."},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"Try it","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#try-it","t":"Do Lab 11 On paper: the robot is at (2, 1) facing 90°. It drives 1 m forward. What's its new pose? On paper: the robot faces 30° and the target faces 120°. How far, and which way, must it turn?"},{"p":"17. Poses and Odometry","h":"Check yourself","u":"going-further/17-odometry.html#check-yourself","t":"The robot is at (2, 1) facing 90° and drives 1 m forward. Where is it? (2, 2), still facing 90°. Facing 90° means facing +Y. Which corner of the field is (0, 0)? The corner on the blue alliance wall, on the blue drivers' right. +X points toward the red wall and +Y to the blue drivers' left. Why does odometry drift? It adds up small steps, and every small error (slip, a wrong wheel diameter, a bump) is added in and never removed. Why pass the sensors' current readings to resetPosition instead of zeroing the sensors? Odometry tracks changes in the readings. Zeroing a sensor makes its reading jump, which odometry sees as sudden movement. Odometry vs. vision: which is fast and smooth, and which never drifts? Odometry is fast and smooth. Vision doesn't drift. Fusion combines both. Parts of this lesson are adapted from the Introduction to Pose Estimation and Odometry lessons of Mantik by Itkan Robotics (CC BY 4.0)."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Why it matters","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#why-it-matters","t":"Odometry from Lesson 17 is smooth and fast, but it drifts, and it only knows where the robot is if you told it where it started. A camera that can see the field fixes both problems. Every FRC field has AprilTags : square black-and-white markers, each with a number, mounted at known spots. When a camera sees a tag, it can work out exactly where the camera must be to see the tag at that size and angle. WPILib knows where every tag is on the field, so that tells the robot where it is. RoboJacks uses PhotonVision . This lesson covers how it works, how to read it from robot code, and how to fuse its measurements with odometry so the pose is both smooth and correct."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Read this","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#read-this","t":"AprilTag Introduction (WPILib) PhotonVision documentation , especially \"Installation\", \"Camera Calibration\", and \"PhotonLib\" Pose Estimators (WPILib) Example: PhotonVisionSubsystem.java , from the NMRC code camp (level 2, 2026). This lesson follows its approach. Licenses What License What it means for you PhotonLib (the vendor library in your robot code) MIT Use it in robot code freely. Keep its copyright notice if you copy its source files. PhotonVision (the program on the coprocessor) GNU GPL v3 Free to install and run. If you change PhotonVision itself and share it, you must share your changes under GPL v3 too. Your robot code isn't affected: it only talks to PhotonVision over NetworkTables. PhotonVision documentation CC BY 4.0 You may copy and adapt it if you credit PhotonVision and link the license, the same way this course credits WPILib. NMRC code camp example No license file in the repository Read it and learn from it, but don't copy it into team code word for word without asking its authors. This lesson's code is written for the course."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Walkthrough","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"The two halves","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#the-two-halves","t":"Part Runs on What it does PhotonVision A coprocessor (a small computer like an Orange Pi 5) with one or more USB cameras Finds AprilTags in every frame, works out where each tag is relative to the camera, and sends the results over NetworkTables PhotonLib (vendor library) The roboRIO Reads those results in your robot code The coprocessor plugs into the robot's network switch or radio, like the roboRIO. The roboRIO does none of the image processing. It only reads the answers."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Setting up PhotonVision","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#setting-up-photonvision","t":"On a laptop connected to the robot's network, open the PhotonVision web page ( http://photonvision.local:5800 , or the coprocessor's IP address on port 5800). Then: Settings: enter team number 4674 , so PhotonVision finds the roboRIO. Cameras: give each camera a nickname , like frontCamera . Robot code finds the camera by this name, so it must match exactly. Calibrate each camera at the resolution you'll use (Cameras tab → calibration with a printed chessboard). Without calibration, PhotonVision can't measure distance, and 3D results are wrong. Pipeline: set the pipeline type to AprilTag , tag family 36h11 , and turn on 3D mode. Point the camera at a tag. The dashboard should outline it and show its ID. Install PhotonLib in VS Code: WPILib: Manage Vendor Libraries → Install new libraries (online) and pick PhotonLib , or paste the JSON link from the PhotonVision docs. The PhotonLib version should match the version of PhotonVision on the coprocessor."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"What PhotonVision tells you","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#what-photonvision-tells-you","t":"Each camera sends a pipeline result for every frame. A result can hold several tracked targets , one per tag it sees: Method Meaning result.hasTargets() Did this frame see any tags? result.getBestTarget() The best tag in this frame result.getTimestampSeconds() When the picture was taken target.getFiducialId() The tag's number target.getYaw() How far left or right of center the tag is, in degrees target.getBestCameraToTarget() Where the tag is in 3D, relative to the camera target.getPoseAmbiguity() How sure PhotonVision is about that 3D answer: 0 is sure, above about 0.2 is a guess Check the sign of yaw on your camera PhotonVision's own code comments disagree about whether a tag to the right gives a positive or negative yaw. In practice, PhotonVision's aiming examples treat right as positive , which is the opposite of WPILib's counterclockwise-positive rule. Hold a tag to the right of the camera and look at the yaw on the PhotonVision dashboard before you trust any aiming code."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Where the camera is on the robot","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#where-the-camera-is-on-the-robot","t":"To turn \"where the tag is relative to the camera \" into \"where the robot is on the field,\" the code needs to know where the camera is mounted. That's a Transform3d , measured from the center of the robot at floor level , using the robot frame (+X forward, +Y left, +Z up): // Where the front camera is mounted: 14 in forward, centered left-right, 8 in up, // tilted up 10 degrees, facing straight ahead. Measure your real robot! private final Transform3d m_robotToFrontCamera = new Transform3d ( new Translation3d ( Units . inchesToMeters ( 14 ), 0 , Units . inchesToMeters ( 8 )), new Rotation3d ( 0 , Units . degreesToRadians ( - 10 ), 0 )); The Rotation3d is roll, pitch, yaw, in radians. In WPILib's robot frame, negative pitch tilts the camera up . Yaw turns the camera left (positive) or right (negative), like the codecamp example's two cameras angled 15° outward. If this transform is wrong, every pose the camera produces is wrong by the same amount. Measure it carefully, then check it: park the robot at a measured spot and compare the camera's pose with reality."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"From one tag to the robot's pose","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#from-one-tag-to-the-robots-pose","t":"PhotonLib's PhotonUtils.estimateFieldToRobotAprilTag does the geometry. It needs three things: Camera to tag : what the camera measured ( target.getBestCameraToTarget() ) Tag on the field : looked up by ID in WPILib's AprilTagFieldLayout for this season Camera to robot : the mounting transform, flipped around ( m_robotToFrontCamera.inverse() ) Pose2d robotPose = PhotonUtils . estimateFieldToRobotAprilTag ( target . getBestCameraToTarget (), // where the tag is, seen from the camera tagPose . get (), // where the tag is on the field m_robotToFrontCamera . inverse ()) // where the robot is, seen from the camera . toPose2d (); Which field layout? The 2026 game has two field layouts, because some events use welded field elements and some use AndyMark's: AprilTagFields.k2026RebuiltWelded (WPILib's default) and AprilTagFields.k2026RebuiltAndymark . The tags sit in slightly different places. Use the one your event uses; the codecamp example uses AndyMark."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Fusing vision with odometry","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#fusing-vision-with-odometry","t":"You could just replace the odometry pose with the camera's whenever it sees a tag. Don't. Vision is noisy, so the robot would jitter, and one bad frame would teleport it. Instead, WPILib's pose estimator blends them. It runs odometry every loop, like before, and when a vision measurement arrives it nudges the pose part of the way toward it. How far depends on how much you trust each one. flowchart LR C[Camera on coprocessor<br/>PhotonVision] -- \"tag results<br/>over NetworkTables\" --> V[VisionSubsystem<br/>tag → robot pose,<br/>reject bad ones] V -- \"pose, timestamp,<br/>trust\" --> PE[DifferentialDrivePoseEstimator<br/>in DriveSubsystem] E[Encoders] --> PE G[NavX2] --> PE PE --> P[Fused Pose2d]"},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Step 1: A pose estimator in the drive subsystem","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#step-1-a-pose-estimator-in-the-drive-subsystem","t":"In the WCD's DriveSubsystem , swap DifferentialDriveOdometry from Lab 11 for DifferentialDrivePoseEstimator . It needs one extra thing, the drivetrain's kinematics (its track width): // Track width: how far apart the left and right wheels are private final DifferentialDriveKinematics m_kinematics = new DifferentialDriveKinematics ( kDriveConstants . TrackWidthMeters ); // Odometry plus vision (replaces DifferentialDriveOdometry from Lab 11) private final DifferentialDrivePoseEstimator m_poseEstimator ; In the constructor, where Lab 11 created the odometry: m_poseEstimator = new DifferentialDrivePoseEstimator ( m_kinematics , m_gyro . getRotation2d (), getLeftMeters (), getRightMeters (), Pose2d . kZero ); Then use it everywhere Lab 11 used m_odometry , and add one method the vision subsystem will call: /** Where the robot is: odometry, corrected by vision. */ public Pose2d getPose () { return m_poseEstimator . getEstimatedPosition (); } /** * Hands the pose estimator a pose from a camera. * stdDevs says how much to trust it: (x meters, y meters, heading radians). Smaller = more trust. */ public void addVisionMeasurement ( Pose2d visionPose , double timestampSeconds , Matrix < N3 , N1 > stdDevs ) { m_poseEstimator . addVisionMeasurement ( visionPose , timestampSeconds , stdDevs ); } In periodic() , m_odometry.update(...) becomes m_poseEstimator.update(...) with the same arguments, and resetPoseCommand() calls m_poseEstimator.resetPosition(...) with the same arguments."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Step 2: The vision subsystem","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#step-2-the-vision-subsystem","t":"Vision gets its own subsystem. It reads every camera, turns good tag sightings into robot poses, and hands them to the drive subsystem. package frc.robot.subsystems ; import java.util.Optional ; import org.photonvision.PhotonCamera ; import org.photonvision.PhotonUtils ; import org.photonvision.targeting.PhotonPipelineResult ; import org.photonvision.targeting.PhotonTrackedTarget ; import edu.wpi.first.apriltag.AprilTagFieldLayout ; import edu.wpi.first.apriltag.AprilTagFields ; import edu.wpi.first.math.VecBuilder ; import edu.wpi.first.math.geometry.Pose2d ; import edu.wpi.first.math.geometry.Pose3d ; import edu.wpi.first.math.geometry.Rotation3d ; import edu.wpi.first.math.geometry.Transform3d ; import edu.wpi.first.math.geometry.Translation3d ; import edu.wpi.first.math.util.Units ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; import frc.robot.telemetry.Telemetry ; import frc.robot.telemetry.TelemetryTable ; public class VisionSubsystem extends SubsystemBase { // Ignore a tag when PhotonVision is less sure than this about its 3D pose (0 = sure) private static final double kMaxAmbiguity = 0.15 ; private final DriveSubsystem m_drive ; // vision measurements go to its pose estimator private final TelemetryTable m_telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Vision\" ); // Where every AprilTag is on this season's field private final AprilTagFieldLayout m_fieldLayout = AprilTagFieldLayout . loadField ( AprilTagFields . k2026RebuiltWelded ); // The camera, by its nickname in the PhotonVision web page private final PhotonCamera m_frontCamera = new PhotonCamera ( \"frontCamera\" ); // Where the front camera is mounted. Measure your real robot! private final Transform3d m_robotToFrontCamera = new Transform3d ( new Translation3d ( Units . inchesToMeters ( 14 ), 0 , Units . inchesToMeters ( 8 )), new Rotation3d ( 0 , Units . degreesToRadians ( - 10 ), 0 )); // The newest frame from the front camera, for aiming (it may have no targets) private PhotonPipelineResult m_latestFront = new PhotonPipelineResult (); public VisionSubsystem ( DriveSubsystem drive ) { m_drive = drive ; } @Override public void periodic () { PhotonPipelineResult newest = updateCamera ( \"Front\" , m_frontCamera , m_robotToFrontCamera ); if ( newest != null ) { m_latestFront = newest ; // keep the newest frame for aiming } // A second camera is one more line: updateCamera(\"Back\", m_backCamera, m_robotToBackCamera); } /** * Reads every new frame from one camera and sends good robot poses to the drive subsystem. * Returns the newest frame, or null if there were no new frames. */ private PhotonPipelineResult updateCamera ( String name , PhotonCamera camera , Transform3d robotToCamera ) { int used = 0 ; PhotonPipelineResult newest = null ; for ( PhotonPipelineResult result : camera . getAllUnreadResults ()) { newest = result ; if ( ! result . hasTargets ()) { continue ; // no tags in this frame } PhotonTrackedTarget target = result . getBestTarget (); // Where is this tag on the field? (Empty if it's not one of this year's tags.) Optional < Pose3d > tagPose = m_fieldLayout . getTagPose ( target . getFiducialId ()); if ( tagPose . isEmpty () || target . getPoseAmbiguity () > kMaxAmbiguity ) { continue ; // unknown tag, or PhotonVision isn't sure: skip it } // Tag seen from the camera + tag on the field + camera on the robot = robot on the field Pose2d robotPose = PhotonUtils . estimateFieldToRobotAprilTag ( target . getBestCameraToTarget (), tagPose . get (), robotToCamera . inverse ()) . toPose2d (); // Trust it less the farther away the tag is: about 0.1 at 1.2 m, 0.6 at 3 m, 1.1 at 4 m double distance = PhotonUtils . getDistanceToPose ( robotPose , tagPose . get (). toPose2d ()); double stdDev = 0.07 * distance * distance ; m_drive . addVisionMeasurement ( robotPose , result . getTimestampSeconds (), VecBuilder . fill ( stdDev , stdDev , stdDev )); used ++ ; m_telemetry . log ( name + \"/Pose\" , robotPose ); // for AdvantageScope's field view m_telemetry . log ( name + \"/Tag ID\" , target . getFiducialId ()); m_telemetry . log ( name + \"/Distance (m)\" , distance ); } m_telemetry . log ( name + \"/Connected\" , camera . isConnected ()); m_telemetry . log ( name + \"/Measurements Used\" , used ); return newest ; } } In RobotContainer , create it after the drive subsystem, and hand it the drive: private final VisionSubsystem m_vision = new VisionSubsystem ( m_driveSubsystem ); Store the drive subsystem! The constructor must save drive into m_drive . If it doesn't, m_drive stays null , and the robot code crashes with a NullPointerException the first time a camera sees a tag. That only happens when a tag is in view, so it's easy to miss in the shop. (The codecamp example's constructor has this exact bug: it takes driveSubsystem but never saves it.)"},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Piece by piece","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#piece-by-piece","t":"for ( PhotonPipelineResult result : camera . getAllUnreadResults ()) { The camera may have sent several frames since the last loop. getAllUnreadResults() returns every one we haven't read yet, so no measurement is wasted or used twice. if ( tagPose . isEmpty () || target . getPoseAmbiguity () > kMaxAmbiguity ) { continue ; } Skips tags that aren't on this year's field and tags PhotonVision isn't sure about. A single flat tag can look almost the same from two different angles; ambiguity measures how close those two answers are. continue jumps to the next frame. double stdDev = 0.07 * distance * distance ; This is the standard deviation : roughly \"how wrong is this measurement, usually?\" Smaller means more trust. A tag 1 m away is measured precisely; a tag 5 m away is a few pixels across and much less precise. Squaring the distance makes trust drop fast as tags get farther away. m_drive . addVisionMeasurement ( robotPose , result . getTimestampSeconds (), VecBuilder . fill ( stdDev , stdDev , stdDev )); Hands the pose to the estimator with when the picture was taken , not when it arrived. The estimator remembers recent odometry, goes back to that moment, applies the correction there, and replays the movement since. The three numbers are the trust for X, Y, and heading."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Tuning the trust","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#tuning-the-trust","t":"The 0.07 * distance² rule is a starting point. Watch the fused pose in AdvantageScope ( Lesson 20 ) with the camera's pose ( Vision/Front/Pose ) drawn next to it: You see Change The pose jitters while the robot sits still in front of a tag Trust vision less: raise 0.07 The pose is slow to snap back after you push the robot Trust vision more: lower 0.07 Far-away tags drag the pose around Skip tags beyond about 4 m The heading jumps when a tag comes into view Trust vision's heading less than the NavX's, for example VecBuilder.fill(stdDev, stdDev, stdDev * 5)"},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Aiming at a tag","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#aiming-at-a-tag","t":"For simple aiming (turn to face a tag) you don't need the pose at all. Turn by the target's yaw with a P controller from Lesson 12 . This goes in VisionSubsystem or in a command that uses both subsystems: /** Turns the robot in place to face the tag the front camera sees. Ends when lined up. */ public Command aimAtTagCommand () { PIDController aim = new PIDController ( 0.02 , 0 , 0 ); // output per degree of error aim . setTolerance ( 1.0 ); // within 1 degree counts as lined up return m_drive . run (() -> { PhotonPipelineResult result = m_latestFront ; double turn = 0.0 ; if ( result . hasTargets ()) { // Yaw is positive when the tag is to the RIGHT (check yours!). Turning right = left side forward. turn = - aim . calculate ( result . getBestTarget (). getYaw (), 0.0 ); } m_drive . tankDriveVolts ( turn * 12.0 , - turn * 12.0 ); }) . until ( aim :: atSetpoint ) . finallyDo (() -> m_drive . tankDriveVolts ( 0 , 0 )) . withName ( \"Aim at Tag\" ); } aim.calculate(yaw, 0.0) is negative when the yaw is positive (tag to the right), so turn is positive and the left side drives forward: the robot turns right. If your yaw sign turns out the other way, remove the minus sign. Test with the wheels off the ground first. This uses a new DriveSubsystem method, because aimAtTagCommand lives outside it: /** Drives each side at a voltage. Keeps DifferentialDrive's safety timer fed. */ public void tankDriveVolts ( double leftVolts , double rightVolts ) { m_leftLeader . setVoltage ( leftVolts ); m_rightLeader . setVoltage ( rightVolts ); m_drive . feed (); } Why m_latestFront instead of asking the camera? getAllUnreadResults() hands each frame out only once . If the aim command read frames itself, periodic() would miss them and the pose estimator would lose those measurements. So periodic() reads every frame and keeps the newest one for aiming. (PhotonLib's older getLatestResult() is deprecated and will be removed.)"},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Is the camera's pose any good?","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#is-the-cameras-pose-any-good","t":"Before fusing, test the camera alone. Park the robot at a spot you've measured (in the shop, measure from a tag taped to the wall), then compare Vision/Front/Pose with reality: Real pose (measured) Camera pose Error If the error is the same everywhere, the camera mount transform is off. If it gets worse with distance, recalibrate the camera and skip far-away tags."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Going further","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#going-further","t":"PhotonPoseEstimator does the tag-to-robot math for you, and can combine several tags in one frame (multi-tag), which is much more accurate than one tag. Turn on multi-tag in the PhotonVision pipeline settings and see the PhotonLib docs. Simulation: PhotonLib's VisionSystemSim and PhotonCameraSim fake a camera in the WPILib simulator, so you can test vision code without a robot."},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Try it","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#try-it","t":"Set up the coprocessor: team number, camera nickname, calibration, AprilTag pipeline in 3D mode Hold a tag to the left and right of the camera and write down the sign of the yaw Measure the camera's mounting transform on the robot Add VisionSubsystem and swap Lab 11's odometry for DifferentialDrivePoseEstimator Park the robot at a measured spot and compare the camera's pose with reality Push the robot by hand so odometry is wrong, then point the camera at a tag. Watch the pose slide back. Challenge: add a second camera, angled out to the side like the codecamp robot"},{"p":"18. Vision: AprilTags and PhotonVision","h":"Check yourself","u":"going-further/18-vision.html#check-yourself","t":"Why not just use the camera's pose whenever it sees a tag? Vision is noisy and sometimes wrong. Snapping to it makes the pose jitter, and one bad frame teleports the robot. The pose estimator blends it in gradually. What does a smaller standard deviation mean? More trust. The estimator moves the pose further toward a measurement with a small standard deviation. Why does the standard deviation grow with distance squared? Far-away tags are only a few pixels across, so a tiny pixel error becomes a big pose error. Trust should drop quickly with distance. Why skip targets with high pose ambiguity? A single tag can look almost the same from two different spots. High ambiguity means PhotonVision can't tell which spot is right, so the pose could be badly wrong. Why does addVisionMeasurement need the picture's timestamp? The picture was taken a little while ago. The estimator applies the correction at the moment the picture was taken, then replays the movement since. The camera's pose is always 20 cm too far left, everywhere on the field. What's wrong? The camera's mounting transform is wrong. A consistent offset points to the mount measurements. The robot code crashes with a NullPointerException, but only when a camera sees a tag. What's the likely cause? The vision subsystem's constructor didn't save the drive subsystem, so m_drive is null when it tries to add a measurement. The vision subsystem follows the approach of PhotonVisionSubsystem.java from the NMRC code camp (level 2, 2026), adapted for a differential drive. Parts of the fusion and calibration explanations are adapted from Mantik by Itkan Robotics (CC BY 4.0). API details follow PhotonLib 2026's source."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Why it matters","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#why-it-matters","t":"The autos from Lesson 11 and Lab 8 are lists of moves: drive 1 m, turn 90°, drive 1 m. Each move stops before the next one starts, and every stop wastes time. PathPlanner lets you draw a smooth path on a picture of the field and have the robot follow it at full speed, curving through turns without stopping. You can drop actions onto the path (\"start the intake here\") and chain paths into whole autos, all without changing robot code. PathPlanner needs to know where the robot is, so it builds on odometry from Lesson 17 and Lab 11 . It also needs to drive the wheels at exact speeds , which takes feedforward from Lesson 13 and SysId ."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Read this","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#read-this","t":"PathPlanner documentation PathPlannerLib: Build an Auto PathPlannerLib Java API Licenses PathPlanner (the app), PathPlannerLib (the vendor library), and the PathPlanner documentation are all in one repository, mjansen4857/pathplanner , under the MIT License (© Michael Jansen). What License What it means for you PathPlannerLib (in your robot code) MIT Use it freely. Keep the copyright notice if you copy its source files. PathPlanner app MIT Free to install and use. PathPlanner documentation (pathplanner.dev) MIT (same repository) You may copy and adapt it if you keep the copyright and license notice."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Walkthrough","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"The two halves","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#the-two-halves","t":"Part Runs on What it does PathPlanner (the app) Your laptop Draw paths and autos; set the robot's size, weight, and speed limits. Saves files into your project's src/main/deploy/pathplanner/ folder. PathPlannerLib (vendor library) The robot Reads those files and turns them into commands that follow the paths. Download the app from the PathPlanner releases or the Microsoft Store. Install the library like REVLib: WPILib: Manage Vendor Libraries → Install new libraries (online) , and pick PathplannerLib from the list (or paste the JSON link from the PathPlanner docs)."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Words PathPlanner uses","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#words-pathplanner-uses","t":"Word Meaning Path One smooth drive from A to B, drawn with waypoints you drag around Auto A whole autonomous routine: paths in order, plus commands between them Named command A robot command you give a name, like \"Raise Elevator\" , so you can use it in the app Event marker A spot on a path where a named command starts, while the robot keeps driving Constraints Max speed, max acceleration, and max turning speed for a path"},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"What PathPlanner needs from the drivetrain","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#what-pathplanner-needs-from-the-drivetrain","t":"PathPlannerLib's AutoBuilder needs four things from your drive subsystem: It needs Method You have it from Where the robot is getPose() Lab 11 A way to set where the robot is resetPose(Pose2d) Lab 11 ( resetPosition ) How fast it's moving right now getChassisSpeeds() New: encoder rates A way to drive at a speed driveRobotRelative(ChassisSpeeds) New: feedforward + PID ChassisSpeeds is WPILib's way to say how the whole robot moves: forward speed (m/s), sideways speed (always 0 for a tank drive), and turning speed (rad/s). The kinematics object converts between that and left/right wheel speeds: flowchart LR CS[\"ChassisSpeeds<br/>forward m/s, turn rad/s\"] -- \"toWheelSpeeds()\" --> WS[\"Left m/s, right m/s\"] WS -- \"toChassisSpeeds()\" --> CS"},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Step 1: Characterize the drivetrain","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#step-1-characterize-the-drivetrain","t":"To drive at an exact speed, each side needs feedforward : kS (volts to overcome friction), kV (volts per m/s), and kA (volts per m/s²). Run SysId on the drivetrain ( Lesson 13.1 ) with the robot on the floor, and put the results in Constants.java : // From SysId, drivetrain test (Lesson 19). These are examples: use your own numbers. public static final double kS = 0.15 ; // volts public static final double kV = 2.60 ; // volts per (m/s) public static final double kA = 0.40 ; // volts per (m/s²) public static final double kVelocityP = 1.0 ; // extra volts per (m/s) of speed error No SysId yet? You can estimate kV: drive straight at a known voltage (say 6 V) and read the speed from getRate() . kV ≈ (6 − kS) ÷ speed. It's rough, but enough to get a first path moving. Use SysId before competition."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Step 2: Speeds in and out","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#step-2-speeds-in-and-out","t":"In DriveSubsystem.java , add the kinematics, one feedforward, and a speed PID controller for each side: // Converts between whole-robot speeds and left/right wheel speeds private final DifferentialDriveKinematics m_kinematics = new DifferentialDriveKinematics ( kDriveConstants . TrackWidthMeters ); // Voltage needed for a speed (from SysId), plus a little PID to fix what's left private final SimpleMotorFeedforward m_feedforward = new SimpleMotorFeedforward ( kDriveConstants . kS , kDriveConstants . kV , kDriveConstants . kA ); private final PIDController m_leftSpeedPID = new PIDController ( kDriveConstants . kVelocityP , 0 , 0 ); private final PIDController m_rightSpeedPID = new PIDController ( kDriveConstants . kVelocityP , 0 , 0 ); Use the effective track width you measured in Lab 11, Step 9. PathPlanner turns better with it. Then three methods: /** How fast the robot is moving right now, from the encoder rates. */ public ChassisSpeeds getChassisSpeeds () { return m_kinematics . toChassisSpeeds ( new DifferentialDriveWheelSpeeds ( m_leftEncoder . getRate (), m_rightEncoder . getRate ())); } /** Drives at a whole-robot speed. PathPlanner calls this every loop while following a path. */ public void driveRobotRelative ( ChassisSpeeds speeds ) { DifferentialDriveWheelSpeeds wheels = m_kinematics . toWheelSpeeds ( speeds ); double leftVolts = m_feedforward . calculate ( wheels . leftMetersPerSecond ) + m_leftSpeedPID . calculate ( m_leftEncoder . getRate (), wheels . leftMetersPerSecond ); double rightVolts = m_feedforward . calculate ( wheels . rightMetersPerSecond ) + m_rightSpeedPID . calculate ( m_rightEncoder . getRate (), wheels . rightMetersPerSecond ); m_leftLeader . setVoltage ( leftVolts ); m_rightLeader . setVoltage ( rightVolts ); m_drive . feed (); // tell DifferentialDrive's safety timer the motors are being driven } /** Tells odometry the robot is at this pose. PathPlanner calls it at the start of an auto. */ public void resetPose ( Pose2d pose ) { m_odometry . resetPosition ( m_gyro . getRotation2d (), getLeftMeters (), getRightMeters (), pose ); } getRate() is in meters per second because Lab 8 set the encoders' distance per pulse in meters. setVoltage respects the right side's inversion from Lab 2, so positive volts drive both sides forward. Test before the first path Put the robot on blocks and call driveRobotRelative(new ChassisSpeeds(1.0, 0, 0)) from a button with run(...) . Both encoder rates should read about 1.0 m/s. If one side reads negative, or the speed is way off, fix that first: PathPlanner can't follow a path with a drivetrain that can't hold a speed."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Step 3: Tell PathPlanner about the robot","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#step-3-tell-pathplanner-about-the-robot","t":"Open the PathPlanner app, open your robot project folder, and go to Settings . Fill in Robot Config : Setting WCD value Drive mode Differential (not holonomic) Robot mass Weigh it, with bumpers and battery Trackwidth Your effective track width from Lab 11 Wheel radius Half of WheelDiameterMeters Drive gearing Your gearbox ratio (motor turns per wheel turn) True max drive speed Measure: full voltage, straight line, read getRate() Wheel COF 1.2 is a common starting value for treaded wheels on carpet Drive motor CIM , 2 per side Drive current limit The CurrentLimit from Constants PathPlanner saves this to src/main/deploy/pathplanner/settings.json , and the robot reads it with RobotConfig.fromGUISettings() . Keep the robot config honest PathPlanner uses these numbers to predict how the robot will move. A mass from last year's robot, or a wrong max speed, makes every path track a little wrong, and it looks exactly like bad tuning. Update the config when the robot changes."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Step 4: Configure AutoBuilder","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#step-4-configure-autobuilder","t":"At the end of the DriveSubsystem constructor: // Let PathPlanner drive this subsystem (Lesson 19) try { RobotConfig config = RobotConfig . fromGUISettings (); // reads the app's Robot Config AutoBuilder . configure ( this :: getPose , // where the robot is this :: resetPose , // set where the robot is this :: getChassisSpeeds , // how fast it's moving ( speeds , feedforwards ) -> driveRobotRelative ( speeds ), // drive at a speed new PPLTVController ( 0.02 ), // the path-following controller for tank drives (20 ms loop) config , // Flip paths to the red side when we're red. The field origin stays on the blue side. () -> DriverStation . getAlliance (). orElse ( Alliance . Blue ) == Alliance . Red , this ); // following a path requires this subsystem } catch ( Exception e ) { DriverStation . reportError ( \"PathPlanner config didn't load: \" + e . getMessage (), e . getStackTrace ()); } PPLTVController is the controller for differential drives. It watches how far the robot is from the path (ahead, behind, to the side, wrong heading) and adjusts the speeds to get back on. Swerve robots use PPHolonomicDriveController instead."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Step 5: Draw a path","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#step-5-draw-a-path","t":"In the app: Paths → + to make a path. Drag the start and end points. Drag the round handles to bend the curve. Set Global Constraints low for the first test: 1.0 m/s and 1.0 m/s². Autos → + to make an auto. Set its starting pose to where the robot will start, then add your path. Draw a simple first auto: 2 m straight ahead. Then an S-curve. Then something that ends back where it started. Starting pose PathPlanner autos reset odometry to the auto's starting pose when they begin, so the robot must really be there. In the shop, put a tape mark on the floor and treat it as that spot."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Step 6: Named commands","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#step-6-named-commands","t":"To run a mechanism during an auto, register it by name before building any autos. In RobotContainer 's constructor, first thing after the subsystems exist: // Commands PathPlanner autos can use by name (register these before building any auto) NamedCommands . registerCommand ( \"Elevator to L2\" , m_elevator . goToPresetCommand ( Preset . L2 )); NamedCommands . registerCommand ( \"Elevator to Start\" , m_elevator . goToPresetCommand ( Preset . START )); In the app, add a Named Command block to an auto (between paths), or an event marker on a path (runs while driving), and type the same name."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Step 7: Pick an auto in Elastic","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#step-7-pick-an-auto-in-elastic","t":"Lesson 11's Selectable menu still works. Add every PathPlanner auto to it by name: m_autoChooser . addDefault ( \"Do nothing\" , Commands . none ()); m_autoChooser . add ( \"Square\" , squareAuto ()); for ( String name : AutoBuilder . getAllAutoNames ()) { m_autoChooser . add ( \"PP: \" + name , new PathPlannerAuto ( name )); // every auto drawn in the app } Tunables . publish ( \"Auto\" , m_autoChooser ); getAllAutoNames() reads the auto files deployed with your code, so a new auto drawn in the app shows up in the menu after the next deploy. PathPlanner also has AutoBuilder.buildAutoChooser() , but it makes a SendableChooser , which this course doesn't use."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Step 8: Warm up","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#step-8-warm-up","t":"The first time Java runs path-following code, it loads a lot of classes, and the first path can stutter. Pay that cost while the robot is disabled. At the end of the Robot constructor: // Load PathPlanner's code now so the first auto path doesn't stutter CommandScheduler . getInstance (). schedule ( FollowPathCommand . warmupCommand ());"},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Step 9: Run it and watch","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#step-9-run-it-and-watch","t":"Deploy, select the auto in Elastic, and run autonomous. Watch the field map: the robot should follow the path's shape. If it... Check Doesn't move The console for \"PathPlanner config didn't load\"; the auto is selected in Elastic Drives the right shape but too short or long Wheel diameter (Lab 11, Step 9) Turns too much or too little Track width: use the effective one Lags behind the path, then catches up kV too low; robot config max speed too high Wobbles side to side Speed PID too high, or acceleration constraint too high for the carpet Log the target pose and the real pose so you can see where they split apart. PathPlanner publishes its current path and target pose to NetworkTables under /PathPlanner/ automatically; AdvantageScope can draw them on the field next to the robot."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Going further","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#going-further","t":"On-the-fly paths: AutoBuilder.pathfindToPose(targetPose, constraints) drives to a pose from wherever the robot is, avoiding field obstacles. Great with vision ( Lesson 18 ). Choreo: another path tool that computes the fastest possible path for your robot. PathPlannerLib can follow Choreo paths too."},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Try it","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#try-it","t":"Run SysId on the drivetrain and put kS, kV, kA in Constants With the robot on blocks, check driveRobotRelative holds 1.0 m/s on both sides Fill in PathPlanner's Robot Config with real measurements Follow a 2 m straight path, then an S-curve Add an event marker that raises the elevator while driving Make one auto in the app, deploy, and pick it from the Elastic menu Time Lab 8's sensor square, then draw a smooth \"square\" in PathPlanner and time it"},{"p":"19. Autos with PathPlanner","h":"Check yourself","u":"going-further/19-pathplanner.html#check-yourself","t":"What does PathPlanner need from the drivetrain? The pose, a way to reset the pose, the current speeds, and a way to drive at a requested speed. Why does PathPlanner need feedforward? It asks for exact wheel speeds. Feedforward turns a speed into the voltage that produces it; PID only fixes the small error left over. Why register named commands before building autos? Building an auto looks up each name. A name that isn't registered yet becomes a command that does nothing. Which controller does a tank drive use, and which does swerve use? PPLTVController for differential (tank) drives; PPHolonomicDriveController for swerve. The robot drives the right shape, but every path ends 10% short. What's wrong? The wheel diameter (distance per pulse) is off by about 10%, so odometry thinks the robot went farther than it did. Parts of this lesson are adapted from the PathPlanner lesson of Mantik by Itkan Robotics (CC BY 4.0), rewritten for a differential drive. API details follow PathPlannerLib 2026's source and documentation."},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"Why it matters","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#why-it-matters","t":"Something went wrong in a match. The elevator stopped short, or the auto drove into a wall. The robot is already back in the pit, and nobody saw exactly what happened. If the robot was logging , you can find out. A log records every value the robot published, every loop, for the whole match. AdvantageScope opens that log and lets you scrub through the match like a video, with graphs of every value and the robot drawn on the field. Lesson 9 introduced logging. This lesson shows how to use the logs well, and what AdvantageKit adds when your team is ready for it."},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"Read this","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#read-this","t":"On-Robot Telemetry Recording (WPILib DataLogManager) AdvantageScope documentation AdvantageKit documentation"},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"Walkthrough","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"Step 1: Turn on logging","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#step-1-turn-on-logging","t":"In Robot.java 's constructor, before anything else: // Record everything published to NetworkTables into a .wpilog file DataLogManager . start (); // Also record the Driver Station: enabled/disabled, match time, and every joystick DriverStation . startDataLog ( DataLogManager . getLog ()); That's all. Everything the course's telemetry library sends ( /Telemetry/... ), every tunable ( /Tunables/... ), the field map, and the joysticks now go into a log file. Where the log goes When A USB stick in the roboRIO, folder logs If a stick is plugged in. Do this at competition. /home/lvuser/logs on the roboRIO If there's no stick. The roboRIO deletes old logs when it runs low on space. A logs folder in your project In simulation The log file is named for the date and, at an event, the match number, like FRC_20260321_143507_MIC_Q12.wpilog ."},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"Step 2: Get the log","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#step-2-get-the-log","t":"AdvantageScope: File → Download Logs , connect to the robot, pick the file. Easiest. USB stick: pull it out after the match and copy the file. AdvantageScope comes with the WPILib installer. Look for it in the WPILib tools folder or start menu."},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"Step 3: Read a log in AdvantageScope","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#step-3-read-a-log-in-advantagescope","t":"File → Open Log . The left side lists every value in the log as a tree, built from the slashes in the names: Telemetry/Drive/Encoders/Left (m) sits under Telemetry → Drive → Encoders . The tabs across the top are different views. The ones you'll use most: Tab Use it for Line Graph Drag values onto it to graph them over time. Put a target and its measurement on the same graph. 2D Field / 3D Field Drag a Pose2d onto it to draw the robot on the field. Watch odometry, vision poses, and paths. Table Every value at one moment Console Everything printed, errors included, lined up with the graphs Joysticks What the driver was pressing Click anywhere on the timeline to jump to that moment; every tab follows. Live, too File → Connect to Robot shows the same views live, over NetworkTables, while the robot runs. Great for tuning."},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"The habit that makes logs useful: target next to measured","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#the-habit-that-makes-logs-useful-target-next-to-measured","t":"Most debugging questions are \"did it do what we told it to?\" You can only answer that if both halves are in the log. The labs already do this: Lab Target Measured Lab 9 Wheel/Target (rot) Wheel/Position (rot) Lab 10 Elevator/Goal (m) and Elevator/Setpoint (m) Elevator/Height (m) Lab 8 Drive/Gyro/Turn Target (deg) Drive/Gyro/Heading (deg) Lesson 19 /PathPlanner/targetPose Drive/Odometry/Pose Drag a pair onto one Line Graph. Overshoot, lag, a stall, or a target that never changed are all obvious once they're drawn together."},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"Seeing the pose","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#seeing-the-pose","t":"Lab 11 logs Drive/Odometry/Pose as a whole Pose2d (a struct ). In AdvantageScope: Open the 2D Field tab and pick this year's field. Drag Telemetry/Drive/Odometry/Pose onto it. It appears as a robot. Drag /PathPlanner/activePath on too, as a Trajectory , during an auto. Now you can see exactly where the robot left the path. With vision ( Lesson 18 ), the vision subsystem logs each camera's pose as Telemetry/Vision/Front/Pose . Drag it on as a Ghost . You'll see the camera's guess and the fused pose side by side."},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"Name things so you can find them","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#name-things-so-you-can-find-them","t":"A value nobody can find during a match might as well not exist. Start every name with the subsystem: Elevator/... , Drive/... . The library's Telemetry.getTable(\"Elevator\") does this for you. Put the units in the name: Height (m) , Heading (deg) . Log why , not only what : a count like Vision/Front/Measurements Used , or the name of the current preset, explains the jumps in the graphs."},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"Which code was on the robot?","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#which-code-was-on-the-robot","t":"When a log shows something strange, the first question is \"what code was running?\" WPILib can stamp the code version into every log. Add the gversion plugin to the plugins block of build.gradle ; on every build it writes the git commit into a generated BuildConstants.java . Then log it once at startup: // Once, in the Robot constructor, after DataLogManager.start() Telemetry . log ( \"Metadata/Git SHA\" , BuildConstants . GIT_SHA ); Telemetry . log ( \"Metadata/Git Dirty\" , BuildConstants . DIRTY == 1 ); // true = uncommitted changes See the gversion section of the WPILib docs (Including Git Data in Deploy) for the exact Gradle lines. Even without it, commit before every competition deploy ( Lesson 15 ), so the code on the robot matches a commit."},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"AdvantageKit: logging that can replay your code","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#advantagekit-logging-that-can-replay-your-code","t":"Everything above logs outputs : values your code chose to publish. AdvantageKit , from Team 6328, goes further. It logs every input to your code (every sensor reading, every joystick value) in a way that lets you replay a match in simulation. Your real robot code runs again on a laptop, fed the exact sensor values from the match, so you can add new logging or test a fix against what really happened. To make replay possible, AdvantageKit asks you to structure every subsystem around an IO layer : an interface that hides the hardware. /** Everything the elevator reads from hardware. AdvantageKit logs all of it, every loop. */ @AutoLog public class ElevatorIOInputs { public double heightMeters = 0.0 ; public double velocityMetersPerSec = 0.0 ; public double appliedVolts = 0.0 ; public double currentAmps = 0.0 ; } /** The hardware, hidden behind an interface. */ public interface ElevatorIO { default void updateInputs ( ElevatorIOInputs inputs ) {} default void setVoltage ( double volts ) {} } Then there are several versions of the hardware: Class Used when Reads from ElevatorIOSparkMax On the real robot The SPARK MAX and encoder ElevatorIOSim In simulation A WPILib ElevatorSim physics model (none: the empty interface) In replay The log file: AdvantageKit fills the inputs in The subsystem never touches hardware directly: @Override public void periodic () { m_io . updateInputs ( m_inputs ); // read the hardware (or the log, in replay) Logger . processInputs ( \"Elevator\" , m_inputs ); // log it, or replace it with logged values // ...use m_inputs.heightMeters instead of m_encoder.getDistance()... } The rules that make replay work: Rule Why Read hardware only inside updateInputs Anything read elsewhere isn't logged, so replay can't feed it back Inputs go in @AutoLog classes; computed values go to Logger.recordOutput(...) Inputs are fed back during replay; outputs are just recorded No randomness or wall-clock time in your logic Replay must make the same choices the robot did The robot class extends LoggedRobot instead of TimedRobot AdvantageKit controls the loop timing so replay can run faster than real time When should a team switch? AdvantageKit is an architecture, not an add-on. Every subsystem changes shape, and you start from AdvantageKit's template projects rather than the WPILib ones. It pays off when your team has a working robot and wants to debug matches seriously. Learn the WPILib way first (this course), then try AdvantageKit on an off-season robot. The AdvantageKit docs have template projects and a full walkthrough."},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"Try it","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#try-it","t":"Add DataLogManager.start() and DriverStation.startDataLog(...) to the WestCoastDrive project Drive for two minutes, download the log, and open it in AdvantageScope Graph Lab 8's turn target and heading on one Line Graph during a turn Drag the odometry pose onto the 2D Field and replay your drive Find the moment you pressed a button in the Joysticks tab, and see what the robot did next Challenge: sketch an ElevatorIO interface for the Lab 10 elevator. Which values belong in the inputs?"},{"p":"20. Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit","h":"Check yourself","u":"going-further/20-logging.html#check-yourself","t":"Where should the log go at competition, and why? On a USB stick in the roboRIO. The roboRIO's own storage is small and deletes old logs; a stick holds a whole event and can be pulled out after a match. Why log a target next to its measurement? Most problems are \"it didn't do what we told it.\" Graphed together, overshoot, lag, and stalls are obvious. What's the difference between an input and an output in AdvantageKit? An input is read from hardware and is fed back during replay. An output is something your code computed; it's recorded but not fed back. Why must AdvantageKit code read hardware only in updateInputs ? Only inputs are logged and replayed. A sensor read anywhere else would have no value during replay, and the replay wouldn't match the match. Parts of this lesson are adapted from the AdvantageScope, AdvantageKit, and Hardware Abstraction with IO Layers lessons of Mantik by Itkan Robotics (CC BY 4.0)."},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Why it matters","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#why-it-matters","t":"The West Coast Drive can go forward, backward, and turn. To move sideways it has to turn, drive, and turn back. A defender who knows that can pin it against a wall. A swerve drive has four wheels that each steer on their own. Point all four wheels sideways and the robot slides sideways. Point them in a circle and it spins in place. Do both at once and it can drive across the field in a straight line while spinning to face a goal. Most competitive FRC robots now drive swerve. You won't write swerve from scratch: teams start from a vendor template. This lesson explains what the template is doing, using everything from Lessons 12–19, so you can read it, tune it, and fix it."},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Read this","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#read-this","t":"Swerve Drive Kinematics (WPILib) Swerve Drive Odometry (WPILib) Your module's template: CTRE Tuner X Swerve Generator (Kraken/Falcon + CANcoder), REV MAXSwerve template (NEO/Vortex), or YAGSL (many combinations)"},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Walkthrough","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"What's in a swerve module","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#whats-in-a-swerve-module","t":"Each corner of the robot is a module with: Part Job Control Drive motor Spins the wheel Velocity control: feedforward + PID ( Lessons 12–13 ) Steer motor (azimuth) Turns the wheel to point a direction Position control: PID Absolute encoder (CANcoder, or a Through Bore in absolute mode) Knows which way the wheel points, even right after power-on Read by the steer controller So a swerve drive is just four velocity controllers and four position controllers , working together. Every idea in it is one you've already used."},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Holonomic driving","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#holonomic-driving","t":"A tank drive takes two numbers: left and right. A swerve drive takes three, as a ChassisSpeeds (from Lesson 19 ): Number Meaning Tank drive? vx Forward speed (m/s) Yes vy Sideways speed, left is positive (m/s) Always 0 omega Turning speed, counterclockwise is positive (rad/s) Yes A drivetrain that can do all three independently is called holonomic ."},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Kinematics: robot speed → four wheel states","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#kinematics-robot-speed-four-wheel-states","t":"SwerveDriveKinematics needs to know where each module is, measured from the center of the robot (+X forward, +Y left): // Module positions from the center of the robot, in meters. Order: FL, FR, BL, BR. private final SwerveDriveKinematics m_kinematics = new SwerveDriveKinematics ( new Translation2d ( 0.28 , 0.28 ), // front left new Translation2d ( 0.28 , - 0.28 ), // front right new Translation2d ( - 0.28 , 0.28 ), // back left new Translation2d ( - 0.28 , - 0.28 )); // back right Give it a ChassisSpeeds and it returns four SwerveModuleState s: a speed and an angle for each wheel. /** Drives the robot. x and y in m/s, turn in rad/s. */ public void drive ( double xSpeed , double ySpeed , double turnSpeed , boolean fieldRelative ) { ChassisSpeeds speeds = new ChassisSpeeds ( xSpeed , ySpeed , turnSpeed ); if ( fieldRelative ) { // Joystick \"forward\" means \"away from the driver\", whichever way the robot faces speeds = ChassisSpeeds . fromFieldRelativeSpeeds ( speeds , getHeading ()); } // Correct for the robot turning during the 20 ms step, so it doesn't drift sideways while spinning speeds = ChassisSpeeds . discretize ( speeds , 0.02 ); SwerveModuleState [] states = m_kinematics . toSwerveModuleStates ( speeds ); // If any wheel would have to go faster than it can, slow all four down by the same ratio SwerveDriveKinematics . desaturateWheelSpeeds ( states , kMaxSpeedMetersPerSecond ); for ( int i = 0 ; i < 4 ; i ++ ) { m_modules [ i ] . setDesiredState ( states [ i ] ); } } The order FL, FR, BL, BR must be the same everywhere: in the kinematics, the module array, and the odometry. Swap two and the robot drives confidently in the wrong direction."},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Field-relative driving","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#field-relative-driving","t":"Robot-relative: pushing the stick forward drives toward the robot's front. Hard to drive once the robot has turned around. Field-relative: pushing the stick forward drives away from the driver , no matter which way the robot faces. fromFieldRelativeSpeeds uses the gyro heading to rotate the joystick's direction into the robot's frame. Almost every team drives field-relative. Red alliance WPILib's field +X points away from the blue wall. On red, \"away from the driver\" is −X, so field-relative driving must add 180° to the heading (or flip the joystick) when the alliance is red. Templates handle this; check it on yours before a red match."},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Inside one module","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#inside-one-module","t":"Each module turns its desired state into two motor commands: /** Point the wheel and spin it at the requested state. */ public void setDesiredState ( SwerveModuleState desired ) { Rotation2d current = getSteerAngle (); // Never turn the wheel more than 90°: spinning the wheel backward is the same as turning it 180° desired . optimize ( current ); // Slow the wheel down while it's still turning toward the right direction desired . cosineScale ( current ); // Drive motor: velocity control (feedforward + PID), like Lesson 19's tank drive double driveVolts = m_driveFeedforward . calculate ( desired . speedMetersPerSecond ) + m_drivePID . calculate ( getDriveVelocity (), desired . speedMetersPerSecond ); // Steer motor: position control. Continuous input makes -179° and +179° neighbors. double steerVolts = m_steerPID . calculate ( current . getRadians (), desired . angle . getRadians ()); m_driveMotor . setVoltage ( driveVolts ); m_steerMotor . setVoltage ( steerVolts ); } Line Why optimize If the wheel points at 0° and you ask for 170°, it's faster to point it at −10° and spin backward. The wheel never has to turn more than 90°. cosineScale While the wheel is still 60° from where it should point, driving it at full speed would push the robot the wrong way. Scaling by the cosine of the error makes it wait. Continuous input m_steerPID.enableContinuousInput(-Math.PI, Math.PI) tells the PID that the angle wraps around, so it goes the short way from 179° to −179° (2°), not the long way (358°). Real templates usually run both loops on the motor controller (Phoenix 6 or REVLib closed-loop control) instead of on the roboRIO, because it's faster. The ideas are the same."},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Absolute encoder offsets","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#absolute-encoder-offsets","t":"Every steer encoder reads some angle when the wheel points straight ahead, and it's different for every module. That reading is the module's offset . Get it wrong and that wheel points the wrong way, and the robot fights itself. Templates have a procedure for this: point all four wheels straight ahead (bevel gears all facing the same side), then read and save the offsets. CTRE's Tuner X generator does it for you. Redo it any time a module is taken apart or swapped."},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Odometry and the rest","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#odometry-and-the-rest","t":"Swerve odometry works like the WCD's ( Lesson 17 ), with one difference: each module reports how far it rolled and which way it pointed, as a SwerveModulePosition . SwerveDrivePoseEstimator replaces DifferentialDrivePoseEstimator for vision ( Lesson 18 ). For PathPlanner ( Lesson 19 ), set Holonomic mode in the app and use PPHolonomicDriveController instead of PPLTVController . Holonomic paths can turn the robot independently of the direction it's driving. In AdvantageScope's Swerve tab ( Lesson 20 ), log the desired and measured SwerveModuleState[] and you'll see four arrows: what each module was told to do and what it actually did."},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Getting started on a real swerve","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#getting-started-on-a-real-swerve","t":"Pick the template for your modules (see \"Read this\"). Enter the hardware: CAN IDs, gear ratios, wheel diameter, module positions. Set the encoder offsets with the wheels pointing straight. Check with the robot on blocks: push forward on the stick, and all four wheels should point forward and spin forward. Then left, then rotate. Fix one thing at a time. Tune the steer PID first (snappy, no wobble), then the drive feedforward (SysId) and PID. Measure the real max speed and put it in the template and in PathPlanner's robot config."},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Try it","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#try-it","t":"On paper: the robot drives forward at 1 m/s with no turning. What angle and speed does each module get? On paper: the robot spins in place counterclockwise. Which way does each wheel point? Open a swerve template for your team's modules and find the kinematics, optimize , and the encoder offsets Run a template in simulation and drive it field-relative with an Xbox controller"},{"p":"21. Swerve Drive","h":"Check yourself","u":"going-further/21-swerve.html#check-yourself","t":"What does 'holonomic' mean? The robot can move forward, sideways, and turn, all independently and at the same time. Why does optimize never turn a wheel more than 90°? Pointing the wheel 180° the other way and spinning it backward moves the robot the same way. So any turn over 90° can be replaced by a smaller turn plus reversing the wheel. What's the difference between robot-relative and field-relative driving? Robot-relative: the stick's forward is the robot's front. Field-relative: the stick's forward is away from the driver, whichever way the robot faces. Field-relative needs the gyro. One wheel always points 30° off from the others. What's the first thing to check? That module's absolute encoder offset. A swerve module is two motors. What kind of control does each use? The drive motor uses velocity control; the steer motor uses position control. Parts of this lesson are adapted from the Swerve Drive Example and Odometry lessons of Mantik by Itkan Robotics (CC BY 4.0)."},{"p":"Where to Go Next","h":"","u":"reference/next-steps.html","t":"Once you're comfortable with Lessons 0–16, these are the topics most teams tackle next. Lessons 17–21 ( Going Further ) cover the first five. They're listed roughly in the order teams usually learn them, with the best place to start for each."},{"p":"Where to Go Next","h":"Odometry and pose estimation","u":"reference/next-steps.html#odometry-and-pose-estimation","t":"Odometry tracks where the robot is on the field by combining wheel encoders and the gyro. It's the foundation for path following and auto-aiming. Start: Lesson 17 and Lab 11 , then WPILib's Kinematics and Odometry Next level: pose estimation , which corrects odometry drift with camera measurements of AprilTags. See State-Space and Pose Estimators ."},{"p":"Where to Go Next","h":"Swerve drive","u":"reference/next-steps.html#swerve-drive","t":"Most competitive teams now drive swerve: each wheel steers and drives independently, so the robot can move in any direction while turning. Start: Lesson 21 Then: the swerve kinematics and odometry pages in the WPILib kinematics section above Then: your swerve module vendor's docs, and their example code Many teams start from a generated template, such as CTRE's swerve project generator in Phoenix Tuner, rather than writing swerve from scratch Everything from Lessons 10–14 still applies: a swerve module is a drive motor with velocity control and a steering motor with position control."},{"p":"Where to Go Next","h":"Vision","u":"reference/next-steps.html#vision","t":"Cameras let the robot see AprilTags (to know where it is) and game pieces (to aim or drive to them). Start: Lesson 18 PhotonVision AprilTag introduction (WPILib)"},{"p":"Where to Go Next","h":"Path following","u":"reference/next-steps.html#path-following","t":"Instead of \"drive 2 meters, turn 90°,\" draw a smooth path on a field diagram and let the robot follow it. Start: Lesson 19 PathPlanner : the most widely used tool, with a path editor, auto builder, and on-the-fly pathfinding Choreo : generates time-optimal paths"},{"p":"Where to Go Next","h":"Characterization","u":"reference/next-steps.html#characterization","t":"System Identification (SysId) , covered in Lesson 13.1 ReCalc : estimate gear ratios, speeds, and feedforward gains before the mechanism is built"},{"p":"Where to Go Next","h":"Logging and replay","u":"reference/next-steps.html#logging-and-replay","t":"Start: Lesson 20 AdvantageKit : logs every input so you can replay a match and re-run your code against it AdvantageScope : graphs, 3D field views, and log analysis"},{"p":"Where to Go Next","h":"Vendor libraries","u":"reference/next-steps.html#vendor-libraries","t":"Your team's motors run through vendor libraries. Read the docs for whichever you use: CTRE Phoenix 6 (TalonFX, Kraken, CANcoder, Pigeon 2) REVLib (SPARK MAX, SPARK Flex, NEO) Learn from other teams Many top teams publish their robot code on GitHub, and some write up their software design each season. Reading a championship robot's code after finishing this course is one of the best ways to level up. Pick one mechanism and figure out how they did it."},{"p":"Glossary","h":"","u":"reference/glossary.html","t":"Term Meaning Lesson Absolute encoder An encoder that reports the shaft's true angle, even right after power-on 8 Autonomous (auto) The first 15 seconds of a match, with no driver input 11 Branch A separate line of work in Git 15 Brownout Battery voltage drops so low the roboRIO disables motor outputs 4 CAN The two-wire bus most motor controllers use to talk to the roboRIO 4 CI Continuous integration: automatically building and testing every change 16 Clone Download a full copy of a Git repository 1 Closed loop Control that measures and corrects (feedback) 12 Command An action that uses one or more subsystems 7 Command scheduler The part of WPILib that runs commands and checks triggers every loop 7.1 Commit A saved snapshot of your code in Git 1 Composition A command built from other commands, like a sequence or parallel group 11 Constraints A motion profile's max velocity and max acceleration 14 Current limit A cap on how many amps a motor can draw, set in code 4 Deadband A small range around zero that's treated as exactly zero 10 Default command The command a subsystem runs when nothing else is using it 7, 7.1 Elastic The drag-and-drop driver dashboard that comes with WPILib 6.1 Encoder A sensor that measures shaft rotation 8 Error Setpoint minus measurement 12 Feedforward Predicting the needed output from a model of the mechanism 13 Gain A tuning constant (kP, kV, etc.) 12 Goal Where a motion profile finally ends up 14 Gradle The build tool WPILib projects use ( ./gradlew ) 2 Gyro / IMU A sensor that measures the robot's rotation and heading 8 Integral windup The I term building up too much while stuck, then causing overshoot 12 kS, kV, kA, kG Feedforward gains for friction, velocity, acceleration, and gravity 13 Lambda A small piece of code passed around to run later: () -> ... 3 Loop overrun The robot's code took longer than its 20 ms loop 6 Measurement The value a sensor reports right now 12 Merge conflict Two changes to the same lines that Git can't combine automatically 1 Motion profile A planned smooth path of position and velocity over time 14 NetworkTables The system that sends values between the robot and dashboards 6.1, 9 Open loop Control without checking a sensor 12 Publisher A NetworkTables object that sends values on one topic 6.1 Pull request (PR) A request to merge a branch, with review 15 Requirement A command declaring that it uses a subsystem 7.1 roboRIO The robot's main computer, which runs your code 4 Sendable A WPILib object (like the command scheduler) that describes itself to a dashboard 6.1 Setpoint The value you want right now (target angle, target speed) 12, 14 Simulation Running robot code on your laptop with fake hardware 0, 9 Solenoid An electrically controlled air valve 5 Steady-state error A small error that's left over after the mechanism settles 12 Subsystem A class representing one robot mechanism and its hardware 7 SysId WPILib tool that measures feedforward constants from real tests 13.1 Teleop The driver-controlled part of a match 10 Topic One named value in NetworkTables, like /Wheel/Position (rot) 6.1 Trigger A condition (like a button) that starts or stops a command 7, 7.1 Tunable A number you can change from the dashboard while the robot runs 6.1 Unit test Code that automatically checks other code 16"},{"p":"Resources","h":"Official documentation","u":"reference/resources.html#official-documentation","t":"WPILib Documentation : the main reference for everything in this course WPILib Java API (JavaDocs) FIRST Robotics Competition resources"},{"p":"Resources","h":"Vendor documentation","u":"reference/resources.html#vendor-documentation","t":"CTRE Phoenix 6 (TalonFX, CANcoder, Pigeon 2) REV Robotics (SPARK MAX / Flex, Pneumatic Hub)"},{"p":"Resources","h":"Learning Java","u":"reference/resources.html#learning-java","t":"dev.java Learn W3Schools Java"},{"p":"Resources","h":"Community","u":"reference/resources.html#community","t":"Chief Delphi : the main FRC forum. Search before you post. Deep Blue Robotics training : another team's course, and an inspiration for this one"},{"p":"Resources","h":"Team-only links","u":"reference/resources.html#team-only-links","t":"Add links your team uses here: code style guide, CAN ID map, driver station laptop notes. Keep this page fresh Vendor doc URLs change often. Check every link as part of the yearly checklist ."},{"p":"Credits","h":"The WPILib documentation","u":"reference/credits.html#the-wpilib-documentation","t":"This course is built on top of the WPILib documentation , the official FRC programming reference, written by the WPILib contributors. Parts of this course are adapted from the WPILib documentation, which is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0) . Source: wpilibsuite/frc-docs (the stable branch, for the 2026 season). Everything adapted from the WPILib docs is marked where it appears: Boxes titled \"From the WPILib docs\" contain text adapted from the docs, with a link to the original page. Diagrams and screenshots from the docs have a caption naming the source page. The interactive PID tuning simulators are WPILib's, with a caption naming the source page. Changes made: text was shortened, reworded for beginners, and combined with this course's own explanations; some terms were changed to match this course (for example, \"measurement\" instead of \"output\"). Diagrams and screenshots are unchanged. The simulator scripts are combined into one file, the same way the WPILib docs build them, and are otherwise unchanged."},{"p":"Credits","h":"Adapted material, by page","u":"reference/credits.html#adapted-material-by-page","t":"In this course Adapted from (WPILib docs) Lesson 0: Setup Creating a Robot Program ; Simulation Specific User Interface Elements Lesson 4: Electronics roboRIO Brownout and Understanding Current Draw ; CAN Wiring Basics Lesson 4.3: Sensors Digital Inputs - Hardware ; Analog Potentiometers - Hardware ; Encoders - Software ; Gyroscopes - Hardware Lesson 7: Command-Based Programming What Is \"Command-Based\" Programming? Lesson 7.1: The Command Scheduler The Command Scheduler ; diagrams scheduler-run-sequence and scheduler from the docs source Lesson 11: Autonomous Diagram concurrent-commands from the docs source Lesson 12: PID Feedback Introduction to PID ; Control System Basics ; turret simulator from Tuning a Turret Position Controller Lesson 13: Feedforward Arm simulator from Tuning a Vertical Arm Position Controller Lesson 13.1: SysId Screenshot from Viewing Diagnostics Lab 0 Screenshots from Creating a Robot Program , 3rd Party Libraries , Building and Deploying Robot Code , and Viewing Console Output Lab 4 , Lab 5 , Lab 6 , Lab 8 Wiring and sensor diagrams from the hardware and sensor pages listed above Lab 9 Turret simulator from Tuning a Turret Position Controller Lab 10 Elevator simulator and tuning steps from Tuning a Vertical Elevator with Motion Profiling The simulators also load a small script of our own, pid-tune-fixes.js , which makes their graphs keep drawing when the simulator is taller than the screen (on phones). It doesn't change how the simulations behave."},{"p":"Credits","h":"Mantik, by Itkan Robotics","u":"reference/credits.html#mantik-by-itkan-robotics","t":"Lessons 17–21 and Lab 11 are partly adapted from Mantik , an FRC programming course by Itkan Robotics. Mantik's lesson text is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0) ; its code is BSD-3-Clause. Changes made: the lessons were rewritten for beginners in this course's format, and the examples were rewritten for the RoboJacks West Coast Drive (Through Bore Encoders on the roboRIO, NavX2, CIMs), WPILib 2026, and the course's telemetry library. Some sections were added (calibration steps for the WCD, differential-drive PathPlanner setup, red-alliance notes); a few statements were corrected against vendor documentation. Each adapted page ends with a credit line. In this course Adapted from (Mantik) Lesson 17: Poses and Odometry Introduction to Pose Estimation; Odometry Lesson 18: Vision Fused Pose Estimation; Pose Estimation Calibration (rewritten for PhotonVision) Lesson 19: PathPlanner PathPlanner: Visual Autonomous Routines Lesson 20: Logs, AdvantageScope & AdvantageKit AdvantageScope; AdvantageKit; Hardware Abstraction with IO Layers Lesson 21: Swerve Drive Swerve Drive Example; Odometry Lab 11: Odometry Odometry; Pose Estimation Calibration The site's search box was inspired by Mantik's site search; it's written for this course and doesn't use Mantik's code."},{"p":"Credits","h":"Third-party libraries and their documentation","u":"reference/credits.html#third-party-libraries-and-their-documentation","t":"Lessons 18–20 teach libraries made by other teams and projects. Their licenses, checked in each project's repository: Project Software license Documentation license PathPlanner and PathPlannerLib MIT , © Michael Jansen MIT (the docs are in the same repository) PhotonLib (robot-side library) MIT, © PhotonVision CC BY 4.0 PhotonVision (coprocessor software) GNU GPL v3 CC BY 4.0 NMRC code camp level 2 (2026) No license file (only the WPILib and AdvantageKit template licenses) — This course doesn't copy text from the PathPlanner or PhotonVision documentation; it links to them. Lesson 18's vision subsystem follows the code camp example's approach but is written for this course."},{"p":"Credits","h":"Other sources","u":"reference/credits.html#other-sources","t":"Motor specifications: REV Robotics NEO V1.1 ; CIM and 775pro figures from RobotPy's motor configurations . NavX2 API details: Studica's AHRS Java documentation. The course's telemetry library copies the class and method names of WPILib 2027's Telemetry and Tunables APIs, from their design documents. The code is written for this course. Lesson 6.1's 2027 comparison is based on WPILib's Telemetry and Tunables design documents in wpilibsuite/allwpilib and the Removed features page. The 2027 APIs were still in preview when this was written. PathPlannerLib API details: the PathPlanner source and documentation (2026 release). PhotonLib API details: the PhotonVision source (v2026.3.4). Lesson 18's vision subsystem follows PhotonVisionSubsystem.java from the NMRC code camp (level 2, 2026), adapted for a differential drive. Elastic connection modes and widget behavior: the Elastic documentation , by Team 353."},{"p":"Credits","h":"Trademarks","u":"reference/credits.html#trademarks","t":"FIRST ®, FIRST® Robotics Competition, and FRC® are trademarks of FIRST . REV, SPARK MAX, NEO, and Through Bore Encoder are trademarks of REV Robotics. NavX is a trademark of Kauai Labs / Studica. This course is made by FRC Team 4674 RoboJacks and isn't affiliated with or endorsed by any of them."},{"p":"How to Contribute","h":"","u":"contributing/index.html","t":"This site is plain HTML. Lessons are written in Markdown in the src/ folder, and a small Python script, build.py , turns them into the HTML pages in docs/ . GitHub Pages serves docs/ directly, so there's no server and nothing to deploy by hand."},{"p":"How to Contribute","h":"Quick edits","u":"contributing/index.html#quick-edits","t":"Edit the lesson's Markdown file in src/ . Run python build.py . If you changed code in a lab page, also run python tools/make_examples.py . It rebuilds the finished projects in examples/ from the lab pages, so they always match. Commit the src/ change and the rebuilt docs/ (and examples/ ) files, and open a pull request. Don't edit files in docs/ directly build.py regenerates everything in docs/ from src/ , so hand edits there get overwritten the next time anyone builds."},{"p":"How to Contribute","h":"Working locally","u":"contributing/index.html#working-locally","t":"git clone https://github.com/Robojacks/frc-course.git cd frc-course pip install -r requirements.txt python build.py Then open docs/index.html in your browser. Rebuild and refresh after each change."},{"p":"How to Contribute","h":"Adding a lesson","u":"contributing/index.html#adding-a-lesson","t":"Copy the lesson template into the right folder under src/ . Add it to the NAV list near the top of build.py . That list controls the sidebar and the Previous / Next buttons. Run python build.py and click through the new page. Open a pull request ( Lesson 15 explains how)."},{"p":"How to Contribute","h":"Changing the look","u":"contributing/index.html#changing-the-look","t":"Every color and font is set in the first block of assets/style.css . Change those values and rebuild. The rest of the stylesheet reads from them."},{"p":"How to Contribute","h":"Style rules","u":"contributing/index.html#style-rules","t":"Write for a freshman who just joined the team. Link to WPILib for reference material. Our job is the tutorial, not rewriting their docs. Every lesson has the five sections: Why it matters, Read this, Walkthrough, Try it, Check yourself. Code examples should run in simulation without vendor libraries when possible."},{"p":"Lesson Template","h":"","u":"contributing/lesson-template.html","t":"Copy everything in the box below into a new .md file. # NN. Lesson Title ## Why it matters One or two sentences: what robot problem does this solve? ## Read this - [ WPILib page title ]( https://docs.wpilib.org/en/stable/... ) ## Walkthrough ### First idea Explain it simply. Use a table or diagram if it helps. ```java // Short code example that runs in simulation ``` !!! warning \"Common mistake\" The thing everyone gets wrong the first time. ## Try it - [ ] A small exercise - [ ] A slightly harder one - [ ] A challenge ## Check yourself ??? question \"A question the student should be able to answer?\" The answer, hidden until clicked."},{"p":"Lesson Template","h":"Available callouts","u":"contributing/lesson-template.html#available-callouts","t":"note , tip , info , warning , danger , example , question . Use !!! for always-open and ??? for collapsible."},{"p":"Yearly Checklist","h":"","u":"contributing/yearly-checklist.html","t":"Do this every fall, before new members start the course."},{"p":"Yearly Checklist","h":"After the WPILib kickoff release","u":"contributing/yearly-checklist.html#after-the-wpilib-kickoff-release","t":"Update the setup lesson for the new installer Check the JDK version used in the CI example (Lesson 16) Skim the WPILib release notes for renamed or deprecated classes used in code examples Rebuild the code examples in a fresh project to confirm they still compile"},{"p":"Yearly Checklist","h":"Links","u":"contributing/yearly-checklist.html#links","t":"Click every link on the Resources page Spot-check the Read this links on each lesson Run mkdocs build --strict (catches broken internal links)"},{"p":"Yearly Checklist","h":"Content","u":"contributing/yearly-checklist.html#content","t":"Update hardware references to match this year's robot (motor controllers, pneumatics module) Ask last year's new members which lesson was most confusing, and fix it Hand ownership of the site to a returning member before seniors graduate"},{"p":"Overview","h":"","u":"testbed/index.html","t":"Labs are hands-on exercises on the team's test bed : a board with real robot electronics on it, so you can write code and watch it run on real hardware without needing the competition robot. The Lessons tab teaches the ideas. The Labs tab is where you try them on hardware."},{"p":"Overview","h":"The labs","u":"testbed/index.html#the-labs","t":"Lab Runs on Do it after What you'll do Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project Test bed Lesson 0 Install the tools, create the TestBed project, deploy it, and see your code run Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor Test bed Lab 0 and a Java course Write your first subsystem and command, and spin a motor with a button Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard Test bed Lab 1 and Lesson 6.1 Send values with NetworkTables, build an Elastic layout, and change the speed live Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive WCD Lab 1.1 Program four CIMs with leaders, followers, and skid steer controls Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers Test bed Lab 2 whileTrue , onTrue , toggles, timeouts, a throttle, and default commands Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch Test bed Lab 3 Read your first sensor and stop the motor automatically Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob Test bed Lab 4 Read an analog sensor, calibrate it, and map it to a speed Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder Test bed Lab 5 Read an encoder, then try to turn exactly 10 times with timing and bang-bang Lab 7: Timed Autonomous WCD Lab 6 Drive a pattern on its own, picked from a dashboard menu Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances WCD Lab 7 Turn by angle with the NavX2, drive exact distances with the drive encoders, and close the square Lab 9: PID on the Wheel Test bed Lab 8 and Lesson 12 Turn exactly 10 times with P, then D, then I Lab 10: Elevator Position Control WCD Lab 9 Move the elevator between START, L2, and L3 with a ProfiledPIDController, in meters, and hold against gravity Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field WCD Lab 10 and Lesson 17 Add odometry, watch the robot move on a field map in Elastic, and calibrate it against a tape measure Do them in order. Each lab adds one new idea. Every lab has finished code on GitHub, in the examples folder : a complete project you can open and deploy if you get stuck. Each lab adds one new idea. Labs on the test bed build up the TestBed project from Lab 0. Labs on the WCD build up the WestCoastDrive project from Lab 2. Commit your work to Git after each lab."},{"p":"Overview","h":"What's on the test bed","u":"testbed/index.html#whats-on-the-test-bed","t":"New to any of this hardware? Lesson 4 covers the electrical system, and Lessons 4.1 (motors), 4.2 (SPARK MAX), and 4.3 (sensors) cover each part below. Part What it does roboRIO The robot's computer. Your code runs here. Power distribution with breakers Splits battery power to everything, with a breaker on each channel Main breaker and battery Power on and off, and the emergency off switch Two SPARK MAX motor controllers Drive the motors, connected to the roboRIO over CAN. The brushless motor's is CAN ID 8. 775pro motor with a gearbox and wheel The motor used in Labs 6 and 9 NEO brushless motor The motor used in Labs 1, 1.1, 3, 4, and 5 REV Through Bore Encoder (quadrature) Measures how far the wheel has turned (Labs 6 and 9) Limit switches On/off sensors (Lab 4) 10-turn potentiometer A knob that reports its position as an analog voltage (Lab 5) The test bed has no radio . Connect your laptop to the roboRIO with an Ethernet or USB cable."},{"p":"Overview","h":"The West Coast Drive","u":"testbed/index.html#the-west-coast-drive","t":"Labs 2, 7, 8, and 10 use the team's West Coast Drive , a six-wheel, skid steer drivetrain on its own robot frame, with an elevator on top. Part Details Motors 4 CIM motors (brushed), 2 per side, each pair in one gearbox Motor controllers 4 SPARK MAXes on CAN, in brushed mode Wheels 3 per side, chained together, with the center wheels slightly lower Gyro NavX2 on the roboRIO's MXP port (Lab 8) Drive encoders A REV Through Bore Encoder on each gearbox output shaft, on the roboRIO's DIO ports (Lab 8) Wheels 6 in Elevator A NEO on a SPARK MAX in brushless mode, with a REV Through Bore Encoder on the roboRIO's DIO ports (Lab 10) Radio Labeled 4674-6 . The Driver Station connects through it. The RoboJacks West Coast Drive chassis, before the elevator goes on. Front is at the top of the photo. WCD safety Test new drive code on blocks first, with the wheels off the ground. On the floor, use a big open space, keep people out of the robot's path, and start at low speed. Keep hands out of the elevator, and never reach under a raised carriage."},{"p":"Overview","h":"Test bed rules","u":"testbed/index.html#test-bed-rules","t":"Safety Say \"Enabling!\" out loud before you enable, and wait until hands are clear. Keep fingers, hair, and loose sleeves away from the wheel. Never hold a motor still while it's pushing. The 775pro burns out if it stalls. In autonomous, nobody is steering. Clear the area before you enable. Know the Driver Station keys: Enter disables, Space is the emergency stop. Turn off the main breaker before touching any wiring. Use a charged battery, and put it back on the charger when you're done. Don't change the wiring without asking. Other people's labs depend on the CAN IDs and ports staying the same. If something on the board looks loose or damaged, stop and tell a mentor."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#why-it-matters","t":"Before you can make anything move, you need the right tools on your laptop and a project that can reach the test bed. That's this lab. Nothing will spin yet. By the end you'll have: every program an FRC programmer needs, installed a robot project named TestBed , with REV's library added that project deployed to the test bed's roboRIO a message you wrote, printed by the real robot You'll keep using this same TestBed project in every lab after this one, so take your time and get it right."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#read-this","t":"WPILib Installation Guide FRC Game Tools 3rd Party Libraries (vendor dependencies) Building and Deploying Robot Code"},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Part 1: Install the tools","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#part-1-install-the-tools","t":"Install all four. Each one is free. Use this season's version of each, the same as the rest of the team. Program What it's for Where to get it WPILib VS Code, Java, and the tools to build and deploy robot code WPILib Installation Guide . Step-by-step in Lesson 0 . FRC Game Tools (Windows only) Installs the Driver Station , the program that enables and disables the robot FRC Game Tools REV Hardware Client (Windows only) Checks and sets up the SPARK MAX controllers: CAN IDs, firmware, and motor type REV Hardware Client Git Saves your work and shares it with the team ( Lesson 1 ) git-scm.com Mac or Chromebook? WPILib runs on Mac and Linux, so you can write and build code. The Driver Station and REV Hardware Client only run on Windows, so you'll need a team laptop to enable the test bed. Check your installs: WPILib VS Code opens (look for the WPILib logo) The FRC Driver Station opens REV Hardware Client opens In a terminal, git --version prints a version number"},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Part 2: Create the TestBed project","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#part-2-create-the-testbed-project","t":"Open WPILib VS Code . Press Ctrl + Shift + P and run WPILib: Create a new project . Fill in the form: Field Value Project type Template Language Java Project base Command Robot Base folder Your robotics folder Project name TestBed Team number 4674 Click Generate Project and open it. The New Project Creator, filled in. Your folder, name, and team number will differ. From the WPILib docs, Creating a Robot Program (CC BY 4.0). No spaces in project names TestBed , not Test Bed . Spaces in folder names cause problems in the terminal ( Lesson 2 ). The team number matters more than it looks. When you deploy, WPILib uses it to find the robot on the network (as roboRIO-4674-FRC.local ). The wrong number means \"roboRIO not found.\""},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Part 3: Look around","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#part-3-look-around","t":"Open these files. You don't need to change them yet, but you should know what each is for: File What it does Robot.java Runs the robot loop and switches between modes ( Lesson 6 ) RobotContainer.java Creates subsystems and binds controller buttons ( Lesson 7 ) Constants.java Holds numbers like CAN IDs, speeds, and ports Main.java Starts the program. Never edit it. subsystems/ExampleSubsystem.java An empty example from the template commands/ExampleCommand.java , Autos.java An example command and auto from the template deploy/ Files copied to the roboRIO along with your code (you won't need it yet) vendordeps/ Vendor libraries. Only WPILib's own are here so far."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Part 4: Install REVLib","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#part-4-install-revlib","t":"The test bed's motor controllers are REV SPARK MAX es. WPILib doesn't include code for them, so you add REV's library, REVLib , as a vendor dependency (\"vendordep\"). Click the WPILib icon in VS Code's left sidebar and open Vendor Dependencies . Find REVLib in the list and click Install . If VS Code asks to build or refresh the project, say yes. Check that a file like REVLib.json now appears in the vendordeps/ folder. The Vendor Dependencies sidebar: installed libraries at the top, available ones below, each with an Install button. From the WPILib docs, 3rd Party Libraries (CC BY 4.0). If you don't see the sidebar, use Ctrl + Shift + P → WPILib: Manage Vendor Libraries instead. Same season, same library The REVLib version must match your WPILib season. Installing from the WPILib vendor list picks a compatible one for you. You need internet for this step."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Part 5: Say hello","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#part-5-say-hello","t":"Open Robot.java and find the constructor, public Robot() . Add one line at the top, with your real name: public Robot () { System . out . println ( \"Hello from the TestBed! Written by YOUR NAME.\" ); // Instantiate our RobotContainer. This will perform all our button bindings, and put our // autonomous chooser on the dashboard. m_robotContainer = new RobotContainer (); } This line runs once , when the robot program starts."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Part 6: Build","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#part-6-build","t":"Make sure the code compiles before touching the robot: Ctrl + Shift + P → WPILib: Build Robot Code You should see BUILD SUCCESSFUL in the terminal at the bottom. If it fails, read the first error: line, fix it, and build again ( Lesson 2 ). Where to look: BUILD SUCCESSFUL appears in the terminal panel (1). From the WPILib docs, Building and Deploying Robot Code (CC BY 4.0)."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Part 7: Power up and connect","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#part-7-power-up-and-connect","t":"Safety check The template has no motor code, so nothing should move in this lab. Still, build the habit now: before powering the test bed, check that hands are clear of the wheel and that the battery is secured. Plug a charged battery into the test bed and turn on the main breaker . Wait about 30 seconds for the roboRIO to boot. Connect your laptop to the roboRIO with an Ethernet or USB cable. The test bed has no radio, so there's no Wi-Fi. Open the Driver Station and set the team number to 4674 (the gear tab, on the left). The Communications light on the Driver Station should turn green. The Robot Code light is probably red. That's fine: there's no code on the robot yet, or it's someone else's old code. The roboRIO must be set up for this season Each season, the roboRIO gets a new system image that matches that year's WPILib. A mentor or lead programmer does this once. If deploy complains about the image version, ask them."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Part 8: Deploy","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#part-8-deploy","t":"Ctrl + Shift + P → WPILib: Deploy Robot Code This builds your code, copies it to the roboRIO, and restarts the robot program. It takes about 15 to 30 seconds. Near the end, the terminal shows lines like this: -C-> . /etc/profile.d/natinst-path.sh; /usr/local/frc/bin/frcKillRobot.sh -t -r ... BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 12s frcKillRobot stops the old robot program and starts yours. When it finishes, the Robot Code light on the Driver Station turns green . That's your code running on real hardware."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Part 9: See your message","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#part-9-see-your-message","t":"Your println went to the robot's console. Open it either way: In VS Code: Ctrl + Shift + P → WPILib: Start RioLog In the Driver Station: click the gear icon above the message box and choose View Console The RioLog window. Your messages appear in the big area; the buttons below pause, clear, and filter it. From the WPILib docs, Viewing Console Output (CC BY 4.0). Look for: Hello from the TestBed! Written by YOUR NAME. If you missed it, restart the robot code from the Driver Station ( Restart Robot Code on the Diagnostics tab) and watch it print again."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Part 10: Enable (nothing should move)","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#part-10-enable-nothing-should-move","t":"Say \"Enabling!\" out loud. In the Driver Station, choose TeleOperated and click Enable . Watch the orange Robot Signal Light on the test bed: it blinks while enabled. Click Disable , or press Enter . The light goes back to solid . Know your emergency keys In the Driver Station, Enter disables the robot, and Space is the emergency stop . An emergency stop requires restarting the roboRIO before you can enable again. Use it if anything goes wrong."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Part 11: Save it with Git","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#part-11-save-it-with-git","t":"git init git add . git commit -m \"Lab 0: TestBed project with REVLib\" From now on, commit after each lab, so you can always get back to working code."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"When it doesn't work","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#when-it-doesnt-work","t":"Problem Fix Deploy says it can't find the roboRIO Check the cable, wait for the roboRIO to finish booting, and check that the team number is 4674 ( .wpilib/wpilib_preferences.json ) Driver Station Communications light stays red Same as above, plus check the team number in the Driver Station Deploy says the roboRIO image is the wrong version The roboRIO needs this season's image. Ask a mentor. Build fails after installing REVLib You may have been offline, so it couldn't download the library. Connect to the internet and build again. Robot Code light red after deploy Your code crashed on startup. Open RioLog and read the first error. No message in the console Make sure you deployed after adding the line Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab00-testbed-setup on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#try-it","t":"Install WPILib, FRC Game Tools, REV Hardware Client, and Git Create TestBed from the Command Robot template with team number 4674 Install REVLib and find its file in vendordeps/ Add your hello message and build successfully Deploy to the test bed and get a green Robot Code light Find your message in RioLog or the Driver Station console Enable and disable, and watch the Robot Signal Light change Commit TestBed to Git"},{"p":"Lab 0: Set Up the TestBed Project","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab0-first-project.html#check-yourself","t":"What does each of the four programs do? WPILib writes, builds, and deploys code. The Driver Station enables and disables the robot. The REV Hardware Client sets up SPARK MAX controllers. Git saves and shares your code. Why does the project need the team number? Deploy uses it to find the robot on the network, at roboRIO-4674-FRC.local . What's a vendor dependency, and why does TestBed need REVLib? A library from a hardware maker that adds code for their devices. The test bed's SPARK MAX controllers are made by REV, and WPILib doesn't include code for them. What's the difference between Build and Deploy? Build only compiles the code on your laptop. Deploy builds it, copies it to the roboRIO, and restarts the robot program. How can you tell your code is running on the robot? The Driver Station's Robot Code light is green, and your message shows up in the console. Which key disables the robot from the Driver Station? Enter . Space is the emergency stop."},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#why-it-matters","t":"This is the moment every new programmer waits for: you press a button, and something you wrote makes a real motor spin. In this lab you'll write your first subsystem and your first command , bind it to a controller button, and spin the test bed's brushless motor. It's the same pattern every mechanism on a real robot uses, from intakes to shooters, so you'll use it again and again. Before you start: finish Lab 0 . You'll add to the same TestBed project. Lesson 7 explains subsystems and commands in depth; you can do this lab first and read it after. New to the NEO or the SPARK MAX? Read Lessons 4.1 and 4.2 . You should also have finished an interactive Java course through classes and objects ( see the checklist ); keep the Java Reference open while you work."},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#read-this","t":"Subsystems Binding Commands to Triggers REVLib: SPARK MAX vs. SPARK Flex"},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"The hardware","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#the-hardware","t":"Part Details Motor A brushless motor (like a REV NEO) Motor controller SPARK MAX, CAN ID 8 , connected to the roboRIO over CAN Controller An Xbox controller plugged into the Driver Station laptop Brushless means brushless In code, a brushless motor like a NEO must be set up as MotorType.kBrushless . Running a brushless motor in brushed mode can damage the motor and the SPARK MAX. Double-check this line every time."},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Step 1: Check the SPARK MAX","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#step-1-check-the-spark-max","t":"Before writing code, make sure the hardware is what the code will expect. Power the test bed and connect the SPARK MAX to your laptop with a USB-C cable. Open the REV Hardware Client . The SPARK MAX should appear. Check that its CAN ID is 8 . If the code and the controller disagree on the ID, nothing spins and there's no error. If the Hardware Client says a firmware update is available, ask a mentor before updating."},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Step 2: Constants","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#step-2-constants","t":"Never put numbers like CAN IDs and speeds directly in your code. Put them in Constants.java , so there's one place to change them. Rename the template's controller first The WPILib template names the controller m_driverController , and its port kDriverControllerPort . This course calls them m_controller and kControllerPort . In VS Code, click on m_driverController , press F2 ( Rename Symbol ), and type m_controller . Do the same for kDriverControllerPort → kControllerPort . F2 renames every use at once, so nothing breaks. Then open Constants.java and add a new class inside Constants : public final class Constants { public static class OperatorConstants { public static final int kControllerPort = 0 ; } public static class kBrushlessConstants { public static final int BrushlessMotorCanId = 8 ; // must match the REV Hardware Client public static final double BrushlessMotorSpeed = 0.5 ; // 50% power public static final int BrushlessMotorCurrentLimit = 40 ; // amps } } Part What it means public static class kBrushlessConstants A group of constants for the brushless motor, inside Constants . You'll use it as Constants.kBrushlessConstants.BrushlessMotorSpeed . public Any file can read it static It belongs to the class itself, so you don't need to create a Constants object to use it final It can never change while the robot runs. That's what makes it a constant. int / double A whole number (CAN IDs, amps) / a number with a decimal point (speeds) Why a current limit of 40 amps? The test bed's power distribution has 40 A breakers . A limit above 40 A can trip the breaker if the motor gets stuck. If your motor is a small NEO 550 , use about 20 A instead, because it can overheat quickly at 40."},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Step 3: Create the subsystem","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#step-3-create-the-subsystem","t":"A subsystem is a class that owns a piece of hardware and offers actions for it. Ours owns one motor. In VS Code, right-click the subsystems folder. Choose Create a new class/command , then Subsystem (New) . Name it SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . Replace what's in the file with this. Read every comment. package frc.robot.subsystems ; import com.revrobotics.PersistMode ; import com.revrobotics.ResetMode ; import com.revrobotics.spark.SparkLowLevel.MotorType ; import com.revrobotics.spark.SparkMax ; import com.revrobotics.spark.config.SparkBaseConfig.IdleMode ; import com.revrobotics.spark.config.SparkMaxConfig ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.Command ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; import frc.robot.Constants ; public class SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem extends SubsystemBase { // Create the motor controller: CAN ID 8, brushless motor. // private final = only this class can use it, and it can't be swapped for another. private final SparkMax m_brushlessMotor = new SparkMax ( Constants . kBrushlessConstants . BrushlessMotorCanId , MotorType . kBrushless ); public SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem () { // Collect the settings we want in a config object SparkMaxConfig brushlessMotorConfig = new SparkMaxConfig (); // Limit how much current the motor can pull, to protect the motor and the breaker brushlessMotorConfig . smartCurrentLimit ( Constants . kBrushlessConstants . BrushlessMotorCurrentLimit ); // Brake mode: when we stop, the motor actively stops instead of coasting brushlessMotorConfig . idleMode ( IdleMode . kBrake ); // Send the settings to the SPARK MAX. // kResetSafeParameters: start from known defaults, then apply ours. // kPersistParameters: save them, so they survive if the SPARK MAX loses power mid-match. m_brushlessMotor . configure ( brushlessMotorConfig , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); } /** Sets the motor's power, from -1.0 (full reverse) to 1.0 (full forward). */ public void setMotorSpeed ( double speed ) { m_brushlessMotor . set ( speed ); } /** * A command that spins the motor at a speed while it runs, * and stops the motor when it ends. */ public Command spinMotorCommand ( double speed ) { return startEnd ( () -> setMotorSpeed ( speed ), // runs once, when the command starts () -> setMotorSpeed ( 0.0 ) // runs once, when the command ends ). withName ( \"Spin Brushless\" ); } }"},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"The subsystem, piece by piece","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#the-subsystem-piece-by-piece","t":"That's a lot of new code at once. Here it is again, one piece at a time. 1. The package and the imports package frc.robot.subsystems ; import com.revrobotics.spark.SparkMax ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; import frc.robot.Constants ; // ...and the others package frc.robot.subsystems; says which folder this file lives in. It must match the folder, or the build fails. Each import lets this file use a class from somewhere else, by its short name. com.revrobotics... classes come from REVLib (Lab 0, Part 4). edu.wpi.first... classes come from WPILib . frc.robot.Constants is your own file. You rarely type imports yourself. When VS Code underlines a class name in red, click it and use the lightbulb (or Ctrl + . ) to add the import. 2. The class line public class SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem extends SubsystemBase { public class SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem creates a new class. The class name must match the file name exactly. extends SubsystemBase makes it a subsystem : WPILib's name for one mechanism and its hardware. Because of extends , this class gets everything SubsystemBase already has for free, including command builders like startEnd , and a periodic() method the robot calls every 20 ms. 3. The motor controller private final SparkMax m_brushlessMotor = new SparkMax ( Constants . kBrushlessConstants . BrushlessMotorCanId , MotorType . kBrushless ); Part What it means private Only code inside this class can touch the motor. Everything else has to go through the subsystem's methods. final This variable will always point at this same motor controller. It can't be swapped out by accident. SparkMax The type: a REV SPARK MAX motor controller m_brushlessMotor The variable's name. The m_ means \"member\": it belongs to the object, not to one method. new SparkMax(8, MotorType.kBrushless) Creates the object that talks to the SPARK MAX with CAN ID 8 , which is driving a brushless motor The CAN ID comes from Constants , so if the wiring ever changes, you only change one number in one file. 4. The constructor: setting up the motor public SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem () { SparkMaxConfig brushlessMotorConfig = new SparkMaxConfig (); brushlessMotorConfig . smartCurrentLimit ( Constants . kBrushlessConstants . BrushlessMotorCurrentLimit ); brushlessMotorConfig . idleMode ( IdleMode . kBrake ); m_brushlessMotor . configure ( brushlessMotorConfig , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); } A constructor is the method with the same name as the class and no return type. It runs once , when RobotContainer creates the subsystem with new SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem() . Line What it does new SparkMaxConfig() Makes an empty list of settings. Nothing is sent to the motor controller yet. smartCurrentLimit(40) Adds a setting: never pull more than 40 amps, to protect the motor and the breaker idleMode(IdleMode.kBrake) Adds a setting: when the motor is told 0, actively stop instead of coasting configure(config, ...) Sends all the settings to the SPARK MAX in one go ResetMode.kResetSafeParameters First reset the SPARK MAX to known defaults, so old settings from someone else's code don't linger PersistMode.kPersistParameters Save the settings inside the SPARK MAX, so they survive a power blip during a match 5. A method that sets the speed public void setMotorSpeed ( double speed ) { m_brushlessMotor . set ( speed ); } public : other classes (like RobotContainer ) can call it. void : it doesn't return anything. It just does something. double speed : it takes one number. set() expects -1.0 (full reverse) to 1.0 (full forward); 0.5 is half power. 6. The command public Command spinMotorCommand ( double speed ) { return startEnd ( () -> setMotorSpeed ( speed ), // runs once, when the command starts () -> setMotorSpeed ( 0.0 ) // runs once, when the command ends ). withName ( \"Spin Brushless\" ); } This method doesn't spin the motor. It builds and returns a command that knows how to spin it. The command runs later, when a button starts it. Part What it means public Command The method hands back a Command object startEnd(start, end) A WPILib command builder: run start once when the command begins, and end once when it stops () -> setMotorSpeed(speed) A lambda : a little piece of code saved to run later. () means it takes no inputs; -> points at what it does. .withName(\"Spin Brushless\") Gives the command a name, which shows up on the dashboard and in error messages Because startEnd comes from the subsystem, the command automatically requires the subsystem: while it runs, no other command can use this motor at the same time. Where does startEnd come from? Your class extends SubsystemBase , so it gets WPILib's command-building methods for free: startEnd , run , runOnce , and more ( Lesson 7 )."},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Step 4: Bind it to a button","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#step-4-bind-it-to-a-button","t":"Open RobotContainer.java . Make three changes. 1. Import the subsystem at the top, with the other imports: import frc.robot.subsystems.SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem ; 2. Create the subsystem , next to the example one: private final ExampleSubsystem m_exampleSubsystem = new ExampleSubsystem (); private final SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem = new SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem (); 3. Bind the A button in configureBindings() : private void configureBindings () { // Spin the brushless motor while A is held m_controller . a (). whileTrue ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . spinMotorCommand ( Constants . kBrushlessConstants . BrushlessMotorSpeed )); } Read it out loud: \"While A is held, spin the brushless motor at the speed from Constants.\" whileTrue starts the command when you press A and cancels it when you let go. Canceling runs the command's end action, setMotorSpeed(0.0) , which stops the motor."},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Step 5: Build, deploy, and spin","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#step-5-build-deploy-and-spin","t":"Build ( Ctrl + Shift + P → WPILib: Build Robot Code ) and fix any errors. Power the test bed, connect, and Deploy . Plug the Xbox controller into the Driver Station laptop. On the Driver Station's USB tab (the icon that looks like a USB plug), make sure it's in slot 0 and the bar moves when you press buttons. Clear the area, say \"Enabling!\" , and enable TeleOperated . Hold A. The motor spins. Let go. The motor stops. 🎉 That's your code moving real hardware."},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Step 6: Commit","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#step-6-commit","t":"git add . git commit -m \"Lab 1: spin brushless motor on A\""},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"When it doesn't work","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#when-it-doesnt-work","t":"Problem Likely cause Nothing happens when you hold A Robot not enabled, or the controller isn't in slot 0 on the Driver Station USB tab Still nothing, and the console mentions CAN or SPARK MAX errors The CAN ID in Constants doesn't match the REV Hardware Client, or a CAN wire is loose Motor keeps spinning after you let go The command doesn't stop the motor when it ends. Check that you used startEnd with setMotorSpeed(0.0) as the end action. Motor spins the wrong way Add brushlessMotorConfig.inverted(true); in the constructor Build error: cannot find symbol SparkMax REVLib isn't installed. Go back to Lab 0, Part 4. Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab01-spin-motor on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#try-it","t":"Spin the motor with A, and stop it by letting go Change BrushlessMotorSpeed to 0.25, then 0.75. Deploy each time. Can you hear the difference? Add a B button that spins the motor backward at the same speed (hint: a negative speed) Add an X button that spins it at full speed, 1.0 Change IdleMode.kBrake to IdleMode.kCoast . How is stopping different? Change it back. Break it on purpose: change spinMotorCommand to use runOnce(() -> setMotorSpeed(speed)) instead of startEnd . Hold A, then let go. What happens, and why? (Disable the robot to stop it.) Then change it back."},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#check-yourself","t":"What does MotorType.kBrushless tell the SPARK MAX, and why does it matter? That it's driving a brushless motor like a NEO. Brushed and brushless motors are driven completely differently, and the wrong setting can damage the hardware. Why are the CAN ID, speed, and current limit in Constants instead of in the subsystem? So there's one place to find and change them, and the code says what each number means. What does startEnd do? It builds a command that runs one action when it starts (spin the motor) and another when it ends (stop the motor). Why does the motor stop when you let go of A? whileTrue cancels the command when the button is released, and canceling runs the command's end action, which sets the speed to 0. In the 'break it on purpose' exercise, why did the motor keep spinning? runOnce sets the speed once and then finishes immediately . When you release A, there's nothing left to cancel, so nothing sets the speed back to 0. The SPARK MAX keeps doing what it was last told until the robot is disabled."},{"p":"Lab 1: Spin a Brushless Motor","h":"Next","u":"testbed/lab1-spin-motor.html#next","t":"In Lab 1.1 , you'll put the motor's speed and current on a dashboard and change its speed without redeploying."},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#why-it-matters","t":"In Lab 1 the motor spun, but you couldn't see how fast, or how much current it pulled. Every lab after this one depends on watching numbers change live: encoder positions, errors, PID outputs. And when you tune something, redeploying for every small change wastes a lot of robot time. In this lab you'll: add the course's telemetry library , which sends values over NetworkTables 4 using WPILib 2027's API send the motor's speed, current, and output to Elastic build an Elastic layout with graphs change the motor's speed from the dashboard , without redeploying Before you start: finish Lab 1 . You'll add to the same TestBed project. Read Lesson 6.1 first; it explains NetworkTables, Elastic, and why this course doesn't use SmartDashboard.putNumber ."},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#read-this","t":"Publishing and Subscribing to a Topic Elastic Elastic: Connecting to your robot"},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"Step 1: Add the telemetry library","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#step-1-add-the-telemetry-library","t":"The course has a small library for dashboards, in a folder called telemetry . It's a 2026 copy of the Telemetry and Tunables APIs that WPILib adds in 2027, with the same names, so everything you write in these labs already works the 2027 way ( Lesson 6.1 ). File What it's for Telemetry.java Where you start: Telemetry.getTable(\"Brushless\") TelemetryTable.java One folder of values: table.log(\"Current (A)\", amps) Tunables.java Numbers you change from the dashboard: Tunables.addDouble(...) TunableDouble.java One tunable number: speed.get() Selectable.java A dropdown menu (you'll use it in Lab 7) You don't type these in. Copy them: In VS Code, right-click the frc/robot folder (the one with Robot.java ), choose New Folder , and name it telemetry . Open the telemetry folder on GitHub . For each of the five files: click it, click the Copy raw file button, then in VS Code right-click your telemetry folder → New File , give it the same name , and paste. Check that each file's first line is package frc.robot.telemetry; . Then open TelemetryTable.java and read it. Every line is commented, and it uses exactly the publishers from Lesson 6.1 ."},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"Step 2: Send the motor's values","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#step-2-send-the-motors-values","t":"Open SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem.java . 1. Import the library with the other imports: import frc.robot.telemetry.Telemetry ; import frc.robot.telemetry.TelemetryTable ; 2. Add a field below the motor: // Everything this subsystem sends shows up in /Telemetry/Brushless on the dashboard private final TelemetryTable m_telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Brushless\" ); Telemetry.getTable(\"Brushless\") gets the Brushless folder inside Telemetry on the dashboard. You keep it in a field, m_telemetry , so every method in the subsystem can log into it. TelemetryTable is the field's type: one folder of dashboard values. 3. Add a periodic() method at the bottom of the class, before the last } : @Override public void periodic () { // Runs every 20 ms, enabled or not m_telemetry . log ( \"Velocity (RPM)\" , m_brushlessMotor . getEncoder (). getVelocity ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Current (A)\" , m_brushlessMotor . getOutputCurrent ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Applied Output\" , m_brushlessMotor . getAppliedOutput ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Temperature (C)\" , m_brushlessMotor . getMotorTemperature ()); Command running = getCurrentCommand (); m_telemetry . log ( \"Command\" , running == null ? \"none\" : running . getName ()); } Line What it sends getEncoder().getVelocity() The NEO's built-in encoder speed, in RPM getOutputCurrent() How many amps the motor is pulling right now getAppliedOutput() The output the SPARK MAX is actually applying, from -1 to 1 getMotorTemperature() The NEO's temperature, in °C getCurrentCommand() The command that owns this subsystem right now, or null if none @Override means this replaces the empty periodic() that every subsystem already has. If you misspell the name, @Override makes the build fail instead of silently never running. m_telemetry.log(name, value) sends one value. The first time a name is logged, it appears on the dashboard; after that, it just updates. Command running = getCurrentCommand(); asks the subsystem which command is using it right now, and saves the answer in a local variable. running == null ? \"none\" : running.getName() is a ternary : \"if nothing is running, send none ; otherwise send the command's name.\""},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"Step 3: Open Elastic and connect","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#step-3-open-elastic-and-connect","t":"Build and Deploy . The robot can stay disabled. Open the Driver Station and connect to the test bed (the Communications light is green). In VS Code, press Ctrl + Shift + P , run WPILib: Start Tool , and choose Elastic . Click the gear and set the IP address mode to Driver Station . Look at the bar at the bottom. It should turn green and show the roboRIO's address. If it stays red (\"Network Tables: Disconnected\"), check that the Driver Station is connected and that your code deployed."},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"Step 4: Build a layout","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#step-4-build-a-layout","t":"Make a new tab and name it Test Bed . Open Elastic's list of topics, then Telemetry → Brushless . Drag these onto the grid: Value Show it as Why Velocity (RPM) Graph See it climb and level off Current (A) Graph See the jump at startup Applied Output Number Bar See the output at a glance Temperature (C) Text Display Keep an eye on heat Command Text Display See which command is running Resize the graphs so they're easy to read. Now clear the area, say \"Enabling!\" , enable TeleOperated, and hold A : Value What you'll see Velocity (RPM) Climbs quickly, then levels off at a steady speed Current (A) Jumps up as the motor starts, then drops once it's at speed Applied Output 0.5 while A is held, 0 when released Command Spin Brushless while A is held, none when released That jump in current at startup is why current limits matter: starting a motor takes much more current than keeping it spinning."},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"Step 5: Change the speed from the dashboard","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#step-5-change-the-speed-from-the-dashboard","t":"Right now the speed comes from Constants , so changing it means redeploying. Make it tunable instead. 1. In the subsystem , add a field below m_telemetry : // A speed you can change in Elastic. It starts at the value in Constants. private final TunableDouble m_tunableSpeed = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Brushless/Tunable Speed\" , Constants . kBrushlessConstants . BrushlessMotorSpeed ); And import it at the top: import frc.robot.telemetry.TunableDouble ; import frc.robot.telemetry.Tunables ; Part What it means TunableDouble A number the dashboard can change while the robot runs Tunables.addDouble(name, start) Makes the tunable number and puts it on the dashboard at /Tunables/Brushless/Tunable Speed Constants.kBrushlessConstants.BrushlessMotorSpeed The starting value, 0.5. Every time the robot code starts, it goes back to this. A field , not inside a method It's created once , when the subsystem is created, and kept for the whole match 2. Add a command below spinMotorCommand : /** Spins the motor at whatever speed is on the dashboard, reading it every loop. */ public Command spinAtTunableSpeedCommand () { return runEnd ( () -> setMotorSpeed ( m_tunableSpeed . get ()), // every loop while it runs () -> setMotorSpeed ( 0.0 ) // once, when it ends ). withName ( \"Spin Tunable\" ); } Notice this uses runEnd , not startEnd . startEnd sets the speed once , so a change on the dashboard wouldn't do anything until you pressed the button again. runEnd reads the dashboard every loop , so the motor follows your changes while you hold the button. 3. Bind it to B in RobotContainer 's configureBindings() : // Spin at the dashboard's speed while B is held m_controller . b (). whileTrue ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . spinAtTunableSpeedCommand ()); 4. Try it. Deploy. In Elastic, drag Tunable Speed (in Tunables → Brushless ) onto your tab as a Number Slider (or a Text Display you can edit). Hold B, then change the speed. The motor follows right away. Keep tunable speeds small A tunable lets anyone at the laptop change how the robot moves while it's enabled. Keep the test bed clear, and don't go above 1.0 or below -1.0. In real robot code, clamp tunable values with MathUtil.clamp(value, -1, 1) . 5. Restart the robot code (Driver Station → Restart Robot Code ). Tunable Speed goes back to 0.5. Dashboard changes are never saved: when you find a speed you like, copy it into Constants.java ."},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"Step 6: Save your layout and commit","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#step-6-save-your-layout-and-commit","t":"Save the layout in Elastic, then export it from Elastic's file menu into your TestBed project folder. Commit: git add . git commit -m \"Lab 1.1: telemetry library, Elastic layout, tunable speed\" Your layout file is now in Git, so a teammate can load the same dashboard."},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"When it doesn't work","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#when-it-doesnt-work","t":"Problem Likely cause Elastic stays red and says disconnected The Driver Station isn't connected, or the IP mode isn't Driver Station . In simulation, use localhost . Connected, but there's no Telemetry → Brushless folder The new code isn't deployed, or periodic() is missing @Override or has a typo in its name Values show up but never change periodic() isn't being called: check that the subsystem is created in RobotContainer Changing Tunable Speed does nothing The command uses startEnd instead of runEnd , or B isn't bound Build error: cannot find symbol Telemetry The telemetry folder isn't inside frc/robot , a file is missing, or the import is missing Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab01b-dashboard on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#try-it","t":"Get all five Brushless values showing in Elastic Hold A and watch the startup current jump. What's the highest number you see? Add an X binding that spins at 1.0. Compare the startup current to 0.5. Use the B button and Tunable Speed to find the slowest speed where the motor still turns reliably Add m_telemetry.log(\"At Speed\", ...) that's true when the velocity is above 2000 RPM. Show it as a Boolean Box . Run the code in simulation , connect Elastic with localhost , and find the same values. Which ones still change in simulation, and which don't?"},{"p":"Lab 1.1: Put It on the Dashboard","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab1b-dashboard.html#check-yourself","t":"Where does m_telemetry.log(\\\"Current (A)\\\", ...) show up in Elastic? In Telemetry → Brushless , as /Telemetry/Brushless/Current (A) . Why does spinAtTunableSpeedCommand use runEnd instead of startEnd ? runEnd runs its first action every loop, so it reads the dashboard value 50 times a second. startEnd only sets the speed once, when the command starts. You found a great speed with the slider, and then the robot rebooted. What happened, and what should you do? Tunables start at their default every time the code starts, so the change was lost. Copy the number into Constants.java and deploy. Why does the current jump when the motor starts? A motor that isn't moving yet pulls much more current than one that's already spinning. The current limit keeps that jump from tripping the breaker or browning out the robot. What changes in this lab's code when the team moves to WPILib 2027? Only the imports. Delete the telemetry folder and import WPILib's Telemetry , TelemetryTable , Tunables , and TunableDouble instead. The rest stays the same."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#why-it-matters","t":"In Lab 1 you spun one motor. Now you'll drive a whole robot. The drivetrain is the most important mechanism on any robot: if it can't drive well, nothing else matters. In this lab you'll program the team's West Coast Drive (WCD) so a driver can move it around with an Xbox controller. Along the way you'll learn three ideas every drivetrain uses: Leaders and followers: several motors acting as one Inversion: why one side of the robot has to run \"backward\" Skid steer driving: each joystick runs one side of the robot, just like the two levers on a skid steer loader Before you start: finish Lab 1.1 . Lesson 10 explains the drive code in more depth; read it before or after."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#read-this","t":"Using the WPILib Drive Classes Joysticks"},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"What's a West Coast Drive?","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#whats-a-west-coast-drive","t":"A WCD steers like a skid steer loader : the left wheels all move together, the right wheels all move together, and there's no steering. To turn, one side drives faster than the other, and the wheels skid sideways across the floor. That's where the name skid steer comes from. flowchart LR subgraph L [\"Left side\"] LG[Gearbox<br/>2 CIMs] --> LW[3 wheels,<br/>chained together] end subgraph R [\"Right side\"] RG[Gearbox<br/>2 CIMs] --> RW[3 wheels,<br/>chained together] end Each side has one gearbox with two CIM motors. Both CIMs turn the same gear, so they must always push in the same direction. The middle wheels sit slightly lower (\"dropped center\"), so the robot rocks onto fewer wheels and turns more easily."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"The hardware","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#the-hardware","t":"Part Details Motors 4 CIM motors, 2 per side. CIMs are brushed motors. Motor controllers 4 SPARK MAXes, one per CIM, on CAN CAN IDs Left leader 1 , left follower 2 , right leader 3 , right follower 4 (check yours, Step 1) Breakers 40 A, one per motor Controller An Xbox controller on the Driver Station laptop The RoboJacks West Coast Drive chassis, before the elevator goes on. Front is at the top of the photo. Find each of these on the real chassis before you write any code: In the photo What it is Gray box near the top The roboRIO , where your code runs Four black motors in the middle The drive motors , two on each side, each pair driving one gearbox Chains along the left and right edges Connect each gearbox to that side's three wheels, so all three turn together Black and red board at the bottom The Power Distribution Hub , with one breaker per motor White box labeled 4674-6 The radio : the Driver Station connects to the robot through it Breaker with a red button, bottom right The main breaker : press the red button to turn off all power White box labeled SPARK MAX A SPARK MAX motor controller Trace the wires from one motor to its SPARK MAX, and from the SPARK MAX to its breaker on the Power Distribution Hub. Knowing which controller drives which motor makes Step 1 much easier. CIMs are brushed motors Every SPARK MAX in this lab must be set up as MotorType.kBrushed . The wrong motor type can damage the motor and the controller. This is the opposite of Lab 1's NEO, so read that line twice."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"New project","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#new-project","t":"The WCD is its own robot with its own roboRIO, so it gets its own project. Repeat Lab 0 , Parts 2 to 4, but name the project WestCoastDrive . Remember to install REVLib . Then copy the telemetry folder from your TestBed project ( Lab 1.1 ) into the new project's frc/robot folder, next to Robot.java . (It's also on GitHub .) Every subsystem uses it to send values to Elastic."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Step 1: Check the SPARK MAXes","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#step-1-check-the-spark-maxes","t":"Connect each SPARK MAX to your laptop over USB-C and open the REV Hardware Client . Write down each one's CAN ID and which motor it drives (left front, left back, and so on). Put tape labels on the controllers. Pick one controller on each side as the leader . The other is its follower . Check that no two SPARK MAXes share an ID. A duplicate makes motors twitch or not respond ( Lesson 4 )."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Step 2: Constants","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#step-2-constants","t":"Open Constants.java and add a class for the drivetrain. Use the CAN IDs you wrote down. public final class Constants { public static class OperatorConstants { public static final int kControllerPort = 0 ; public static final double kDeadband = 0.08 ; // ignore tiny stick movements } public static class kDriveConstants { public static final int LeftLeaderCanId = 1 ; public static final int LeftFollowerCanId = 2 ; public static final int RightLeaderCanId = 3 ; public static final int RightFollowerCanId = 4 ; public static final int CurrentLimit = 40 ; // amps, matches the 40 A breakers public static final double MaxOutput = 0.5 ; // top speed, 0 to 1. Start slow! } } MaxOutput caps the robot at 50% speed. Raise it only after the robot drives correctly."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Step 3: Leaders and followers","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#step-3-leaders-and-followers","t":"Each side has two motors in one gearbox. Instead of sending every command to both, you send commands to the leader , and tell the follower to copy it. Leader Follower Your code tells it The speed to run Just \"follow the leader\" What it does Runs at that speed Matches the leader's output automatically Following happens on the SPARK MAXes themselves, so the code only ever talks to two motors: left leader and right leader."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Step 4: Why one side is inverted","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#step-4-why-one-side-is-inverted","t":"The left and right gearboxes face opposite directions . If you send \"forward\" to both sides, the left wheels roll forward and the right wheels roll backward, and the robot spins in place. The fix is to invert one side's leader, so \"forward\" means forward on both. We'll invert the right side. If your robot drives backward or spins when you push forward, you'll invert the other side instead (Step 8)."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Step 5: The drive subsystem","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#step-5-the-drive-subsystem","t":"Create subsystems/DriveSubsystem.java (right-click subsystems → Create a new class/command → Subsystem (New) ). Replace its contents: package frc.robot.subsystems ; import java.util.function.DoubleSupplier ; import com.revrobotics.PersistMode ; import com.revrobotics.ResetMode ; import com.revrobotics.spark.SparkLowLevel.MotorType ; import com.revrobotics.spark.SparkMax ; import com.revrobotics.spark.config.SparkBaseConfig.IdleMode ; import com.revrobotics.spark.config.SparkMaxConfig ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.RobotController ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.drive.DifferentialDrive ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.Command ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; import frc.robot.Constants.kDriveConstants ; import frc.robot.telemetry.Telemetry ; import frc.robot.telemetry.TelemetryTable ; public class DriveSubsystem extends SubsystemBase { // Four SPARK MAXes, all BRUSHED, because CIMs are brushed motors. private final SparkMax m_leftLeader = new SparkMax ( kDriveConstants . LeftLeaderCanId , MotorType . kBrushed ); private final SparkMax m_leftFollower = new SparkMax ( kDriveConstants . LeftFollowerCanId , MotorType . kBrushed ); private final SparkMax m_rightLeader = new SparkMax ( kDriveConstants . RightLeaderCanId , MotorType . kBrushed ); private final SparkMax m_rightFollower = new SparkMax ( kDriveConstants . RightFollowerCanId , MotorType . kBrushed ); // DifferentialDrive is WPILib's class for any drivetrain with a left side and a right side. // We only hand it the two leaders. The followers copy them. private final DifferentialDrive m_drive = new DifferentialDrive ( m_leftLeader :: set , m_rightLeader :: set ); // Everything this subsystem sends shows up under \"Drive\" in Elastic private final TelemetryTable m_telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Drive\" ); public DriveSubsystem () { // Left leader: normal direction SparkMaxConfig leftLeaderConfig = baseConfig (); // Left follower: copy the left leader SparkMaxConfig leftFollowerConfig = baseConfig (); leftFollowerConfig . follow ( kDriveConstants . LeftLeaderCanId ); // Right leader: inverted, because the right gearbox faces the other way SparkMaxConfig rightLeaderConfig = baseConfig (); rightLeaderConfig . inverted ( true ); // Right follower: copy the right leader SparkMaxConfig rightFollowerConfig = baseConfig (); rightFollowerConfig . follow ( kDriveConstants . RightLeaderCanId ); m_leftLeader . configure ( leftLeaderConfig , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); m_leftFollower . configure ( leftFollowerConfig , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); m_rightLeader . configure ( rightLeaderConfig , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); m_rightFollower . configure ( rightFollowerConfig , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); // Cap the top speed while we're learning. Raise this in Constants later. m_drive . setMaxOutput ( kDriveConstants . MaxOutput ); } /** Settings every drive motor shares. */ private static SparkMaxConfig baseConfig () { SparkMaxConfig config = new SparkMaxConfig (); config . smartCurrentLimit ( kDriveConstants . CurrentLimit ); // protects motors and breakers config . idleMode ( IdleMode . kBrake ); // stop quickly when sticks are released return config ; } /** * Differential drive with two sticks: one value for the left side, one for the right. * Suppliers are read every loop, so the robot follows the sticks as they move. */ public Command differentialDriveCommand ( DoubleSupplier leftSpeed , DoubleSupplier rightSpeed ) { return run (() -> m_drive . tankDrive ( leftSpeed . getAsDouble (), rightSpeed . getAsDouble ())) . withName ( \"Differential Drive\" ); } @Override public void periodic () { m_telemetry . log ( \"Left Output\" , m_leftLeader . getAppliedOutput ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Right Output\" , m_rightLeader . getAppliedOutput ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Left Leader Current (A)\" , m_leftLeader . getOutputCurrent ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Left Follower Current (A)\" , m_leftFollower . getOutputCurrent ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Right Leader Current (A)\" , m_rightLeader . getOutputCurrent ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Right Follower Current (A)\" , m_rightFollower . getOutputCurrent ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Battery (V)\" , RobotController . getBatteryVoltage ()); } }"},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"The drive subsystem, piece by piece","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#the-drive-subsystem-piece-by-piece","t":"1. Four motor controllers, all brushed private final SparkMax m_leftLeader = new SparkMax ( kDriveConstants . LeftLeaderCanId , MotorType . kBrushed ); private final SparkMax m_leftFollower = new SparkMax ( kDriveConstants . LeftFollowerCanId , MotorType . kBrushed ); // ...and the same for the right leader and right follower One SparkMax object for each SPARK MAX on the robot, each with its own CAN ID from Constants . They're MotorType.kBrushed because CIMs are brushed motors. (Lab 1's NEO was kBrushless .) Notice the import at the top: import frc.robot.Constants.kDriveConstants; . Importing the inner class lets you write kDriveConstants.LeftLeaderCanId instead of Constants.kDriveConstants.LeftLeaderCanId . 2. The DifferentialDrive private final DifferentialDrive m_drive = new DifferentialDrive ( m_leftLeader :: set , m_rightLeader :: set ); DifferentialDrive is WPILib's class for any robot with a left side and a right side. It does the math for driving, and it has a safety feature: if your code stops sending it speeds, it stops the motors. Part What it means m_leftLeader::set A method reference : \"to set the left side's speed, call m_leftLeader.set(...) .\" It's a shorter way to write the lambda speed -> m_leftLeader.set(speed) . Only the two leaders The followers copy their leaders on their own, so DifferentialDrive never needs to know about them 3. The telemetry table private final TelemetryTable m_telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Drive\" ); Everything this subsystem sends goes in Telemetry → Drive in Elastic ( Lab 1.1 ). 4. The constructor: one config per motor SparkMaxConfig leftLeaderConfig = baseConfig (); SparkMaxConfig leftFollowerConfig = baseConfig (); leftFollowerConfig . follow ( kDriveConstants . LeftLeaderCanId ); SparkMaxConfig rightLeaderConfig = baseConfig (); rightLeaderConfig . inverted ( true ); SparkMaxConfig rightFollowerConfig = baseConfig (); rightFollowerConfig . follow ( kDriveConstants . RightLeaderCanId ); Each motor controller gets its own settings. All four start from baseConfig() (piece 5), then: Motor Extra setting Why Left leader None It runs normally Left follower follow(LeftLeaderCanId) \"Copy whatever the controller with this CAN ID does.\" Following happens on the SPARK MAXes themselves, over CAN. Right leader inverted(true) The right gearbox faces the other way, so this flips it. Now \"forward\" is forward on both sides (Step 4). Right follower follow(RightLeaderCanId) Copies the right leader, including its inversion m_leftLeader . configure ( leftLeaderConfig , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); // ...and the same for the other three m_drive . setMaxOutput ( kDriveConstants . MaxOutput ); configure(...) sends each set of settings to its SPARK MAX, the same way as Lab 1. Then setMaxOutput(0.5) tells DifferentialDrive to scale every output down to at most 50%, so full stick is only half speed while you're learning. 5. Shared settings in one place private static SparkMaxConfig baseConfig () { SparkMaxConfig config = new SparkMaxConfig (); config . smartCurrentLimit ( kDriveConstants . CurrentLimit ); config . idleMode ( IdleMode . kBrake ); return config ; } All four motors need the same current limit and brake mode. Instead of writing those lines four times, this helper method builds a config with them and returns it. Each call makes a brand-new config, so changing one motor's config (like inverting it) doesn't change the others. private : only this class uses it. static : it doesn't use any of the subsystem's fields, so it doesn't need an object to run. 6. The drive command public Command differentialDriveCommand ( DoubleSupplier leftSpeed , DoubleSupplier rightSpeed ) { return run (() -> m_drive . tankDrive ( leftSpeed . getAsDouble (), rightSpeed . getAsDouble ())) . withName ( \"Differential Drive\" ); } Part What it means DoubleSupplier leftSpeed Not a number, but something you can ask for a number, any time, with getAsDouble() . Here, it will read the joystick. run(...) A command that runs its code every loop (every 20 ms) until something stops it. Like startEnd , it requires the subsystem. m_drive.tankDrive(left, right) Sends one speed to the left side and one to the right, each from -1 (full backward) to 1 (full forward) Why a DoubleSupplier instead of a plain double ? A double would be read once , when the command is created, and the robot would drive at that one speed forever. A supplier is read every loop , so the robot follows the sticks as they move. 7. Sending values to the dashboard @Override public void periodic () { m_telemetry . log ( \"Left Output\" , m_leftLeader . getAppliedOutput ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Left Leader Current (A)\" , m_leftLeader . getOutputCurrent ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Battery (V)\" , RobotController . getBatteryVoltage ()); // ... } periodic() runs every 20 ms, enabled or not. @Override means \"this replaces the empty periodic() that SubsystemBase already has.\" The values let you check the robot without guessing: each side's output, every motor's current, and the battery voltage."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Step 6: Skid steer controls","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#step-6-skid-steer-controls","t":"If you've ever run a skid steer loader, you already know how to drive this robot. A skid steer has two levers: the left lever runs the left wheels and the right lever runs the right wheels. Push both forward to go straight, push one further to turn, and push them opposite ways to spin in place. The Xbox controller's two sticks are the two levers. In code, WPILib calls this kind of drivetrain a differential drive , and this control style tank drive . Stick Controls Left stick , up and down Left side wheels Right stick , up and down Right side wheels To do this Push the sticks (like skid steer levers) Drive straight forward Both forward, the same amount Turn gently right Left stick a little more than the right Spin in place to the right (a skid steer's \"counter-rotate\") Left stick forward, right stick back Stop Let go of both m_drive.tankDrive(left, right) sends those two values straight to the two sides. It also squares them by default, which gives finer control at low speed: half stick gives about a quarter of full speed ( Lesson 10 ). Rename the template's controller first The WPILib template names the controller m_driverController , and its port kDriverControllerPort . This course calls them m_controller and kControllerPort . In VS Code, click on m_driverController , press F2 ( Rename Symbol ), and type m_controller . Do the same for kDriverControllerPort → kControllerPort . F2 renames every use at once, so nothing breaks. Open RobotContainer.java . Add the imports and create the subsystem: import edu.wpi.first.math.MathUtil ; import frc.robot.subsystems.DriveSubsystem ; private final DriveSubsystem m_driveSubsystem = new DriveSubsystem (); Then set the drive's default command in configureBindings() : private void configureBindings () { // Left stick drives the left side, right stick drives the right side, // whenever no other command is using the drivetrain m_driveSubsystem . setDefaultCommand ( m_driveSubsystem . differentialDriveCommand ( () -> - MathUtil . applyDeadband ( m_controller . getLeftY (), OperatorConstants . kDeadband ), () -> - MathUtil . applyDeadband ( m_controller . getRightY (), OperatorConstants . kDeadband ))); } Piece Why setDefaultCommand The drive command runs all the time, unless another command takes over the drivetrain () -> ... A lambda: read the stick every loop , not just once ( Lesson 3 ) getLeftY() and getRightY() The up-and-down axis of each stick The minus signs Xbox sticks report pushing forward as negative . WPILib wants forward to be positive. applyDeadband A stick resting in the middle rarely reads exactly 0. Without a deadband, the robot creeps. Delete or comment out the template's example subsystem and its auto in getAutonomousCommand() if you like; for now, make it return null so autonomous does nothing: public Command getAutonomousCommand () { return null ; // no autonomous yet }"},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Step 7: First drive, on blocks","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#step-7-first-drive-on-blocks","t":"Wheels off the ground first The first time new drive code runs, put the robot on blocks so all six wheels are off the ground. A drivetrain with a wrong sign can lurch or spin the moment it's enabled. Everyone stands clear. One person drives, one person watches the wheels, and the driver keeps a finger near Enter (disable). Build and Deploy . Plug in the Xbox controller and check it's in slot 0 on the Driver Station USB tab. Say \"Enabling!\" and enable TeleOperated . Gently push one stick at a time , then both together. Check every row: You do Should happen If not Nothing (sticks centered) All wheels still Raise kDeadband a little Left stick forward Only the left three wheels roll forward See Step 8 Right stick forward Only the right three wheels roll forward See Step 8 Both sticks forward All six wheels roll forward See Step 8 Both sticks back All six wheels roll backward See Step 8 Left forward, right back Left wheels forward, right wheels backward See Step 8 Let go Everything stops Check the default command is set Listen to the gearboxes If a gearbox grinds, buzzes, or hums without the wheels turning , or a breaker trips right away, disable immediately . It usually means a leader and follower are pushing against each other in the same gearbox. Check that the follower is set to follow the correct leader's CAN ID, and look at the dashboard: a follower drawing current while its side barely moves is the giveaway."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Step 8: Fixing directions","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#step-8-fixing-directions","t":"Change one thing at a time , deploy, and test again on blocks. What you see Fix One side rolls backward when its stick goes forward That side's direction is flipped. If it's the right side, remove inverted(true) from the right leader. If it's the left, add inverted(true) to the left leader. Both sides roll backward when both sticks go forward Move inverted(true) from the right leader to the left leader, or check both minus signs The left stick moves the right wheels The left and right CAN IDs are swapped in Constants . Swap them back. One side doesn't move at all That side's leader CAN ID is wrong, or its breaker is tripped A gearbox grinds or a breaker trips Leader and follower are fighting. Check the follow CAN IDs. Nothing moves, and the console repeats [Spark Max] IDs: 1, timed out while waiting for Period Status 0: HAL: CAN Receive has Timed Out and Setpoint command: Unknown error status The roboRIO can't hear that SPARK MAX on CAN. Check, in order: the yellow and green CAN wires aren't swapped (yellow to H , green to L , especially at the roboRIO); the controller's CAN ID in the REV Hardware Client matches Constants ; it has power; the CAN chain is connected all the way to the PDH, with TERM on ( Lesson 4 )."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Step 9: Drive on the floor","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#step-9-drive-on-the-floor","t":"Once every row in Step 7 passes on blocks: Put the robot on the floor in a big open space, with nobody in front of it. Keep MaxOutput at 0.5. Drive slowly: forward, backward, gentle turns, then a figure eight. Watch Elastic while you drive (put the currents and battery voltage on Graph widgets). Look at the current on each side when you start moving and when you turn in place. Spinning in place usually pulls the most current, because the wheels have to skid sideways across the carpet, the same reason a skid steer tears up a lawn when it turns. Watch the battery voltage drop while you do it. That's where brownouts come from ( Lesson 4 )."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Step 10: Commit","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#step-10-commit","t":"git add . git commit -m \"Lab 2: differential drive on the WCD\" Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab02-west-coast-drive on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#try-it","t":"Get every row in the Step 7 table to pass on blocks Drive a figure eight on the floor at 50% max output Add slow mode : while the right bumper is held, multiply both stick values by 0.4. (Hint: m_controller.rightBumper().getAsBoolean() inside the lambdas.) Drive a straight line across the room. Is it hard to push both sticks exactly the same? What could the code do to help? Change IdleMode.kBrake to IdleMode.kCoast for all motors. How does stopping feel different? Which would a driver want? Raise MaxOutput to 0.75 and watch the battery voltage while turning in place Challenge: some WCDs have 3 CIMs per side . What would you add to support a third motor on each side?"},{"p":"Lab 2: Drive a West Coast Drive","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab2-west-coast-drive.html#check-yourself","t":"Why are the CIMs set to kBrushed when Lab 1's motor was kBrushless ? CIMs are brushed motors and NEOs are brushless. The SPARK MAX drives them completely differently, and the setting must match the motor. What does a follower do, and why do we use them? It copies its leader's output. Both motors in a gearbox must always push together, so the code only commands the leader and the follower matches it automatically. Why is one side of the drivetrain inverted? The two gearboxes face opposite directions. Without inverting one side, \"forward\" on both would spin the robot in place. How does a skid steer (or this robot) turn without steering wheels? It runs the left and right sides at different speeds, and the wheels skid sideways. The faster side swings around the slower one. Running them in opposite directions spins the robot in place. What does tankDrive(left, right) do with the two stick values? It sends the left value to the left side and the right value to the right side, after squaring them for finer control at low speed. Why do we negate getLeftY() and getRightY() ? Xbox controllers report pushing a stick forward as a negative number, and WPILib expects forward to be positive. Why do the stick values go in lambdas ( () -> ... )? So they're read every loop. A plain number would be read once at startup, and the robot would never respond to the sticks again. A gearbox buzzes and gets hot, but the wheels barely turn. What's wrong, and what do you do? Disable immediately. The two motors in that gearbox are pushing against each other, usually because a follower is following the wrong leader or is set to the opposite direction. Why put the robot on blocks for the first test? If a direction or sign is wrong, the robot could lurch or spin into someone. On blocks, you can see every wheel's direction safely."},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#why-it-matters","t":"In Lab 1 you made one button spin a motor while you held it. Real robots need more than that: tap a button to start something, tap another to stop it, flip a mode on and off, run something for exactly two seconds, or use a trigger as a gas pedal. All of that comes down to how a command is started and how it ends . In this lab you'll wire up five different kinds of button on the same motor and watch exactly how each one behaves. Once you've felt the difference, you'll never mix up whileTrue and onTrue again. Before you start: finish Lab 1 . You'll add to the same TestBed project and the same SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . Lesson 7.1 explains what's happening underneath."},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#read-this","t":"Binding Commands to Triggers Command Decorators"},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Two questions every command answers","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#two-questions-every-command-answers","t":"Every command you bind to a button has to answer two questions: When does it start? That's decided by the binding : whileTrue , onTrue , toggleOnTrue , and so on. When does it end? That's decided by the binding and by the command itself: does it finish on its own, or run until something stops it? Get either one wrong and the motor won't stop when you expect, or won't start at all."},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Step 1: Add more commands to the subsystem","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#step-1-add-more-commands-to-the-subsystem","t":"Open SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem.java . You already have spinMotorCommand . Add these three below it: /** Stops the motor once, then ends immediately. */ public Command stopCommand () { return runOnce (() -> setMotorSpeed ( 0.0 )). withName ( \"Stop\" ); } /** Spins at a speed for a set number of seconds, then stops on its own. */ public Command spinForSecondsCommand ( double speed , double seconds ) { return spinMotorCommand ( speed ). withTimeout ( seconds ). withName ( \"Spin \" + seconds + \" s\" ); } /** Spins at whatever speed the supplier gives, read fresh every 20 ms. */ public Command spinWithSupplierCommand ( DoubleSupplier speed ) { return runEnd ( () -> setMotorSpeed ( speed . getAsDouble ()), // every loop: read the value, set the motor () -> setMotorSpeed ( 0.0 ) // when it ends: stop ). withName ( \"Throttle\" ); } Add this import at the top: import java.util.function.DoubleSupplier ; The three commands, piece by piece stopCommand return runOnce (() -> setMotorSpeed ( 0.0 )). withName ( \"Stop\" ); runOnce(...) builds a command that runs its code one time and then finishes right away . Here, it sets the speed to 0. Because it requires the subsystem, starting it also interrupts whatever command was using the motor. spinForSecondsCommand return spinMotorCommand ( speed ). withTimeout ( seconds ). withName ( \"Spin \" + seconds + \" s\" ); This doesn't write a new command from scratch. It reuses spinMotorCommand and adds a decorator : Part What it does spinMotorCommand(speed) The command from Lab 1: start spinning, stop when it ends .withTimeout(seconds) Wraps it so it ends by itself after that many seconds. Ending runs spinMotorCommand 's end action, so the motor stops. \"Spin \" + seconds + \" s\" Joins text and a number into one name, like Spin 2.0 s spinWithSupplierCommand return runEnd ( () -> setMotorSpeed ( speed . getAsDouble ()), // every loop () -> setMotorSpeed ( 0.0 ) // when it ends ). withName ( \"Throttle\" ); Part What it does DoubleSupplier speed Something you can ask for a number at any time. Here it will be \"how far is the trigger squeezed?\" runEnd(run, end) Runs the first lambda every loop while the command runs, and the second once when it ends speed.getAsDouble() Asks the supplier for the current number, fresh every 20 ms Compare runEnd with Lab 1's startEnd : startEnd sets the speed once at the start, which is fine for a fixed speed. A trigger keeps changing, so it has to be read every loop . When does each command end? Command Ends on its own? How it ends spinMotorCommand No Only when something cancels it. Then it stops the motor. stopCommand Yes, instantly Sets the speed to 0 once and finishes spinForSecondsCommand Yes After the time runs out. withTimeout cancels it, which stops the motor. spinWithSupplierCommand No When canceled. Then it stops the motor."},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Step 2: Bind five buttons","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#step-2-bind-five-buttons","t":"Open RobotContainer.java . Keep Lab 1's A binding and add the rest in configureBindings() : private void configureBindings () { // A: spin WHILE held (Lab 1) m_controller . a (). whileTrue ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . spinMotorCommand ( Constants . kBrushlessConstants . BrushlessMotorSpeed )); // B: tap to START spinning. Nothing in this binding stops it! m_controller . b (). onTrue ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . spinMotorCommand ( Constants . kBrushlessConstants . BrushlessMotorSpeed )); // X: tap to STOP m_controller . x (). onTrue ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . stopCommand ()); // Y: tap once to start, tap again to stop m_controller . y (). toggleOnTrue ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . spinMotorCommand ( Constants . kBrushlessConstants . BrushlessMotorSpeed )); // Right bumper: tap to spin for exactly 2 seconds m_controller . rightBumper (). onTrue ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . spinForSecondsCommand ( Constants . kBrushlessConstants . BrushlessMotorSpeed , 2.0 )); // Right trigger: squeeze like a gas pedal. Harder = faster. m_controller . rightTrigger (). whileTrue ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . spinWithSupplierCommand ( () -> m_controller . getRightTriggerAxis () * 0.6 )); // capped at 60% // Show which commands are running, live SmartDashboard . putData ( CommandScheduler . getInstance ()); } Add the imports CommandScheduler and SmartDashboard if VS Code underlines them (click the lightbulb). The bindings, piece by piece Every binding has the same shape: a trigger , then a binding type , then a command . m_controller . b (). onTrue ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . spinMotorCommand (...) ); // trigger binding command Trigger Becomes true when m_controller.a() , b() , x() , y() That button is pressed m_controller.rightBumper() The right bumper is pressed m_controller.rightTrigger() The right trigger is squeezed past halfway Binding type What it does with the command whileTrue(cmd) Starts it when the trigger becomes true, cancels it when the trigger becomes false onTrue(cmd) Starts it when the trigger becomes true. Never cancels it. toggleOnTrue(cmd) Each press flips it: starts it if it isn't running, cancels it if it is So A ( whileTrue ) spins only while you hold it, but B ( onTrue ) starts the same command and never stops it. Step 4 is about that. The last line, SmartDashboard.putData(CommandScheduler.getInstance()) , puts the command scheduler on the dashboard: a live list of every command that's running. Why SmartDashboard here? The scheduler isn't a single number. It's a Sendable , a WPILib object that describes itself to the dashboard. In 2026, Sendables still go on the dashboard with SmartDashboard.putData . Plain values always use the course's Telemetry library ( Lesson 6.1 ). rightTrigger() vs. getRightTriggerAxis() rightTrigger() is a button : it becomes true once you squeeze past halfway. getRightTriggerAxis() is a number from 0 to 1 that tells how far you're squeezing. The binding uses the button to start the command, and the command reads the number every loop."},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Step 3: Test each button and fill in the table","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#step-3-test-each-button-and-fill-in-the-table","t":"Deploy, open Elastic, find Scheduler in the SmartDashboard table, drag it onto your tab, and enable. Test one button at a time . For each, write down what you observe: Button Binding What starts it? What stops it? Your notes A whileTrue B onTrue X onTrue (stop) Y toggleOnTrue Right bumper onTrue + timeout Right trigger whileTrue + supplier Watch the Scheduler widget while you test. You'll see each command's name appear when it starts and disappear when it ends."},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Step 4: The B button puzzle","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#step-4-the-b-button-puzzle","t":"Tap B . The motor spins and keeps spinning after you let go. Now try each of these and explain what happens: Tap B , then tap X . Tap B , then hold A and let go. Tap B , then tap the right bumper and wait. In every case the motor stops, but for the same reason: only one command can use a subsystem at a time . All of these commands belong to SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem , so starting any of them interrupts whatever was running. The interrupted command's end action runs, and the new command takes over. The classic beginner bug onTrue with a command that never ends means \"start this and never stop it.\" That's fine when another button stops it (like B and X here), but it's a bug when you meant \"while held.\" If a motor won't stop when you let go, check whether it's bound with onTrue ."},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Step 5: A default command","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#step-5-a-default-command","t":"Right now, when no command is running, the SPARK MAX just keeps doing whatever it was last told. A default command runs whenever nothing else is using the subsystem, so you can make \"stopped\" the resting state. In configureBindings() , after the button bindings: m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . setDefaultCommand ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . run (() -> m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . setMotorSpeed ( 0.0 )) . withName ( \"Idle\" )); Part What it does setDefaultCommand(cmd) Tells the scheduler: \"whenever no other command is using this subsystem, run this one\" m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem.run(...) Builds a command from outside the subsystem. It still requires the subsystem, because you called run on it. () -> ...setMotorSpeed(0.0) Every loop, set the speed to 0 Deploy and watch the Scheduler widget. Idle shows whenever no button command is running. Press any button: Idle disappears, the button's command runs, and when it ends, Idle comes right back. Think about it The default command uses run , not runOnce . Why? (Hint: what would happen if the default command finished instantly? See Lesson 7.1 .)"},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Step 6: Commit","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#step-6-commit","t":"git add . git commit -m \"Lab 3: button bindings\" Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab03-buttons-triggers on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#try-it","t":"Fill in the whole table in Step 3 Do the three B puzzles in Step 4 and explain each one in a sentence Change the right bumper's time from 2.0 to 0.5 seconds Add a left bumper binding with onFalse : the motor spins for 1 second when you release the bumper Make the right trigger spin the motor backward instead (hint: one minus sign) Bind the left trigger so it only works while A is also held, using m_controller.leftTrigger().and(m_controller.a()) . What happens when you press only one of them?"},{"p":"Lab 3: Buttons and Triggers","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab3-buttons-triggers.html#check-yourself","t":"What's the difference between whileTrue and onTrue ? whileTrue starts the command when the button is pressed and cancels it when released. onTrue starts it when pressed and then lets it run until it ends on its own or something interrupts it. Why does tapping X stop the motor that B started? Both commands use the same subsystem, and only one command can use a subsystem at a time. Starting X's command interrupts B's command, which runs its end action and stops the motor. How does withTimeout(2.0) make a never-ending command end? It cancels the command after 2 seconds. Canceling runs the command's end action, which stops the motor. Why does the throttle command use a DoubleSupplier instead of a plain number? So it reads the trigger every loop. A plain number would be read once when the robot starts, and the speed would never change. What does a default command do? It runs whenever no other command is using that subsystem, so the subsystem always has a known resting behavior."},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#why-it-matters","t":"So far the robot has only done what the driver told it. Now it starts sensing the world. A limit switch is the simplest sensor there is: pressed or not pressed. Robots use them everywhere to know when a mechanism has reached the end of its travel: an elevator at the bottom, an arm against its hard stop, a climber fully retracted. Running a motor into a hard stop without stopping can strip gears, snap belts, or burn out the motor. In this lab you'll read a limit switch, show it on the dashboard, and make the motor stop by itself the moment the switch is pressed. Before you start: finish Lab 3 . You'll add to the same TestBed project. Lesson 8 covers sensors in more depth. Lesson 4.3 explains how limit switches are wired, including NO vs. NC."},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#read-this","t":"Digital Inputs – Software Limit Switches"},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"How a limit switch talks to the roboRIO","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#how-a-limit-switch-talks-to-the-roborio","t":"A limit switch connects to one of the roboRIO's DIO (digital input/output) ports. Each DIO port has three pins: signal , 5 V , and ground . A basic limit switch uses two of them: signal and ground . Inside the roboRIO, the signal pin is gently pulled up to \"high\" ( true ). When the switch closes, it connects signal to ground, which pulls it \"low\" ( false ). Switch Wired as \"normally open\" (NO) What get() returns Not pressed Circuit open true Pressed Circuit closed to ground false Pressed reads false This surprises everyone the first time. With the usual wiring, get() returns false when the switch is pressed. That's why the code below uses ! (not). Always check on the dashboard instead of guessing. A limit switch on a DIO port. Only signal and ground are used. From the WPILib docs, Digital Inputs - Hardware (CC BY 4.0). The test bed's switches have both NO (normally open) and NC (normally closed) contacts. If yours is wired to NC, the logic flips: pressed reads true . The dashboard test in Step 3 tells you which you have. On the test bed, the switch isn't attached to a mechanism, so you will be the mechanism: pressing the switch by hand is like an arm reaching its hard stop."},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Step 1: Constants","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#step-1-constants","t":"Find which DIO port the switch is wired to (follow the wire to the roboRIO). Add a new class to Constants.java : public static class kSensorConstants { public static final int LimitSwitchDio = 2 ; // the DIO port your switch uses }"},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Step 2: Add the switch to the subsystem","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#step-2-add-the-switch-to-the-subsystem","t":"Open SpinBrushlessMotorSubsystem.java . Add the switch next to the motor: private final DigitalInput m_limitSwitch = new DigitalInput ( Constants . kSensorConstants . LimitSwitchDio ); Part What it means DigitalInput WPILib's class for anything on a DIO port that's simply on or off new DigitalInput(2) \"Watch DIO port 2.\" The port number comes from Constants . m_limitSwitch.get() Reads the port right now: true or false Import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.DigitalInput . Then add a method that says what the switch means : /** True when the limit switch is pressed. */ public boolean isLimitPressed () { return ! m_limitSwitch . get (); // pressed pulls the signal low, so flip it } public boolean : the method answers a yes-or-no question, true or false . ! means \"not\": it flips true to false and back. With the usual wiring, the port reads false when the switch is pressed, so ! turns that into \"pressed = true .\" The /** ... */ comment above it is a Javadoc comment. VS Code shows it when you hover over isLimitPressed() anywhere in your code. Name it for what it means isLimitPressed() is much clearer than getSwitch() . Everywhere else in your code, if (isLimitPressed()) reads like English and hides the \"pressed is false\" wiring detail in one place. Add it to periodic() so you can see it: m_telemetry . log ( \"Limit Pressed\" , isLimitPressed ());"},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Step 3: Check it on the dashboard","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#step-3-check-it-on-the-dashboard","t":"Deploy (the robot can stay disabled ). In Elastic, find Limit Pressed in Telemetry → Brushless and show it as a Boolean Box . Not touching the switch: shows false Holding the switch down: shows true If it's backward, your switch is wired NC. Remove the ! in isLimitPressed() and deploy again. Getting this right now saves confusion later."},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Step 4: Spin until the switch is pressed","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#step-4-spin-until-the-switch-is-pressed","t":"Add a command that runs the motor and stops by itself when the switch is hit: /** Spins until the limit switch is pressed, then stops. */ public Command spinUntilLimitCommand ( double speed ) { return spinMotorCommand ( speed ) . until ( this :: isLimitPressed ) . withName ( \"Spin Until Limit\" ); } Part What it does spinMotorCommand(speed) Lab 1's command: spin, and stop when it ends .until(this::isLimitPressed) Checks isLimitPressed() every 20 ms. The moment it's true , the command ends , and spinMotorCommand 's end action stops the motor. this::isLimitPressed A method reference : \"call this subsystem's isLimitPressed() whenever you need the answer.\" It's the short way to write () -> isLimitPressed() . Bind it in RobotContainer : // D-pad up: spin until the limit switch is pressed m_controller . povUp (). onTrue ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . spinUntilLimitCommand ( Constants . kBrushlessConstants . BrushlessMotorSpeed )); It's onTrue because the command ends by itself. Tap once and let it go. Test it: Enable. Tap D-pad up . The motor spins. Press the limit switch by hand. The motor stops immediately . Tap D-pad up while holding the switch down . What happens? Why?"},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Step 5: Don't even start if you're already at the limit","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#step-5-dont-even-start-if-youre-already-at-the-limit","t":"In step 3 of the test, the command started, saw the switch already pressed, and ended in the same loop. That works, but it's cleaner to say \"don't start at all\": public Command spinUntilLimitCommand ( double speed ) { return spinMotorCommand ( speed ) . until ( this :: isLimitPressed ) . unless ( this :: isLimitPressed ) // don't start if already pressed . withName ( \"Spin Until Limit\" ); } .unless(condition) checks the condition once, when the command is about to start . If it's true , the command is skipped entirely: it never starts, so the motor never twitches. .until and .unless look alike but answer different questions: .until is \"when should I stop?\", and .unless is \"should I start at all?\""},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Step 6: A trigger made from a sensor","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#step-6-a-trigger-made-from-a-sensor","t":"Buttons aren't the only triggers. Any true/false check can start a command. Add this in configureBindings() : // Whenever the limit switch becomes pressed, print a message new Trigger ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem :: isLimitPressed ) . onTrue ( Commands . print ( \"Limit switch hit!\" )); Part What it does new Trigger(...) Makes a trigger from any true/false check, not just a button m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem::isLimitPressed The check: is the switch pressed? The trigger asks every 20 ms. .onTrue(...) Runs the command each time the check changes from false to true Commands.print(\"...\") A ready-made command that prints a message to the console and ends Import Trigger and Commands if needed. Deploy, open RioLog, and press the switch. The message prints once each time the switch goes from not-pressed to pressed, not 50 times a second while you hold it. That's what onTrue means: it fires on the change ."},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Step 7: Commit","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#step-7-commit","t":"git add . git commit -m \"Lab 4: limit switch\""},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"When it doesn't work","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#when-it-doesnt-work","t":"Problem Likely cause Dashboard always shows the same value Wrong DIO port in Constants , or the wire isn't on the signal pin Value is backward The switch is wired NC. Remove the ! . Motor doesn't stop when the switch is pressed .until is missing, or isLimitPressed() returns the wrong value \"Limit switch hit!\" prints over and over You used whileTrue instead of onTrue , or the switch is bouncing (see the last exercise) Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab04-limit-switch on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#try-it","t":"Get Limit Pressed reading correctly in Elastic Spin until the switch is pressed with D-pad up Hold the switch, then tap D-pad up. Explain what happens with and without .unless . Use the second limit switch on the test bed. Make D-pad down spin backward until it's pressed, like an elevator going down to its bottom switch. Make A (from Lab 1) also refuse to spin while the switch is pressed Switches can \"bounce,\" flickering on and off for a few milliseconds when pressed. Add .debounce(0.05) to the Trigger from Step 6. What does it change?"},{"p":"Lab 4: Stop at a Limit Switch","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab4-limit-switch.html#check-yourself","t":"Why does a pressed limit switch usually read false ? The roboRIO pulls the signal pin high when nothing is connected. Pressing a normally-open switch connects the signal to ground, which pulls it low. Why put the ! inside isLimitPressed() instead of everywhere you check the switch? So the wiring detail lives in one place. The rest of the code reads clearly, and if the wiring changes, you fix one line. What does .until(this::isLimitPressed) do? It checks the switch every loop and ends the command the moment the switch is pressed. Ending the command runs its end action, which stops the motor. What's the difference between .until(...) and .unless(...) ? .until ends a running command when the condition becomes true. .unless stops the command from starting at all if the condition is already true. Why does the print in Step 6 happen once instead of 50 times a second? onTrue fires when the trigger changes from false to true, not while it stays true."},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#why-it-matters","t":"A limit switch only knows two things: pressed or not. Most sensors report a range of values instead: how far, how fast, what angle. These are analog sensors. The test bed's 10-turn potentiometer (\"pot\") is a knob that reports how far it's been turned. In this lab you'll read it, turn its raw reading into something useful, and use it to set the motor's speed: turn the knob one way to spin forward, the other way to spin backward, and leave it in the middle to stop. Turning one range of numbers into another is something you'll do with almost every sensor, so this lab is about more than knobs. Before you start: finish Lab 4 . You'll add to the same TestBed project. Lesson 4.3 explains how a potentiometer works."},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#read-this","t":"Analog Potentiometers – Software Analog Inputs – Software"},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"How a potentiometer works","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#how-a-potentiometer-works","t":"A potentiometer has three wires. Two connect across a strip of resistive material (5 V at one end, ground at the other), and the third, the wiper , slides along the strip as you turn the knob. Knob position Wiper voltage Turned fully one way About 0 V Halfway About 2.5 V Turned fully the other way About 5 V It plugs into one of the roboRIO's Analog In ports (0 to 3), which measure that voltage. Ours is a 10-turn pot: it takes ten full turns of the knob to go from one end to the other, which gives very fine control. It has hard stops A multi-turn pot stops at each end. Don't force it past the end, and never connect one to a motor that could drive it past the stops. A potentiometer on an Analog In port. Swap the two outer wires to reverse which end reads 0 V. From the WPILib docs, Analog Potentiometers - Hardware (CC BY 4.0)."},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"Step 1: Constants","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#step-1-constants","t":"Find which Analog In port the pot is wired to, and add it to kSensorConstants : public static class kSensorConstants { public static final int LimitSwitchDio = 2 ; public static final int KnobAnalogPort = 0 ; // the Analog In port your pot uses public static final double KnobTurns = 10.0 ; // it's a 10-turn pot }"},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"Step 2: Read the knob","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#step-2-read-the-knob","t":"WPILib's AnalogPotentiometer turns the voltage into a number for you. Give it the port and the full range you want it to report. With a range of 10, it reports turns : about 0 at one end and about 10 at the other. Open RobotContainer.java and add the knob as a field, next to the controller: private final AnalogPotentiometer m_knob = new AnalogPotentiometer ( Constants . kSensorConstants . KnobAnalogPort , Constants . kSensorConstants . KnobTurns , 0.0 ); // offset: added to every reading Argument What it means KnobAnalogPort (0) Which Analog In port on the roboRIO the knob is wired to KnobTurns (10.0) The full range : when the knob is turned all the way, get() reports 10. Halfway reports 5. 0.0 The offset , added to every reading. 0 means \"no shift.\" Import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.AnalogPotentiometer . Add a telemetry table for the knob's values, below the knob (import frc.robot.telemetry.Telemetry and frc.robot.telemetry.TelemetryTable ): private final TelemetryTable m_knobTelemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Knob\" ); Why put the knob in RobotContainer? The knob is a control , like the Xbox controller, not part of the motor's mechanism. Controls live in RobotContainer ; mechanisms live in subsystems. Put the reading on the dashboard. RobotContainer doesn't have a periodic() , so add this line to the end of robotPeriodic() in Robot.java , after the scheduler runs: m_robotContainer . updateDashboard (); And add the method to RobotContainer : /** Called every loop from Robot.robotPeriodic(). */ public void updateDashboard () { m_knobTelemetry . log ( \"Turns\" , m_knob . get ()); } public void updateDashboard() : a new method you wrote. Robot calls it every loop, so the knob's value stays fresh on the dashboard. m_knob.get() reads the knob right now, as a number of turns from 0 to 10."},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"Step 3: Calibrate","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#step-3-calibrate","t":"Real pots almost never read exactly 0 and exactly 10 at the ends. Deploy (disabled is fine) and watch Turns in Telemetry → Knob . Turn the knob all the way one direction. Write down the reading: min = ______ Turn it all the way the other direction. max = ______ Find the middle: center = (min + max) ÷ 2 = ______ Add your numbers to kSensorConstants : public static final double KnobMin = 0.1 ; // your measured values public static final double KnobMax = 9.9 ; If the reading goes down when you turn the knob clockwise, that's fine. You'll decide which way is \"forward\" in the next step."},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"Step 4: Map the knob to a speed","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#step-4-map-the-knob-to-a-speed","t":"We want the knob's range to become a motor speed: Knob Speed min -1.0 (full backward) center 0 (stopped) max +1.0 (full forward) That's a linear map : a straight line from one range onto another. Here's the math, step by step: center = (min + max) / 2 halfRange = (max - min) / 2 speed = (turns - center) / halfRange Worked example with min = 0.1 and max = 9.9: center = 5.0, halfRange = 4.9 Knob at 7.45 turns: speed = (7.45 − 5.0) ÷ 4.9 = 0.5 Knob at 2.55 turns: speed = (2.55 − 5.0) ÷ 4.9 = −0.5 Add this method to RobotContainer : /** Turns the knob position into a motor speed from -1 to 1. */ private double knobToSpeed () { double min = Constants . kSensorConstants . KnobMin ; double max = Constants . kSensorConstants . KnobMax ; double center = ( min + max ) / 2.0 ; double halfRange = ( max - min ) / 2.0 ; double speed = ( m_knob . get () - center ) / halfRange ; speed = MathUtil . clamp ( speed , - 1.0 , 1.0 ); // never go past full speed speed = MathUtil . applyDeadband ( speed , 0.05 ); // a small dead zone in the middle return speed * 0.5 ; // cap at 50% while learning } Reading it from the top: Line What it does private double knobToSpeed() A method only RobotContainer uses ( private ) that answers with a number ( double ) double min = ...; and double max = ...; Local variables : short names for the two constants, so the math below is easier to read. They only exist while the method runs. double center = (min + max) / 2.0; The knob reading that should mean \"stopped\" double halfRange = (max - min) / 2.0; How far the knob can turn from center to either end double speed = (m_knob.get() - center) / halfRange; The linear map: how far from center, as a fraction of the way to the end. Center gives 0, either end gives 1 or -1. speed = MathUtil.clamp(speed, -1.0, 1.0); Overwrites speed with a version that can't go below -1 or above 1 return speed * 0.5; Hands the answer back to whoever called the method Why the safety lines matter: Line Why clamp If the knob reads a bit past your measured min or max, the speed still stays between -1 and 1 applyDeadband It's hard to leave a knob exactly at center. The dead zone makes \"close to center\" mean stopped. * 0.5 A safety cap while you test Add the speed to updateDashboard() so you can watch it: m_knobTelemetry . log ( \"Speed\" , knobToSpeed ());"},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"Step 5: Let the knob drive the motor","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#step-5-let-the-knob-drive-the-motor","t":"You already wrote spinWithSupplierCommand in Lab 3. It reads a speed every loop, which is exactly what the knob needs. Bind it in configureBindings() : // D-pad right: tap to let the knob control the motor, tap again to stop m_controller . povRight (). toggleOnTrue ( m_spinBrushlessMotorSubsystem . spinWithSupplierCommand ( this :: knobToSpeed )); this::knobToSpeed is a method reference : \"call knobToSpeed() every time you need a value.\" ( Lesson 3 ) Test it: Set the knob to the middle. Enable. Tap D-pad right . The motor shouldn't move. Slowly turn the knob one way. The motor speeds up. Watch Speed in Telemetry → Knob . Turn it back through the middle and the other way. The motor slows, stops, and reverses. Tap D-pad right again to hand control back. If the motor spins the \"wrong\" way for your taste, flip the sign: return -speed * 0.5;"},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"Step 6: Commit","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#step-6-commit","t":"git add . git commit -m \"Lab 5: knob controls speed\" Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab05-knob-speed on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#try-it","t":"Calibrate the knob and record min, max, and center Control the motor speed with the knob Graph Knob/Turns with the knob sitting still. Is the reading perfectly steady, or does it wiggle a tiny bit? That wiggle is noise , and every analog sensor has some. Change the dead zone from 0.05 to 0.15. How does the middle feel different? Make a second mapping where the knob only goes forward: min = stopped, max = full speed. Bind it to D-pad left. Combine labs: make the knob-controlled motor stop when the limit switch from Lab 4 is pressed"},{"p":"Lab 5: Control Speed with a Knob","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab5-knob-speed.html#check-yourself","t":"What's the difference between a digital and an analog sensor? A digital sensor reports only true or false. An analog sensor reports a range of values, like a voltage from 0 to 5 V. With min = 0.2 and max = 9.8, what speed does a knob reading of 7.4 give (before the 50% cap)? Center = 5.0, halfRange = 4.8. Speed = (7.4 − 5.0) ÷ 4.8 = 0.5 Why calibrate instead of assuming 0 and 10? Real pots rarely reach their exact ends. Using the real min and max makes center truly mean stopped, and makes the full range of the knob reach full speed. Why do we clamp after mapping? If the knob reads slightly past the measured min or max, the math would give a speed beyond ±1. Clamping keeps it in range. Why does the knob command use this::knobToSpeed instead of knobToSpeed() ? knobToSpeed() would run once, when the robot starts, and hand over a single number. this::knobToSpeed hands over the method itself, so the command can call it every loop and follow the knob."},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#why-it-matters","t":"The limit switch told you whether something happened. The knob told you where a dial was. An encoder tells you how far a shaft has turned , and how fast it's turning, which is what you need to move a mechanism a precise amount. In this lab you'll read the encoder on the test bed's 775pro wheel, turn its raw counts into wheel rotations, and then try to make the wheel turn exactly 10 times two different ways. Neither way will be perfect. Seeing exactly how they miss is what makes PID make sense in Lab 9 . Before you start: finish Lab 5 . You'll add to the same TestBed project. Lesson 8 explains encoders and units. Lessons 4.1 (the 775pro) and 4.3 (the Through Bore Encoder) cover this lab's hardware."},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#read-this","t":"Encoders – Software Encoders – Hardware"},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"The hardware","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#the-hardware","t":"Part Connected to In code 775pro motor driving the wheel through a gearbox SPARK MAX, brushed mode , on CAN REVLib SparkMax Quadrature encoder on the wheel's shaft roboRIO DIO ports (channel A and B) WPILib Encoder A second Xbox controller Driver Station, slot 1 CommandXboxController(1) Wiring the encoder The REV Through Bore Encoder's cable ends in four 3-pin plugs. Only two go to the roboRIO: The encoder end of the cable is the white JST plug (right). The four 3-pin Dupont plugs go to the roboRIO. Each has a red wire (5 V), a black wire (ground), and one colored signal wire. Plug (signal wire color) Signal Goes to Blue Channel A DIO 0 Yellow Channel B DIO 1 Green Index (once per turn) Not used. Leave it unplugged and tape it out of the way. White Absolute position Not used in these labs. Leave it unplugged and tape it out of the way. Which way around? Each roboRIO DIO port is three pins in a row, and the roboRIO has a label printed next to each row of pins: the ground symbol (⏚), V (5 V), and S (signal). Turn each plug so the black wire is on ground (⏚), the red wire is on V , and the blue or yellow wire is on S . Every plug on this cable has its own red and black, so each one powers itself; that's normal. ( Lesson 4.3 has more.) We count turns of the wheel , not the motor. A 775pro spins about 18,700 RPM with no load, so 10 turns of the motor shaft would be over in a blink. The gearbox slows it down enough to watch. Why a second controller? Your first controller already has buttons for the brushless motor from Labs 1 to 5. Giving the wheel its own controller keeps every binding working. Real robots do the same thing: a driver controller and an operator controller. No second controller? Comment out the Lab 3 bindings while you do this lab. Safety Keep fingers, hair, and loose sleeves away from the wheel. The 775pro has a lot of power and burns out if it stalls , so never hold the wheel still while the motor is pushing. Have one person ready to disable at all times."},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"How a quadrature encoder counts","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#how-a-quadrature-encoder-counts","t":"A quadrature encoder sends two square-wave signals, A and B , a quarter-cycle apart. Each full cycle means the shaft turned a tiny bit. Which signal changes first tells the roboRIO the direction . Which channel rises first tells the roboRIO the direction. From the WPILib docs, Encoders - Software (CC BY 4.0). The roboRIO counts every rising and falling edge on both channels: that's 4 counts per cycle (\"4x decoding\"). A REV Through Bore Encoder makes 2048 cycles per revolution, which is 8192 counts . Term Through Bore Encoder Cycles per revolution (WPILib calls these \"pulses\") 2048 Counts per revolution (4x decoding) 8192 The roboRIO's FPGA chip counts in hardware, so it never misses a count, even though your code only runs every 20 ms."},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Step 1: Check the SPARK MAX","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#step-1-check-the-spark-max","t":"Open the REV Hardware Client and write down the CAN ID of the 775pro's SPARK MAX. It's a brushed motor, so this SPARK MAX must be in brushed mode, unlike the NEO's."},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Step 2: Constants","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#step-2-constants","t":"Add this class inside Constants in Constants.java . Fill in your real CAN ID and DIO ports. public static class kWheelConstants { public static final int MotorCanId = 9 ; public static final boolean MotorInverted = false ; public static final int EncoderChannelA = 0 ; public static final int EncoderChannelB = 1 ; public static final boolean EncoderInverted = false ; // WPILib counts in quadrature CYCLES. Through Bore Encoder = 2048 (not 8192). public static final int EncoderCyclesPerRev = 2048 ; public static final int CurrentLimitAmps = 30 ; // protects the 775pro public static final double MaxVolts = 6.0 ; // keeps PID tests gentle in Lab 9 }"},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Step 3: The subsystem","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#step-3-the-subsystem","t":"Create subsystems/WheelSubsystem.java . This part sets up the motor and encoder, converts to wheel rotations, and publishes telemetry. No movement commands yet. package frc.robot.subsystems ; import com.revrobotics.PersistMode ; import com.revrobotics.ResetMode ; import com.revrobotics.spark.SparkLowLevel.MotorType ; import com.revrobotics.spark.SparkMax ; import com.revrobotics.spark.config.SparkBaseConfig.IdleMode ; import com.revrobotics.spark.config.SparkMaxConfig ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.CounterBase.EncodingType ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.Encoder ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.Command ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; import frc.robot.Constants.kWheelConstants ; import frc.robot.telemetry.Telemetry ; import frc.robot.telemetry.TelemetryTable ; public class WheelSubsystem extends SubsystemBase { private final SparkMax m_motor = new SparkMax ( kWheelConstants . MotorCanId , MotorType . kBrushed ); private final Encoder m_encoder = new Encoder ( kWheelConstants . EncoderChannelA , kWheelConstants . EncoderChannelB , kWheelConstants . EncoderInverted , EncodingType . k4X ); private double m_target = 0.0 ; // where the current attempt is trying to stop private double m_lastVolts = 0.0 ; // for the dashboard private final TelemetryTable m_telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Wheel\" ); public WheelSubsystem () { SparkMaxConfig config = new SparkMaxConfig (); config . smartCurrentLimit ( kWheelConstants . CurrentLimitAmps ); config . idleMode ( IdleMode . kBrake ); config . inverted ( kWheelConstants . MotorInverted ); m_motor . configure ( config , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); // One \"pulse\" = one quadrature cycle. After this, getDistance() is in wheel rotations. m_encoder . setDistancePerPulse ( 1.0 / kWheelConstants . EncoderCyclesPerRev ); } public double getRotations () { return m_encoder . getDistance (); } public double getRps () { return m_encoder . getRate (); } private void setVolts ( double volts ) { m_lastVolts = volts ; m_motor . setVoltage ( volts ); } private void stop () { m_lastVolts = 0.0 ; m_motor . stopMotor (); } /** Every attempt turns a number of rotations FROM WHEREVER THE WHEEL IS NOW. */ private void setTargetFromHere ( double rotations ) { m_target = getRotations () + rotations ; } public Command zero () { return runOnce ( m_encoder :: reset ). ignoringDisable ( true ). withName ( \"Zero\" ); } public Command idle () { return run ( this :: stop ). withName ( \"Idle\" ); } @Override public void periodic () { m_telemetry . log ( \"Position (rot)\" , getRotations ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Target (rot)\" , m_target ); m_telemetry . log ( \"Error (rot)\" , m_target - getRotations ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Velocity (rot per s)\" , getRps ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Output (V)\" , m_lastVolts ); m_telemetry . log ( \"Current (A)\" , m_motor . getOutputCurrent ()); } }"},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"The wheel subsystem, piece by piece","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#the-wheel-subsystem-piece-by-piece","t":"1. The motor: a brushed 775pro private final SparkMax m_motor = new SparkMax ( kWheelConstants . MotorCanId , MotorType . kBrushed ); The same kind of field as Lab 1, but this SPARK MAX drives a 775pro , which is brushed , on CAN ID 9. 2. The encoder private final Encoder m_encoder = new Encoder ( kWheelConstants . EncoderChannelA , kWheelConstants . EncoderChannelB , kWheelConstants . EncoderInverted , EncodingType . k4X ); Argument What it means EncoderChannelA , EncoderChannelB The two DIO ports the encoder's A and B wires are plugged into (0 and 1) EncoderInverted true flips the direction, so \"forward\" counts up. You'll check this in Step 5. EncodingType.k4X Count every edge of both signals: 4 counts per cycle, the most precise setting Encoder is a WPILib class: the encoder is wired to the roboRIO, not to the SPARK MAX. 3. Remembering the target and the output private double m_target = 0.0 ; // where the current attempt is trying to stop private double m_lastVolts = 0.0 ; // for the dashboard private final TelemetryTable m_telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Wheel\" ); These aren't final , because they change while the robot runs. m_target is where the current move is trying to stop. m_lastVolts remembers the last voltage sent, so the dashboard can show it. 4. The constructor SparkMaxConfig config = new SparkMaxConfig (); config . smartCurrentLimit ( kWheelConstants . CurrentLimitAmps ); config . idleMode ( IdleMode . kBrake ); config . inverted ( kWheelConstants . MotorInverted ); m_motor . configure ( config , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); m_encoder . setDistancePerPulse ( 1.0 / kWheelConstants . EncoderCyclesPerRev ); The first five lines set up the motor like Lab 1, with a 30 A limit (a 775pro overheats quickly) and a setting to flip the motor's direction if needed. The last line is the important one. setDistancePerPulse tells the encoder how far one \"pulse\" (one full cycle) moves. One wheel rotation is 2048 cycles, so one cycle is 1.0 / 2048 of a rotation. After this line, getDistance() answers in wheel rotations instead of raw counts. (The 4X counting is handled for you; that's why it's 2048, not 8192.) 5. Small methods with clear names public double getRotations () { return m_encoder . getDistance (); } public double getRps () { return m_encoder . getRate (); } Method Answers getRotations() How many rotations the wheel has turned since the encoder was zeroed getRps() How fast it's turning right now, in rotations per second. getRate() uses the same distance-per-pulse setting. These are written on one line each because they're so short. Giving them names means the rest of the code reads like English: getRotations() >= m_target . 6. Setting the motor, and stopping it private void setVolts ( double volts ) { m_lastVolts = volts ; m_motor . setVoltage ( volts ); } private void stop () { m_lastVolts = 0.0 ; m_motor . stopMotor (); } setVoltage asks for a number of volts (up to about 12), instead of a fraction like set(0.5) . 4 V is 4 V whether the battery is full or tired, so tests are more repeatable. Both methods also update m_lastVolts , so the dashboard always shows what the motor was told. They're private : only this subsystem's commands can move the motor. 7. Picking a target from where the wheel is now private void setTargetFromHere ( double rotations ) { m_target = getRotations () + rotations ; } \"Turn 10 rotations\" means 10 more from here , not \"go to position 10.\" If the wheel is at 3.2, the target becomes 13.2. That way you can run an attempt again without zeroing first. 8. Two simple commands public Command zero () { return runOnce ( m_encoder :: reset ). ignoringDisable ( true ). withName ( \"Zero\" ); } public Command idle () { return run ( this :: stop ). withName ( \"Idle\" ); } Code What it does runOnce(m_encoder::reset) Sets the encoder's count back to 0, once .ignoringDisable(true) Lets this command run while the robot is disabled , so you can zero it without enabling run(this::stop) Calls stop() every loop. It becomes the default command, so the wheel stays stopped when nothing else is running. 9. The dashboard @Override public void periodic () { m_telemetry . log ( \"Position (rot)\" , getRotations ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Target (rot)\" , m_target ); m_telemetry . log ( \"Error (rot)\" , m_target - getRotations ()); // ...velocity, output, and current } Error is the target minus the position: how far is left to go. It's positive before the target and negative after an overshoot. You'll watch it a lot in this lab and in Lab 9."},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Step 4: Bind the wheel controller","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#step-4-bind-the-wheel-controller","t":"Open RobotContainer.java . Add the wheel subsystem and a second controller: private final WheelSubsystem m_wheel = new WheelSubsystem (); private final CommandXboxController m_wheelController = new CommandXboxController ( 1 ); In configureBindings() , keep everything from earlier labs and add: // ---- Wheel (controller 1) ---- m_wheel . setDefaultCommand ( m_wheel . idle ()); m_wheelController . back (). onTrue ( m_wheel . zero ()); You'll add the move commands in Steps 6 and 7."},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Step 5: Check the sensor before anything else","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#step-5-check-the-sensor-before-anything-else","t":"Deploy, enable nothing yet, and open the dashboard. Direction. Turn the wheel by hand in the direction the motor will push. Position must go up . If it goes down, flip kEncoderInverted . Scale. Press Back on the wheel controller to zero, then turn the wheel exactly one full turn (put a tape mark on it). Position should read about 1.00 . If it reads about 0.25 or 4.0, your cycles-per-rev is wrong. Don't skip this If the encoder counts backward, the bang-bang command below never sees the target and runs until its timeout. In Lab 9, PID would push the wheel the wrong way and speed up instead of stopping. Thirty seconds of checking now saves a scary moment later."},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Step 5b: Look at the raw counts","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#step-5b-look-at-the-raw-counts","t":"m_encoder.getDistance() already gives rotations. To see what it's built from, add two more lines to periodic() : m_telemetry . log ( \"Raw Counts\" , m_encoder . getRaw ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Cycles\" , m_encoder . get ()); Zero the encoder and turn the wheel exactly once by hand. You should see about 8192 raw counts, about 2048 cycles, and 1.00 rotations. The setDistancePerPulse(1.0 / 2048) line is what turns cycles into rotations."},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Step 6: Attempt 1, timed (open loop)","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#step-6-attempt-1-timed-open-loop","t":"The idea: \"If it turns about 5 times a second at 4 volts, running it for 2 seconds should give 10 turns.\" public Command turnTimed ( double volts , double seconds ) { return startEnd (() -> setVolts ( volts ), this :: stop ) . withTimeout ( seconds ) . withName ( \"Attempt 1: Timed\" ); } Part What it does startEnd(() -> setVolts(volts), this::stop) When it starts, set the voltage. When it ends, stop. .withTimeout(seconds) End after this many seconds Nowhere does this code read the encoder. That's what open loop means: it acts without checking the result. Run it once with a guess like turnTimed(4.0, 2.0) . See how many turns you got. Adjust the time until one run lands near 10. Now run it three times without changing anything , and record the results. Hold a cloth lightly against the wheel (friction, like carpet) and run it again. What you'll see: Each run lands somewhere different, and the extra friction cuts it short. The code never looks at the encoder, so it has no idea how far the wheel actually went. Battery level, temperature, and friction all change the result. Bind it on the wheel controller: m_wheelController . a (). onTrue ( m_wheel . turnTimed ( 4.0 , 2.0 )); // Attempt 1"},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Step 7: Attempt 2, bang-bang (stop when you get there)","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#step-7-attempt-2-bang-bang-stop-when-you-get-there","t":"The idea: \"Use the encoder. Run until the position reaches 10, then stop.\" public Command turnBangBang ( double rotations , double volts ) { return runOnce (() -> setTargetFromHere ( rotations )) . andThen ( run (() -> setVolts ( volts ))) // full speed the whole way . until (() -> getRotations () >= m_target ) // stop the moment we arrive . withTimeout ( 5.0 ) // safety: give up if the encoder never gets there . finallyDo ( this :: stop ) . withName ( \"Attempt 2: Bang-Bang\" ); } Read it as a list of steps that run in order: Part What it does runOnce(() -> setTargetFromHere(rotations)) First, once: pick the target, 10 rotations from here .andThen(run(() -> setVolts(volts))) Then, every loop: run at full voltage .until(() -> getRotations() >= m_target) Every loop, check: have we reached the target? If so, end. .withTimeout(5.0) Safety: if 5 seconds pass and it still hasn't arrived (a loose encoder wire, say), give up .finallyDo(this::stop) However the command ends (arrived, timed out, or interrupted by another button), stop the motor >= is \"greater than or equal to.\" It uses >= instead of == because the position jumps past 10.000 between loops; it would almost never be exactly 10. It's called bang-bang because the output is either fully on or fully off, nothing in between. Run turnBangBang(10, 4.0) three times. Then try it at 2 V and at 6 V. What you'll see: Much more consistent than timing, but it always overshoots . When the code says stop, the wheel is still moving at full speed, and it takes time to coast to a halt, even in brake mode. The faster it's going, the further it overshoots. Watch Error go negative on the dashboard. Think about it How could you make it stop closer to 10? You could stop early, say at 9.8. But how early depends on speed, battery, and friction. What you really want is to slow down as you get close. That's exactly what PID does, in Lab 9 . Bind it: m_wheelController . b (). onTrue ( m_wheel . turnBangBang ( 10 , 4.0 )); // Attempt 2 Both use onTrue because each command ends by itself. Press once and let it run."},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"The scorecard","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#the-scorecard","t":"Run each attempt three times and record the final position from the dashboard. Keep this table. You'll add three more rows in Lab 9. Attempt Run 1 Run 2 Run 3 Worst error Notes 1. Timed 2. Bang-bang at 2 V 2. Bang-bang at 4 V 2. Bang-bang at 6 V"},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Commit","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#commit","t":"git add . git commit -m \"Lab 6: encoder, timed and bang-bang\" Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab06-encoder on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#try-it","t":"Check direction and scale, and get 1.00 for one turn by hand Watch Raw Counts , Cycles , and Position for one turn. Explain how they're related. Spin the wheel by hand and watch Velocity . What's the fastest you can spin it, in rotations per second? Complete the scorecard Press B three times in a row, waiting for each move to finish. Does the overshoot add up? Hold a cloth lightly against the wheel during a timed run and a bang-bang run. Which one still reaches 10? Challenge: write turnBangBang for backward moves too (negative rotations)"},{"p":"Lab 6: Count Turns with an Encoder","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab6-encoder.html#check-yourself","t":"How many counts per revolution does a Through Bore Encoder give with 4x decoding? How many cycles? 8192 counts, which is 2048 cycles. WPILib's setDistancePerPulse uses cycles. Why does a quadrature encoder need two channels? The order the two signals change tells the roboRIO which direction the shaft is turning. One channel alone could count, but couldn't tell forward from backward. Why does the timed attempt land in a different place each run? It never checks the encoder. Battery voltage, friction, and temperature change how far the wheel goes in a fixed time, and the code can't notice. Why does bang-bang always overshoot? It stops pushing only when the wheel reaches the target, but the wheel is still moving at full speed then, and it takes time to stop. Why does bang-bang overshoot more at 6 V than at 2 V? The wheel is moving faster when it reaches the target, so it carries more momentum and takes longer to stop."},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#why-it-matters","t":"The first 15 seconds of every FRC match are autonomous : the robot runs entirely on its own code, with no driver input. A robot that does something useful in auto starts every match ahead. In this lab you'll make the West Coast Drive drive a pattern by itself, and let the drive team pick which pattern from a menu on the dashboard. You'll use only time to decide how far to go, and you'll find out why that's not good enough. That sets up Lab 8 , where the robot uses a gyro to know how far it has really turned. Before you start: finish Lab 2 . You'll add to the same WestCoastDrive project. Lesson 11 covers autonomous in more depth."},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#read-this","t":"Command Compositions Lesson 6.1: A dropdown menu with Selectable"},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#walkthrough","t":"Autonomous safety In auto, nobody is steering . The robot drives the moment it's enabled. Clear a big space and keep everyone out of the robot's path. One person's only job is the disable key ( Enter ) and the emergency stop ( Space ). Test each new routine on blocks first . Keep MaxOutput at 0.5 from Lab 2."},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"How autonomous runs","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#how-autonomous-runs","t":"You don't have to write any of this, but you should know it's there. In Robot.java , the template already does: When auto starts, autonomousInit() asks RobotContainer.getAutonomousCommand() for a command and schedules it. The scheduler runs it every 20 ms until it ends. When teleop starts, teleopInit() cancels it, in case it's still running. So your whole job is to make getAutonomousCommand() return the right command. While an auto command is using the drivetrain, the joystick default command pauses, then comes back when auto ends."},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"Step 1: A command that drives for a set time","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#step-1-a-command-that-drives-for-a-set-time","t":"Open DriveSubsystem.java and add: /** Drives each side at a fixed speed for a set time, then stops. */ public Command driveForSecondsCommand ( double leftSpeed , double rightSpeed , double seconds ) { return run (() -> m_drive . tankDrive ( leftSpeed , rightSpeed , false )) // false = don't square . withTimeout ( seconds ) . finallyDo (() -> m_drive . stopMotor ()) . withName ( \"Drive \" + seconds + \" s\" ); } Piece Why double leftSpeed, double rightSpeed, double seconds Three inputs, so one method can make any timed drive: forward, backward, or spinning run(...) Every loop, send the speeds. DifferentialDrive needs a fresh speed every loop, or its safety feature stops the motors. tankDrive(left, right, false) The false turns off squaring, so 0.4 means 40%, not 16% withTimeout(seconds) Ends the command after that many seconds finallyDo(...) Runs when the command ends for any reason (finished, timed out, or canceled), so the motors always stop With this one command you can drive forward (both sides the same), backward (both negative), or spin in place (sides opposite). Remember the skid steer levers from Lab 2."},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"Step 2: Build routines from pieces","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#step-2-build-routines-from-pieces","t":"Routines are just commands chained together. Add these methods to RobotContainer.java : /** Drive straight forward for 2 seconds. */ private Command driveForwardAuto () { return m_driveSubsystem . driveForSecondsCommand ( 0.4 , 0.4 , 2.0 ) . withName ( \"Drive Forward\" ); } /** One side of a square: drive forward, pause, spin left about 90 degrees, pause. */ private Command squareSide () { return Commands . sequence ( m_driveSubsystem . driveForSecondsCommand ( 0.4 , 0.4 , 1.0 ), // forward m_driveSubsystem . driveForSecondsCommand ( 0.0 , 0.0 , 0.3 ), // pause m_driveSubsystem . driveForSecondsCommand ( - 0.4 , 0.4 , 0.5 ), // spin left (guess the time!) m_driveSubsystem . driveForSecondsCommand ( 0.0 , 0.0 , 0.3 )); // pause } /** Try to drive a square and end where it started. */ private Command squareAuto () { return Commands . sequence ( squareSide (), squareSide (), squareSide (), squareSide ()) . withName ( \"Square\" ); } Import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.Commands . Piece by piece: Code What it does private Command driveForwardAuto() A method that builds a routine and hands it back. Nothing moves until the routine is scheduled. driveForSecondsCommand(0.4, 0.4, 2.0) Both sides at 40% for 2 seconds: straight forward Commands.sequence(a, b, c, d) One command that runs a , then b , then c , then d . Each starts when the one before it ends. driveForSecondsCommand(-0.4, 0.4, 0.5) Left side backward, right side forward: spin left in place, for a guessed 0.5 seconds Commands.sequence(squareSide(), squareSide(), ...) A sequence made of sequences: four sides make a square .withName(\"Square\") The name the dashboard shows while it runs Why pause by driving at 0 instead of Commands.waitSeconds ? DifferentialDrive has a safety feature: if nothing sends it a speed for about a tenth of a second, it stops the motors and prints an \"Output not updated often enough\" warning. Driving at 0 for the pause keeps it fed, so the console stays quiet. Each squareSide() call builds a new set of commands, which matters: one command object can't be used twice in the same routine. The spin time is a guess Nobody knows how long a 90° spin takes. Start with 0.5 seconds and adjust it until one turn looks like 90°. That guessing is the point of this lab."},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"Step 3: Let the drive team pick","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#step-3-let-the-drive-team-pick","t":"A Selectable puts a dropdown menu in Elastic, sent over NetworkTables like everything else. It's part of the course's telemetry folder you copied in Lab 2, and it's the same as WPILib 2027's Selectable ( Lesson 6.1 ). Add a field to RobotContainer : private final Selectable < Command > m_autoChooser = new Selectable <> (); In the constructor, after configureBindings() , add the options and put the menu on the dashboard: m_autoChooser . addDefault ( \"Do nothing\" , Commands . none ()); m_autoChooser . add ( \"Drive forward\" , driveForwardAuto ()); m_autoChooser . add ( \"Square\" , squareAuto ()); Tunables . publish ( \"Auto\" , m_autoChooser ); // shows up as /Tunables/Auto And make getAutonomousCommand() return the choice: public Command getAutonomousCommand () { return m_autoChooser . getSelected (); } Import frc.robot.telemetry.Selectable and frc.robot.telemetry.Tunables . Code What it does Selectable<Command> A menu where each option is a Command . The <Command> tells Java what kind of thing is in the menu. new Selectable<>() Makes an empty menu. The <> means \"the same type as the field.\" addDefault(\"Do nothing\", Commands.none()) Adds an option and makes it the default. Commands.none() is a command that does nothing and ends right away. add(\"Square\", squareAuto()) Adds another option. squareAuto() runs now, when the menu is built, but only builds the routine; nothing moves yet. Tunables.publish(\"Auto\", m_autoChooser) Puts the menu on the dashboard getSelected() Returns the command for whatever the drive team picked Why not SendableChooser ? You'll see SendableChooser in older code and examples. It only reaches the dashboard through SmartDashboard.putData , which is going away in WPILib 2027. Selectable is its 2027 replacement, and the same lines of code work in 2027 ( Lesson 6.1 ). Why \\\"Do nothing\\\" is the default If the drive team forgets to pick, a robot that sits still is far better than one that runs a routine meant for a different starting spot and hits someone else's robot."},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"Step 4: Run it","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#step-4-run-it","t":"Deploy. In Elastic, find Tunables → Auto , drag it onto your tab (it shows up as a ComboBox Chooser ), and pick Drive forward . On blocks first: in the Driver Station, select Autonomous , say \"Enabling!\" , and enable. The wheels run for 2 seconds and stop. On the floor: put tape on the floor at the robot's front edge, and run it again. Mark where it stops. Run Square on blocks, then on the floor. Adjust the spin time in squareSide() until it looks close to a square."},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"Step 5: How repeatable is it?","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#step-5-how-repeatable-is-it","t":"Run Square five times without changing anything. Each time, measure how far the robot ends up from where it started. Run Distance from start Ended facing which way? 1 2 3 4 5 Then swap in a less charged battery and run it twice more. What you'll see: The square doesn't close, and it's different every time. Turns that were 90° become 80° or 100°, and the errors add up corner by corner. A weaker battery makes every move shorter. The problem is the same one from Lab 6's timed attempt: the robot doesn't measure how far it turned. It only knows how long it tried. In Lab 8 you'll add a gyro so it does."},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"Step 6: Commit","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#step-6-commit","t":"git add . git commit -m \"Lab 7: timed autonomous\" Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab07-timed-auto on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#try-it","t":"Run Drive forward and Square from the chooser Tune the spin time until a single turn is close to 90° Fill in the repeatability table, including the less charged battery Add a Drive backward option to the chooser Write a \" there and back \" routine: forward 1.5 seconds, spin 180°, forward 1.5 seconds. Does it end where it started? Make the spin a gentle curve instead of turning in place (hint: both sides forward, one faster)"},{"p":"Lab 7: Timed Autonomous","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab7-timed-auto.html#check-yourself","t":"What decides which command runs in autonomous? Whatever getAutonomousCommand() returns. Robot.java schedules it when auto starts and cancels it when teleop starts. What does Commands.sequence(a, b, c) do? Runs a , then b after a ends, then c after b ends. Why does driveForSecondsCommand use finallyDo to stop the motors? So the motors stop however the command ends: timed out, finished, or canceled when teleop starts. Why should \\\"Do nothing\\\" be the default auto? If nobody picks one, the robot stays still instead of running a routine meant for a different starting spot. Why doesn't the timed square end where it started? Time isn't distance or angle. Battery charge, carpet, and wheel wear change how far each timed move goes, and the errors add up at every corner."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#why-it-matters","t":"In Lab 7 , the timed square never closed. Every \"90° turn\" was really just \"spin for half a second,\" and the robot had no idea how far it had actually turned. A gyro fixes that. It measures how far the robot has rotated , so instead of \"spin for 0.5 seconds\" you can say \"spin until you've turned 90°.\" In this lab you'll add the team's NavX2 to the West Coast Drive, read the robot's heading, and use it to make turns that land where you meant. Then you'll fix the other half of the square. The sides were timed too. The WCD has an encoder on each gearbox, so you'll measure how far each side has rolled, in meters, and drive exact distances. By the end, the square is driven entirely by sensors: encoders for the straight sides and the gyro for the turns. Before you start: finish Lab 7 and keep its repeatability table. You'll add to the same WestCoastDrive project. Lesson 8 covers gyros. Lesson 4.3 covers the NavX2's hardware and mounting."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#read-this","t":"Gyroscopes – Software Encoders – Software NavX2-MXP (Studica)"},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"What a gyro measures","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#what-a-gyro-measures","t":"The NavX2 measures rotation around three axes. For driving, the one that matters is yaw : how far the robot has turned left or right, seen from above. That's the robot's heading . Robot turns Heading (WPILib convention) Left (counterclockwise from above) Goes up Right (clockwise from above) Goes down Yaw is the robot turning left or right. That's the heading. From the WPILib docs, Gyroscopes - Hardware (CC BY 4.0). The heading keeps counting past 360°. Spin left twice and it reads about 720°. Two sign conventions The NavX's own getAngle() and getYaw() count clockwise as positive. WPILib counts counterclockwise as positive. To avoid mixing them up, this lab only uses getRotation2d() , which already follows WPILib's convention."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"The hardware","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#the-hardware","t":"The NavX2 plugs into the roboRIO's MXP port (the wide connector in the middle). It talks to the roboRIO over SPI through that port. If your NavX is connected differently Over USB, use NavXComType.kUSB1 instead of kMXP_SPI in Step 3. Everything else stays the same. Each side's gearbox also has a REV Through Bore Encoder on its output shaft , the shaft that drives the wheels. Because the encoder turns at the same speed as the wheels, one encoder rotation is one wheel rotation. Both encoders are wired to the roboRIO's DIO ports, two ports each, the same way as the elevator's encoder in Lab 10 and the test bed wheel in Lab 6 . Encoder DIO ports Left gearbox 2 and 3 Right gearbox 4 and 5 Check your robot's wiring and use your real port numbers in Step 8."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 1: Calibration","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-1-calibration","t":"When the robot powers on, the NavX spends a few seconds calibrating : measuring itself while sitting perfectly still. If the robot moves during that time, the heading will drift for the rest of the session. Keep it still at power-on Turn on the robot and don't touch it for about 10 seconds. You'll add a dashboard light in Step 4 that shows when calibration is done."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 2: Install the NavX library","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-2-install-the-navx-library","t":"The NavX library comes from Studica, the company that makes it. Open WPILib → Vendor Dependencies in the VS Code sidebar. Find Studica (the NavX library) and click Install . If it isn't in the list, use Ctrl + Shift + P → WPILib: Manage Vendor Libraries → Install new library (online) and paste the vendordep URL from Studica's NavX documentation. Check that a Studica .json file appears in vendordeps/ ."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 3: Add the gyro to the drive subsystem","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-3-add-the-gyro-to-the-drive-subsystem","t":"Open DriveSubsystem.java . Add the imports: import com.studica.frc.AHRS ; import com.studica.frc.AHRS.NavXComType ; Add the gyro as a field: // NavX2 on the roboRIO's MXP port, talking over SPI private final AHRS m_gyro = new AHRS ( NavXComType . kMXP_SPI ); Part What it means AHRS Studica's class for the NavX. AHRS stands for \"attitude and heading reference system\": a sensor that knows which way it's pointing. NavXComType.kMXP_SPI How the NavX talks to the roboRIO: plugged into the MXP port, using the fast SPI connection Add methods to read and reset the heading: /** Robot heading in degrees. Turning left (counterclockwise) makes it go up. */ public double getHeadingDegrees () { return m_gyro . getRotation2d (). getDegrees (); } /** Makes the current direction the new 0°. Works while disabled. */ public Command zeroHeadingCommand () { return runOnce ( m_gyro :: reset ). ignoringDisable ( true ). withName ( \"Zero Heading\" ); } Code What it does m_gyro.getRotation2d() The robot's heading as a WPILib Rotation2d , where left (counterclockwise) is positive , the WPILib standard .getDegrees() Turns that into a plain number of degrees runOnce(m_gyro::reset) Makes \"the way the robot is pointing right now\" the new 0° .ignoringDisable(true) Lets you zero it while the robot is disabled, before a match"},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 4: Show it on the dashboard","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-4-show-it-on-the-dashboard","t":"Add to the drive subsystem's periodic() . These go in Telemetry → Drive , in a Gyro folder: m_telemetry . log ( \"Gyro/Heading (deg)\" , getHeadingDegrees ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Gyro/Connected\" , m_gyro . isConnected ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Gyro/Calibrating\" , m_gyro . isCalibrating ()); And bind a button to zero the heading, in configureBindings() : m_controller . back (). onTrue ( m_driveSubsystem . zeroHeadingCommand ());"},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 5: Check the gyro by hand","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-5-check-the-gyro-by-hand","t":"Deploy. Leave the robot disabled . Wait until Calibrating shows false and Connected shows true. Press Back to zero the heading. It reads about 0. Push the robot by hand so it turns left about a quarter turn. The heading should read about +90 . Turn it back. The heading returns to about 0. Turn it right a quarter turn. About −90 . Leave it completely still for a minute and watch the heading. Does it change at all? That slow creep is drift , and every gyro has a little. If Connected is false, check the NavX is fully seated on the MXP port and that the library is installed."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 6: Turn by the gyro (bang-bang)","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-6-turn-by-the-gyro-bang-bang","t":"Now replace \"spin for 0.5 seconds\" with \"spin until the heading changes by 90°.\" Add to DriveSubsystem : private double m_turnTarget = 0.0 ; /** * Spins in place until the heading has changed by the given degrees. * Positive = turn left, negative = turn right. */ public Command turnDegreesCommand ( double degrees , double speed ) { double direction = Math . signum ( degrees ); // +1 for left, -1 for right return runOnce (() -> m_turnTarget = getHeadingDegrees () + degrees ) . andThen ( run (() -> m_drive . tankDrive ( - direction * speed , direction * speed , false ))) . until (() -> direction > 0 ? getHeadingDegrees () >= m_turnTarget // turning left: wait until we pass the target : getHeadingDegrees () <= m_turnTarget ) // turning right: wait until we pass it going down . withTimeout ( 3.0 ) // safety: give up if the gyro never gets there . finallyDo (() -> m_drive . stopMotor ()) . withName ( \"Turn \" + degrees + \" deg\" ); } Reading it: Piece What it does private double m_turnTarget A field, so periodic() can show the target on the dashboard while the turn runs double direction = Math.signum(degrees); Math.signum gives +1 for a positive number and -1 for a negative one. Positive degrees means turn left, so direction is +1 for left and -1 for right. runOnce(() -> m_turnTarget = getHeadingDegrees() + degrees) Remembers where to stop: 90° from wherever the robot is now tankDrive(-direction * speed, direction * speed, false) Spins in place. For a left turn, left side back and right side forward. .until(...) Stops the moment the heading reaches the target direction > 0 ? A : B A ternary : \"if turning left, use check A; otherwise use check B.\" Turning left, the heading goes up to the target ( >= ); turning right, it goes down ( <= ). .withTimeout(3.0) If the gyro is unplugged, the heading never changes. This stops the robot anyway. Add the debug values to periodic() so you can watch it: m_telemetry . log ( \"Gyro/Turn Target (deg)\" , m_turnTarget ); m_telemetry . log ( \"Gyro/Turn Error (deg)\" , m_turnTarget - getHeadingDegrees ()); Bind it to test, in configureBindings() : m_controller . x (). onTrue ( m_driveSubsystem . turnDegreesCommand ( 90 , 0.4 )); // turn left 90° m_controller . b (). onTrue ( m_driveSubsystem . turnDegreesCommand ( - 90 , 0.4 )); // turn right 90° On the floor , in open space: press X, let the robot turn, and read Heading after it stops. Try speeds of 0.3, 0.5, and 0.7. Speed Target Where it stopped Overshoot 0.3 90 0.5 90 0.7 90 What you'll see: It always turns at least 90°, so it's far better than timing. But it overshoots , more at higher speeds. When the gyro says \"90!\", the robot is still spinning and takes time to stop. It's the same bang-bang overshoot you saw on the wheel in Lab 6 ."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 7: Slow down near the end","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-7-slow-down-near-the-end","t":"One simple fix: turn fast for most of the way, then slow for the last part. This command picks its speed every loop from how far it still has to turn: /** Fast until 20 degrees from the target, then slow. */ public Command twoSpeedTurnCommand ( double degrees ) { double direction = Math . signum ( degrees ); return runOnce (() -> m_turnTarget = getHeadingDegrees () + degrees ) . andThen ( run (() -> { double remaining = Math . abs ( m_turnTarget - getHeadingDegrees ()); double speed = ( remaining > 20.0 ) ? 0.6 : 0.25 ; // fast far away, slow up close m_drive . tankDrive ( - direction * speed , direction * speed , false ); })) . until (() -> direction > 0 ? getHeadingDegrees () >= m_turnTarget : getHeadingDegrees () <= m_turnTarget ) . withTimeout ( 3.0 ) . finallyDo (() -> m_drive . stopMotor ()) . withName ( \"Two-Speed Turn \" + degrees ); } The target is set once , at the start, so the whole turn aims at the same spot. Only the speed changes along the way. The new part is inside run(...) , which now has curly braces { } because it does several things every loop: Line What it does double remaining = Math.abs(m_turnTarget - getHeadingDegrees()); How many degrees are left. Math.abs drops the minus sign, so it works for left and right turns. double speed = (remaining > 20.0) ? 0.6 : 0.25; Another ternary: more than 20° to go, use 0.6; otherwise 0.25 m_drive.tankDrive(-direction * speed, direction * speed, false); Spin at that speed, in the right direction Bind it to Y: m_controller . y (). onTrue ( m_driveSubsystem . twoSpeedTurnCommand ( 90 )); Compare its overshoot with the one-speed turn from Step 6. Think about it Two speeds work better than one. What about ten speeds, each a bit slower as the robot gets closer? Or a speed that shrinks smoothly with the distance left to turn? That idea has a name: it's the P in PID, and it's coming in Lab 9 ."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 8: Add the drive encoders","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-8-add-the-drive-encoders","t":"First, wire them. Each Through Bore Encoder's cable ends in four 3-pin plugs. Only the blue and yellow plugs go to the roboRIO: The encoder end of the cable is the white JST plug (right). The four 3-pin Dupont plugs go to the roboRIO. Each has a red wire (5 V), a black wire (ground), and one colored signal wire. Left gearbox encoder: Plug (signal wire color) Signal Goes to Blue Channel A DIO 2 Yellow Channel B DIO 3 Green Index (once per turn) Not used. Leave it unplugged and tape it out of the way. White Absolute position Not used in these labs. Leave it unplugged and tape it out of the way. Right gearbox encoder: Plug (signal wire color) Signal Goes to Blue Channel A DIO 4 Yellow Channel B DIO 5 Green Index (once per turn) Not used. Leave it unplugged and tape it out of the way. White Absolute position Not used in these labs. Leave it unplugged and tape it out of the way. Which way around? Each roboRIO DIO port is three pins in a row, and the roboRIO has a label printed next to each row of pins: the ground symbol (⏚), V (5 V), and S (signal). Turn each plug so the black wire is on ground (⏚), the red wire is on V , and the blue or yellow wire is on S . Every plug on this cable has its own red and black, so each one powers itself; that's normal. Follow each cable back to its gearbox as you plug it in, so the left encoder doesn't end up on the right side's ports. ( Lesson 4.3 has more.) Then add the code. Add these to kDriveConstants in Constants.java , and add import edu.wpi.first.math.util.Units; at the top of the file: // Lab 8: Through Bore Encoders on each gearbox output shaft, wired to the roboRIO public static final int LeftEncoderChannelA = 2 ; public static final int LeftEncoderChannelB = 3 ; public static final int RightEncoderChannelA = 4 ; public static final int RightEncoderChannelB = 5 ; public static final boolean LeftEncoderInverted = false ; public static final boolean RightEncoderInverted = true ; // the right side faces the other way public static final int EncoderCyclesPerRev = 2048 ; // Through Bore: 2048 cycles per turn public static final double WheelDiameterMeters = Units . inchesToMeters ( 6.0 ); public static final double MetersPerRotation = Math . PI * WheelDiameterMeters ; // about 0.479 m public static final double TrackWidthMeters = 0.6 ; // measure yours: left wheels' center to right wheels' center Constant What it's for ...ChannelA , ...ChannelB The two DIO ports each encoder is wired to RightEncoderInverted = true The right gearbox faces the other way, so its encoder counts backward when the robot drives forward. This flips it, the same idea as inverting the right motor in Lab 2. WheelDiameterMeters 6-inch wheels. Units.inchesToMeters does the conversion (6 in = 0.1524 m). MetersPerRotation How far the robot rolls when a wheel turns once: the wheel's circumference , π × diameter TrackWidthMeters The distance between the left and right wheels. You'll use it in Step 11. Measure it with a tape measure. Now open DriveSubsystem.java . Add the imports: import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.CounterBase.EncodingType ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.Encoder ; Add the encoders as fields, below the gyro: // Through Bore Encoders on each side's gearbox output shaft (Lab 8) private final Encoder m_leftEncoder = new Encoder ( kDriveConstants . LeftEncoderChannelA , kDriveConstants . LeftEncoderChannelB , kDriveConstants . LeftEncoderInverted , EncodingType . k4X ); private final Encoder m_rightEncoder = new Encoder ( kDriveConstants . RightEncoderChannelA , kDriveConstants . RightEncoderChannelB , kDriveConstants . RightEncoderInverted , EncodingType . k4X ); At the end of the constructor, tell them how far one pulse is: // After this, getDistance() is in meters the wheels have rolled double metersPerPulse = kDriveConstants . MetersPerRotation / kDriveConstants . EncoderCyclesPerRev ; m_leftEncoder . setDistancePerPulse ( metersPerPulse ); m_rightEncoder . setDistancePerPulse ( metersPerPulse ); One wheel turn is 2048 pulses and 0.479 m, so each pulse is 0.479 ÷ 2048 meters. After this, getDistance() answers in meters , not counts. (It's 2048, not 8192, for the same reason as Lab 6: WPILib already handles the 4X counting.) Add methods to read them: /** How far the left wheels have rolled, in meters, since the last reset. */ public double getLeftMeters () { return m_leftEncoder . getDistance (); } /** How far the right wheels have rolled, in meters, since the last reset. */ public double getRightMeters () { return m_rightEncoder . getDistance (); } /** How far the robot has driven: the average of the two sides. */ public double getAverageMeters () { return ( getLeftMeters () + getRightMeters ()) / 2.0 ; } /** Sets both encoders back to 0. Works while disabled. */ public Command resetEncodersCommand () { return runOnce (() -> { m_leftEncoder . reset (); m_rightEncoder . reset (); }). ignoringDisable ( true ). withName ( \"Reset Encoders\" ); } Method What it answers getLeftMeters() , getRightMeters() How far each side has rolled. Forward is positive. getAverageMeters() How far the robot as a whole has gone. Driving straight, both sides match. Spinning in place, they cancel out to about 0. resetEncodersCommand() Makes \"here\" the new 0 m, like zeroing the gyro Put them on the dashboard, in periodic() : m_telemetry . log ( \"Encoders/Left (m)\" , getLeftMeters ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Encoders/Right (m)\" , getRightMeters ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Encoders/Average (m)\" , getAverageMeters ()); And bind Start to reset them, in configureBindings() : m_controller . start (). onTrue ( m_driveSubsystem . resetEncodersCommand ());"},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 9: Check the encoders","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-9-check-the-encoders","t":"Deploy. Leave the robot disabled , and open Telemetry → Drive → Encoders in Elastic. Press Start to reset. Both read 0. Push the robot forward by hand. Both Left and Right must go up . If one goes down, flip that side's ...EncoderInverted in Constants . Put a tape mark on the floor, push the robot forward exactly 1 meter , and read Average . It should be close to 1.00 . What you see Likely cause Average reads about 4.0 after 1 m EncoderCyclesPerRev is 8192 instead of 2048 Average reads about 0.25 after 1 m The wrong number somewhere in metersPerPulse , or one encoder isn't counting (so the average is half). Check both sides separately. One side always reads 0 That encoder's DIO ports are wrong, or a wire is loose A little off is normal Wheels squish a bit under the robot's weight, and tread wears down, so a \"6-inch\" wheel rolls a little less than 0.479 m. If you're off by a steady few percent, adjust WheelDiameterMeters until 1 m reads 1.00."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 10: Drive a distance","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-10-drive-a-distance","t":"Add a command to DriveSubsystem that drives until the encoders say it's gone far enough. It's built just like the gyro turn: private double m_distanceTarget = 0.0 ; // where the current drive is trying to stop /** * Drives straight until the robot has gone the given distance, in meters. * Positive = forward, negative = backward. */ public Command driveDistanceCommand ( double meters , double speed ) { double direction = Math . signum ( meters ); // +1 forward, -1 backward return runOnce (() -> m_distanceTarget = getAverageMeters () + meters ) . andThen ( run (() -> m_drive . tankDrive ( direction * speed , direction * speed , false ))) . until (() -> direction > 0 ? getAverageMeters () >= m_distanceTarget // forward: wait until we pass it : getAverageMeters () <= m_distanceTarget ) // backward: wait until we pass it going down . withTimeout ( 5.0 ) // safety: give up if the encoders never get there . finallyDo (() -> m_drive . stopMotor ()) . withName ( \"Drive \" + meters + \" m\" ); } Piece What it does m_distanceTarget = getAverageMeters() + meters Remembers where to stop: 1 m from wherever the robot is now , so you don't have to reset first tankDrive(direction * speed, direction * speed, false) Both sides the same speed: straight forward (or straight back) .until(...) Stops the moment the average distance passes the target .withTimeout(5.0) If an encoder is unplugged, the distance never changes. This stops the robot anyway. Add the target to periodic() so you can watch it: m_telemetry . log ( \"Encoders/Distance Target (m)\" , m_distanceTarget ); Bind it to A , in configureBindings() : m_controller . a (). onTrue ( m_driveSubsystem . driveDistanceCommand ( 1.0 , 0.4 )); // drive 1 meter On the floor , with a tape mark at the robot's front edge: press A and measure where it stops. Try speeds of 0.3, 0.5, and 0.7. What you'll see: It goes at least 1 m every time, with a little overshoot that grows with speed. It's the same bang-bang overshoot as the gyro turns and the Lab 6 wheel. Unlike the timed drive in Lab 7, though, a tired battery doesn't make it come up short: it just takes longer to get there."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 11: Why turns use the gyro, not the encoders","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-11-why-turns-use-the-gyro-not-the-encoders","t":"If the left side rolls backward and the right side rolls forward by the same amount, the robot spins. So you could estimate the heading from the encoders alone: /** Heading estimated from the encoders alone, in degrees. Watch how wrong it gets. */ public double getEncoderHeadingDegrees () { return Math . toDegrees (( getRightMeters () - getLeftMeters ()) / kDriveConstants . TrackWidthMeters ); } Part What it means getRightMeters() - getLeftMeters() How much farther the right side has rolled than the left / TrackWidthMeters Divided by the distance between the wheels, that's the angle turned, in radians Math.toDegrees(...) Radians to degrees Add it to periodic() : m_telemetry . log ( \"Encoders/Heading Estimate (deg)\" , getEncoderHeadingDegrees ()); Try it: zero the gyro ( Back ), reset the encoders ( Start ), and press X to turn 90° by the gyro. Compare Gyro → Heading with Encoders → Heading Estimate . What you'll see: The encoder estimate says the robot turned much more than 90°, sometimes 120° or more. A skid steer skids : to turn, its wheels slide sideways across the floor, so they roll farther than the robot actually turns. The encoders count every bit of that rolling. The gyro measures the robot's real rotation, so it's the sensor to trust for turns. Encoders are great for straight driving, where the wheels don't slide."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 12: A square that closes","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-12-a-square-that-closes","t":"Go back to RobotContainer 's squareSide() from Lab 7. Swap the timed forward drive for an encoder drive, and the timed spin for a gyro turn: private Command squareSide () { return Commands . sequence ( m_driveSubsystem . driveDistanceCommand ( 1.0 , 0.4 ), // forward 1 m by the encoders m_driveSubsystem . driveForSecondsCommand ( 0.0 , 0.0 , 0.3 ), // pause m_driveSubsystem . twoSpeedTurnCommand ( 90 ), // turn left by the gyro m_driveSubsystem . driveForSecondsCommand ( 0.0 , 0.0 , 0.3 )); // pause } Import Commands if needed. Run Square five times and fill in the same table as Lab 7. Compare the two tables: the timed square from Lab 7, and this one, driven entirely by sensors."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Step 13: Commit","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#step-13-commit","t":"git add . git commit -m \"Lab 8: NavX gyro turns and encoder distances\""},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"When it doesn't work","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#when-it-doesnt-work","t":"Problem Likely cause Build error: cannot find symbol AHRS The Studica library isn't installed (Step 2) Connected is false NavX not fully seated on the MXP port, or the wrong NavXComType Heading slowly creeps while sitting still Normal drift, but a lot of it means the robot moved during calibration. Restart the robot and keep it still. Heading goes down when turning left You used getAngle() or getYaw() instead of getRotation2d() Robot spins forever, then stops after 3 seconds It turned the opposite way from the target: check the signs in tankDrive for the turn One encoder counts down when pushing forward Flip that side's ...EncoderInverted in Constants Drive distance never stops (until the 5 second timeout) An encoder isn't counting, or it counts backward, so the average never reaches the target Distances are about 4 times too big EncoderCyclesPerRev is 8192; it should be 2048 Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab08-navx-gyro on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#try-it","t":"Check the heading by hand: +90 left, −90 right Measure drift: how much does the heading change in one minute sitting still? Fill in the speed vs. overshoot table for one-speed turns Compare one-speed and two-speed turns Check both encoders by hand: both count up going forward, and 1 m reads about 1.00 Drive 1 m at three speeds and measure the overshoot Compare the gyro heading and the encoder heading estimate after a 90° turn. How far off is the estimate? Run the sensor square and compare with your Lab 7 table Challenge: make driveDistanceCommand slow down for the last 0.2 m, like the two-speed turn Turn 360° and see if the robot ends facing the same way Challenge: write turnToHeadingCommand(double heading) that turns to an absolute heading (like \"face 0°\") instead of \"turn by\" an amount"},{"p":"Lab 8: Gyro Turns and Encoder Distances","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab8-navx-gyro.html#check-yourself","t":"Why is the right encoder inverted? The right gearbox faces the opposite way, so its shaft spins the opposite direction when the robot drives forward. Inverting it makes forward count up on both sides. With 6-inch wheels, how far does the robot roll when a wheel turns once? π × 6 in ≈ 18.85 in ≈ 0.479 m Why use the average of both encoders for driving distance? Driving straight, both sides roll the same distance. If the robot drifts a little to one side, the average is still about the distance its center traveled. Why does the encoder heading estimate say the robot turned more than it really did? A skid steer's wheels slide sideways when it turns, so they roll farther than the robot rotates. The encoders count all of that rolling. The gyro measures the real rotation. What does a gyro measure? How much the robot has rotated. Yaw, the rotation seen from above, is the robot's heading. Why must the robot stay still when it powers on? The NavX calibrates during the first few seconds. If the robot moves, the calibration is off and the heading drifts all session. Why use getRotation2d() instead of getAngle() ? getRotation2d() counts counterclockwise as positive, matching WPILib. The NavX's own getAngle() counts clockwise as positive, which flips every sign. Why does turnDegreesCommand store a target instead of just using 90? The robot might not be at 0° when the turn starts. Storing \"current heading + 90\" makes it turn 90° from wherever it is. Why does the gyro turn still overshoot? It's bang-bang: full speed until the heading reaches the target, then stop. The robot is still spinning at that moment and takes time to stop. Why does the two-speed turn overshoot less? It's going slower when it reaches the target, so it carries less momentum and stops sooner."},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#why-it-matters","t":"In Lab 6 you tried to turn the wheel exactly 10 times. Timing missed by a different amount every run. Bang-bang was consistent, but always overshot, because the wheel was still moving fast when it reached 10. What you needed was to slow down as you get close , and to correct if you miss. That's feedback control , and PID is how almost every robot does it. In this lab you'll add PID to the same WheelSubsystem one letter at a time, and watch each letter fix the previous attempt's problem. It's the same problem as driving exactly 2 meters in auto, raising an elevator to a scoring height, or spinning a turret to a target. Get this right on the test bed and you're ready for the elevator in Lab 10 . Before you start: finish Lab 6 and keep its scorecard. Read Lesson 12 (PID) first."},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#read-this","t":"Introduction to PID PID Control in WPILib"},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#walkthrough","t":"Safety Same rules as Lab 6: keep clear of the wheel, never stall the 775pro, and keep someone ready to disable. A badly tuned PID can swing the wheel back and forth hard."},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"The scorecard","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#the-scorecard","t":"Add three rows to your Lab 6 scorecard: Attempt Run 1 Run 2 Run 3 Worst error Notes 3. P only 4. P + D 5. P + I + D"},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Step 1: Add the PID gains","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#step-1-add-the-pid-gains","t":"// Add these inside kWheelConstants public static final double P = 1.0 ; // volts per rotation of error public static final double I = 0.0 ; // volts per (rotation × second) of error public static final double D = 0.0 ; // volts per (rotation per second) public static final double IZone = 0.5 ; // only build up I within 0.5 rotations public static final double ToleranceRot = 0.02 ; // 0.02 rotations ≈ 7° public static final double ToleranceRps = 0.1 ; // \"nearly stopped\""},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Step 2: Add a PID controller to the subsystem","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#step-2-add-a-pid-controller-to-the-subsystem","t":"Open WheelSubsystem.java . Add the imports: import edu.wpi.first.math.MathUtil ; import edu.wpi.first.math.controller.PIDController ; Add the controller as a field, next to the encoder: private final PIDController m_pid = new PIDController ( kWheelConstants . P , kWheelConstants . I , kWheelConstants . D ); PIDController is WPILib's PID class. You give it the three gains: kP , kI , and kD . It doesn't know anything about motors or encoders. You hand it numbers, and it hands back a number. And at the end of the constructor: m_pid . setTolerance ( kWheelConstants . ToleranceRot , kWheelConstants . ToleranceRps ); m_pid . setIZone ( kWheelConstants . IZone ); // limits integral windup (Attempt 5) Line What it does setTolerance(0.02, 0.1) How close counts as \"there\": within 0.02 rotations of the target and slower than 0.1 rotations per second . Both have to be true, so it doesn't count as arrived while still swinging past. setIZone(0.5) Only let the I term build up when within 0.5 rotations of the target. You'll see why in Attempt 5."},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Step 3: Make the gains tunable","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#step-3-make-the-gains-tunable","t":"You'll try a lot of gains in this lab. Redeploying for every one would take forever, so make them tunable from Elastic ( Lab 1.1 ). Add these fields to the subsystem, below the others: // Gains you can change in Elastic. They start at the values in Constants. private final TunableDouble m_kP = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Wheel/kP\" , kWheelConstants . P ); private final TunableDouble m_kI = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Wheel/kI\" , kWheelConstants . I ); private final TunableDouble m_kD = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Wheel/kD\" , kWheelConstants . D ); private final TunableDouble m_iZone = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Wheel/IZone\" , kWheelConstants . IZone ); Import frc.robot.telemetry.TunableDouble and frc.robot.telemetry.Tunables . Then, at the top of periodic() , copy the dashboard's numbers into the controller every loop: // Use whatever gains are on the dashboard right now m_pid . setPID ( m_kP . get (), m_kI . get (), m_kD . get ()); m_pid . setIZone ( m_iZone . get ()); setPID replaces all three gains at once. Doing it every loop means a change in Elastic takes effect within 20 ms, even in the middle of a move. In Elastic, add kP , kI , kD , and IZone from Tunables → Wheel as Text Display widgets, so you can type new values. Add a Graph with Position (rot) and Target (rot) from Telemetry → Wheel together. In WPILib 2027 This is already the 2027 API: in WPILib 2027, the same lines work with WPILib's own Tunables ( Lesson 6.1 )."},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Warm up in the simulator","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#warm-up-in-the-simulator","t":"Before touching the real wheel, try the same idea in WPILib's turret simulator below. A turret is a lot like the test bed wheel: a motor turning something to an exact angle, with no gravity. It's running PID only . Click a spot around the circle to set a new angle. Interactive simulator from the WPILib docs, Tuning a Turret Position Controller (CC BY 4.0). With kP, kI, and kD at 0, click a new angle. Nothing happens. Why? Raise kP until the turret moves sharply to each new angle. If it oscillates, lower it. Raise kD until the overshoot settles down. Now do the same thing on the real wheel."},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Attempt 3: P only (slow down as you get close)","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#attempt-3-p-only-slow-down-as-you-get-close","t":"The idea: \"Push in proportion to how far away you are.\" Far away: push hard. Almost there: push gently. That's the P in PID. volts = kP × error With kP = 1.0, at 10 rotations away that's 10 V (clamped to 6 V). At 1 rotation away, 1 V. At 0.1 away, 0.1 V. public Command turnPid ( double rotations ) { return runOnce (() -> { setTargetFromHere ( rotations ); m_pid . reset (); // clear old history }) . andThen ( run (() -> { double volts = m_pid . calculate ( getRotations (), m_target ); setVolts ( MathUtil . clamp ( volts , - kWheelConstants . MaxVolts , kWheelConstants . MaxVolts )); })) . until ( m_pid :: atSetpoint ) // within tolerance AND nearly stopped . withTimeout ( 5.0 ) // give up if it never gets there . finallyDo ( this :: stop ) . withName ( \"Attempts 3-5: PID\" ); } Piece by piece: First, once: return runOnce (() -> { setTargetFromHere ( rotations ); m_pid . reset (); // clear old history }) Pick the target, 10 rotations from here (like Lab 6). Then m_pid.reset() clears what the controller remembers from the last move: the I term's running total, and the last error the D term compares against. Without it, an old move could kick this one. The lambda has curly braces { } because it does two things. Each line inside ends with ; . Then, every loop: . andThen ( run (() -> { double volts = m_pid . calculate ( getRotations (), m_target ); setVolts ( MathUtil . clamp ( volts , - kWheelConstants . MaxVolts , kWheelConstants . MaxVolts )); })) Line What it does m_pid.calculate(measurement, setpoint) The PID math: give it where the wheel is and where it should be , and it returns how many volts to apply. With P only, that's kP × (target − position). MathUtil.clamp(volts, -6, 6) Never send more than 6 V either way, however big the error is. Far from the target, PID would ask for 10 V; this keeps the test gentle. setVolts(...) Sends it to the motor (and remembers it for the dashboard) Until it arrives, or gives up: . until ( m_pid :: atSetpoint ) . withTimeout ( 5.0 ) . finallyDo ( this :: stop ) Line What it does .until(m_pid::atSetpoint) End when the controller says it's within the tolerance you set in Step 2: close enough and slow enough .withTimeout(5.0) Give up after 5 seconds, in case it never quite gets there .finallyDo(this::stop) However it ends, stop the motor Bind it on the wheel controller: m_wheelController . x (). onTrue ( m_wheel . turnPid ( 10 )); // Attempts 3 to 5 Make sure kI and kD are 0 in Elastic ( Tunables → Wheel ). Run turnPid(10) three times with kP = 1.0. Record the results. Try kP = 0.3, then 3, then 10. For each, watch Position and Target on your Elastic graph. What you'll see: kP Behavior Too low Slow, and stops short of 10. Near the target the voltage gets so small it can't overcome the gearbox's friction, so the wheel stalls a little early. That leftover is steady-state error . About right Fast, and stops close to 10 Too high Shoots past 10, swings back, maybe oscillates Unlike bang-bang, if the wheel overshoots, P pushes it back . It corrects itself in both directions. Why a timeout? If kP is too low, the wheel may stall just short of the tolerance and atSetpoint() never becomes true. The timeout ends the command anyway, so the motor doesn't sit there pushing forever."},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Attempt 4: P + D (stop the overshoot)","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#attempt-4-p-d-stop-the-overshoot","t":"The idea: \"Slow down when you're approaching fast.\" D looks at how fast the error is shrinking and pushes against that motion, like a shock absorber. No new code: the same turnPid command uses whatever gains are in the controller. Set kP high enough that it overshoots a little. Raise kD in small steps (try 0.02, 0.05, 0.1) until the overshoot disappears. If the output starts to buzz or jitter, kD is too high. Back it off. Run three times with your best gains and record the results. What you'll see: With good P and D, the wheel moves quickly and slows smoothly without overshooting. It may still stop a hair short of 10, for the same friction reason as Attempt 3."},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Attempt 5: P + I + D (fix the leftover error)","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#attempt-5-p-i-d-fix-the-leftover-error","t":"The idea: \"If you've been a little short for a while, push a little harder.\" I adds up the error over time. While the wheel sits just short of 10, that total keeps growing, so the push keeps growing until it's enough to beat friction and finish the move. Still no new code. Keep your P and D from Attempt 4. Set kI small, like 0.1, and run turnPid(10) . Watch Error on the dashboard: after the wheel slows down, the last little bit should creep to 0. Raise kI (try 0.3, then 0.5) until the leftover error goes away quickly. Too much kI makes the wheel overshoot and slowly swing back and forth. Back it off. Run three times with your best gains and record the results. Copy all three gains into Constants.java (dashboard edits are lost on reboot). Integral windup, and why there's an IZone If I added up error the whole way from 0 to 10, it would build up a huge total during the move and fling the wheel past the target. That's integral windup . setIZone(0.5) in the constructor fixes it: the integral only builds up when the wheel is within 0.5 rotations of the target, and resets to zero outside that. Try setting IZone to 20 in Elastic and watch what happens to the overshoot. What you'll see: With P, I, and D tuned, the wheel moves quickly, slows smoothly, and finishes within the tolerance of 10, run after run, even with the cloth on it."},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"What you just learned","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#what-you-just-learned","t":"Attempt Uses the sensor? Slows down near the target? Corrects mistakes? 1. Timed (Lab 6) No No No 2. Bang-bang (Lab 6) Only to decide when to stop No No 3. P only Every loop Yes Yes, but may stop short 4. P + D Every loop Yes, smoothly Yes, but may stop a hair short 5. P + I + D Every loop Yes, smoothly Yes, all the way to the target Each term fixes the previous attempt's problem: P gets you close, D stops the overshoot, and I cleans up whatever is left."},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Commit","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#commit","t":"git add . git commit -m \"Lab 9: PID on the wheel\" Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab09-pid-wheel on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#try-it","t":"Complete the scorecard: Attempts 3 to 5, three runs each Graph Position and Target for Attempts 2 through 5, and screenshot each Hold the cloth against the wheel during Attempts 4 and 5. Which one still reaches 10? Press X three times in a row, waiting for each to finish. Does the wheel end at 10, 20, and 30? Compare with bang-bang in Lab 6. Add turnPid(-10) on another button and make sure it comes back to where it started Challenge: use the test bed's 10-turn knob from Lab 5 to set the target, so the wheel follows the knob turn for turn"},{"p":"Lab 9: PID on the Wheel","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab9-pid-wheel.html#check-yourself","t":"With kP = 2.0, the wheel is 0.5 rotations from the target. What voltage does P command? 2.0 × 0.5 = 1.0 V Why does a low kP stop short of 10? Near the target, the error is tiny, so the voltage is tiny, and it's not enough to overcome friction in the gearbox. The wheel stalls a little early. What does adding kD do, and what happens if it's too high? D pushes against fast motion, so the wheel slows down before reaching the target instead of overshooting. Too much D makes the output buzz, because it reacts to small noisy changes in the encoder. The wheel stops 0.05 rotations short every time. Which term fixes it, and how? I. While the wheel sits short, the integral keeps adding up the error, so the output grows until it beats friction and the wheel finishes the move. What is integral windup, and what does setIZone do about it? Windup is the integral building up a huge total during a long move, then flinging the mechanism past the target. setIZone only lets the integral build up when the error is small, near the target. Why does turnPid have a timeout? If the wheel stalls just outside the tolerance, atSetpoint() never becomes true. The timeout ends the command so the motor doesn't keep pushing forever."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#why-it-matters","t":"Elevators are on almost every FRC robot: lifting game pieces to score high, reaching shelves, climbing. Every one needs the same thing: go to an exact height, and stay there . In Lab 9 you used PID to turn a wheel exactly 10 times. An elevator adds one big new problem: gravity . The wheel stopped when the motor stopped. An elevator falls. In this lab you'll move the WCD's elevator between three preset heights ( START , L2 , and L3 ) and hold it there. You'll use a ProfiledPIDController : a PID controller that plans a smooth path to each new height instead of jumping at it. Everything is in meters , worked out from the sprocket that drives the elevator's chain. You'll see exactly how each of P, I, and D handles gravity. Before you start: finish Lab 9 . You'll add to the WestCoastDrive project from Labs 2, 7, and 8. Lessons 4.1 (the NEO) and 4.3 (the Through Bore Encoder) cover this lab's hardware."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#read-this","t":"PID Control in WPILib Introduction to PID Tuning a Vertical Elevator with Motion Profiling Combining Motion Profiling and PID (ProfiledPIDController) Lesson 14: Motion Profiles , the goal vs. setpoint section"},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#walkthrough","t":"Elevator safety An elevator can pinch, crush, and drop. Before every test: Hands, heads, and tools out of the elevator and away from the carriage's path, above and below. Start every session with the elevator all the way down . One person's only job is the disable key ( Enter ). Keep MaxVolts low (4 V) while you set up and tune. The carriage can drop when the robot is disabled . Never reach under a raised carriage."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Try it in the simulator first","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#try-it-in-the-simulator-first","t":"WPILib's elevator simulator below runs PID only , exactly like this lab. Click a height along the elevator's vertical support to set a new target. Interactive simulator from the WPILib docs, Tuning a Vertical Elevator with Motion Profiling (CC BY 4.0). From the WPILib docs: Tuning an elevator with PID only Set kP, kI, and kD to zero. Increase kP until the elevator responds to a sudden change in setpoint by moving sharply to the new position. If it oscillates too much, reduce kP until it stops. Increase kI when the elevator gets \"stuck\" before reaching the setpoint. Increase kD to help it track smoothly moving setpoints and further reduce oscillation. Feedback-only control is not a good control scheme for vertical elevators, so don't be surprised if the simulator doesn't behave consistently, even with good gains. There's no perfect answer: gains around kP = 10, kI = 2.5, kD = 0 work, but with overshoot and long settling times, and the elevator acts differently depending on the setpoint, overshooting near the top and undershooting near the bottom. Adapted from Tuning a Vertical Elevator with Motion Profiling , CC BY 4.0. That's exactly what you'll see on the real elevator, and why: gravity pulls the same way no matter which direction the elevator is moving. Real robots usually add feedforward for gravity ( Lesson 13 ). This lab sticks to PID (following a motion profile) so you can see what each of P, I, and D does on its own. Find gains that reach a new height within a few seconds Notice how the elevator behaves going up vs. going down Write down your gains. Your real elevator will need different numbers, but the same steps."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"The hardware","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#the-hardware","t":"Part Connected to In code NEO driving the elevator through a gearbox SPARK MAX, brushless mode, on CAN REVLib SparkMax Sprocket on the elevator's drive shaft, pulling the carriage's chain Its pitch diameter sets meters per turn REV Through Bore Encoder on that same shaft The WCD roboRIO's DIO ports (channels A and B) WPILib Encoder A second Xbox controller (the operator) Driver Station, slot 1 CommandXboxController(1) The NEO is brushless , like Lab 1's, unlike the CIMs on the drivetrain. Check its CAN ID in the REV Hardware Client. Wiring the elevator's encoder: its cable ends in four 3-pin plugs. Only the blue and yellow plugs go to the WCD's roboRIO: The encoder end of the cable is the white JST plug (right). The four 3-pin Dupont plugs go to the roboRIO. Each has a red wire (5 V), a black wire (ground), and one colored signal wire. Plug (signal wire color) Signal Goes to Blue Channel A DIO 0 Yellow Channel B DIO 1 Green Index (once per turn) Not used. Leave it unplugged and tape it out of the way. White Absolute position Not used in these labs. Leave it unplugged and tape it out of the way. Which way around? Each roboRIO DIO port is three pins in a row, and the roboRIO has a label printed next to each row of pins: the ground symbol (⏚), V (5 V), and S (signal). Turn each plug so the black wire is on ground (⏚), the red wire is on V , and the blue or yellow wire is on S . Every plug on this cable has its own red and black, so each one powers itself; that's normal. The drive encoders from Lab 8 are already on DIO 2 to 5, so the elevator uses 0 and 1. ( Lesson 4.3 has more.)"},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"The big idea: goal, setpoint, and holding against gravity","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#the-big-idea-goal-setpoint-and-holding-against-gravity","t":"A plain PIDController sees a big error the instant you pick a new height and shoves the carriage at it as hard as it can. A ProfiledPIDController plans the move first: speed up gently, cruise, slow down, arrive ( Lesson 14 ). What it is Example Goal The preset you picked L2, 0.25 m Setpoint Where the profile says the carriage should be right now , on its way to the goal 0.11 m, moving up at 0.25 m/s Height Where the encoder says the carriage actually is 0.10 m Every 20 ms the setpoint takes a small step toward the goal, and PID only has to close the small gap between the setpoint and the height. The elevator also needs voltage just to stand still , because gravity is always pulling the carriage down. So the controller can never simply stop when it arrives. This elevator works like this: A hold command runs all the time as the default command, following the profile toward the goal and holding the goal once it gets there. A preset command sets a new goal, drives toward it, and finishes when it arrives . Then the hold command takes over and keeps holding that same goal."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Step 1: Constants","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#step-1-constants","t":"Add this class inside Constants in the WestCoastDrive project, and add import edu.wpi.first.math.util.Units; at the top of the file. Fill in your CAN ID, DIO ports, and your sprocket's pitch diameter. You'll find MaxHeightMeters in Step 5. public static class kElevatorConstants { // Motor public static final int MotorCanId = 5 ; public static final boolean MotorInverted = false ; public static final int CurrentLimit = 40 ; // amps public static final double MaxVolts = 4.0 ; // gentle while tuning // Through Bore Encoder, on the same shaft as the sprocket public static final int EncoderChannelA = 0 ; public static final int EncoderChannelB = 1 ; public static final boolean EncoderInverted = false ; public static final int EncoderCyclesPerRev = 2048 ; // NOT 8192: see below // Sprocket: one turn moves the chain (and the carriage) one circumference public static final double PitchDiameterInches = 1.432 ; // from your sprocket's spec sheet public static final double PitchDiameterMeters = Units . inchesToMeters ( PitchDiameterInches ); public static final double MetersPerRevolution = Math . PI * PitchDiameterMeters ; public static final double DistancePerPulse = MetersPerRevolution / EncoderCyclesPerRev ; // Travel limits public static final double MinHeightMeters = 0.0 ; // bottom public static final double MaxHeightMeters = 0.5 ; // you'll measure this in Step 5 // Presets, in meters (pick your own in Step 6) public static final double StartMeters = 0.02 ; public static final double L2Meters = 0.25 ; public static final double L3Meters = 0.45 ; // PID, in volts per meter of error public static final double kP = 10.0 ; public static final double kI = 0.0 ; public static final double kD = 0.0 ; public static final double ToleranceMeters = 0.006 ; // within 6 mm counts as \"there\" public static final double IZoneMeters = 0.063 ; // only build up I within 6.3 cm // Motion profile limits public static final double MaxVelocityMetersPerSecond = 0.25 ; public static final double MaxAccelerationMetersPerSecondSquared = 0.5 ; } Where DistancePerPulse comes from. When the sprocket turns once, the chain moves one circumference of the sprocket, measured at its pitch diameter (the circle through the middle of the chain's pins, not the outside of the teeth): meters per revolution = π × pitch diameter distance per pulse = meters per revolution ÷ 2048 With a 1.432 in pitch diameter: π × 1.432 in = 4.50 in = 0.114 m per turn. The sprocket's spec sheet lists its pitch diameter. If there's gearing between the encoder and the sprocket, divide by that ratio too. 2048, not 8192 The Through Bore Encoder reports 8192 counts per turn with 4X decoding, so it's tempting to divide by 8192. Don't. WPILib's Encoder already accounts for 4X decoding, and the WPILib docs say each \"pulse\" in setDistancePerPulse is always one full cycle (four edges) : 2048 for the Through Bore. Dividing by 8192 makes every height read 4 times too small ."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Step 2: The elevator subsystem","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#step-2-the-elevator-subsystem","t":"Create subsystems/ElevatorSubsystem.java : package frc.robot.subsystems ; import com.revrobotics.PersistMode ; import com.revrobotics.ResetMode ; import com.revrobotics.spark.SparkLowLevel.MotorType ; import com.revrobotics.spark.SparkMax ; import com.revrobotics.spark.config.SparkBaseConfig.IdleMode ; import com.revrobotics.spark.config.SparkMaxConfig ; import edu.wpi.first.math.MathUtil ; import edu.wpi.first.math.controller.ProfiledPIDController ; import edu.wpi.first.math.trajectory.TrapezoidProfile ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.CounterBase.EncodingType ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.DriverStation ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.Encoder ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.Command ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.SubsystemBase ; import frc.robot.Constants.kElevatorConstants ; import frc.robot.telemetry.Telemetry ; import frc.robot.telemetry.TelemetryTable ; import frc.robot.telemetry.TunableDouble ; import frc.robot.telemetry.Tunables ; public class ElevatorSubsystem extends SubsystemBase { /** The heights the operator can pick. */ public enum Preset { START , L2 , L3 } // The NEO is BRUSHLESS (the drivetrain CIMs are brushed) private final SparkMax m_motor = new SparkMax ( kElevatorConstants . MotorCanId , MotorType . kBrushless ); private final Encoder m_encoder = new Encoder ( kElevatorConstants . EncoderChannelA , kElevatorConstants . EncoderChannelB , kElevatorConstants . EncoderInverted , EncodingType . k4X ); // PID that follows a motion profile: it plans a smooth path to the goal, // then uses PID to follow that path. private final ProfiledPIDController m_pid = new ProfiledPIDController ( kElevatorConstants . kP , kElevatorConstants . kI , kElevatorConstants . kD , new TrapezoidProfile . Constraints ( kElevatorConstants . MaxVelocityMetersPerSecond , kElevatorConstants . MaxAccelerationMetersPerSecondSquared )); private double m_lastVolts = 0.0 ; // for the dashboard // Values go to /Telemetry/Elevator. The gains and limits are tunable (/Tunables/Elevator) while you test. private final TelemetryTable m_telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Elevator\" ); private final TunableDouble m_kP = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Elevator/kP\" , kElevatorConstants . kP ); private final TunableDouble m_kI = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Elevator/kI\" , kElevatorConstants . kI ); private final TunableDouble m_kD = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Elevator/kD\" , kElevatorConstants . kD ); private final TunableDouble m_maxVelocity = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Elevator/Max Velocity (m per s)\" , kElevatorConstants . MaxVelocityMetersPerSecond ); private final TunableDouble m_maxAcceleration = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Elevator/Max Accel (m per s2)\" , kElevatorConstants . MaxAccelerationMetersPerSecondSquared ); public ElevatorSubsystem () { SparkMaxConfig config = new SparkMaxConfig (); config . smartCurrentLimit ( kElevatorConstants . CurrentLimit ); config . idleMode ( IdleMode . kBrake ); // resists falling when no voltage is applied config . inverted ( kElevatorConstants . MotorInverted ); m_motor . configure ( config , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); // After this, getDistance() is in meters of elevator travel m_encoder . setDistancePerPulse ( kElevatorConstants . DistancePerPulse ); m_encoder . reset (); // the elevator MUST start at the bottom: this is height 0 m_pid . setTolerance ( kElevatorConstants . ToleranceMeters ); m_pid . setIZone ( kElevatorConstants . IZoneMeters ); holdHere (); } public double getHeightMeters () { return m_encoder . getDistance (); } /** True when the carriage has reached the GOAL, not just the profile's current step. */ public boolean atGoal () { return m_pid . atGoal (); } /** Every voltage goes through here, so the soft limits can't be skipped. */ private void setVolts ( double volts ) { double height = getHeightMeters (); if ( height >= kElevatorConstants . MaxHeightMeters && volts > 0 ) { volts = 0 ; // at the top: never push higher } if ( height <= kElevatorConstants . MinHeightMeters && volts < 0 ) { volts = 0 ; // at the bottom: never push lower } m_lastVolts = volts ; m_motor . setVoltage ( volts ); } /** Starts a fresh profile from where the carriage is right now, with the goal right here. */ private void holdHere () { m_pid . reset ( getHeightMeters ()); m_pid . setGoal ( getHeightMeters ()); } /** Picks a new goal. The profile plans a smooth path from where the carriage is now. */ private void setGoalMeters ( double meters ) { m_pid . reset ( getHeightMeters (), m_encoder . getRate ()); m_pid . setGoal ( MathUtil . clamp ( meters , kElevatorConstants . MinHeightMeters , kElevatorConstants . MaxHeightMeters )); } private static double presetMeters ( Preset preset ) { return switch ( preset ) { case START -> kElevatorConstants . StartMeters ; case L2 -> kElevatorConstants . L2Meters ; case L3 -> kElevatorConstants . L3Meters ; }; } /** One step: move the setpoint along the profile, then PID toward it. */ private void followProfile () { double volts = m_pid . calculate ( getHeightMeters ()); setVolts ( MathUtil . clamp ( volts , - kElevatorConstants . MaxVolts , kElevatorConstants . MaxVolts )); } // ---------------------------------------------------------------- Commands /** The default command: follow the profile to the goal, then hold it. Never ends. */ public Command holdCommand () { return run ( this :: followProfile ). withName ( \"Hold\" ); } /** Sets a new goal and drives to it. Ends on arrival; then Hold keeps it there. */ public Command goToPresetCommand ( Preset preset ) { return run ( this :: followProfile ) . beforeStarting (() -> setGoalMeters ( presetMeters ( preset ))) . until ( this :: atGoal ) . withName ( \"Elevator to \" + preset ); } /** Manual control while held. When released, hold wherever the carriage is. */ public Command manualCommand ( double volts ) { return runEnd (() -> setVolts ( volts ), this :: holdHere ) . withName ( \"Manual \" + volts + \" V\" ); } /** Call this height 0. Only use it when the elevator is all the way down. */ public Command zeroCommand () { return runOnce (() -> { m_encoder . reset (); holdHere (); }). ignoringDisable ( true ). withName ( \"Zero\" ); } @Override public void periodic () { // While disabled, keep the profile and goal on the real height, // so enabling never makes the elevator suddenly jump. if ( DriverStation . isDisabled ()) { holdHere (); } // Use whatever gains and limits are on the dashboard right now m_pid . setPID ( m_kP . get (), m_kI . get (), m_kD . get ()); m_pid . setConstraints ( new TrapezoidProfile . Constraints ( m_maxVelocity . get (), m_maxAcceleration . get ())); m_telemetry . log ( \"Height (m)\" , getHeightMeters ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Goal (m)\" , m_pid . getGoal (). position ); m_telemetry . log ( \"Setpoint (m)\" , m_pid . getSetpoint (). position ); m_telemetry . log ( \"Setpoint Velocity (m per s)\" , m_pid . getSetpoint (). velocity ); m_telemetry . log ( \"Error (m)\" , m_pid . getGoal (). position - getHeightMeters ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Output (V)\" , m_lastVolts ); m_telemetry . log ( \"Current (A)\" , m_motor . getOutputCurrent ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"At Goal\" , atGoal ()); } }"},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"The elevator subsystem, piece by piece","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#the-elevator-subsystem-piece-by-piece","t":"It's the longest file in the labs, but most of it is pieces you already know. Here it is in order. 1. The presets public enum Preset { START , L2 , L3 } An enum is a type with a fixed list of values. A Preset can only be START , L2 , or L3 . If you type Preset.L4 by mistake, the code won't build, which is much better than an elevator going to a height you didn't mean. It's public and inside the class, so RobotContainer uses it as ElevatorSubsystem.Preset.L2 . 2. The motor and the encoder private final SparkMax m_motor = new SparkMax ( kElevatorConstants . MotorCanId , MotorType . kBrushless ); private final Encoder m_encoder = new Encoder ( kElevatorConstants . EncoderChannelA , kElevatorConstants . EncoderChannelB , kElevatorConstants . EncoderInverted , EncodingType . k4X ); The same patterns as Labs 1 and 6: a NEO on a SPARK MAX in brushless mode, and the Through Bore Encoder on two DIO ports with 4X counting. 3. The controller private final ProfiledPIDController m_pid = new ProfiledPIDController ( kElevatorConstants . kP , kElevatorConstants . kI , kElevatorConstants . kD , new TrapezoidProfile . Constraints ( kElevatorConstants . MaxVelocityMetersPerSecond , kElevatorConstants . MaxAccelerationMetersPerSecondSquared )); Like Lab 9's PIDController , it takes kP, kI, and kD. It also takes constraints : the fastest the carriage may move (0.25 m/s) and how quickly it may speed up or slow down (0.5 m/s²). The controller uses those to plan the smooth path from where the carriage is to the goal. TrapezoidProfile.Constraints is named for the shape of the plan: speed ramps up, stays flat, and ramps down, which looks like a trapezoid on a graph ( Lesson 14 ). 4. The dashboard and the tunable numbers private double m_lastVolts = 0.0 ; // for the dashboard private final TelemetryTable m_telemetry = Telemetry . getTable ( \"Elevator\" ); private final TunableDouble m_kP = Tunables . addDouble ( \"Elevator/kP\" , kElevatorConstants . kP ); // ...kI, kD, Max Velocity, and Max Accel the same way Values go to Telemetry → Elevator . Five numbers are tunable from Tunables → Elevator : the three gains and the two profile limits. 5. The constructor SparkMaxConfig config = new SparkMaxConfig (); config . smartCurrentLimit ( kElevatorConstants . CurrentLimit ); config . idleMode ( IdleMode . kBrake ); // resists falling when no voltage is applied config . inverted ( kElevatorConstants . MotorInverted ); m_motor . configure ( config , ResetMode . kResetSafeParameters , PersistMode . kPersistParameters ); m_encoder . setDistancePerPulse ( kElevatorConstants . DistancePerPulse ); m_encoder . reset (); // the elevator MUST start at the bottom: this is height 0 m_pid . setTolerance ( kElevatorConstants . ToleranceMeters ); m_pid . setIZone ( kElevatorConstants . IZoneMeters ); holdHere (); Line What it does idleMode(IdleMode.kBrake) When no voltage is applied, the motor resists turning. It slows a falling carriage, but doesn't hold it up by itself. setDistancePerPulse(DistancePerPulse) After this, getDistance() answers in meters of elevator travel (Step 1) m_encoder.reset() Calls wherever the elevator is right now 0 m . That's why it must start at the bottom. setTolerance(0.006) Within 6 mm counts as \"there\" setIZone(0.063) Only build up I within 6.3 cm of the goal (Attempt 4) holdHere() Starts the controller off holding the current height (piece 8) 6. Reading the height public double getHeightMeters () { return m_encoder . getDistance (); } public boolean atGoal () { return m_pid . atGoal (); } atGoal() is true when the carriage has reached the goal you picked. ( atSetpoint() only checks the profile's current step, which would be true the whole way up.) 7. Every voltage goes through one method, with soft limits private void setVolts ( double volts ) { double height = getHeightMeters (); if ( height >= kElevatorConstants . MaxHeightMeters && volts > 0 ) { volts = 0 ; // at the top: never push higher } if ( height <= kElevatorConstants . MinHeightMeters && volts < 0 ) { volts = 0 ; // at the bottom: never push lower } m_lastVolts = volts ; m_motor . setVoltage ( volts ); } Line What it does if (height >= Max && volts > 0) At or above the top and trying to go up? && means both must be true. volts = 0; Then don't. Pushing down from the top is still allowed. The second if The same check for the bottom Every command moves the motor through setVolts , so no command can skip these checks. These are soft limits : limits in code, as opposed to the hard stop , the metal the carriage hits. 8. Starting a fresh plan private void holdHere () { m_pid . reset ( getHeightMeters ()); m_pid . setGoal ( getHeightMeters ()); } private void setGoalMeters ( double meters ) { m_pid . reset ( getHeightMeters (), m_encoder . getRate ()); m_pid . setGoal ( MathUtil . clamp ( meters , kElevatorConstants . MinHeightMeters , kElevatorConstants . MaxHeightMeters )); } Method What it does holdHere() Throws away the old plan, starts a new one from the real height , and makes that same height the goal: \"stay right here\" setGoalMeters(meters) Starts a new plan from the real height and speed ( getRate() ), toward a new goal MathUtil.clamp(meters, Min, Max) If a goal is outside the elevator's travel, use the nearest end instead reset(...) matters because the profile plans from wherever it thinks the carriage is. If that's out of date (after manual driving, or after being disabled), the first step of the plan would be far from reality, and the elevator would jump. 9. Turning a preset into meters private static double presetMeters ( Preset preset ) { return switch ( preset ) { case START -> kElevatorConstants . StartMeters ; case L2 -> kElevatorConstants . L2Meters ; case L3 -> kElevatorConstants . L3Meters ; }; } A switch expression picks one value based on which preset it's given: \"if it's START , the answer is StartMeters ; if it's L2 , L2Meters ...\" Java checks that every preset is covered, so if you add L4 to the enum and forget it here, the build tells you. 10. One step of control private void followProfile () { double volts = m_pid . calculate ( getHeightMeters ()); setVolts ( MathUtil . clamp ( volts , - kElevatorConstants . MaxVolts , kElevatorConstants . MaxVolts )); } m_pid.calculate(height) does two things in one call: it moves the setpoint one 20 ms step along the plan toward the goal, then runs PID on the gap between the setpoint and the height. The answer, clamped to ±4 V, goes to setVolts . Every command that moves the elevator by itself calls this once per loop. 11. The commands public Command holdCommand () { return run ( this :: followProfile ). withName ( \"Hold\" ); } The default command . Every loop, follow the plan, and once at the goal, keep holding it. It never ends, because the elevator always needs voltage to fight gravity. public Command goToPresetCommand ( Preset preset ) { return run ( this :: followProfile ) . beforeStarting (() -> setGoalMeters ( presetMeters ( preset ))) . until ( this :: atGoal ) . withName ( \"Elevator to \" + preset ); } Part What it does .beforeStarting(...) Once, right before the first loop: set the new goal run(this::followProfile) Every loop: follow the plan .until(this::atGoal) End on arrival. Then the default Hold command starts again and keeps holding the same goal. Because it ends, an auto routine can chain it: goToPresetCommand(Preset.L2).andThen(...) . public Command manualCommand ( double volts ) { return runEnd (() -> setVolts ( volts ), this :: holdHere ) . withName ( \"Manual \" + volts + \" V\" ); } While the button is held, apply a fixed voltage (still through the soft limits). When it's released, holdHere() makes wherever the carriage stopped the new goal, so it holds there instead of sliding back to the old goal. public Command zeroCommand () { return runOnce (() -> { m_encoder . reset (); holdHere (); }). ignoringDisable ( true ). withName ( \"Zero\" ); } Calls the current height 0, then starts a fresh plan from there. It works while disabled, so you can lower the carriage by hand and zero it. 12. periodic() : safety, tuning, and the dashboard if ( DriverStation . isDisabled ()) { holdHere (); } m_pid . setPID ( m_kP . get (), m_kI . get (), m_kD . get ()); m_pid . setConstraints ( new TrapezoidProfile . Constraints ( m_maxVelocity . get (), m_maxAcceleration . get ())); m_telemetry . log ( \"Height (m)\" , getHeightMeters ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Goal (m)\" , m_pid . getGoal (). position ); m_telemetry . log ( \"Setpoint (m)\" , m_pid . getSetpoint (). position ); // ...setpoint velocity, error, output, current, and At Goal Part What it does if (DriverStation.isDisabled()) holdHere(); While disabled, keep the plan and goal on the real height. Someone might push the carriage by hand; when you enable, it holds right there instead of lurching back. setPID(...) and setConstraints(...) Copy the tunable numbers from the dashboard into the controller every loop m_pid.getGoal().position The preset you picked m_pid.getSetpoint().position and .velocity Where the plan says the carriage should be right now, and how fast it should be moving Three safety features are built in: Feature What it protects against Soft limits in setVolts Code that asks for a height above the top or below the bottom. The motor won't push past either end. Goal clamping in setGoalMeters A typo like L3Meters = 4.5 (4.5 meters!) just becomes \"go to the top.\" Profile and goal follow the height while disabled Enabling with an old goal, which would make the elevator lurch the instant you enable"},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Step 3: Bind the operator controller","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#step-3-bind-the-operator-controller","t":"In RobotContainer.java , add the subsystem and a second controller: private final ElevatorSubsystem m_elevator = new ElevatorSubsystem (); private final CommandXboxController m_operator = new CommandXboxController ( 1 ); In configureBindings() , keep the drive bindings and add: // ---- Elevator (operator controller, slot 1) ---- m_elevator . setDefaultCommand ( m_elevator . holdCommand ()); m_operator . povUp (). whileTrue ( m_elevator . manualCommand ( 2.0 )); // manual up m_operator . povDown (). whileTrue ( m_elevator . manualCommand ( - 1.0 )); // manual down (gravity helps) m_operator . back (). onTrue ( m_elevator . zeroCommand ()); // Presets: you'll add these in Step 6 Manual down uses less voltage than manual up, because gravity is already pulling the carriage down."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Step 4: Check directions and the scale","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#step-4-check-directions-and-the-scale","t":"Deploy with the elevator all the way down . In Elastic, add Height (m) from Telemetry → Elevator . Direction: Enable. Hold D-pad up for a moment, gently. The carriage must go up , and Height must increase . What you see Fix Carriage doesn't move at 2 V Gravity is stronger than 2 V. Try 3 V (stay at or under MaxVolts ). Carriage goes down on D-pad up Set MotorInverted = true Carriage goes up, but Height decreases Set EncoderInverted = true Don't continue until both directions are right. Scale: check that the pitch diameter math matches the real elevator. Lower the elevator to the bottom and press Back (zero). Use D-pad up to raise it partway. Disable. Measure how far the carriage rose with a tape measure, in meters (or inches × 0.0254). Compare it with Height on the dashboard. They should match within about a centimeter. What you see Likely cause Height is about 4 times too small DistancePerPulse divides by 8192 instead of 2048 Height is off by some other steady ratio Wrong pitch diameter, gearing between the encoder and the sprocket, or a cascade elevator (where the top stage moves faster than the chain) If it's off and you can't find why, scale it from your measurement: change MetersPerRevolution to MetersPerRevolution × (tape measure ÷ Height) ."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Step 5: Find the top","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#step-5-find-the-top","t":"Use D-pad up to raise the elevator slowly toward the top. Stop a few centimeters before the hard stop and read Height . Set MaxHeightMeters to that number, minus 2 to 3 cm of safety margin. Deploy. Now hold D-pad up near the top. The soft limit should stop the motor just below your max."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Step 6: Preset heights","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#step-6-preset-heights","t":"Pick three heights between 0 and your max, and put them in StartMeters , L2Meters , and L3Meters . Then bind them. Add import frc.robot.subsystems.ElevatorSubsystem.Preset; at the top of RobotContainer : m_operator . a (). onTrue ( m_elevator . goToPresetCommand ( Preset . START )); m_operator . b (). onTrue ( m_elevator . goToPresetCommand ( Preset . L2 )); m_operator . y (). onTrue ( m_elevator . goToPresetCommand ( Preset . L3 )); Set up Elastic for tuning: one Graph with Goal , Setpoint , and Height together, plus Text Display widgets for kP , kI , kD , Max Velocity , and Max Accel , and Error and Output (V) . Press B . Watch the graph: Goal jumps to L2, Setpoint ramps smoothly up to it, and Height follows the setpoint."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Attempt 1: P only, and the sag","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#attempt-1-p-only-and-the-sag","t":"With kI and kD at 0 and kP = 10, press B (L2) and let it settle. What you'll see: The carriage follows the setpoint a little below it the whole way up, then settles below the goal and stays there. That gap is sag , and gravity causes it. Here's why. To hold the carriage still, the motor needs some voltage, call it the hold voltage . P only makes voltage when there's error: volts = kP × error So the carriage sinks until the error is just big enough to make the hold voltage: sag = hold voltage ÷ kP Measure it. Fill in this table, waiting for the elevator to settle each time: kP Sag (Error, m) Output (V) while holding kP × sag 10 20 40 The last column should come out about the same every time. That number is your elevator's hold voltage: what it costs to fight gravity. Think about it Doubling kP cuts the sag in half. Why can't you just make kP huge to get rid of the sag?"},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Attempt 2: Higher P, and the bounce","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#attempt-2-higher-p-and-the-bounce","t":"Keep raising kP in Elastic ( Tunables → Elevator ). Move between presets A , B , and Y . What you'll see: The sag shrinks. Because the profile slows the setpoint down before the goal, there's much less overshoot than a plain PID would have, but with very high kP the carriage starts to jitter or bounce around the setpoint. Moving down usually bounces more than moving up: on the way down, gravity is helping the motor. Back kP off to the highest value that doesn't bounce badly."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Attempt 3: Add D to stop the bounce","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#attempt-3-add-d-to-stop-the-bounce","t":"Raise kD in small steps (try 0.5, then 1.0, then 2.0) until the bounce settles. If the motor starts to buzz, kD is too high. With D tamping down the bounce, you can usually raise kP a bit more, which shrinks the sag further. But the sag never completely goes away."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Attempt 4: Add I to get rid of the sag","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#attempt-4-add-i-to-get-rid-of-the-sag","t":"This is where I earns its place. While the carriage sits below the goal, the integral keeps adding up that error, so the voltage keeps rising until it's exactly the hold voltage, and the error goes to zero . Set kI small, like 2. Press B . Watch the graph: the carriage arrives a little low, then creeps up to the goal over a second or two. Watch Output (V) as it creeps. It climbs until it reaches about the hold voltage you measured in Attempt 1. Raise kI until the creep is quick but doesn't overshoot. Too much kI makes the carriage slowly swing above and below the goal. Why the IZone matters here During a long move from START to L3, the carriage trails the setpoint the whole way. Without a limit, I would pile up that error on the way and fling the carriage past L3. setIZone(0.063) only lets I build up within 6.3 cm, where it's needed for the last bit."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Attempt 5: Speed up the profile","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#attempt-5-speed-up-the-profile","t":"The profile limits are tunable too. Raise Max Velocity and Max Accel in Elastic a little at a time, and move between presets. What you'll see: Moves get faster. Then, at some point, Height falls further and further behind Setpoint during the move, and overshoots at the end. That's the motor running out of power: the profile is asking for more than the elevator can do (or more than MaxVolts allows). Back the limits off to where Height stays close to Setpoint."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Attempt 6: Put it all together","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#attempt-6-put-it-all-together","t":"With your best gains and limits: Move START → L3 → L2 → START. Every preset should arrive smoothly, without much bounce, and settle exactly on the goal. Carefully set a light object (like a bag of small parts) on the carriage while it holds at L2. Watch it dip, then watch I bring it back to the goal. Remove it, and watch it recover the other way. Copy your gains and limits into Constants (dashboard edits are lost on reboot)."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Scorecard","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#scorecard","t":"Gains Arrives in (s) Overshoot (m) Error after settling (m) Holds with weight? P only P + D P + I + D P + I + D, faster profile"},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Commit","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#commit","t":"git add . git commit -m \"Lab 10: elevator position control\""},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"When it doesn't work","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#when-it-doesnt-work","t":"Problem Likely cause Elevator runs away upward and stops at the soft limit Motor and encoder directions disagree. Redo the direction check in Step 4. Elevator doesn't move when you press a preset kP is too small to overcome gravity, or the preset is outside the min/max limits Elevator falls a little after a preset command finishes The default hold command isn't set Elevator lurches the instant you enable The disabled check in periodic() is missing Every height is 4 times too small DistancePerPulse divides by 8192 instead of 2048 Height falls far behind Setpoint during moves Max Velocity or Max Accel is too high for the elevator Heights are all wrong after a restart The elevator wasn't at the bottom when the robot turned on. Lower it and press Back. Slow swinging above and below the target I is too high Big overshoot on long moves only IZone is too big or missing, or the profile is too fast Finished code Stuck, or missed a lab? lab10-elevator on GitHub is the complete project as it looks at the end of this lab. It's a full WPILib 2026 project you can open in VS Code, build, and deploy. Try to finish the lab yourself first."},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#try-it","t":"Work out DistancePerPulse from your sprocket's pitch diameter, and check it with a tape measure Find the top and set a safe MaxHeightMeters Fill in the sag table for three values of P and find your hold voltage Tune P, then D, then I, then the profile limits, and fill in the scorecard Do the weight test with P only, then with P + I + D. What's different? Add a fourth preset, L4 , to the enum, presetMeters , and the right bumper Make the WCD's auto raise the elevator to L2, drive forward, then lower it to START. goToPresetCommand ends when it arrives, so you can chain it with .andThen(...) . Challenge: if the elevator has a bottom limit switch, make it re-zero the encoder automatically whenever it's pressed ( Lab 4 )"},{"p":"Lab 10: Elevator Position Control","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab10-elevator.html#check-yourself","t":"What's the difference between the goal and the setpoint? The goal is the preset you picked. The setpoint is where the profile says the carriage should be right now; it moves toward the goal a little every 20 ms. The preset command ends when it arrives. Why doesn't the carriage fall? The hold command is the default command, so it starts again as soon as the preset command ends, and keeps holding the same goal. Why does setGoalMeters call m_pid.reset(...) first? So the profile starts from where the carriage really is, moving at its real speed. Otherwise it could plan from an old position and make the carriage jump. Your sprocket's pitch diameter is 1.757 in. How far does the carriage move per turn? π × 1.757 in ≈ 5.52 in ≈ 0.140 m Why is DistancePerPulse divided by 2048 and not 8192? WPILib's Encoder already accounts for 4X decoding. setDistancePerPulse always wants distance per full cycle, and the Through Bore has 2048 cycles per turn. With P only, why does the elevator settle below the goal? P only makes voltage when there's error. Holding the carriage up takes voltage, so the carriage sinks until the error is big enough to make it: sag = hold voltage ÷ kP. The hold voltage is 0.8 V and kP = 20. How much does the elevator sag with P only? 0.8 ÷ 20 = 0.04 m (4 cm) Why does I remove the sag when P alone can't? I keeps adding up the error for as long as it lasts, so its output keeps growing until it supplies the hold voltage by itself, and the error reaches zero. Why does moving down tend to overshoot more than moving up? Going down, gravity helps the motor, so the carriage speeds up more and carries more momentum into the target. What are the soft limits in setVolts for? They refuse to push past the top or below the bottom, no matter what any command asks for."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Why it matters","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#why-it-matters","t":"In Lab 8 , the robot learned two things: how far each side has rolled (the encoders) and which way it's facing (the NavX2). Each answer is useful on its own. Put together, they answer a much bigger question: where is the robot on the field? That's odometry . Every 20 ms, the robot asks \"how far did I just roll, and which way was I facing?\" and adds that small step to its position. Do that 50 times a second and you get a running estimate of the robot's pose : its X and Y position in meters, and its heading. In this lab you'll add odometry to the West Coast Drive, watch a little robot move on a field map in Elastic as you drive, and calibrate it until the map matches the floor. Path following ( Lesson 19 ) and vision ( Lesson 18 ) both start from the pose you build here. Before you start: finish Lab 8 , including the encoders (Steps 8–12). Read Lesson 17 first. You'll add to the same WestCoastDrive project."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Read this","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#read-this","t":"Lesson 17: Where Is the Robot? Differential Drive Odometry (WPILib) Field2d Widget (WPILib)"},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Walkthrough","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#walkthrough","t":""},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"What you'll build","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#what-youll-build","t":"Piece What it does DifferentialDriveOdometry WPILib's class that turns encoder distances and gyro heading into a pose getPose() Returns where the robot thinks it is resetPoseCommand() Puts the robot back at (0, 0), facing 0° Field2d A field map in Elastic with the robot drawn on it The robot starts at (0, 0) facing 0° , wherever you set it down. Driving forward makes X go up. Driving to the robot's left makes Y go up. Turning left makes the heading go up, the same as the gyro in Lab 8."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Step 1: Measure the track width","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#step-1-measure-the-track-width","t":"Odometry needs one new measurement: the track width , the distance between the left wheels and the right wheels, measured center of wheel to center of wheel , in meters. Lab 8 already has a placeholder for it in Constants.java : public static final double TrackWidthMeters = 0.6 ; Measure your chassis with a tape measure and put the real number in. You'll fine-tune it in Step 9. Why does odometry need the track width if heading comes from the gyro? DifferentialDriveOdometry takes its heading from the gyro, not the encoders, so the track width doesn't change its answer. You'll still need the measured number for path following in Lesson 19 , and Step 9 uses it to check the encoders. Measuring it now means it's right when you need it."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Step 2: Add the odometry and the field","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#step-2-add-the-odometry-and-the-field","t":"Open DriveSubsystem.java . Add these imports at the top with the others: import edu.wpi.first.math.geometry.Pose2d ; import edu.wpi.first.math.kinematics.DifferentialDriveOdometry ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.smartdashboard.Field2d ; import edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.smartdashboard.SmartDashboard ; Then add two fields below the encoders: // Odometry: adds up encoder distances and gyro headings into a position on the field (Lab 11) private final DifferentialDriveOdometry m_odometry ; // A field map for Elastic, with the robot drawn where odometry thinks it is private final Field2d m_field = new Field2d (); m_odometry is final but not set yet. Java lets you set a final field once, in the constructor. You'll do that next, because odometry needs the encoders to be set up first."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Step 3: Create the odometry in the constructor","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#step-3-create-the-odometry-in-the-constructor","t":"At the end of the constructor, after the two setDistancePerPulse lines, add: // Start odometry at (0, 0) facing 0°, using the sensors' current readings as the starting point m_odometry = new DifferentialDriveOdometry ( m_gyro . getRotation2d (), getLeftMeters (), getRightMeters ()); // Show the field map in Elastic (it appears under SmartDashboard/Field) SmartDashboard . putData ( \"Field\" , m_field ); Odometry remembers these first readings. From then on, it only cares how much they change . That's why it doesn't matter whether the encoders read 0 or 3.7 at startup. Why SmartDashboard.putData ? A Field2d is a Sendable , not a single number, so it still goes through SmartDashboard.putData in 2026. Lesson 6.1 explains why. It's sent over NetworkTables 4 like everything else."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Step 4: Update it every loop","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#step-4-update-it-every-loop","t":"In periodic() , add these lines at the top , before the telemetry lines: // Odometry: add this loop's small step to the robot's position m_odometry . update ( m_gyro . getRotation2d (), getLeftMeters (), getRightMeters ()); m_field . setRobotPose ( getPose ()); And at the bottom of periodic() , with the other telemetry: Pose2d pose = getPose (); m_telemetry . log ( \"Odometry/X (m)\" , pose . getX ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Odometry/Y (m)\" , pose . getY ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Odometry/Heading (deg)\" , pose . getRotation (). getDegrees ()); m_telemetry . log ( \"Odometry/Pose\" , pose ); // the whole pose, for AdvantageScope periodic() runs every 20 ms whether or not a command is running, so odometry never misses a step."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Step 5: Read and reset the pose","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#step-5-read-and-reset-the-pose","t":"Add these two methods above periodic() : /** Where odometry thinks the robot is: X and Y in meters, and heading. */ public Pose2d getPose () { return m_odometry . getPoseMeters (); } /** * Tells odometry the robot is at (0, 0) facing 0°, right where it is now. * The encoders and gyro aren't touched. Works while disabled. */ public Command resetPoseCommand () { return runOnce (() -> m_odometry . resetPosition ( m_gyro . getRotation2d (), getLeftMeters (), getRightMeters (), Pose2d . kZero )) . ignoringDisable ( true ) . withName ( \"Reset Pose\" ); } resetPosition needs the sensors' current readings so it knows the new starting point. Pose2d.kZero is (0, 0) facing 0°."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Step 6: Change the reset buttons","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#step-6-change-the-reset-buttons","t":"In Lab 8, Back zeroed the gyro and Start reset the encoders. Both of those now cause trouble: if a sensor reading suddenly jumps to 0, odometry thinks the robot just moved or spun, and the robot on the field map jumps. resetPoseCommand() doesn't touch the sensors, so use it instead. In RobotContainer.java , find these two lines in configureBindings() : m_controller . back (). onTrue ( m_driveSubsystem . zeroHeadingCommand ()); m_controller . start (). onTrue ( m_driveSubsystem . resetEncodersCommand ()); Delete both and put this one in their place: // Start: \"the robot is at (0, 0) facing 0°\" (Lab 11) m_controller . start (). onTrue ( m_driveSubsystem . resetPoseCommand ()); The turn and distance commands from Lab 8 still work. They measure from wherever the sensors are when they start, so they never needed the sensors at 0."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Step 7: Put the field in Elastic","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#step-7-put-the-field-in-elastic","t":"Deploy, then open Elastic. Right-click an empty spot → Add Widget , or open the widget list. Find SmartDashboard → Field and drag it onto the layout. Elastic shows it as a Field widget: a picture of this year's field with a robot on it. Make the widget big. Turn on the robot outline if it's off. Also add Telemetry → Drive → Odometry → X (m) , Y (m) , and Heading (deg) as text displays, so you can see the numbers. With the robot disabled , push it by hand. The robot on the map should move the same way. Press Start and it jumps back to the corner. Why is the robot in the corner? (0, 0) on the field map is the bottom-left corner, on the blue alliance wall. Your robot is really in the shop, so it starts in that corner. In Lesson 18 , cameras tell the robot where it really is on the field."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Step 8: Check the directions","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#step-8-check-the-directions","t":"Enable and drive gently. Check each one: Do this X Y Heading On the map Drive forward 1 m goes up about 1.0 stays near 0 stays near 0 moves right (away from the blue wall) Turn left 90° (press X ) stays stays goes up to about 90 turns to face the top of the map Then drive forward 1 m stays goes up about 1.0 stays near 90 moves up If something goes the wrong way: Problem Fix Driving forward makes X go down Both encoders count backward. Flip LeftEncoderInverted and RightEncoderInverted in Constants. Driving straight makes the robot curve on the map One encoder counts backward. Check each one alone (Lab 8, Step 9) and flip that one. Turning left makes the heading go down You're using getAngle() somewhere. Use getRotation2d() everywhere, as in Lab 8. The map doesn't move at all Check m_odometry.update(...) is in periodic() , and that the field widget is SmartDashboard/Field ."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Step 9: Calibrate","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#step-9-calibrate","t":"Odometry is only as good as its numbers. Two quick tests find most problems. Wheel diameter (straight line). Put tape on the floor, line the robot up on it, and press Start . Drive straight for about 3 m and stop. Measure how far the robot really went with a tape measure. Real distance (m) Odometry X (m) Ratio (real ÷ X) If the ratio isn't close to 1.00, multiply WheelDiameterMeters by it. A 6 in wheel squishes under a heavy robot, so the \"real\" diameter is often a little smaller than 6 in. Run the test again until odometry is within about 2 cm of the tape. Track width (spin in place). This checks TrackWidthMeters against the gyro, which you trust for turns. Press Start , then spin the robot in place to the left exactly 5 full turns (watch the gyro heading reach about 1800°). Read Encoders/Left (m) and Encoders/Right (m) from Lab 8 before and after. effective track width = (Δ right − Δ left) ÷ (turns × 2π) For 5 turns, divide by 5 × 2π ≈ 31.42 . Δ Left (m) Δ Right (m) Effective track width (m) Tape-measured track width (m) On a skid-steer like the WCD, the effective number usually comes out bigger than the tape measure, because the wheels slide sideways while turning (you saw this in Lab 8, Step 11). Path following works better with the effective number, so write both down. You'll use the effective one in Lesson 19 ."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Step 10: Drive the sensor square","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#step-10-drive-the-sensor-square","t":"Select Square in the Auto dropdown (since Lab 8 it drives by the encoders and gyro), set the robot on a tape mark, press Start , and run autonomous. When it finishes, the robot should be back near (0, 0) on the map. Fill in: Run Final X (m) Final Y (m) Final heading (°) Real distance from the tape mark (cm) 1 2 3 Compare the map with the floor. If odometry says (0.02, 0.01) but the robot is 15 cm from the mark, odometry has drifted: the wheels slipped, and the encoders counted rolling that didn't move the robot."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Step 11: Commit","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#step-11-commit","t":"git add . git commit -m \"Lab 11: odometry and field map\""},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"When it doesn't work","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#when-it-doesnt-work","t":"Symptom Likely cause Robot on the map jumps when you press Back You still have the old zero-heading binding. Step 6. Pose slowly spins while the robot sits still The NavX was moved while calibrating. Power-cycle with the robot still (Lab 8, Step 1). X is about twice (or half) the real distance EncoderCyclesPerRev is wrong. It's 2048 for the Through Bore Encoder. Pose drifts sideways after hard turns Normal for skid-steer wheels sliding. Calibrate (Step 9), and drive turns gently. Vision fixes this for real in Lesson 18 . variable m_odometry might not have been initialized The m_odometry = new ... line from Step 3 isn't in the constructor."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Piece by piece","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#piece-by-piece","t":"private final DifferentialDriveOdometry m_odometry ; The odometry object. It's declared here but built in the constructor, after the encoders know their distance per pulse. m_odometry = new DifferentialDriveOdometry ( m_gyro . getRotation2d (), getLeftMeters (), getRightMeters ()); Gives odometry its starting readings. It starts at the pose (0, 0, 0°) unless you pass a fourth argument with a different starting pose. m_odometry . update ( m_gyro . getRotation2d (), getLeftMeters (), getRightMeters ()); The heart of odometry. It compares these readings with last loop's. The change in the average encoder distance says how far the robot rolled; the gyro says which way it was facing. It adds that step to the pose. m_field . setRobotPose ( getPose ()); Moves the robot on Elastic's field map to match. m_telemetry . log ( \"Odometry/Pose\" , pose ); Sends the whole Pose2d as one value. Elastic can't graph it, but AdvantageScope can draw it on a 2D or 3D field. See Lesson 20 . m_odometry . resetPosition ( m_gyro . getRotation2d (), getLeftMeters (), getRightMeters (), Pose2d . kZero ) \"Whatever the sensors read right now, call this spot (0, 0) facing 0°.\" Odometry stores the readings as the new starting point."},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Try it","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#try-it","t":"Watch the robot on the field map while pushing the robot by hand Check all three rows of the direction table in Step 8 Calibrate the wheel diameter until a 3 m drive is within 2 cm Measure the effective track width and compare it with the tape-measure number Run the sensor square 3 times and fill in the table Drive a figure eight in teleop and come back to the start mark. How far off is odometry? Challenge: add resetPoseCommand(Pose2d pose) that sets any pose, and start the robot at (2, 4) facing 180° Challenge: write driveToXCommand(double x) that drives until the pose's X reaches a value"},{"p":"Lab 11: Track the Robot on the Field","h":"Check yourself","u":"testbed/lab11-odometry.html#check-yourself","t":"What three numbers make up a pose? X and Y position (meters) and the heading (which way the robot faces). Why does differential drive odometry take its heading from the gyro instead of the encoders? The wheels slide sideways when a skid-steer turns, so the encoders say it turned more than it really did. The gyro measures the real rotation. Why does pressing the old Back button (zero gyro) make the robot jump on the map? Odometry compares each reading with the last one. A gyro that suddenly reads 0 looks like the robot spun instantly, so the pose spins too. Why must m_odometry.update(...) be in periodic() ? Odometry adds up small steps. periodic() runs every loop, even when no command is running. Skipping loops means missing steps. Odometry says the robot is back at the start, but it's 15 cm away. Why? Odometry drifts. Wheel slip, a slightly wrong wheel diameter, and bumps all add small errors, and nothing corrects them. Vision is what corrects drift. Parts of this lab are adapted from the Odometry and Pose Estimation Calibration lessons of Mantik by Itkan Robotics (CC BY 4.0), rewritten for the RoboJacks West Coast Drive."}];
